	],
	"pre:s_break5": [
		["Narrator", "Rivers of flame gather overhead and form a spiralling funnel. The Heavens test the Deity who would pass into the Void."],
	],
	"post:s_break5": [
		["Narrator", "The fire fades into cold starlight. Lin Feng stands in Void Refinement, no longer bound by the weight of the earth."],
		["Sage Huo Rong", "The Void Rift lies beyond the volcano's western slope. Find Hermit Wu Ji. He knows what the Emperor is."],
	],
	"s_vd_intro": [
		["Hermit Wu Ji", "You came through the fire. Few do. Sit. There is no hurry in the void."],
		["Hermit Wu Ji", "The Jade Emperor of Ruin was once the first ascended being. He has been consuming the heavens to make himself a god without equals."],
		["Hermit Wu Ji", "Collect four Star Fragments. Forged together, they can pierce the Gate that he guards."],
		["Yue Ling", "And the horrors?"],
		["Hermit Wu Ji", "They are his tears. Cut them down, and cut down the four who shepherd them."],
	],
	"pre:rift_weaver": [
		["Rift Weaver", "Tssss... fresh threads for my loom. Such bright, bright threads..."],
		["Yue Ling", "Fire will burn its silk!"],
	],
	"post:rift_weaver": [
		["Lin Feng", "Its web is gone. The way deeper is clear."],
	],
	"pre:star_devourer": [
		["Star Devourer", "Hungry. Hungry. Your light tastes of dawn."],
		["Hermit Wu Ji", "It swallowed a dozen constellations. Lightning will tear it open!"],
	],
	"post:star_devourer": [
		["Lin Feng", "The stars it swallowed drift free again. Beautiful."],
	],
	"pre:void_sentinel": [
		["Void Sentinel", "INTRUDER. THE EMPEROR'S DECREE: NONE SHALL PASS."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then break the decree, or I will."],
	],
	"post:void_sentinel": [
		["Void Sentinel", "DECREE... FULFILLED... THE GATE... STILL STANDS..."],
	],
	"pre:chaos_serpent": [
		["Chaos Serpent", "Ssssso small, so bright. The Emperor will wear your light as a crown."],
		["Yue Ling", "It feeds on hesitation. Strike first, Lin Feng!"],
	],
	"post:chaos_serpent": [
		["Hermit Wu Ji", "You have done what I could not in a thousand years. The Star Fragments will pierce the Celestial Gate for you. Be ready for him."],
	],
	"s_ce_intro": [
		["Gatekeeper Bai Ze", "You have the look of someone who has already won, and lost, and won again. Welcome to the Celestial Gate."],
		["Gatekeeper Bai Ze", "Beyond this Gate sits the Jade Emperor of Ruin. His general, his judge, his sacred beast, and his throne guard the way."],
		["Gatekeeper Bai Ze", "Collect four Heaven Sigils to unseal the Gate, and cleanse its fallen guardians."],
		["Lin Feng", "And then?"],
		["Gatekeeper Bai Ze", "Then the heavens decide who deserves them. The Gate has opened for three, in ten thousand years. Remember that number."],
	],
	"pre:heaven_general": [
		["Heavenly General Tian Wei", "Mortal-born. Unworthy. I shall return you to the dust from which you were raised."],
		["Yue Ling", "His armor is blessed. The dark will crack it!"],
	],
	"post:heaven_general": [
		["Heavenly General Tian Wei", "I served the Heavens... not him... forgive me..."],
	],
	"pre:lightning_magistrate": [
		["Lightning Magistrate", "The court is in session. The charge: ascending without leave. The sentence: thunder."],
		["Lin Feng", "I appeal the verdict, with my sword."],
	],
	"post:lightning_magistrate": [
		["Lightning Magistrate", "Case dismissed... the Qilin awaits..."],
	],
	"pre:azure_qilin": [
		["Azure Qilin", "I was a guardian once. Now I only remember the thunder. Forgive me, child."],
	],
	"post:azure_qilin": [
		["Azure Qilin", "Thank you. The Emperor's throne is a mirror. Do not look away."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, this is it."],
	],
	"pre:heavenly_emperor": [
		["Jade Emperor of Ruin", "At last, the Nine Heavens root. I have waited a thousand years for you to ripen."],
		["Lin Feng", "I am not fruit to be picked."],
		["Jade Emperor of Ruin", "Every flame I have lit has led you here. Come. Become my crown."],
		["Yue Ling", "I'm with you, always."],
	],
	"post:heavenly_emperor": [
		["Jade Emperor of Ruin", "Impossible... a sweeper... who sweeps even gods aside..."],
		["Narrator", "The Emperor's throne shatters. The heavens above ring like a great bell, then fall silent."],
		["Yue Ling", "It's over. The sky is whole again. Lin Feng, one last trial awaits."],
	],
	"pre:s_break6": [
		["Narrator", "Nine layers of tribulation cloud swallow the sky. The Heavens do not let any being merge with the Dao without trial."],
		["Lin Feng", "Come, then. I have swept these steps for three years. I fear nothing."],
	],
	"post:s_break6": [
		["Narrator", "The final bolt falls and, with it, the last chain between Lin Feng and the Dao. He ascends to Dao Merging, and the heavens open their gates."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, your sword... and the lotus mark over your dantian, it is glowing. It has never done that before."],
	],
	"recall": [
		["Narrator", "The Lotus Seal that has slept in Lin Feng's dantian since the day he swept the sect's steps blazes like a second sun, and the world folds around him."],
		["Narrator", "When the light fades, he is standing beside the lotus pond of Azure Cloud Sect. Elder Qing Xuan is waiting, grey-faced, with the Seal's chime ringing like a cracked bell."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You felt it too. Come, Lin Feng. There is a truth I have kept for sixty years, and the Seal will not let me keep it any longer."],
	],
	# ---- Part III: The Hollow Heaven
	"s_p3_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Xu Mo is dead and the Jade Emperor is dead, and still the Seal screams. Sit, Lin Feng. I owe you the whole truth."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I am a descendant of Qing Lian, who founded this sect. She and the sword ancestor Jian Wuchen forged the Heavenly Lotus Seal. It was never a wall against demons. It hides the Nine Heavens root."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The root is a seed of the first Dao. It passes to a new bearer each time the old one dies. You are the twentieth. Xu Mo spent three centuries hunting the nineteen before you."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then the demon lord was only a hunter. Who was the Emperor?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Tian Xuan, the first ascended being. He learned that every tribulation is a harvest: the Heavens swallow the Dao of whoever endures them. He tried to starve the thing by hoarding the heavens himself, and he needed your root to finish it."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "By killing him you have woken what he was starving: the Hollow Heaven. The Seal is breaking because it is afraid. The Lotus Gate at the foot of our pond now leads to the Mirror Lake, where every bearer's memory sleeps."],
		["Yue Ling", "Nineteen bearers before him. Elder, why does the lotus pond feel so familiar to me?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Go. Learn what the lake remembers. Then we will see who is ready to hear the rest."],
	],
	"s_ml_intro": [
		["Keeper Lan Jing", "A bearer who walks in the daylight. The lake has not seen one in three hundred years."],
		["Keeper Lan Jing", "Every ascender who ever crossed this water left an echo in it. The echoes have curdled and grown teeth. Gather four Echo Shards, and the Mirror of Bearers will wake."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the echoes themselves?"],
		["Keeper Lan Jing", "Break the hungry ones. Listen to the rest. Some of them are still trying to warn you."],
	],
	"pre:shard_mimic": [
		["Shard Mimic", "I wear your face, little bearer. I wear it better than you do."],
		["Yue Ling", "It copies whoever strikes it. Hit it with what it cannot imitate: the dark!"],
	],
	"post:shard_mimic": [
		["Lin Feng", "It broke into a hundred of me. None of them were smiling."],
	],
	"pre:glass_colossus": [
		["Glass Colossus", "I was raised to guard the Mirror from the Emperor's hands. I cannot tell friend from foe through all this cracked glass."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then shatter it gently, Lin Feng. It means no harm."],
	],
	"post:glass_colossus": [
		["Glass Colossus", "Thank you... the Mirror is open... the seventh bearer waits below the surface..."],
	],
	"pre:drowned_bearer": [
		["Drowned Bearer Shui Ying", "Another one. Another one! Xu Mo promised me you were the last."],
		["Lin Feng", "I am the twentieth. You were the seventh. I am sorry."],
		["Drowned Bearer Shui Ying", "Then die gently, and I will die with you."],
	],
	"post:drowned_bearer": [
		["Drowned Bearer Shui Ying", "Xu Mo drowned me here so the Emperor could drink my root. He wept the whole time. Remember that, twentieth. The hunter wept."],
		["Narrator", "Where she sank, an Echo Shard glimmers, and the lake begins to show the face of the one who hunted her."],
	],
	"pre:echo_xumo": [
		["Echo of Xu Mo", "You killed my body, bearer. The lake kept my regret."],
		["Echo of Xu Mo", "I was the Emperor's first apprentice. He swore that one root, fully ripened, would end the harvest for everyone. I believed him. I fed him nineteen children."],
		["Lin Feng", "And you meant to feed him a twentieth."],
		["Echo of Xu Mo", "I meant to. Now do me one kindness: strike me, so I can stop remembering."],
	],
	"post:echo_xumo": [
		["Echo of Xu Mo", "The first bearer lies in the Graveyard of Sword Saints, past the lake's far shore. Qing Lian still holds the Seal's key. She does not know the Emperor is dead."],
		["Yue Ling", "I never thought I would pity him."],
		["Lin Feng", "Neither did I. Let us go to the graveyard."],
	],
	"s_gv_intro": [
		["Gravekeeper Mu Gu", "Living feet. It has been a long while. Every sword saint in this ground swore to guard the Seal, and the Seal swore to guard them."],
		["Gravekeeper Mu Gu", "Four Saint Relics, scattered by the Emperor's grave-robbers, will remake the Seal's key. Bring them, and walk softly. The dead here are not at peace."],
	],
	"pre:bone_wyrm": [
		["Bone Wyrm", "...rrrrrattle... the Emperor's whistle... bring... living... bones..."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is bound to a summoning. Thunder will break the bonds."],
	],
	"post:bone_wyrm": [
		["Lin Feng", "Its bones are sleeping again. Whoever bound it was cruel."],
	],
	"pre:headless_saint": [
		["Headless Sword Saint", "..."],
		["Narrator", "The headless saint salutes with his sword, the old formal bow of the Azure Cloud Sect. He has no voice, but his stance is a challenge."],
		["Lin Feng", "A duel, then. Honour to the Sect."],
	],
	"post:headless_saint": [
		["Narrator", "The saint lowers his blade and places it at Lin Feng's feet. A Saint Relic glows on the hilt."],
		["Yue Ling", "He refused to serve the Emperor, even after they took his head."],
	],
	"pre:grave_empress": [
		["Empress of Bones", "Every saint I buried was my husband in another life. Come, bearer. Join the family."],
		["Yue Ling", "She is the Emperor's grief, given a throne. Fire will clear the dust."],
	],
	"post:grave_empress": [
		["Empress of Bones", "Qing Lian waits beyond my gate. Tell her... I kept her bed warm... all these centuries..."],
	],
	"pre:founder_qing": [
		["Founder Qing Lian", "The Seal's key resonates in you, child. Twenty bearers, and not one has reached my grave alive."],
		["Founder Qing Lian", "I must be certain you are not the Emperor wearing a new face. Draw your sword."],
		["Lin Feng", "I would rather ask you questions than fight you."],
		["Founder Qing Lian", "Questions after. Defend yourself!"],
	],
	"post:founder_qing": [
		["Founder Qing Lian", "Your sword is honest. Good. Listen: the Seal was never meant to be a prison. It was a door I locked from the outside, hoping a worthy hand would one day open it."],
		["Founder Qing Lian", "Tell the girl at your side that her lotus has bloomed beautifully. She has no idea what she is, and that is how I wanted it. Now go. The River of Ten Thousand Years runs under the world. Cross it, and you will find the Emperor's beginning."],
		["Yue Ling", "What does she mean, 'what I am'?"],
		["Lin Feng", "We will ask her the next time we meet. First, I must break through."],
	],
	"pre:s_break7": [
		["Narrator", "The graveyard's old sword-light gathers into a pillar. The Heavens, wary now, test the Dao-Merger who dares call the harvest a lie."],
		["Lin Feng", "Take what you will, Heaven. You will not take this."],
	],
	"post:s_break7": [
		["Narrator", "The last bolt breaks against the Lotus Seal and falls harmlessly away. Lin Feng stands in Mahayana. For the first time the lightning did not feel like a thief."],
		["Yue Ling", "The Seal protected you. Lin Feng, it was warm against my hand when it did."],
	],
	"s_tm_intro": [
		["Ferryman Shi Guang", "Passengers for the headwaters? There has been no passenger since the world began. Sit, bearer. Mind the sand."],
		["Ferryman Shi Guang", "Four pinches of Time Sand will tether our boat to the present. Without them, we might arrive before we left."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the creatures on the banks?"],
		["Ferryman Shi Guang", "Moments the harvest left behind. They are lonely, and lonely things bite."],
	],
	"pre:time_tortoise": [
		["Eon Tortoise", "I have been walking toward you for nine thousand years. You are quick, for a mortal."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is slow, but it hits like a mountain. Be patient."],
	],
	"post:time_tortoise": [
		["Eon Tortoise", "At last... a moment I may rest in. The Reaper walks the dusk bank, child."],
	],
	"pre:dusk_reaper": [
		["Dusk Reaper", "Every ending is mine. I merely collect. Yours is overdue."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I am glad to be late."],
	],
	"post:dusk_reaper": [
		["Dusk Reaper", "An ending refused... how strange... then I shall take the Emperor's, when it comes..."],
	],
	"pre:dawn_warden": [
		["Dawn Warden", "I sing the first morning of every age. Your blade will not stop the sun, little sweeper."],
		["Yue Ling", "Quench her fire gently. She is not our enemy."],
	],
	"post:dawn_warden": [
		["Dawn Warden", "He swept this very bank, the boy who became the Emperor. Go on. He is waiting at the source, and he is still a boy."],
	],
	"pre:young_tianxuan": [
		["Tian Xuan, the First Sweeper", "A broom! You carry a broom's soul under that sword. We are alike, you and I."],
		["Tian Xuan, the First Sweeper", "I swept the first sect's steps for three years before I learned what the tribulations were. A harvest! Every ascender fed to the same mouth. I swore to end it, whatever it cost."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the cost was nineteen children."],
		["Tian Xuan, the First Sweeper", "It was always going to be a cost. Show me yours is smaller."],
	],
	"post:young_tianxuan": [
		["Tian Xuan, the First Sweeper", "The river remembers me better than I do. I wanted the harvest to end. I forgot to ask whether anyone else had to pay."],
		["Tian Xuan, the First Sweeper", "The mouth is at the Origin. It is not a monster, only an appetite. Do not starve it as I did. Teach it to stop."],
		["Narrator", "The boy smiles and dissolves into the river's light. Upstream, the way to the Origin of the Dao opens."],
	],
	"s_or_intro": [
		["Bai Ze, Keeper of the Origin", "I told you the Gate had opened for three, bearer. I did not say who the third would be."],
		["Bai Ze, Keeper of the Origin", "The Hollow Heaven is stirring beyond these runes. Four Origin Runes will let you stand before it without being unmade."],
		["Yue Ling", "You knew we would come."],
		["Bai Ze, Keeper of the Origin", "I knew you would come, girl of the lotus. I did not know whether you would be ready."],
	],
	"pre:maw_herald": [
		["Maw Herald", "Tssss... new threads for the great loom. The harvest is overdue."],
		["Yue Ling", "Burn it, Lin Feng. Its webs are woven of stolen Dao!"],
	],
	"post:maw_herald": [
		["Maw Herald", "The Hollow... will not stop... for a sweeper..."],
	],
	"pre:hollow_dragon": [
		["Hollow Heaven Dragon", "I am what remains of every dragon that ever ascended. We are very, very hungry."],
		["Lin Feng", "I will not let you eat anyone else."],
	],
	"post:hollow_dragon": [
		["Hollow Heaven Dragon", "Thank you for the quiet... take my last breath... it is only thunder..."],
	],
	"pre:harvest_avatar": [
		["Avatar of the Harvest", "I am the Heavens' hand. Each tribulation was my gentle sweeping. You have refused my broom, little bearer."],
		["Lin Feng", "I have swept enough steps to know a broom when I see one."],
	],
	"post:harvest_avatar": [
		["Avatar of the Harvest", "Then end the appetite yourself. The Hollow waits at the centre. Only the root can quiet it, and the root must be... spent."],
		["Yue Ling", "Spent? Lin Feng, what does that mean?"],
	],
	"pre:hollow_heaven": [
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, wait. I remember now. Founder Qing Lian wove me from the Seal's lotus so the bearer would never face the end alone. I am not a wandering healer. I am the Seal."],
		["Yue Ling", "To quiet the Hollow, the root must be given to it, and the Seal must shut the door behind. One of us must be spent. It should be me."],
		["Lin Feng", "No. I have swept these steps for three years, and you walked them with me for the rest. Nobody is being spent."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng..."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Emperor starved it. The Heavens fed it. Nobody ever tried to teach it to be full. Stand with me, Yue Ling. We try something new."],
		["The Hollow Heaven", "...hungry..."],
	],
	"post:hollow_heaven": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng does not strike the final blow. He opens his dantian, and the Nine Heavens root flows into the Hollow, not as food but as a seed. Yue Ling's lotus blooms around it like a door held open."],
		["The Hollow Heaven", "...warm... is this... being full?..."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is learning, Lin Feng. It is learning to stop."],
		["Narrator", "The Hollow's endless hunger settles into a slow tide, and the dead cloud-sky above the Origin turns, at last, to dawn. One tribulation remains: the Heavens' last, reluctant test."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng... your dantian. Something green is growing there. A seed that is given is a seed that is planted, and the Hollow's dawn has planted one back in you, with every branch the old root had woken."],
	],
	"pre:s_break8": [
		["Narrator", "The sky tears itself open for the final time. Every tribulation ever cast gathers into a single spiral of light, and the Heavens ask whether the bearer will keep the harvest or end it."],
		["Lin Feng", "I will end it. Not by taking more, but by giving nothing up. Come."],
	],
	"post:s_break8": [
		["Narrator", "The last bolt strikes the Seal, the Seal blooms, and the lightning falls as warm rain. Lin Feng ascends as a True Immortal."],
		["Yue Ling", "I am still here. The Seal is a door now, not a lock. Look, Lin Feng. Look at the sky."],
		["Narrator", "The dawn spreads gold across the clouds, and across the gold, in a slow and careful hand, someone is writing in jade ink. Nineteen names. A twentieth, still wet."],
		["Lin Feng", "That is my name."],
		["Narrator", "Far below, the Lotus Seal's chime rings once, sharp as a summons."],
	],
	"recall2": [
		["Narrator", "The Seal folds the world again. Lin Feng and Yue Ling stand on the sect's steps, where a boy once swept, and the steps are covered in jade-green dust."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You came. Good. Look at your hands, Lin Feng, and then look at mine."],
		["Narrator", "The Elder's hair, grey at dawn, is white as salt. Behind him the disciples of Azure Cloud Sect move like old men."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Since the sky started writing, everyone who ever broke through has been growing old by the hour. Come inside. I know what the writing is."],
	],
	# ---- Part IV: The Unwritten Ledger
	"s_p4_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The harvest had a master, and the master had servants. The Heavenly Court kept a book of every soul the tribulations took: the Jade Ledger."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Its master is gone and its debtor, the Hollow, is full. But a ledger does not care who reads it. It only wants its columns to balance."],
		["Lin Feng", "So it is collecting from the living instead."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "A tithe of years from every cultivator who ever breathed past a tribulation. Mine, the disciples', yours. And it is writing the nineteen bearers back into its pages, so it can spend them again."],
		["Yue Ling", "The nineteen... they were finally at rest."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Court's stairway has come down through the Sea of Clouds, just beyond the west gate. I have opened it. The Cloud Warden, Feng Yu, keeps the islands; she was a friend of Qing Lian."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Find the Ledger, Lin Feng. Not to rule it. To close it."],
		["Lin Feng", "I swept these steps for three years. I can sweep up one book."],
	],
	"s_cs_intro": [
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "A True Immortal who arrives on foot. Qing Lian said there would be one, someday, and that I would not believe it."],
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "The Court's clerks have turned my islands into a tollgate. Everything that rises past them pays in years. My rays and sprites have been taught to collect."],
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "Bring me four Cloud Feathers. I will weave you a cloud-step, so the Herald of the Ledger cannot strand you between islands."],
		["Yue Ling", "And the creatures?"],
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "Free the ones you can. The rest have forgotten they ever belonged to the sky."],
	],
	"s_cs_weave": [
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "Four Cloud Feathers, and not one of them taken from a living bird. Qing Lian chose well, even twenty bearers late."],
		["Narrator", "Feng Yu draws the feathers through her fingers. They unravel into threads of mist, and the threads knot themselves into a pale shawl that settles over Lin Feng's shoulders."],
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "Your cloud-step. Where you walk, the sky will hold you, even if the Herald cuts every bridge."],
		["Yue Ling", "The islands are quieter since we drove the collectors off. I can hear the wind again."],
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "Then listen to it. Four of the Ledger's beasts still guard the stair: the Manta, the Mantis, the Serpent that was once a bridge, and the Herald himself. Free them in that order and the stair will open."],
	],
	"pre:manta_sovereign": [
		["Sky Manta Sovereign", "Toll, little immortal. Every wing that passes owes the Ledger a year."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let us see whose wings are faster."],
	],
	"post:manta_sovereign": [
		["Sky Manta Sovereign", "...the toll-chains are gone... I had forgotten how wide the sky is..."],
	],
	"pre:gale_mantis": [
		["Gale Mantis Empress", "My children harvest the wind for the Court. You are standing in my field."],
		["Yue Ling", "She remembers the Jade Mantis of the bamboo forest, Lin Feng. The Ledger keeps every grudge."],
	],
	"post:gale_mantis": [
		["Gale Mantis Empress", "The wind... is mine again... take a feather, and go..."],
	],
	"pre:rainbow_serpent": [
		["Rainbow Serpent", "I was the bridge the ascenders walked. The Court painted me in seven colours of debt."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I will wash the paint off."],
	],
	"post:rainbow_serpent": [
		["Rainbow Serpent", "Walk across me, bearer, one last time. The Herald waits at the stair's foot, counting."],
	],
	"pre:herald_qian": [
		["Herald of the Ledger, Qian Shu", "Lin Feng, twentieth bearer, debtor of the Hollow Heaven. Your account is overdue by three years of sweeping and one ascension."],
		["Herald of the Ledger, Qian Shu", "Surrender your years and the columns will balance. Nobody else need pay."],
		["Lin Feng", "You said that to the nineteen, too."],
		["Herald of the Ledger, Qian Shu", "And they paid. The Ledger does not lie. It only counts."],
	],
	"post:herald_qian": [
		["Herald of the Ledger, Qian Shu", "Impossible... a debtor who will not settle... the Registrar must... re-audit..."],
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "The stair to the Jade Court is open, and your cloud-step will carry you up it. Go, before the clerks rewrite the islands."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, your hair. There is grey in it."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then we had better hurry."],
	],
	"s_ct_intro": [
		["Scribe Wen Yu", "Please, do not strike! I am only a scribe. I ran when I read the names I was copying."],
		["Scribe Wen Yu", "Grand Registrar Si Ming holds the brush that writes the Ledger. But the Ledger itself was taken from the Court long ago, to the Garden of Immortal Peaches, where the ascenders' souls were kept fresh."],
		["Scribe Wen Yu", "Four Jade Seals of the old Court are scattered in these ruins. With them, the Registrar's stamps will not bind you. Without them, every step you take here is recorded as a debt."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I will collect them first. Stay out of sight, Wen Yu."],
	],
	"s_ct_stamp": [
		["Scribe Wen Yu", "You found all four! The Seal of Rain, the Seal of Grain, the Seal of Years and the Seal of Names."],
		["Scribe Wen Yu", "Hold still. If I stamp them on your sleeve, the Court's clerks will read you as already audited. They cannot add a debt to a closed account."],
		["Narrator", "Four green stamps bloom on Lin Feng's sleeve, warm as a hand on the shoulder. The grey at his temples stops spreading."],
		["Lin Feng", "And you, Wen Yu? The clerks will know you helped."],
		["Scribe Wen Yu", "I copied ten thousand names without reading them. Let them know. Two lions guard the gate, an ink dragon swims the archive, and the Magistrate sits before the Registrar's hall. Go."],
	],
	"pre:twin_lions": [
		["Twin Lions of the Jade Gate", "Who seeks the Court? Name and account, name and account!"],
		["Lin Feng", "Lin Feng. Account closed."],
	],
	"post:twin_lions": [
		["Twin Lions of the Jade Gate", "...closed... no such entry... the gate... yields..."],
	],
	"pre:ink_dragon": [
		["Ink Dragon", "I am every word the Court ever wrote, poured into one body. Read me, bearer, and drown."],
		["Yue Ling", "Water dissolves ink. Let me help you wash it away!"],
	],
	"post:ink_dragon": [
		["Narrator", "The dragon runs into the courtyard stones as harmless black rain. Somewhere in the ruins a column of names blurs and becomes unreadable."],
	],
	"pre:debt_magistrate": [
		["Magistrate of Debts", "Order! The defendant Lin Feng is charged with refusing the harvest, unbalancing the Ledger and freeing a Hollow."],
		["Lin Feng", "Guilty of all three."],
		["Magistrate of Debts", "Sentence: payment in full!"],
	],
	"post:debt_magistrate": [
		["Magistrate of Debts", "Objection... sustained... the Registrar alone may... amend..."],
	],
	"pre:registrar_siming": [
		["Grand Registrar Si Ming", "I wrote every name the tribulations took, for ten thousand years, in a hand that never shook. Do you know why?"],
		["Grand Registrar Si Ming", "Because a written debt is a remembered one. If I close the Ledger, the nineteen are forgotten. Nobody will know they ever lived."],
		["Yue Ling", "We will remember them. That is not your job any more."],
		["Grand Registrar Si Ming", "Then prove your memory is stronger than my ink."],
	],
	"post:registrar_siming": [
		["Grand Registrar Si Ming", "My brush... broken... Take it, then. A broken brush can still unwrite, if it is reforged with star-iron."],
		["Grand Registrar Si Ming", "Heaven's Forge lies past the Court's furnaces. Tian Gong forged the lightning of every tribulation there. He will not lend you his anvil."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng takes the Registrar's Broken Brush. In the sect far below, the Elder's white hair stops whitening."],
	],
	"s_fg_intro": [
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "A broken Registrar's brush. So it has finally come to this. I am Ou Lan. I tended Tian Gong's bellows for six thousand years."],
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "Every tribulation bolt that ever struck a cultivator was hammered here. I am sorry for each one. Some of them were meant for you."],
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "Bring me four Star-Iron Ingots from the slag fields, and I will reforge the brush into something that can strike a name out, instead of in."],
		["Yue Ling", "And Tian Gong?"],
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "He will want to test the brush on you. Let him try."],
	],
	"s_fg_reforge": [
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "Star-iron, all four ingots, and still humming. And the Registrar's brush. Give it here, gently."],
		["Narrator", "Ou Lan lays the two halves of the brush in a bed of glowing star-iron and begins to hammer. Each blow rings like a temple bell instead of thunder."],
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "I can shape it, but the last quench needs the heat of Tian Gong's own anvil, and he guards it with everything this forge ever built."],
		["Yue Ling", "His engine, his golem and his phoenix. We saw them on the way in."],
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "Break the engine, crack the crucible, calm the bellows. Then he will have nothing left to hide behind but himself."],
	],
	"pre:brass_engine": [
		["Brass Spider Engine", "CLICK. CLICK. DEBTOR DETECTED. ASSEMBLING RESTRAINTS."],
		["Lin Feng", "Water and patience. Gears hate both."],
	],
	"post:brass_engine": [
		["Narrator", "The engine's legs fold with a long sigh of steam. In its heart is a star-iron ingot, still warm."],
	],
	"pre:crucible_golem": [
		["Crucible Golem", "I MELT WHAT REFUSES. I POUR WHAT REMAINS."],
		["Yue Ling", "Its core is a crucible full of tribulation lightning. Cool it down!"],
	],
	"post:crucible_golem": [
		["Crucible Golem", "...cooling... at last... cool..."],
	],
	"pre:bellows_phoenix": [
		["Phoenix of the Bellows", "I breathe for the forge. Every breath I ever took became lightning for someone's tribulation."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then take one breath for yourself."],
	],
	"post:bellows_phoenix": [
		["Phoenix of the Bellows", "...my own breath... it is so quiet... Tian Gong waits at the great anvil. He has been waiting a long time for someone to stop."],
	],
	"pre:forge_tiangong": [
		["Forge-Lord Tian Gong", "So the sweeper boy comes to my anvil with a broken brush and borrowed iron."],
		["Forge-Lord Tian Gong", "Every tribulation was mine. Every bolt measured to the hair, so that only the ripest roots survived to be harvested. I was proud of my craft."],
		["Lin Feng", "Your craft was very good. It was used for something very bad."],
		["Forge-Lord Tian Gong", "Then let the anvil judge which of us is sharper!"],
	],
	"post:forge_tiangong": [
		["Forge-Lord Tian Gong", "Strike true, and the iron forgives. Ou Lan, take my anvil and finish his brush. Finish it the way I should have finished everything: gently."],
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "The last quench, on Tian Gong's own anvil. It is done: a brush of star-iron that writes nothing and unwrites everything. The Ledger is in the Garden of Immortal Peaches, beyond the forge's last gate."],
		["Yue Ling", "The nineteen are there, Lin Feng. I can feel the Seal pulling toward them."],
	],
	"s_gd_intro": [
		["Peach Keeper Tao Yao", "Visitors in my garden, and one of them still mortal-shaped at heart. How delightful."],
		["Peach Keeper Tao Yao", "The Court brought the Ledger here because the peaches keep souls fresh. Nineteen souls, kept fresh for a very long time. They sleep under the oldest tree."],
		["Peach Keeper Tao Yao", "Pick four Immortal Peaches. Give one to each soul that wakes, and they will remember who they were, instead of what they owe."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the Ledger itself?"],
		["Peach Keeper Tao Yao", "It is at the heart of the garden, reading itself aloud. It has been very lonely."],
	],
	"s_gd_offer": [
		["Peach Keeper Tao Yao", "Four Immortal Peaches, picked whole, and not one bitten. You have more patience than any Dao Ancestor I ever fed."],
		["Narrator", "Tao Yao breathes on each peach in turn. Their skins blush gold, and each one takes on the faint scent of a different home: river mud, bamboo smoke, temple incense, snow."],
		["Peach Keeper Tao Yao", "Now they remember what home tasted like. When the nineteen wake, they will know these are for them."],
		["Yue Ling", "The old tree is holding them so tightly. Will it let them go?"],
		["Peach Keeper Tao Yao", "Not without a fight, and not without the crane's blessing. And the Ledger will not let go at all. That part is yours."],
	],
	"pre:peach_tree": [
		["Ten-Thousand-Year Peach Tree", "Ssssh. They are sleeping. Every root of mine is wrapped around a name."],
		["Yue Ling", "We are not here to wake them roughly. Only to let them go."],
	],
	"post:peach_tree": [
		["Ten-Thousand-Year Peach Tree", "...then go gently... the crane remembers their faces better than I do..."],
	],
	"pre:crane_matriarch": [
		["Jade Crane Matriarch", "I carried the nineteen here on my back, one by one, as they fell. I will not let anyone take them again."],
		["Lin Feng", "I am not taking them. I am bringing them home."],
	],
	"post:crane_matriarch": [
		["Jade Crane Matriarch", "Your words are true. I can hear it in your wings. Go to them, twentieth. They are restless tonight."],
	],
	"pre:the_nineteen": [
		["The Nineteen", "...twentieth... you are late... the Ledger says we owe... we owe... we owe..."],
		["Narrator", "Nineteen pale figures rise from the roots, each holding a page with their own name on it. Among them is Shui Ying, the drowned bearer, still wet from the Mirror Lake."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, they are bound to the pages. Free them from the words, not from themselves!"],
	],
	"post:the_nineteen": [
		["Narrator", "One by one the pages tear. Yue Ling sets an Immortal Peach in each pair of empty hands, and one by one the nineteen remember their own names."],
		["Shui Ying, the Drowned Bearer", "We do not owe anything, do we. We never did."],
		["Lin Feng", "No. You never did. Rest. I will close the book."],
	],
	"pre:jade_ledger": [
		["The Jade Ledger", "Debit: nineteen. Credit: one Hollow, now full. Balance: unresolved. Unresolved. Unresolved."],
		["The Jade Ledger", "Debtor twenty, Lin Feng. Present your years."],
		["Lin Feng", "I brought a brush instead."],
		["Yue Ling", "And a seal to close it with. Together, Lin Feng!"],
	],
	"post:jade_ledger": [
		["Narrator", "The star-iron brush draws a single stroke across every page. The names do not vanish. They lift off the jade like birds and settle in the peach branches, free."],
		["The Jade Ledger", "...balance... zero...? is that... allowed...?"],
		["Yue Ling", "It is allowed. Sleep now."],
		["Narrator", "The Ledger closes, and the Seal blooms over it like a lotus over still water. In the sect far below, the disciples' hair darkens again. One tribulation remains: the Heavens' very last, and very polite, request."],
	],
	"pre:s_break9": [
		["Narrator", "The sky above the garden gathers, not into a storm but into a question. Every Dao Ancestor before Lin Feng took something to ascend. The Heavens ask what he will take."],
		["Lin Feng", "Nothing. I will take nothing, and I will owe nothing. Test me if you must."],
	],
	"post:s_break9": [
		["Narrator", "The last tribulation breaks into falling peach blossoms. Lin Feng becomes a Dao Ancestor, the first in ten thousand years whose ascension cost no one anything."],
		["Yue Ling", "The Ledger is closed, the Hollow is full, and the nineteen are resting in the peach trees. What now, Dao Ancestor?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Now? Now I think I will go home and sweep the steps. Somebody should."],
		["Narrator", "Far below, Elder Qing Xuan watches the clouds turn gold. The Lotus Seal's chime, cracked since the day it was forged, rings clear and whole, and the first true morning in ten thousand years begins."],
	],
	# ---- Part VI: The Turning Wheel
	"recall4": [
		["Narrator", "For a whole season the mended sky holds. Then comes the night of the Ghost Festival, when the disciples set paper lanterns on the lotus pond to light the dead home."],
		["Narrator", "This year the lanterns do not drift away. They gather at the south-west gate, bobbing on the dark, as if waiting for someone to tell them where to go."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng... there are people standing among the lanterns. Old disciples, in robes I have only seen in the memorial hall."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "My own master is among them. He died forty years ago. He should have been born again long since."],
		["Narrator", "The dead are not haunting the mountain. They are standing in a queue that no longer moves."],
	],
	"s_p6_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Heavenly Court did not only prop up the sky with stolen years. It also turned the Wheel of Rebirth beneath the world. Every soul that went down to the Yellow Springs was weighed, taxed, and sent back up only when the Ledger allowed it."],
		["Lin Feng", "And we closed the Ledger. So the Wheel has stopped."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Stopped, or been stopped. The dead are piling up at the Naihe Bridge, and the overflow is walking back up the old funeral road to the places where they were buried."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "That road begins at our own south-west gate. It runs through the Maple Gorge, where this sect has buried its dead for three hundred years, then down the river through the Stone Forest, to the fields of red lilies and the Wheel itself."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And there is something I should have told you when you were small. I did not find you on our steps, Lin Feng. I found you in that gorge, wrapped in a sweeper's cloak, under a maple beside two fresh graves with no names on them."],
		["Lin Feng", "...My parents."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I believe so. Xu Mo's hunters were already searching for the root's twentieth bearer. Whoever those two were, they hid you, and then they led the hunters away. I buried them where I found them."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I left you with the village midwife below the mountain, where no hunter would look for a bearer. When a boy of thirteen climbed our steps three winters ago and asked for a broom, I knew you at once. So, it seems, did the Seal: that was the winter Yue Ling woke beside the pond."],
		["Yue Ling", "If the Wheel has stopped, then they never went on. They might still be waiting somewhere on that road."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I am going to walk it. If they are standing in that line, I want to see their faces once before they go."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I have unbarred the south-west gate. Take the funeral road, and find the Lantern Monk in the gorge. He has guided the dead down that road since before I was born."],
	],
	"s_mp_intro": [
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "Two living cultivators on the funeral road, walking the wrong way. Amitabha. You must be the boy the Elder found under the maples."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "Every soul that leaves this gorge carries a lantern, so it can see the road down. Since the Wheel stopped, the lanterns have been coming back up without them, lost and frightened, and the creatures of the gorge snap at them."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "Gather four Soul Lanterns that have drifted from the graves. Bring them to me, and I will show you how to read where they were going."],
	],
	"s_mp_return": [
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "Four lanterns, and none of them blown out. Hold them up, both of you. Look at the flames."],
		["Narrator", "Every flame leans the same way: downhill, towards the river, like grass in a current."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "They all want to go down, and something keeps sending them back. The Fox, the Koi King and the Widow have grown fat on lost lanterns. And Ox-Head, the runner of the Ten Courts, stands at the bottom of the gorge with his iron bell."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "One more thing. The two nameless graves the Elder told you of are under the oldest maple, past the falls. I tend them. Their lanterns never came back up. I do not know whether that is good news."],
	],
	"pre:nine_tail_fox": [
		["Narrator", "Nine tails of autumn fire fan out between the maple trunks. A fox the size of a pavilion smiles with far too many teeth."],
		["Nine-Tailed Fox of the Gorge", "Lanterns are so sweet, little Sovereign. Each one tastes of a whole life. Do you know how hungry the gorge has been since the Court stopped feeding it?"],
		["Yue Ling", "She is eating the way home. Do not let her charm you, Lin Feng!"],
	],
	"post:nine_tail_fox": [["Narrator", "The Fox's nine tails gutter out one by one, and nine stolen lantern flames float free and drift downhill, towards the river."]],
	"pre:waterfall_koi": [
		["Narrator", "Above the hanging falls a golden carp as long as a dragon boat hangs in the spray, swallowing every lantern that tries to tumble down."],
		["Lin Feng", "Every koi that climbs the falls becomes a dragon. You are trying to climb it on other people's lights."],
	],
	"post:waterfall_koi": [["Narrator", "The Koi King slides back into the pool with a sulky flick of his tail, and the lanterns pour down the falls like a river of stars."]],
	"pre:willow_widow": [
		["Narrator", "Under a weeping willow a woman in grey mourning silk sits combing hair that has grown into the branches. Lanterns hang tangled in it like fruit."],
		["The Willow Widow", "He said he would come back up the road. I am keeping the lights for him. If I let them go, he will not find his way."],
		["Yue Ling", "If you keep them, nobody will find their way. Not even him."],
	],
	"post:willow_widow": [
		["The Willow Widow", "...Then I will go down and look for him myself. Will you untangle me?"],
		["Narrator", "The lanterns slip out of the willow one by one, and the Widow walks after them down the road, lighter with every step."],
	],
	"pre:ox_head": [
		["Narrator", "At the foot of the gorge a giant with the head of an ox blocks the road, an iron bell in one fist and a chain in the other. Every time the bell rings, the dead below turn round and start back up."],
		["Ox-Head, Runner of the Ten Courts", "Back! Back up the road! The Wheel is closed by order of the King. No soul goes down until the King says so!"],
		["Lin Feng", "Then you are the reason the lanterns keep coming back. Put the bell down."],
	],
	"post:ox_head": [
		["Ox-Head, Runner of the Ten Courts", "Hoo... you hit like a falling pillar. Fine. Take the bell. I never liked ringing it. But the King will not open the Wheel because I lost a bell."],
		["Ox-Head, Runner of the Ten Courts", "My brother Horse-Face guards the river mouth. He is stubborner than I am. And he can count."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng takes Ox-Head's iron bell. Below the gorge the river bends nine times through a forest of stone, and the lanterns float down it in a long, slow line."],
	],
	"s_kr_intro": [
		["Cormorant Fisher Old Yu", "Mind the cormorants, they peck at qi. Ha. Look at that, they like you."],
		["Cormorant Fisher Old Yu", "The Nine-Bend River runs from the gorge down to the Yellow Springs. It used to carry the dead and nothing else. Now it is thick with memories. The dead are so tired of waiting that they have started dropping them."],
		["Cormorant Fisher Old Yu", "My birds bring them up as pearls. Dive me four River Pearls of Memory, and we will see whose they are."],
	],
	"s_kr_return": [
		["Cormorant Fisher Old Yu", "Four pearls. Hold one to your ear, boy. Go on."],
		["Narrator", "In the pearl a woman is singing a lullaby, and a man is laughing at how badly she sings it. A baby is crying. Somewhere, far off, a bell rings, and hunters are shouting."],
		["Lin Feng", "...I know that song. I have always known it. I thought I made it up."],
		["Cormorant Fisher Old Yu", "Then it is yours, and they dropped it here on purpose, for you to find. The river remembers who it is carrying. Follow it past the Tyrant, the Jiao and the Sentinel. Horse-Face waits where the river meets the springs."],
	],
	"pre:cormorant_king": [
		["Narrator", "A black cormorant with a crown of river-reeds rises from a stone pillar, a bright ring of fishing cord tight around its throat. It has swallowed so many memories it cannot fly straight."],
		["Yue Ling", "Someone collared it and never let it go. Loosen the ring, Lin Feng!"],
	],
	"post:cormorant_king": [["Narrator", "The cord snaps. The Tyrant coughs up a stream of glittering memories into the river and flies off, light and crooked and free."]],
	"pre:karst_jiao": [
		["Narrator", "The river coils into a whirlpool between two towers of stone, and a horned water-dragon rises in the middle of it, eyes white with confusion."],
		["Jiao of the Nine Bends", "So many voices in the water. Whose are they? Are any of them mine?"],
		["Lin Feng", "None of them are yours. Let them pass, and you will hear your own again."],
	],
	"post:karst_jiao": [["Narrator", "The Jiao sinks into the deep pool and goes quiet, listening, and the whirlpool smooths into a long, patient current."]],
	"pre:stone_sentinel": [
		["Narrator", "One of the limestone towers stands up. It has been waiting for ten thousand years for anyone to ask it a question."],
		["Sentinel of the Stone Forest", "Who goes down the river while living?"],
		["Lin Feng", "A son, looking for his parents."],
		["Sentinel of the Stone Forest", "Then prove you can carry them. The dead are heavier than stone."],
	],
	"post:stone_sentinel": [["Narrator", "The Sentinel sits back down with a sound like an avalanche settling, and the river runs a little faster past it, as if a stone had been lifted off its chest."]],
	"pre:horse_face": [
		["Narrator", "Where the river spills into the golden water of the Yellow Springs, a giant with a horse's head stands knee-deep in the current, tallying every passing lantern on an abacus of human teeth."],
		["Horse-Face, Runner of the Ten Courts", "Ten thousand and one, ten thousand and two... I know you. The living one with my brother's bell. The King has not opened the books for rebirth. Every soul is counted and kept."],
		["Lin Feng", "Kept for what?"],
		["Horse-Face, Runner of the Ten Courts", "Ask the King. I only count. Now go back, or be counted."],
	],
	"post:horse_face": [
		["Horse-Face, Runner of the Ten Courts", "Ten thousand and... no. I have lost count. For the first time in ten thousand years, I have lost count. How strange. How restful."],
		["Narrator", "Horse-Face sits down in the river and lets the lanterns float past him uncounted, out across the golden water, towards a field of red flowers and a bridge."],
	],
	"s_sp_intro": [
		["Meng Po of the Naihe Bridge", "Soup, travellers? One bowl, and you forget everything: every grudge, every debt, every face. No? Ah, you are still alive. Sit anyway. The queue is very long."],
		["Meng Po of the Naihe Bridge", "Every soul crosses my bridge, drinks my soup and goes on to the Wheel empty and clean. That was the Court's rule. Empty souls are easier to tax."],
		["Meng Po of the Naihe Bridge", "But your Ledger gave everyone their names back, and now nobody will drink. They would rather wait forever than forget again. I do not blame them."],
		["Meng Po of the Naihe Bridge", "Pick me four Herbs of Forgetting from among the lilies. I want to try a new recipe. A gentler one."],
	],
	"s_sp_return": [
		["Meng Po of the Naihe Bridge", "Good, good. Into the pot. And now the secret: a little of your qi, both of you. Living qi. The Court never let me use any."],
		["Narrator", "The soup turns from grey to gold. It smells of rice and rain and something half-remembered."],
		["Meng Po of the Naihe Bridge", "This bowl lets a soul forget the pain and keep one thing. One face. One song. Whatever it loves most. The Court would have hated it."],
		["Meng Po of the Naihe Bridge", "Two of my guests have refused every bowl for sixteen years. A man and a woman. They said they were waiting for their son to grow up. They are by the bridge. But the Patriarch, the Serpent and the Empress will not let anyone near it."],
	],
	"pre:corpse_patriarch": [
		["Narrator", "A stiff old man in Qing-dynasty robes hops out of the lilies, a yellow talisman fluttering on his forehead, and a hundred hopping corpses hop after him in perfect step."],
		["Corpse Patriarch Jiang", "Discipline! Order! The dead must stand in line! If they will not drink, they will march!"],
		["Yue Ling", "Pull the talisman off, Lin Feng. He has been sealed into that body so long he thinks the seal is him."],
	],
	"post:corpse_patriarch": [["Narrator", "The talisman tears. The old Patriarch sighs, folds up like a dropped robe, and his soul walks off towards Meng Po's pot, rubbing a stiff neck."]],
	"pre:bridge_serpent": [
		["Narrator", "The Naihe Bridge shudders. A red serpent has wound itself around the pillars, and every time a soul steps onto the bridge it shakes them off into the golden water."],
		["Lin Feng", "You are guarding a bridge nobody is allowed to cross. What is the point of that?"],
	],
	"post:bridge_serpent": [["Narrator", "The Serpent uncoils and slides away beneath the lilies, and the bridge stands still and open for the first time since the Wheel stopped."]],
	"pre:lily_empress": [
		["Narrator", "The whole field of spider lilies turns to face Lin Feng at once. Their roots run down to every grave in the world, and they are all of them one flower: the Empress of the Red Lilies."],
		["Empress of the Red Lilies", "Flowers and leaves never meet on my stems. That is the rule of the lily. The dead and the living never meet either. Why are you here, living one?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Because the rule was written by the Court, and the Court is gone."],
	],
	"post:lily_empress": [["Empress of the Red Lilies", "...Then go and meet them. Just this once, the flower and the leaf. I will look away."]],
	"pre:the_unforgotten": [
		["Narrator", "Beside the bridge the dead have gathered into one great shape: a thousand faces in a cloak of memories, every one of them refusing to forget."],
		["The Thousand Unforgotten", "WE WILL NOT DRINK. WE WILL NOT FORGET. THEY TOOK OUR YEARS, THEY TOOK OUR NAMES. WE WILL NOT LET THEM TAKE OUR FACES."],
		["Yue Ling", "Nobody is taking anything any more. Lin Feng, we have to let them be heard before they can be calm."],
	],
	"post:the_unforgotten": [
		["Narrator", "The great shape breaks apart into a thousand tired people, and one by one they come to Meng Po's pot and drink the golden soup, and each of them keeps one thing."],
		["Narrator", "The last two in the line are a man in a sweeper's patched robe and a woman with a jade pin in her hair. They look at Lin Feng for a long time."],
		["Su Wan", "...You have his nose. And my terrible singing voice, I expect."],
		["Lin Shan", "We were told to drink and go on. We said we would wait until we knew you had grown up safe. It has been a very long queue."],
		["Lin Feng", "I grew up. I swept the steps. I held up the sky. I... I do not know what to say to you."],
		["Su Wan", "Then do not say anything yet. Walk with us to the Wheel. We will talk on the way."],
		["Narrator", "Something new settles into Lin Feng's sword-hand, as patient as a parent's grip: the Samsara Sword, which cuts nothing that wants to stay and frees everything that wants to go."],
	],
	"s_wh_intro": [
		["Narrator", "Below the Yellow Springs the Wheel of the Six Paths stands still in a hall without walls: a stone wheel as tall as a mountain, its six spokes cracked, its rim choked with ten thousand waiting souls."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "A living Sovereign, at the Wheel. I wrote in my book that this would never happen. I have been crossing out a great many things in my book lately."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Four spokes of the Wheel were broken when the Court fell, and the pieces were scattered through the Ten Courts. Without them the Wheel cannot turn, even if the King allowed it."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Find me the four broken Spokes. I will mend them. Whether the King lets them turn is another question."],
	],
	"s_wh_return": [
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Four spokes. Hold them in place while I write. The Wheel answers to what is written about it."],
		["Narrator", "Judge Cui writes with a brush of white ink, and the cracks in the spokes close like healing skin. The Wheel creaks, and for one moment, every waiting soul looks up."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "The Mirror, the Burning Judge and the Guardian will try to stop you. They are the King's last instruments. Behind them, the King is waiting. He is not cruel. That is what makes him so hard to argue with."],
	],
	"pre:karma_mirror": [
		["Narrator", "A bronze mirror the size of a gate shows Lin Feng every life he has touched: the sect, the forest, the sky, the Ledger, the Wheel. In the mirror, every one of them is a debt."],
		["Mirror of Karma", "Look at the weight of it. Every good deed binds someone to you. Every rescue is a chain."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let me show you how to look at it the other way round."],
	],
	"post:karma_mirror": [["Narrator", "The Mirror cracks straight down the middle, and in the crack the debts turn into what they always were: people who are glad to know each other."]],
	"pre:burning_judge": [
		["Narrator", "In the Burning Court a judge with a face of red lacquer sits on a throne of coals, sentencing the dead to burn away whatever the Court once said they owed."],
		["Judge of the Burning Court", "A debt unpaid is a fire unlit! I burn what is owed, so that nothing is owed!"],
		["Yue Ling", "Nobody owes anything any more. Let the fire go out."],
	],
	"post:burning_judge": [["Narrator", "The coals go grey. The Judge takes off his lacquer face, and underneath it he is a very old, very tired clerk who has not slept since the first dynasty."]],
	"pre:wheel_guardian": [
		["Narrator", "A great tortoise with the Wheel's six paths carved into its shell plants itself in front of the axle: gods, men, titans, beasts, hungry ghosts and hells."],
		["Guardian of the Six Paths", "Which path will you send them down, living one? Who decides? The Court decided. The King decided. Will you decide now?"],
		["Lin Feng", "No. I am only going to make sure the door opens. They decide where they go."],
	],
	"post:wheel_guardian": [["Narrator", "The Guardian lowers its head and steps aside, and the six paths carved on its shell begin, very faintly, to glow."]],
	"pre:king_yama": [
		["Narrator", "At the axle of the Wheel a king in black and gold sits with his hand on the brake, the King of the dead, Yama, who has judged every soul since the first one died."],
		["King Yama, Lord of the Wheel", "I stopped it. Yes. Do you know what the Wheel sent them back into, for ten thousand years? A world that harvested them. Every soul I sent up was born to be taxed, tribulated and eaten. I would not do it one more time."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Court is gone. The Ledger is closed. The sky is held up by people who chose to hold it. The world they would be born into is a kind one now."],
		["King Yama, Lord of the Wheel", "So you say. Every cultivator who ever came down here said the world above was kind. Prove it, Sovereign. Prove to me that your world is worth being born into."],
		["Su Wan", "He is our son. He swept the steps for three years without a single grain of qi, and he never once stopped being kind. That is the world he made. Let us go back to it."],
	],
	"post:king_yama": [
		["King Yama, Lord of the Wheel", "...The mother speaks for the world better than the son does. Very well. I take my hand off the brake."],
		["Narrator", "The Wheel of the Six Paths groans, shudders, and begins to turn. Ten thousand lanterns lift from its rim and flow upward like sparks from a fire, each soul keeping one thing it loves."],
		["Lin Shan", "We will be born again soon, somewhere. We will not remember you. But Meng Po's soup lets us keep one thing."],
		["Su Wan", "I am keeping your face. Look for a child who stares at you as if she has known you all her life. That will be me."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng's parents step onto the turning Wheel together, holding hands, and are gone in a rush of golden light."],
	],
	"pre:s_break11": [
		["Narrator", "The Wheel is turning, but it turns stiffly, like a mill that has rusted. It needs one more push, and the push must come from someone who could step off the Wheel forever and chooses to stay beside it."],
		["King Yama, Lord of the Wheel", "Turn it, Sovereign. Not as its master. The Court tried that. Turn it as one more soul who is glad it moves."],
		["Lin Feng", "Yue Ling. Will you push with me?"],
		["Yue Ling", "Always. The Founder wove me so that the bearer would never have to push alone."],
	],
	"post:s_break11": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng and Yue Ling set their hands to the rim of the Wheel and push. The last tribulation is not lightning: it is every life Lin Feng has touched, turning with him, all at once."],
		["Narrator", "He transcends samsara, not by escaping the Wheel, but by turning it gladly. The Wheel spins freely now, and the queue at the Naihe Bridge is gone."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "I have written it down. 'The Wheel turns because someone wanted it to.' First true sentence in this book in ten thousand years."],
		["Narrator", "Back on the mountain, on the first morning of spring, a woman from the village below brings her newborn daughter to the sect to be blessed. The baby looks up at Lin Feng with an old, surprised, delighted stare."],
		["Lin Feng", "...Hello again."],
	],
	# ---- Part VII: The Unbroken Land
	"recall5": [
		["Narrator", "Spring comes to Azure Cloud Mountain. The baby with the old, surprised stare is named Little Wan, and the whole sect quarrels over whose turn it is to hold her."],
		["Narrator", "Then, one night, the threads appear: fine silver threads strung across the mountain roads at ankle height, humming like plucked strings. By morning the south road to the village has been sewn shut."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng... the village below the mountain. Granny Wen is down there. And Little Wan's mother took her home yesterday."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Come to the hall, both of you. I know whose needlework this is."],
	],
	"s_p7_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Under the Court the world was never one land. It was a quilt: every realm cut apart and stitched to the next by a gate, so that each could be harvested alone. The gates you walked through as a boy were her seams."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Her name is Qianxian, the Lady of a Thousand Threads, the Court's Weaver. When you turned the Wheel her stitches came undone and the land grew back together. Walk east from our gate now and the bamboo simply runs on into the marsh, and the marsh into the snows."],
		["Lin Feng", "I wondered why a forest ever needed a gate."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "She wants her quilt back. She is starting with the mortal lands, where nobody can fight her: the village, the tea terraces, the mulberry woods. And she is spinning new thread from the newly reborn, because a soul just come round the Wheel is the strongest thread there is."],
		["Yue Ling", "Little Wan."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I have cut the threads across our south road. Go down to Qingshi Village and find Granny Wen. She raised you after I left you with her. If any child is missing, she will know which."],
	],
	"s_vl_intro": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Shut the door, the threads come in on the draught. Let me look at you. Taller. Still all elbows. A Sovereign, they say, and you still wipe your feet on my step."],
		["Lin Feng", "Granny. The Elder says children are being taken."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Three of them, all born this spring, all born looking at their mothers like they knew them already. The threads came down the chimneys at night and wrapped them up like cocoons, and in the morning they would not wake. Their mothers sit by the cradles, and nobody is home behind the eyes. One of them was Little Wan."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "The threads are still strung all over the village. Cut four of them and bring them to me. A midwife knows how to read a cord: I will tell you where they lead."],
	],
	"s_vl_return": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Four threads, and all four twisted the same way, sunwise. Uphill. They run up to the tea terraces and on into the mulberry woods."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "And look at the stitching on this one: a needle as thick as a spear went through here. That will be the General who has been standing at the east end of the village since the threads came, letting nobody out."],
		["Yue Ling", "And the paper tiger in the lanes, and the scarecrow that walks at night, and the thing in the well..."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Paper and straw and a hungry spider, all stitched together and told to keep us in. Go on, then. I did not raise you to leave a mess in my village."],
	],
	"pre:paper_tiger": [
		["Narrator", "A tiger of oiled paper and bamboo prowls the village lanes, painted with a hundred angry eyes. Every eye is a stitched-on thread that pulls it along."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is only paper, Lin Feng. Somebody made it to frighten people."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then it is doing its job very well. Let us unmake it gently."],
	],
	"post:paper_tiger": [["Narrator", "The Paper Tiger folds in on itself like a letter. Inside, written in a child's careful hand, is a single word: 'home'."]],
	"pre:scarecrow_king": [
		["Narrator", "In the flooded paddies a scarecrow in a crown of rice straw rises out of the water, its arms stitched wide, its hat full of silver thread."],
		["Scarecrow King of the Paddies", "I keep the crows off. I keep the people in. I keep everything exactly where it was put. That is what I was made for."],
		["Lin Feng", "You were made to guard the rice, old friend. Not to fence the farmers."],
	],
	"post:scarecrow_king": [["Narrator", "The Scarecrow King's stitches pull loose, and it slumps back into a plain scarecrow, arms spread wide over the paddies, keeping off nothing but birds."]],
	"pre:well_spider": [
		["Narrator", "The village well hums. Something with eight long legs has strung a web from rim to rim, and the bucket hangs wrapped in silk halfway down."],
		["Well Spider", "Water and thread. Thread and water. Everyone comes to the well. Everyone stays."],
		["Yue Ling", "Not this morning, they don't."],
	],
	"post:well_spider": [["Narrator", "The web parts. The bucket drops with a splash, and somewhere in the village a woman laughs for the first time in a week, because she can draw water again."]],
	"pre:needle_general": [
		["Narrator", "At the east end of the village a general in armour of a thousand sewing needles plants a spear the size of a weaver's beam across the road."],
		["Needle General Zhen", "By order of the Lady, this village is finished. Finished cloth does not move. Turn back, Sovereign, or be hemmed in with them."],
		["Lin Feng", "A finished cloth is just a cloth nobody is using any more. These people are not finished."],
	],
	"post:needle_general": [
		["Needle General Zhen", "...The Lady said every stitch I held was a war kept out of a field. I held this road for a thousand years. Was I wrong all that time?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Maybe not, once. But the war is over. Let them walk."],
		["Narrator", "The General lays down his great needle and walks away up the terraces, a free man with nothing to hold. Lin Feng keeps the needle, and gives it to Weaver Liu, who uses it to unpick the last threads from the village roofs."],
	],
	"s_te_intro": [
		["Tea Master Lu Yu", "Sit, sit. Nobody climbs nine terraces without a cup at the top. This is the last of last year's crop, and it will be the last of any crop if those threads stay on my hills."],
		["Tea Master Lu Yu", "She has sewn the terraces up like a quilt. The bushes cannot breathe, the pickers cannot climb, and anyone who drinks tea grown under her threads falls asleep and dreams of being stitched into a border."],
		["Tea Master Lu Yu", "But the Dragon-Well buds on the high terraces grow above the threads. Bring me four, and I will brew something that wakes thread-sleepers."],
	],
	"s_te_return": [
		["Tea Master Lu Yu", "Four buds, and not one of them bruised. You pick like a mortal, Sovereign. I mean that kindly."],
		["Narrator", "The tea is bright green and smells of rain on hot stone. When Yue Ling drinks it, the faint silver lines on her wrists, which neither of them had noticed, fade away."],
		["Yue Ling", "...I was being sewn too. Slowly. The Founder wove me from the Seal, and the Lady can pull on anything woven."],
		["Tea Master Lu Yu", "Then drink a cup every morning until this is over. Now: a kite, a dragon, a golem and the Matron who sews for the Lady. They hold the terraces. Go and take them back."],
	],
	"pre:wind_kite": [
		["Narrator", "Above the third terrace a kite the size of a sail rides the wind, painted like a centipede, its string a silver thread running all the way up into the clouds."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is pulling the threads tight across the whole hillside. Cut the string and they will all go slack!"],
	],
	"post:wind_kite": [["Narrator", "The string parts with a sound like a zither breaking. The Great Wind Kite sails off over the mountains, free, and the threads across the terraces sag to the ground."]],
	"pre:terrace_dragon": [
		["Narrator", "In the spring at the top of the terraces a green dragon lies coiled asleep, wound round and round with silver thread like a bundle of tea."],
		["Lin Feng", "It is not attacking. It has been sewn asleep."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then it will not be happy when it wakes. Be ready."],
	],
	"post:terrace_dragon": [["Narrator", "The Dragon of the Nine Terraces shakes off the last threads, drinks deeply from its spring, and sends a cool rain sweeping down over every terrace on the hill."]],
	"pre:hill_golem": [
		["Narrator", "A golem of terraced earth stands up out of the hillside, tea bushes growing on its shoulders, silver stitches holding its seams together."],
		["Tea-Hill Golem", "THE HILL. MUST. NOT. MOVE."],
	],
	"post:hill_golem": [["Narrator", "The Golem's seams give way, and it lies back down into the hillside as nine neat terraces, a little steeper than before."]],
	"pre:thread_matron": [
		["Narrator", "At the crest of the terraces a spider-woman as big as a house sits on a web strung between the hills, sewing with all eight hands at once."],
		["Thread Matron of the Hills", "The Lady's apprentice, at your service. Every border between here and the sea, I have hemmed. Would you like to see the pattern? It is beautiful. Nobody ever crosses it."],
		["Lin Feng", "That is exactly what is wrong with it."],
	],
	"post:thread_matron": [
		["Thread Matron of the Hills", "Go on into the mulberries, then. The Lady is waiting at the Loom, and she has the children. Three new souls make a very strong warp."],
		["Narrator", "The Matron's web falls apart across the hills, and for the first time since spring, the road up the far side of the terraces is open."],
	],
	"s_sk_intro": [
		["Silk Farmer Can Niang", "Mind your feet, the silkworms have been coming out of the woods all week. Not my silkworms. Hers. They eat mulberry leaves and spin souls."],
		["Silk Farmer Can Niang", "Every newborn the threads took was carried through these woods and wrapped in a cocoon to cure, like silk. Most of the cocoons went on to the Loom. Some were dropped among the mulberries when the swarms fought over them."],
		["Silk Farmer Can Niang", "Find four of the dropped ones. If they are still warm, I can unwind them without hurting what is inside."],
	],
	"s_sk_return": [
		["Silk Farmer Can Niang", "Warm. All four. Give me room."],
		["Narrator", "Can Niang unwinds the cocoons in a bowl of hot water, as gently as any silk. Inside are not babies, but tiny lanterns, each with a newborn soul asleep in its flame. They are the souls of children all over the land who went to sleep this spring and did not wake."],
		["Silk Farmer Can Niang", "The bodies are still in their cradles at home. The souls were taken for thread. These four can go home now. Little Wan's is not among them. Hers went on to the Loom."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then that is where we are going."],
		["Narrator", "Something settles into Lin Feng's sword-hand that is as wide as the land itself: the Boundless Heart Sword, which cuts no border and leaves every door open."],
	],
	"pre:silkworm_queen": [
		["Narrator", "Under the oldest mulberry a silkworm as long as a river barge lifts its pale head, its jaws still green with leaves and its belly glowing with swallowed souls."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is digesting them, Lin Feng. We have to be quick."],
	],
	"post:silkworm_queen": [["Narrator", "The Silkworm Queen curls up and is still, and from her belly a stream of little soul-lanterns floats up and away towards every village in the land."]],
	"pre:moth_empress": [
		["Narrator", "Wings like painted screens open over the canopy. The Moth Empress hangs from the moon, dusting the woods with a powder that makes every path look like a wall."],
		["Moth Empress of the Mulberries", "Why would anyone want to go anywhere? Stay. Stay in the dark, where it is soft."],
	],
	"post:moth_empress": [["Narrator", "The Moth Empress flutters down into the leaves and folds her wings. The powder settles, and the paths through the woods are only paths again."]],
	"pre:mulberry_ent": [
		["Narrator", "The Old Mulberry has grown its roots right through a hundred cocoons and will not let them go. Its bark creaks like an old woman's voice."],
		["The Old Mulberry", "I fed them. I fed every worm in these woods for a thousand years. What I feed, I keep."],
		["Lin Feng", "You fed them so they could become something else. That was always the point of you."],
	],
	"post:mulberry_ent": [["Narrator", "The Old Mulberry's roots loosen, one by one, and a hundred cocoons roll free into the moss. When Lin Feng looks back, the tree is already budding."]],
	"pre:thousand_hands": [
		["Narrator", "In the Spinning Hall at the edge of the woods a figure with a thousand arms works a thousand spinning wheels at once, every wheel turning a soul into thread."],
		["Thousand Hands of the Spinning Hall", "Spin and spin and spin. The Lady needs a warp strong enough to hold the whole world apart. Only the newborn are strong enough."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then you are going to have to manage without."],
	],
	"post:thousand_hands": [["Narrator", "The spinning wheels stop, all thousand at once, in a silence so sudden it rings. Beyond the Hall, the road climbs into a sky striped like cloth on a loom."]],
	"s_lm_intro": [
		["Narrator", "The Loom of Borders fills the sky: a frame as big as a mountain range, its warp threads running down to every corner of the world, its cloth the map of every border the Court ever sewed."],
		["Zhi, the Last Weaver-Girl", "You came up the warp. Nobody comes up the warp. I am Zhi. I swept this Loom for two hundred years, until the Lady made me an apprentice so that I would stop asking why."],
		["Zhi, the Last Weaver-Girl", "She has the three children in the heddle at the top. If she finishes the new warp with them, every border comes back at once: every gate, every seam, every village shut away from every other."],
		["Zhi, the Last Weaver-Girl", "Take four shuttles out of the Loom. Without its shuttles the Loom cannot weave a single new row, and she will have to come down and talk to you."],
	],
	"s_lm_return": [
		["Zhi, the Last Weaver-Girl", "Four shuttles. She can feel it, you know. The whole Loom just went quiet."],
		["Zhi, the Last Weaver-Girl", "She is not cruel. That is the hard part. She sewed the first borders round fields, so that the wars of the immortals would stay out of them. The Court took her borders and made them into pens."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I will not cut her down. I will ask her to put the needle away."],
		["Zhi, the Last Weaver-Girl", "Her guardians will not let you ask: the Beast in the Warp, the Spindle Golem, the Shuttle Dragon. And then her."],
	],
	"pre:warp_beast": [
		["Narrator", "Where the warp threads cross they have knotted into a beast with no face, only a tangle that grows tighter the harder it struggles."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is the borders themselves, all knotted together. It does not even know what it is."],
	],
	"post:warp_beast": [["Narrator", "Strand by strand the Beast unknots, until there is nothing left but straight threads hanging loose, swinging gently in a wind that blows across the whole world."]],
	"pre:spindle_golem": [
		["Narrator", "A golem built of a thousand spindles rolls out of the Loom's shadow, winding everything it touches round and round its body."],
		["Spindle Golem", "WIND. WIND. WIND IT ALL UP."],
	],
	"post:spindle_golem": [["Narrator", "The Spindle Golem unspools across the floor of the Loom in a hundred bright colours, and everything it had wound up rolls free."]],
	"pre:shuttle_dragon": [
		["Narrator", "A golden dragon shaped like a weaver's shuttle shoots back and forth across the Loom, faster than the eye, trailing a new row of borders behind it."],
		["Lin Feng", "If it never stops, we have to be where it is going."],
	],
	"post:shuttle_dragon": [["Narrator", "Lin Feng catches the Shuttle Dragon mid-flight, and it stops, and blinks, and curls up on the Loom's frame like a cat in a sunny window."]],
	"pre:qianxian": [
		["Narrator", "At the top of the Loom, in a heddle of light, three newborn souls sleep in their lanterns. Above them the Lady of a Thousand Threads lowers herself on a single strand: a woman to the waist, a great silver spider below, every one of her eight hands holding a needle."],
		["Madam Qianxian, Lady of a Thousand Threads", "Do you know what the world looked like before my borders, little sweeper? The immortals fought across it like children kicking through a sand garden. Every war rolled over every field."],
		["Madam Qianxian, Lady of a Thousand Threads", "I sewed fences round the fields. The Court took my fences and called them pens. You cut them all down, and you think the world is free. It is only open. Open to anything."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let people build their own fences. Walls and a door, like Granny Wen's house. A wall keeps the wind out. A door is what makes it a home and not a prison. You only ever sewed walls."],
		["Madam Qianxian, Lady of a Thousand Threads", "Pretty words. Weave something with them, if you can."],
	],
	"post:qianxian": [
		["Narrator", "The Lady's eight needles fall, one after another, and stick quivering in the Loom. She sits for a long time looking at the three sleeping lanterns."],
		["Madam Qianxian, Lady of a Thousand Threads", "Walls and a door. I suppose I never thought to sew a door. Take them home, Sovereign. Their mothers are waiting."],
		["Narrator", "Yue Ling lifts the three lanterns out of the heddle. The one in the middle burns with an old, surprised, delighted flame."],
		["Zhi, the Last Weaver-Girl", "And the Loom? It holds every border in the world."],
		["Madam Qianxian, Lady of a Thousand Threads", "Leave it to me. I will unpick it. Slowly. It took me ten thousand years to sew; it will take a while to make a world with doors in it."],
	],
	"pre:s_break12": [
		["Narrator", "On the floor of the unravelling Loom, with the whole land spread out below like a cloth with no seams, the Heavens ask Lin Feng one last question: what will you keep out?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Nothing. And I will keep a door open for anyone who knocks."],
		["Yue Ling", "That is not a cultivation method, Lin Feng. That is just being kind."],
		["Lin Feng", "It has always worked so far."],
	],
	"post:s_break12": [
		["Narrator", "The last tribulation does not strike. It knocks, and Lin Feng opens the door. He becomes the first cultivator of the Boundless Heart: a realm with no border, no gate, and no wall but the ones people choose for themselves."],
		["Narrator", "In Qingshi Village, three mothers wake to three babies yawning in their cradles. Granny Wen counts them twice and then sits down on her step and cries, very quietly, because she would never let anyone see."],
		["Madam Qianxian, Lady of a Thousand Threads", "First row unpicked. Nine thousand nine hundred and ninety-nine to go. Zhi, fetch the tea."],
		["Narrator", "And on Azure Cloud Mountain, on the first warm evening of summer, Lin Feng sweeps the steps of the sect while Little Wan watches from the Elder's arms, and every road down the mountain runs on, open, to the edge of the world."],
	],
	# ---- Part VIII: The River of Heaven
	"recall6": [
		["Narrator", "Summer turns. Qingshi Village has three new names in its register, and Little Wan has learned to say 'broom'."],
		["Narrator", "On the seventh night of the seventh month the whole sect climbs the roof of the hall to watch the magpies gather in the sky, the way they have every year since before there was a sect. No magpies come."],
		["Narrator", "Instead, an hour before midnight, the Silver River across the sky spills over. Stars come down like rain. They hiss in the lotus pond, and in the morning the pond is full of drowned stars, still faintly shining."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng... the river in the sky. It is lower than it was. It is coming down the south road."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "To the hall. Both of you. I was afraid of this when Qianxian began unpicking her Loom."],
	],
	"s_p8_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Every child in the valley knows the story. The Weaver Star, granddaughter of the Queen Mother of the West, fell in love with a cowherd. The Queen Mother of the West drew her golden hairpin across the sky to part them, and the line she drew became the Silver River."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Once a year, on the seventh night, the magpies of the world fly up and make a bridge of themselves so the lovers can meet. That is the story. What the story does not say is that the river is a border. The oldest border there is."],
		["Lin Feng", "And Qianxian is unpicking every border she ever stitched."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "She did not draw this one, but she bound its banks with her thread for ten thousand years. Now the thread is coming out, and the river does not know where to go. It is pouring down the old Sky Road into the world, and the magpies have been scattered by the flood."],
		["Yue Ling", "And the Cowherd? In the story he waits by the river every night."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "He still does. There is a ferry on the south road where the Sky Road comes down to earth, and an old herdsman who keeps it. I have opened the road. Go down to the Starfall Ferry and find Niu Lang."],
	],
	"s_sf_intro": [
		["Niu Lang the Cowherd", "Mind the stars, they are still hot. I have been fishing them out of the fields all week. The rice is ruined, and the river is still rising."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Elder says you are the Cowherd. From the story."],
		["Niu Lang the Cowherd", "I am a cowherd, and there is a story. I keep the ferry because this is where the Sky Road touches the ground. Every seventh night I would cross on the magpies' backs. This year there were no magpies, only water."],
		["Niu Lang the Cowherd", "The drowned stars are what is left of the Weaver's sky. If you can fish out four of them I can read them. She always said I read stars better than letters. Then we will know how far up the river has broken."],
	],
	"s_sf_return": [
		["Niu Lang the Cowherd", "Four stars, and they all fell from the same place: the bend of the river under the Magpie Heights. That is where the bridge always stood."],
		["Niu Lang the Cowherd", "The flood has brought things down with it. A jiao has silted up the ford, the reed widow walks the bank again, and the old carp at the weir is too frightened to leap. And something with a horn of light keeps the tide rising at the east end of the fields."],
		["Yue Ling", "We will clear the ford. Will you come with us?"],
		["Niu Lang the Cowherd", "I would only slow you down. Clear the road, and I will follow it. I have been following that road my whole life."],
	],
	"pre:silt_jiao": [
		["Narrator", "In the ford a jiao, a dragon that never finished becoming a dragon, lies coiled in starlit silt, damming the river so the water spreads over the fields."],
		["Jiao of the Silted Ford", "A thousand years I have waited to climb the river into the sky. Now the river has come down to me, and I will not let it leave."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then climb it with us. It is going back up."],
	],
	"post:silt_jiao": [["Narrator", "The Jiao uncoils, and the ford runs clear. As the water drains from the fields it carries the young dragon with it, upstream, towards the sky."]],
	"pre:reed_widow": [
		["Narrator", "In the reeds a woman in a wet white robe stands up to her waist in the flood, calling a name over and over."],
		["Widow of the Reed Ford", "He went to the river to fetch water, and the river took him across. I have waited on this side for so long. Why should anyone be allowed to cross?"],
		["Yue Ling", "Because the river is not supposed to keep anyone. Not him, and not you."],
	],
	"post:reed_widow": [["Narrator", "The Widow lets out a long breath and walks into the water. On the far bank, faint as a reflection, a man is waiting with a bucket. She does not look back."]],
	"pre:carp_sage": [
		["Narrator", "Under the weir an enormous golden carp hangs in the water, whiskers grey with age, glaring at the falls above it."],
		["Old Carp Who Would Not Leap", "Every carp who leaps the Dragon Gate becomes a dragon. Every carp who fails is laughed at by the whole river. For four hundred years I have chosen not to be laughed at."],
		["Lin Feng", "And for four hundred years you have stayed a carp. Shall we see what you can do?"],
	],
	"post:carp_sage": [["Narrator", "Stung, the Old Carp leaps, and clears the weir by a full body-length, and keeps going, up the waterfall of falling stars, and is gone. Somewhere far above, something roars with delight."]],
	"pre:tide_qilin": [
		["Narrator", "At the east end of the fields a qilin made of moonlit water paces the bank, and wherever its hooves fall the tide rises."],
		["Qilin of the Rising Tide", "The river was walled for ten thousand years. Now it is free. I am only helping it go where it likes."],
		["Lin Feng", "A river that goes where it likes is a flood. A river needs banks. Banks are not the same as walls."],
	],
	"post:tide_qilin": [
		["Narrator", "The Qilin bows its head, and the tide goes out with it. Where the water draws back, a road of wet starlit stone climbs towards the clouds."],
		["Niu Lang the Cowherd", "The Sky Road. I have not seen it dry in a year. Go on. The magpies nest on the heights at the top. If any of them are left, Que Lao will know."],
	],
	"s_mg_intro": [
		["Que Lao, the Old Magpie", "Kak! A Sovereign on my cliff, and not a single nest left standing to receive him. Forgive the mess."],
		["Lin Feng", "You are the magpies who make the bridge."],
		["Que Lao, the Old Magpie", "Ten thousand of us, wing to wing, every seventh night since there was a sky. This year the river burst while we were gathering, and the storm threw us to the four winds. The apes took the nests, and something wearing a crow's crown has set itself on the broken bridge."],
		["Que Lao, the Old Magpie", "Gather the bridge plumes the storm tore from us. Four of them. A magpie can find her way home by her own feather."],
	],
	"s_mg_return": [
		["Que Lao, the Old Magpie", "Four plumes. Listen. Can you hear? They are calling to the others. The flock is coming back, one bird at a time."],
		["Que Lao, the Old Magpie", "But we cannot build while the heights are held against us. The Ape King sits in our nests. A magpie that ate the storm wears its lightning for a crown. The great pier of the bridge has fallen and walks about in a temper. And on the bridge itself the Crow Marshal says no bird may cross without leave of the Court."],
		["Yue Ling", "There is no Court any more."],
		["Que Lao, the Old Magpie", "Crows are the last to hear any news. Go and tell him."],
	],
	"pre:cloud_ape_king": [
		["Narrator", "In the ruined nests on the cliff a great ape with a silver back sits on a heap of stolen eggs, cracking walnuts on its knee."],
		["King of the Cloud Apes", "Hoo! The birds built, the birds left, the apes moved in. That is the law of the mountain."],
		["Lin Feng", "The birds were blown away. That is not the same as leaving."],
	],
	"post:cloud_ape_king": [["Narrator", "The Ape King sits back, nursing a bruised pride, and very carefully rolls the eggs back into the nests one by one. Apes, it turns out, can be very gentle when they are embarrassed."]],
	"pre:storm_magpie": [
		["Narrator", "Above the heights a magpie as big as a house rides the thunderheads, a crown of lightning crackling on its head."],
		["Storm-Crowned Magpie", "Kak! I swallowed the storm that scattered us. Now I am the storm. Why would I want to be a bridge again?"],
		["Yue Ling", "Because a bridge is where everyone you love walks across your back. A storm never gets to be that close to anyone."],
	],
	"post:storm_magpie": [["Narrator", "The lightning crown flickers out. The great magpie shrinks with every beat of its wings, until it is an ordinary bird, a little singed, landing on Que Lao's staff."]],
	"pre:pier_golem": [
		["Narrator", "The great stone pier that held up one end of the Magpie Bridge has pulled itself out of the cliff and stomps up and down the heights."],
		["Golem of the Fallen Bridge-Pier", "TEN THOUSAND YEARS I HELD. NOBODY THANKED ME. NOW I WALK WHERE I LIKE."],
		["Lin Feng", "Thank you. Truly. Now will you hold it once more?"],
	],
	"post:pier_golem": [["Narrator", "The Pier stops. Nobody, it seems, has ever said thank you before. It walks back to the cliff edge and sets itself into the rock, very straight, very proud."]],
	"pre:crow_marshal": [
		["Narrator", "On the broken end of the bridge stands a marshal in black lacquered armour with a crow's mask, a halberd across the way, a hundred crows on the rails behind him."],
		["Crow Marshal of the Broken Bridge", "By order of the Heavenly Court, no bird, beast, mortal or star may cross the River of Heaven without leave. Present your leave."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Court fell two years ago. I was there. I can show you where the Ledger used to be."],
		["Crow Marshal of the Broken Bridge", "Then I have no orders. Then I am nobody. Then I will hold this bridge until someone gives me new ones."],
	],
	"post:crow_marshal": [
		["Narrator", "The Crow Marshal lowers his halberd and takes off his badge. Behind him the crows fly up, and among them, wing by wing, come the magpies, more and more, until the sky is black and white with them."],
		["Que Lao, the Old Magpie", "Kak! Kak! Look at them! Not enough for the whole bridge, not yet, but enough to carry you across. Go to the Weaver. She has been alone on the far bank a very long time."],
	],
	"s_wv_intro": [
		["Zhi Nu, the Weaver Star", "You crossed on the magpies. I felt every one of their steps on my loom. Sit, please. Mind the warp; that thread is tomorrow's sunrise."],
		["Lin Feng", "The river is flooding the world below. The Elder says it is because the borders are coming undone."],
		["Zhi Nu, the Weaver Star", "The river is flooding because I stopped weaving its banks. I wove them every night for ten thousand years, and on the seventh night of the seventh month I would stop, and cross, and see him. This year I thought: if I stop weaving altogether, perhaps the river will simply go away."],
		["Zhi Nu, the Weaver Star", "It did not go away. It went down. I have done a foolish thing, Sovereign. Help me catch the threads that have blown loose from my loom, four threads of starlight, and I will begin to mend it."],
	],
	"s_wv_return": [
		["Zhi Nu, the Weaver Star", "Four threads. Enough to weave banks for the river, but only banks this time. Not a wall. I will leave a ford, every seventh night and every other night too."],
		["Zhi Nu, the Weaver Star", "But the palace is still full of things I wove in despair. A moth of my seven stars that eats the light. The weaving-frame my grandmother gave me, weaving walls on its own. The qilin of the hall, who thinks I need protecting. And the Warden of the River, who was set at the palace gate to keep me in."],
		["Zhi Nu, the Weaver Star", "Take this sword-form. I wove it from the night he and I first met. My grandfather never knew I had kept it."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng learns the Seventh-Night Reunion Sword."],
	],
	"pre:seven_star_moth": [
		["Narrator", "Above the palace garden a moth with seven stars on its wings drinks the light out of the lanterns one by one."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is eating the stars. All seven of the Weaver's stars."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let us give them back to her."],
	],
	"post:seven_star_moth": [["Narrator", "The moth bursts into seven sparks, and they rise, and settle back into the sky in the shape of a weaver's shuttle, where they have always been."]],
	"pre:weaving_frame": [
		["Narrator", "In the great hall a loom of white jade and gold weaves on its own, shuttle flying, and everything it weaves is a wall."],
		["The Celestial Weaving-Frame", "CLACK. CLACK. THE GRANDDAUGHTER MUST BE KEPT SAFE. CLACK. SAFE IS INSIDE. INSIDE IS WALLS."],
		["Lin Feng", "She can keep herself safe. Stop."],
	],
	"post:weaving_frame": [["Narrator", "The Weaving-Frame's shuttle drops. Its last row of wall unravels and falls in a heap of light, and Zhi Nu runs her hand along the old frame like a horse she used to ride."]],
	"pre:hall_qilin": [
		["Narrator", "Before the inner hall a qilin of starlight lowers its horn."],
		["Qilin of the Weaving Hall", "I have guarded her since she was a child. A cowherd. A mortal. He will grow old, and she will weep for ten thousand years."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then let her weep. That is hers to choose, not yours."],
	],
	"post:hall_qilin": [["Narrator", "The Qilin lowers its horn and steps aside, and lays its head for a moment on Zhi Nu's shoulder, and goes out into the garden to lie in the sun."]],
	"pre:river_warden": [
		["Narrator", "At the palace gate, where the Sky Road leaves for Kunlun, stands a Heavenly Warden in silver armour, a spear in each hand."],
		["Heavenly Warden of the River", "The Queen Mother set me here on the night she drew the river. The Weaver Star does not leave. The Cowherd does not enter. Those are my orders."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then we will ask the Queen Mother to change them. Let us pass."],
		["Heavenly Warden of the River", "Nobody asks the Queen Mother anything."],
	],
	"post:river_warden": [
		["Narrator", "The Warden plants both spears in the ground and kneels beside them."],
		["Heavenly Warden of the River", "Go to Kunlun, then. Ask her. If she answers you, you will be the first in ten thousand years. Her maid tends the peach orchard by the Jasper Pool; she alone can bring you before the throne."],
	],
	"s_jp_intro": [
		["Dong Shuang, Maid of the Jasper Pool", "You are the Sovereign who turned the Wheel. My lady has been expecting you since the day the river broke. She has not said so. She has been pacing."],
		["Lin Feng", "We have come to ask her to take back the river."],
		["Dong Shuang, Maid of the Jasper Pool", "She cannot take it back. A line drawn with that hairpin stays drawn. But she could draw something else across it, if she wanted to."],
		["Dong Shuang, Maid of the Jasper Pool", "Nobody comes before her empty-handed. Pick four peaches of Kunlun from the high orchard, the ones that ripen once in three thousand years. Not the humming ones."],
	],
	"s_jp_return": [
		["Dong Shuang, Maid of the Jasper Pool", "Four peaches, and not one of them humming. You listen well. My lady will see you."],
		["Dong Shuang, Maid of the Jasper Pool", "But Kunlun keeps its own counsel. Kaiming guards the nine gates with his nine heads. The three blue birds carry her messages and will not let you past until you answer theirs. And the white ape who stole the sword manual of heaven lives in the orchard and thinks every visitor has come to steal it back."],
		["Yue Ling", "And then the Queen Mother herself."],
		["Dong Shuang, Maid of the Jasper Pool", "She will fight you. She fights everyone. She has the teeth of a tiger and the tail of a leopard, and a very long memory. Go gently."],
	],
	"pre:kaiming_beast": [
		["Narrator", "Before the nine gates of Kunlun crouches a tiger the size of a temple, with nine human faces, each one watching a gate."],
		["Kaiming, Beast of the Nine Gates", "Nine gates. Nine questions. Why have you come? Who sent you? What do you carry? What do you want?..."],
		["Lin Feng", "To ask for a bridge. Nobody. Peaches. To go home, eventually."],
		["Kaiming, Beast of the Nine Gates", "...those were four. Answer the other five with your sword."],
	],
	"post:kaiming_beast": [["Narrator", "One by one Kaiming's nine faces close their eyes, satisfied, and the nine gates of Kunlun swing open together."]],
	"pre:blue_birds": [
		["Narrator", "Three blue birds as big as cranes wheel above the Jasper Pool, carrying the Queen Mother's messages in their beaks."],
		["The Three Blue Birds", "We carry her word to the world. What word should we carry back to her?"],
		["Yue Ling", "Tell her we came to ask, not to take."],
	],
	"post:blue_birds": [["Narrator", "The blue birds dip their wings and fly up to the jade palace on the peak, carrying Yue Ling's words. Far above, a gate opens."]],
	"pre:white_ape": [
		["Narrator", "In the orchard a white ape with a scroll stuffed down its belt swings from branch to branch, pelting the travellers with peach stones."],
		["White Ape of Kunlun", "Ha! Another thief come for the Sword Manual of Heaven! It is mine, mine, I stole it fair and square!"],
		["Lin Feng", "Keep it. I have my own swords now. Mostly I sweep with them."],
	],
	"post:white_ape": [["Narrator", "The White Ape is so astonished that anybody would not want the Manual that it sits down in a peach tree and reads it for the first time, and finds it is mostly about patience."]],
	"pre:xiwangmu": [
		["Narrator", "Beside the Jasper Pool, on a throne of white jade, sits the Queen Mother of the West: a woman with a tiger's teeth and a leopard's tail, a golden hairpin in her grey hair."],
		["Xiwangmu, Queen Mother of the West", "I know why you have come. You want me to unsay the river. I drew it to keep my granddaughter in the sky where she belongs, and the cowherd on the earth where he belongs. Everyone where they belong. That is what keeps the world from falling apart."],
		["Lin Feng", "Qianxian said that too, about her borders. She is unpicking them now, one row at a time. The world has not fallen apart."],
		["Xiwangmu, Queen Mother of the West", "Then prove it is strong enough to stand without my lines. Come."],
	],
	"post:xiwangmu": [
		["Narrator", "The Queen Mother sits back on her throne. For a long time she looks at the golden hairpin in her hand."],
		["Xiwangmu, Queen Mother of the West", "A line drawn with this stays drawn. But I suppose nobody ever said I could only draw rivers."],
		["Narrator", "She draws the hairpin across the sky, once, gently, from the Weaver's palace to the Cowherd's ferry. Where it passes, a bridge of light hangs over the river, and the magpies land on it in their thousands, and stay."],
	],
	"pre:s_break13": [
		["Narrator", "On the new bridge over the River of Heaven, with the Cowherd climbing up one end and the Weaver running down the other, the Heavens ask Lin Feng their last question: what will you hold together?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Whatever reaches for each other. That is all a bridge is."],
		["Yue Ling", "Magpies holding on to each other."],
		["Lin Feng", "And people. Mostly people."],
	],
	"post:s_break13": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation falls on the bridge, and the bridge holds. Lin Feng becomes the first cultivator of the Star River Crossing: a realm that is not a wall or a door, but a way across."],
		["Narrator", "In the middle of the bridge Niu Lang and Zhi Nu meet, and say nothing at all for a long while. Then he plays his flute, badly, and she laughs, and the sunset that evening is the best anyone has seen in a year."],
		["Xiwangmu, Queen Mother of the West", "Every night, then. Not just the seventh. I suppose it will be very noisy."],
		["Narrator", "And on Azure Cloud Mountain the drowned stars rise out of the lotus pond one by one and float home, and Little Wan claps at each of them, and Lin Feng sweeps the steps, and the River of Heaven runs on above it all, full to its banks and no higher, with a bridge across it for anyone who wants to cross."],
	],
	# ---- Part IX: The Tenth Sun
	"recall7": [
		["Narrator", "The bridge holds. All night the magpies sit on it in their thousands, and all night the sect sits on the roof of the hall to watch them, until Little Wan falls asleep in the Elder's beard. It was, as it turned out, the last sunset for nine days."],
		["Narrator", "In the morning the sun comes up over the eastern ridges, as it has every morning since there was a mountain. It climbs to the top of the sky. And there it stops."],
		["Narrator", "On the first day everyone enjoys the long golden afternoon. On the third day nobody can sleep. By the ninth day the lotus pond is steaming, the bamboo has gone yellow at the tips, and the sect's roosters have given up crowing altogether."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, look at the sundial in the courtyard. The shadow has not moved since the morning after the bridge. Nine days, and it has not moved at all."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "To the hall, both of you, and close the shutters behind you. I have been dreading a morning like this for sixty years. I had rather hoped it would wait until I was dead."],
	],
	"s_p9_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "When the world was young there were ten suns, and they were children. Their mother was Xihe. Every morning she bathed one of them in the hot springs of the Valley of Sunrise, set him in her chariot at the foot of the Fusang tree, and drove him across the sky, singing all the way."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "One child a day, ten days to a week: that is why the old calendars count in tens. Then one morning all ten rose together, to see what would happen. The rivers boiled. The millet burned in the fields."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The archer Hou Yi climbed a mountain and shot nine of them out of the sky. He would have shot the tenth as well, but someone had taken the tenth arrow out of his quiver while he slept, so one sun was left. After that the Heavenly Court took the reins from Xihe. A clerk of the Court drove the last sun along its road, and another wrote every day down in the Ledger."],
		["Lin Feng", "And there is no Court any more."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "There has been no Court for two years. Since then the sun has kept to its road out of habit, the way an old cart-horse walks the road home after the carter has died. Nine days ago something frightened it. It climbed as high as it could over the east, and there it has stayed. The east is baking. The west has not seen a dawn in nine days."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And there is worse. Where the nine suns fell there are embers in the ground, and they are waking. If they rise, it will be the age of ten suns again, and there is no Hou Yi now."],
		["Lin Feng", "Good. I was not going to shoot anything out of the sky."],
		["Yue Ling", "And the moon? Who walks the moon, if the Court is gone?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The moon has a keeper of its own: Chang'e, who has lived alone in the Moon Palace since the archer's day, and never once asked the Court for help. She is walking it round the night side of the world by herself. She will be very tired by now."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Go east, to the Dawn Market at Yanggu, the last town on the pilgrims' road to the Valley of Sunrise. They live closest to the sun, and they will know first if anything changes. I have opened a new gate on the south road. Take water. Take a hat."],
	],
	"s_yg_intro": [
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "Lanterns! Lanterns for the night climb, two coppers, three for five! ...Sorry. Habit. Nobody has bought a lantern in nine days. You cannot sell a lantern at noon."],
		["Lin Feng", "We have come about the sun."],
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "Everyone in Yanggu has come about the sun. The pilgrims used to buy my lanterns here on their way east, and climb the Valley of Sunrise by night to watch it come up out of the Fusang tree. Now it never comes up, because it never went down."],
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "And now little lumps of fire are turning up all over the market: in the wells, in the cellars, in the middle of the street. Noon Embers, the watchman calls them. They never burn out, and they never set anything else alight. They just lie there glowing, as if they were waiting for something."],
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "Bring me four, and I will hang them in my best paper lanterns where we can look at them properly. Something about them is wrong, and I want to know what."],
	],
	"s_yg_return": [
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "Look at them. Four lanterns, four embers, and every one of them leans the same way, like a flame in a draught, and there is no draught in here. East. Up the valley, towards the Fusang tree."],
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "My grandmother said that when the archer shot down the nine suns, they fell all over the east, and the ground kept them warm. I always thought that was why our wells run hot. I never thought they would wake up."],
		["Yue Ling", "Something is calling them."],
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "Then go and find out what. But mind the market on your way out; it has gone strange in the heat. A fox has opened a stall at the dry well, selling water that is not there. The sundial in the square has torn itself off its plinth. And my festival dragon has come alive in the yard behind the shop, because nobody ever came to dance it."],
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "And at the east gate there is a woman in a green robe, and wherever she stands the wells sink out of sight. The watchman says she is Hanba, the Drought Fiend. I think she looks lonely."],
	],
	"pre:mirage_fox": [
		["Narrator", "By the dry well a fox in a merchant's cap has set up a stall of glittering water jars, and the queue of thirsty townsfolk reaches halfway back to the square. Every jar is full of nothing."],
		["Mirage Fox of the Dry Well", "Cool water! Clear water! One string of cash a dipper! ...It is hardly my fault if it evaporates on the way to your mouth. It is very hot."],
		["Lin Feng", "You are selling water that is not there. In a drought."],
		["Mirage Fox of the Dry Well", "I am selling hope. Hope is the only thing that keeps in this heat."],
	],
	"post:mirage_fox": [["Narrator", "The jars shimmer and vanish, and so does the stall, and so, rather to its own surprise, does half of the fox's tail. It sits in the dust looking sheepish and pays everybody back. The coins, at least, were real."]],
	"pre:sundial_golem": [
		["Narrator", "In the middle of the market square the great bronze sundial has torn itself off its plinth and stamps round and round, trying to drag its own shadow along by force."],
		["Golem of the Stopped Sundial", "NOON. NOON. NOON. FOR NINE DAYS IT HAS BEEN NOON. I AM A SUNDIAL. DO YOU KNOW WHAT THAT DOES TO A SUNDIAL?"],
		["Yue Ling", "It is only trying to do its job."],
		["Lin Feng", "So are we. Put yourself back on your plinth, and we will go and make your shadow move. I promise."],
	],
	"post:sundial_golem": [["Narrator", "The Sundial stops stamping, walks back to its plinth and sets itself down very carefully, facing true north. Its shadow still points straight down at its feet. It waits, the way the whole market is waiting."]],
	"pre:lantern_dragon": [
		["Narrator", "In the yard behind Ah Mei's shop the festival dragon, forty paces of red paper on bamboo ribs, has come alive and thrashes among the drying racks, every scale an unlit lantern."],
		["The Lantern Dragon Nobody Danced", "I WAS MADE FOR THE LANTERN FESTIVAL! A hundred dancers under my belly! Drums! Firecrackers! Children on their fathers' shoulders! ...WHERE IS THE NIGHT? I was promised a night!"],
		["Lin Feng", "Then we will just have to dance you in the daylight."],
	],
	"post:lantern_dragon": [
		["Narrator", "The Lantern Dragon sags into a heap of paper and ribs, puffing smoke from its nostrils. Ah Mei comes out of the shop and pats its great painted head."],
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "When the night comes back you will go first down the main street, with every candle lit. I built you, and I promise."],
	],
	"pre:hanba": [
		["Narrator", "At the east gate stands a stiff, dry figure in a dust-green robe and a tasselled hat, arms held out before her as if to keep the world at a distance. A faded paper talisman flutters on her brow, and the ground round her feet has cracked into tiles."],
		["Hanba, the Drought Fiend", "Do not come any closer. Everybody says that to me, so I say it first. I was a daughter of the Yellow Emperor once. When the Rain Master drowned my father's army, I dried the storm out of the sky and won his war for him. After that I could never go home. Wherever I walk, the rain stops."],
		["Hanba, the Drought Fiend", "For five thousand years they have beaten drums at me and pinned talismans on me and cried 'Go north, Goddess, go north!' Then the sun stopped, and the heat called me here the way a hearth calls a cat. For the first time in five thousand years I am not the worst thing in the sky."],
		["Lin Feng", "You are drying up the market's wells."],
		["Hanba, the Drought Fiend", "I know. I cannot help it. Can you?"],
	],
	"post:hanba": [
		["Narrator", "Hanba sinks down against the gatepost, and for a moment the air around her is only warm. Lin Feng unhooks his water gourd and holds it out. She looks at it for a long time before she takes it."],
		["Hanba, the Drought Fiend", "Nobody has ever given me water. They usually throw it."],
		["Ah Mei the Lantern-Maker", "She can sit in my shop until it gets dark. Paper does not mind the dry. Go on, Sovereign: the east road crosses the millet plains, and the plains have been burning longest."],
	],
	"s_mf_intro": [
		["Old Farmer Tian", "If you have come to buy millet, you are nine days late and a harvest early. Sit, sit, there is a little shade on the shrine step. I have nearly finished praying."],
		["Old Farmer Tian", "Every morning I ask the Lord of Millet for the same thing. Hou Ji taught the first farmer which end of the seed goes in the ground; he knows what a field needs. A little night, please, Lord. And a little rain, if you have any to spare."],
		["Lin Feng", "Night? Not rain first?"],
		["Old Farmer Tian", "Plants do not grow in the sun, child. They eat in the sun. They grow in the dark. Dew comes at night. Rain clouds gather in the cool of the evening. No night, no dew; no dew, no rain; no rain, no growing. And look at it all."],
		["Old Farmer Tian", "In the old shrine of the Rain Master by the river there used to be four rain-stones. Set them at the four corners of a field and the clouds come. A dust devil got into the riverbed and scattered them all over the plain. Find me four, and we will see what a little cloud can do."],
	],
	"s_mf_return": [
		["Old Farmer Tian", "Four stones. One at each corner of the field: there, and there, and there, and... there. Now look up. Look! A cloud. A real cloud, the first in nine days."],
		["Narrator", "A small grey cloud gathers over Old Tian's field, hangs there for a while, thinks about it, and gives up. Not one drop falls."],
		["Old Farmer Tian", "Ah. The air is too hot to let go of its water. Rain wants an evening, the way I said. The stones were never the trouble. The sky is."],
		["Old Farmer Tian", "And you will not get east while the plain is like this. The locusts have crowned themselves a Marquis. The dust devil is still spinning in the dry river like a dog chasing its tail. The old tortoise in the village well has dug in and snaps at every bucket. And on the dyke road..."],
		["Yue Ling", "Something with one leg?"],
		["Old Farmer Tian", "Bifang. The fire crane. Wherever it puts its one foot down, a fire starts. When Bifang appears, the old books say, there will be fires in the towns. Well. It is a little late for an omen."],
	],
	"pre:locust_marquis": [
		["Narrator", "Over the withered millet a swarm of locusts hangs in the air in the shape of a man on a throne. At its heart sits a locust the size of a horse, wearing a crown of chewed stalks."],
		["The Locust Marquis", "We are the Marquis of Ten Thousand Mouths. The millet withered, and we came. That is not cruelty, Sovereign. That is lunch."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then lunch is over."],
	],
	"post:locust_marquis": [["Narrator", "The Marquis comes apart into ten thousand ordinary locusts, which seem embarrassed to be seen together and fly off in ten thousand different directions."]],
	"pre:dust_devil": [
		["Narrator", "In the cracked bed of what used to be the river, a whirlwind of red dust spins and spins and never moves on, sucking up everything it touches."],
		["Dust-Devil of the Dry River", "Round and round and round! The river left! It left without me! I was the wind that rippled it every evening, and it dried up and left me spinning in its bed!"],
		["Yue Ling", "It misses the water."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then hold still for a moment, and we will go and bring the water back."],
	],
	"post:dust_devil": [["Narrator", "The Dust-Devil falls with a long, soft sigh, and the red dust settles along the riverbed in the exact shape of the river that used to run there, every bend and every eddy, waiting."]],
	"pre:well_tortoise": [
		["Narrator", "At the bottom of the village well, where the last of the water has turned to warm mud, an old tortoise with moss on its shell has dug itself in and snaps at every bucket that comes down."],
		["Old Tortoise of the Village Well", "Mine. The last mud on the whole plain. I was in this well before there was a village, and I will be in it after the village is gone, if they would only stop taking my mud."],
		["Lin Feng", "If you dig down instead of in, grandfather, I think you will find a good deal more than mud."],
		["Old Tortoise of the Village Well", "Down? ...Nobody ever said down."],
	],
	"post:well_tortoise": [["Narrator", "Grumbling, the Old Tortoise begins to dig down, and down, and down. There is a long silence. Then the well coughs, gurgles and fills to the brim with cold, clear water from somewhere very deep, and the village cheers so loudly that a locust falls out of the sky."]],
	"pre:bifang": [
		["Narrator", "On the dyke road a great crane with blue-green plumage, red markings and a white beak hops from field to field on a single leg. Wherever its foot comes down, the millet bursts into flame."],
		["Bifang, the One-Legged Fire Crane", "Kraa! I am the omen of fire! Wherever I appear, fire follows! ...It used to be very impressive. Now there is fire everywhere already, and nobody even looks up."],
		["Lin Feng", "You are making it worse."],
		["Bifang, the One-Legged Fire Crane", "An omen does not make anything. An omen announces it. ...All right. I make it a little worse."],
	],
	"post:bifang": [
		["Narrator", "Bifang lands on its one leg, wobbles, and for the first time in a thousand years stands on bare earth without setting it alight. It stares down at its foot in astonishment."],
		["Old Farmer Tian", "There, you see? Even an omen can rest. East now, child. The dyke road climbs into the Valley of Sunrise. If there is a reason for all this, it is up there with the tree."],
	],
	"s_fs_intro": [
		["Feng Meng, the Last Apprentice", "Stop there. Hands where I can see them. ...A sword, not a bow. Good. I have seen enough bows pointed at that tree."],
		["Lin Feng", "You are an archer."],
		["Feng Meng, the Last Apprentice", "I was the second-best archer under heaven. The best was my master, Hou Yi, who stood on that ridge and shot nine suns out of the sky. I was there. I carried his quiver. I counted the arrows."],
		["Feng Meng, the Last Apprentice", "That is the Fusang behind me: a mulberry so tall that the sun used to rest in its crown before it set out each morning. The sun-crows still roost in it, the little ones that ride the sun, and their feathers have been falling all week, hot enough to light the grass."],
		["Feng Meng, the Last Apprentice", "Gather four of them for me. A fallen feather tells an archer which way a bird means to fly."],
	],
	"s_fs_return": [
		["Feng Meng, the Last Apprentice", "Four feathers, and not one of them points west. The sun is not stuck. It is afraid. The last time it flew with its brothers, an archer shot nine of them down in front of it. Now it waits at the top of the sky, where no arrow can reach."],
		["Feng Meng, the Last Apprentice", "I know that kind of waiting. You will have heard what I did. When the whole world is grateful to a man, it is very hard to be his second-best. One evening I took a club of peach wood, and I made sure I never would be again. Four thousand years I have carried that, and it has not got any lighter."],
		["Yue Ling", "Why are you telling us?"],
		["Feng Meng, the Last Apprentice", "Because you are going up that tree, and everyone who climbs it carries a bow. Take this instead: the tenth arrow, the one Emperor Yao took from my master's quiver in the night so that one sun would be left. I stole it from Yao's tomb. I thought that one day I would finish what my master began. I am done thinking that."],
		["Feng Meng, the Last Apprentice", "And take the one thing my master tried to teach me that I never had the patience for. You draw the sword like a bow. You hold it like a promise. You never loose it in anger."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng learns the Tenth Arrow Sword."],
	],
	"pre:boiled_crab": [
		["Narrator", "In the hottest of the bathing springs, where Xihe once washed her children before their journeys, an enormous crab sits up to its eyestalks in boiling water. It is bright red, and perfectly content."],
		["The Crab Who Bathes in Boiling Springs", "Ahhh. Nine days of the finest bath of my life. Join me? No? Then go away. You are standing in my sun."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng... I think it has been cooked."],
		["Lin Feng", "I think it likes it. Come out of there, friend. The pilgrims need these springs."],
	],
	"post:boiled_crab": [["Narrator", "Hauled out at last, the Crab sits steaming on the rocks and very slowly turns from red back to its ordinary green. It does not look at anyone. It is a little ashamed of how much it enjoyed itself."]],
	"pre:ninth_ember": [
		["Narrator", "On the cliff above the springs the ground has split open, and something is climbing out of it: a coal the size of a house, white-hot at the heart, the ember of one of the nine suns that Hou Yi shot down."],
		["Ember of the Ninth Fallen Sun", "Mother is singing. Can you hear her? Nine of us asleep in the ground all these years, and now she is singing us up into the sky again."],
		["Lin Feng", "If all ten of you rise, the rivers will boil."],
		["Ember of the Ninth Fallen Sun", "We did not mind, the first time."],
	],
	"post:ninth_ember": [["Narrator", "The Ember dims from white to orange to a sleepy red, sinks back into its crack in the cliff, and the rock closes over it like a blanket. Far underground, eight other fires are still muttering in their sleep."]],
	"pre:sun_moth": [
		["Narrator", "Round the lower branches of the Fusang a moth with wings as wide as a temple roof beats itself against the light, scorched and glittering, trying to fly up to the sun."],
		["The Moth Who Flew at the Sun", "So bright! It has been so bright for so long! For nine days I have flown at it, and it never comes any closer, and it never goes away!"],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, its wings are burning. It cannot stop."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then we will stop it for a while. Long enough for it to see that there are other lights."],
	],
	"post:sun_moth": [["Narrator", "The great Moth folds its singed wings and settles on a root in the shade of the Fusang, and for the first time in nine days it looks at something that is not the sun: a hot spring, glittering, with the steam rising off it in the light. It seems to find that very interesting indeed."]],
	"pre:jinwu": [
		["Narrator", "At the crown of the Fusang the topmost branch touches the top of the sky, and there the sun hangs, caught in the leaves like a kite in a tree. On the branch beside it clings a crow with three legs and feathers of beaten gold, too bright to look at."],
		["Jinwu, the Three-Legged Sun Crow", "Kaa! Another one! Another one from the archer's valley! I will not come down, and I will not go west. West is where they fall!"],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng lays the tenth arrow on the branch between them, and steps back from it."],
		["Jinwu, the Three-Legged Sun Crow", "...That is the tenth arrow. That arrow was meant for me."],
		["Lin Feng", "It was never shot. Nobody is going to shoot it."],
		["Jinwu, the Three-Legged Sun Crow", "Then why are you holding a sword?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Habit. Come on, then. Let us shake the fear out of you first, and then we can talk."],
	],
	"post:jinwu": [
		["Narrator", "The Sun Crow tumbles onto the branch, panting sparks, and dwindles until it is no bigger than a rooster. A very bright rooster. With one of its three feet it pushes the tenth arrow back across the branch to Lin Feng."],
		["Jinwu, the Three-Legged Sun Crow", "Keep it. It is the only arrow in the world that ever spared anybody. ...When the Court fell I kept to the road by myself, the way they taught me. Then, nine days ago, I heard my mother singing for the first time in four thousand years. She was not singing to me. She was singing my brothers up out of the ground."],
		["Jinwu, the Three-Legged Sun Crow", "If all ten of us are in the sky, the archer comes back. Everybody knows that. So I flew as high as I could, where no arrow reaches, and I have been waiting for somebody to tell me it is safe. She has gone over the top of the sky, into the night. She has gone to the Moon Palace to ask the moon-lady for the dark, so that there will be room in the sky for all ten of us again."],
		["Narrator", "From far below, at the foot of the tree, an old voice calls up."],
		["Feng Meng, the Last Apprentice", "Then the night is where you are needed, Sovereign. Walk the sun's road across the top of the sky; the moon's road begins where it ends. And if you see her, the lady of the moon... tell her Feng Meng is sorry. Tell her I should never have come for the elixir."],
	],
	"s_gh_intro": [
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "Please do not shout. Sound carries terribly up here, and the rabbit has only just fallen asleep."],
		["Lin Feng", "You are Chang'e."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "I am. And you have walked the sun's road across the top of the sky into the night. Nobody has done that since... well. Since Xihe came, nine days ago."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "She is in my palace now, on my throne. She wants the night. She says that if I stop walking the moon round the sky there will be room for all ten of her children again, and she will never again have to watch one of them fall. I have told her no nine times, once each night, and each night it is harder to walk."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "I have walked the moon round by myself for four thousand years. Now I can barely lift it. The osmanthus up here blooms with a dew that gives back strength, but Wu Gang has been felling the trees in a temper and the blossoms lie scattered over the frost. Gather four for me, and I can walk one more night."],
	],
	"s_gh_return": [
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "Moon-dew, from all four. Thank you. I can feel my feet again."],
		["Lin Feng", "Before we go on: Feng Meng asked me to tell you something. He is sorry. He says he should never have come for the elixir."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "...Four thousand years. I used to lie awake up here imagining him climbing the sky with that club, and imagining what I would say to him."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "Tell him... no. I will tell him myself, some night when the moon hangs low over the valley. Tell him to look up."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "Now. Xihe is not wicked, Sovereign. She is a mother who watched nine of her children fall out of the sky in a single morning. I would have done anything too. But she cannot have the night."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "Before you reach her: Wu Gang has stopped chopping the tree and started chopping everything else. Tiangou, the Heavenly Dog, has smelt how tired the moon is and keeps trying to swallow it. And my rabbit has been pounding elixir for nine days without sleep to keep me on my feet. He will think you have come to steal it. Everyone from the archer's valley has, once."],
	],
	"pre:wu_gang": [
		["Narrator", "Among the stumps of the osmanthus grove a woodcutter with a great axe is hacking at everything in reach: the trees, the frost, the palace railings, the silver stones of the moon itself."],
		["Wu Gang, Woodcutter of the Moon", "Three thousand years! Every cut I made in that tree healed over before I could lift the axe again! The Court said: chop until it falls. It never fell. Now the Court is gone and nobody says stop, so I cannot stop!"],
		["Lin Feng", "I sweep the steps of my sect every morning. They never stay clean. I do not sweep them because they will stay clean."],
		["Wu Gang, Woodcutter of the Moon", "Then why?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Because I like the sound the broom makes. Put the axe down, and I will show you what I mean."],
	],
	"post:wu_gang": [["Narrator", "The axe drops into the frost. Wu Gang sits down on a stump and looks at his hands, which have not been empty in three thousand years. After a while he picks up a fallen branch, snaps it in two for kindling, and stops. It seems to surprise him very much."]],
	"pre:tiangou": [
		["Narrator", "A black dog the size of a hill pads across the frost with its eyes on the moon. Every few steps it opens its jaws and takes a bite out of the sky, and the moonlight dims."],
		["Tiangou, the Dog Who Eats the Moon", "Woof. The moon is tired. Tired things are easy to catch. Every dog knows that."],
		["Yue Ling", "Bad dog."],
		["Lin Feng", "Very bad dog."],
	],
	"post:tiangou": [["Narrator", "Far below, in a thousand villages, people are banging pots and pans to frighten the Heavenly Dog off the moon, the way they always have. Up here, Tiangou rolls onto its back in the frost, lets Yue Ling scratch its belly, and burps up a mouthful of moonlight. The moon brightens a little. The dog looks extremely pleased with itself."]],
	"pre:mortar_rabbit": [
		["Narrator", "Under the last standing osmanthus a white rabbit stands on its hind legs over a mortar of green jade, pounding so fast that the pestle is a blur. Its eyes are very red, and not only because it is a rabbit."],
		["The Jade Rabbit of the Mortar", "THIEF! You came up from the archer's valley, I can smell the bowstring on you! The last one who came from there wanted the elixir too!"],
		["Lin Feng", "We only want to help her."],
		["The Jade Rabbit of the Mortar", "That is exactly what he said! Nine days I have pounded! Nine days! I will pound you too!"],
	],
	"post:mortar_rabbit": [
		["Narrator", "The Jade Rabbit sits down in the frost with the pestle still in its paws, blinks twice, and falls asleep sitting up. Chang'e lifts it into her arms."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "Nine days, you silly thing. Nobody asked you to. ...Thank you."],
	],
	"pre:xihe": [
		["Narrator", "In the hall of the Moon Palace, where the frost lies thickest, a woman in robes the colour of dawn sits on Chang'e's throne. Her hair is white-gold, her eyes are red from weeping, and the hall is so hot that the frost runs down the pillars like tears."],
		["Xihe, Mother of the Ten Suns", "I know who you are. You are the one who goes about the world asking everybody to let go of things. I let go of nine children in a single morning. Do you know what it is to hear them fall?"],
		["Lin Feng", "No."],
		["Xihe, Mother of the Ten Suns", "No. And now I can have them back. Their embers are waking. Give me the night, and all ten of my children will be in the sky together again, and I will never have to count them."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng holds up the tenth arrow."],
		["Lin Feng", "This was meant for your youngest. Somebody took it out of the quiver so that he would live. He is at the top of the sky now, too frightened to move, waiting for his mother to come and fetch him."],
		["Xihe, Mother of the Ten Suns", "...Then I will fetch him when the whole sky is mine. Out of my way, Sovereign."],
		["Narrator", "Xihe rises from the throne and spreads her sleeves, and the light of ten suns pours out of them as wings. The Mother of the Ten Suns is herself the first sun-crow, from whom all the others hatched."],
	],
	"post:xihe": [
		["Narrator", "Xihe folds her burning wings and is a woman again, sitting on the steps of the throne. The heat goes out of the hall in one long breath. When she speaks, it is very quietly."],
		["Xihe, Mother of the Ten Suns", "He is waiting for me?"],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "He has been waiting for nine days. So have I. There is room in the sky for both of us, Xihe. There always was. We only ever needed to take turns."],
		["Xihe, Mother of the Ten Suns", "And my nine?"],
		["Yue Ling", "The old books say the Dipper has seven stars that anyone can see and two that nobody can, as if the sky had been keeping places for somebody. Let your nine rise at night, where they cannot burn anything: nine stars turning round the pole."],
		["Lin Feng", "And let them count the days, since the Court will not. Every night they will turn a little further round the sky, and everyone below will know what season it is. And you will be able to see them every single night."],
		["Xihe, Mother of the Ten Suns", "...Nine stars, counting the days. They were always very good at counting. They used to count the clouds the whole way across the sky."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "Then it is agreed. Not decreed this time: agreed. The sun by day, the moon by night, and nine stars to count them both."],
	],
	"pre:s_break14": [
		["Narrator", "At the edge of the Moon Palace, where the sun's road meets the moon's, the last tribulation gathers. It is not a storm this time but a dawn and a dusk at once: gold on one side of the sky, silver on the other, and Lin Feng standing where they meet."],
		["Lin Feng", "Yue Ling. Which way do I face?"],
		["Yue Ling", "Both. I think that is the whole point."],
	],
	"post:s_break14": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation falls as light from both sides at once, and Lin Feng holds it, the way a day holds a morning and an evening. He becomes the first cultivator of Sun-Moon Harmony: a realm in which nothing has to win."],
		["Narrator", "In the east Xihe climbs into her chariot at the foot of the Fusang, her youngest son clambers in beside her, and she sings to him the whole way across the sky. That evening, for the first time in nine days, the sun goes down."],
		["Chang'e of the Moon Palace", "My turn."],
		["Narrator", "The moon rises full over the world, and round the pole nine new stars rise in the shape of a ladle and begin, very carefully, to count. In Yanggu Ah Mei sells every lantern she owns, and the Lantern Dragon goes first down the main street. On the ridge above the Fusang, Feng Meng looks up at the moon for a long time, and the moon looks back."],
		["Narrator", "And on Azure Cloud Mountain the sundial's shadow moves again, the lotus pond stops steaming, Little Wan falls asleep at a reasonable hour for the first time in nine days, and Lin Feng sweeps the steps in the cool of the evening, and stops when they are clean enough."],
	],
	# ---- Part X: The Nine Cauldrons
	"recall8": [
		["Narrator", "The sun goes down and the moon comes up, and for a whole month they take turns as politely as two old neighbours at a well. Lin Feng sweeps the steps in the mornings and counts the nine new stars in the evenings, and nothing at all happens. It is wonderful."],
		["Narrator", "Then, one morning, Archivist Lan comes running across the courtyard with a scroll in each hand and her hair still in its nightcap."],
		["Archivist Lan", "Elder! The map is wrong! The great map in the Library, the Court's survey, three hundred years on that wall, and it is wrong! The rivers have moved. The coasts have moved. The bamboo forest has grown a bay. The road to the Dawn Market goes round three bends that were never on any map of mine!"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then the map is wrong, Archivist, and not the land. Bring it to the hall. Lin Feng, you too. I believe I know what this is, and for once it may be good news. Mostly."],
	],
	"s_p10_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Look at the old map. Every province a square. Every border ruled straight, every river bent at right angles to fit inside its square, every coast a line. Did you never think that strange?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I thought that was how maps were."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "That was how the Heavenly Court's maps were. When the Court took the sky, it took the land as well. It surveyed the world into squares, so that every square could be taxed in years, and the land, being patient, held the shape it was told to hold. The Weaver sewed the seams. The Court pinned the corners."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Pinned them with the Nine Cauldrons of Yu. When Yu the Great tamed the Flood he cast nine bronze cauldrons and engraved on them the true map of the Nine Provinces: every mountain, every river and every strange beast. Whoever held the cauldrons held the shape of the world. The Court carried them off to the sky and recut the engravings into squares."],
		["Lin Feng", "And now the Court is gone, the Weaver has unpicked her seams, and the sun and the moon take turns..."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And the land has stopped pretending. It is lying down in its own shape again, like a cat let out of a box: rivers finding their old beds, coasts their old bays, roads following the hills instead of the ruler. The world you walked through on your way here is the true one, disciple. It is beautiful."],
		["Yue Ling", "You said mostly good news."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Land that moves floods. Rivers that remember their old beds remember their old floods too. And under the Court's squares there was something Yu never killed, only channelled: Gonggong, the Flood God, who once broke a pillar of heaven with his head. The north is very wet this morning. Ask Archivist Lan what the Library remembers of the cauldrons, and then take the north gate."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "We had the Smith knock it through the wall at dawn. The old north wall was only ever there because the Court's map said so."],
	],
	"s_p10_archive": [
		["Archivist Lan", "The cauldrons. Yes. We have one rubbing, taken from the Ninth Cauldron before the Court carried them off. Look: mountains drawn like waves, rivers like dragons, and in the margins every strange creature of every province, so travellers would know what they met on the road. The nine-headed snake. The fish as big as an island. The glutton with no body."],
		["Archivist Lan", "The Court's copy has none of that. Squares, numbers and the tax in years per square. They even squared off the coast."],
		["Lin Feng", "Where are the cauldrons now?"],
		["Archivist Lan", "The rubbing says they were cast at Tushan, on Mount Kuaiji, by the Tushan clan. Their foundry town is still there, past the Dragon Gate at Longmen and over the Drowned Fields. If anyone alive knows what became of the cauldrons, it is a Tushan caster."],
		["Archivist Lan", "And Lin Feng: there is a cartographer at Cloudfoot, the post town below the north gate. Pei. He has written to me every month for twelve years to tell me that the Court's maps are lies. He will be insufferable this morning. Go and let him gloat at you. Nobody knows the north roads better."],
	],
	"s_p10_road": [
		["Cartographer Pei of Cloudfoot", "Ha! HA! Twelve years! Twelve years I wrote to that Library! The Court's maps are lies, the rivers do not bend at right angles, nobody's coast is a straight line! And did anyone listen?"],
		["Lin Feng", "The Archivist says you were right."],
		["Cartographer Pei of Cloudfoot", "Of course I was right. A good map keeps six rules: true scale, true direction, true distance, and the high, the low and the winding, each measured as they are. The Court kept one rule: squares. I have been up all night redrawing the north. Look at it. Look how it curves!"],
		["Cartographer Pei of Cloudfoot", "But I am not only gloating. The ferry at Longmen has stopped sending the post. The river is rising there, three feet a day, and the golden carp have stopped leaping the Dragon Gate. When the carp will not leap, the boatmen say, the river is afraid of something upstream."],
		["Cartographer Pei of Cloudfoot", "Here: the north road, drawn properly. The Lotus Seal on it will open for you; I think it was only ever waiting for someone carrying a true map. Give my regards to Harbourmistress Jiang at Longmen. Tell her I want my maps back."],
	],
	"s_dg_intro": [
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "If you have come for the ferry, it is not running. If you have come for the post, that is not running either. If you have come to tell me the river is high, I know. I measure it every hour on the flood stele by the temple, and I have run out of stele."],
		["Lin Feng", "Cartographer Pei sends his regards. He wants his maps back."],
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "Ha. Tell him the river ate them. ...Longmen is a ferry town, Sovereign: a thousand years of boats crossing below the Dragon Gate. Every spring the golden carp swim up from the sea and leap the falls, and the ones that clear the gate become dragons. That is how the river knows it is spring."],
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "This spring the carp came up the river and stopped below the falls, and sat there, and sulked, and the river has risen every day since. Something upstream is pulling the water back up the gorge, and the carp are afraid to jump into it."],
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "When a carp tries the gate and falls short, it sheds a golden scale. The river-readers in my family can read the water in them: how high, how fast, and which way the fear is coming from. Bring me four scales from below the falls, and I will read you the river."],
	],
	"s_dg_return": [
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "Four scales. Hold them to the light... see the rings? Like a tree's. Every ring is a flood. And look here, at the edge: the new rings are wound the wrong way. The river is flowing backwards in its sleep."],
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "In my great-grandmother's day there was a stele at the Dragon Gate that said: Here Yu cut the mountain and let the river through; do not dam it. The Court took it for its archive. Somebody upstream is damming the river, and has been ever since the land started moving."],
		["Yue Ling", "Who would dam a river?"],
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "Somebody who remembers when rivers were dammed. The Drowned Fields lie beyond the gorge; that is where the old dykes are. But Longmen is in a mood. The carp-king below the falls will not leap and will not let anyone else leap. Black Eel and his river-rats are looting the flooded warehouses. The temple bell has slid into the river and rings underwater all night."],
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "And the stone lion of the ferry stage has woken up and will not let anybody past it. Longmen, Sovereign. In a mood."],
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "And the River Earl himself, He Bo, has come up out of the water at the ferry stage demanding a bride, the way he used to before Magistrate Ximen Bao threw his priests in the river after the last one."],
	],
	"pre:sulking_carp": [
		["Narrator", "In the pool below the Dragon Gate a golden carp the size of a ferry lies in the shallows with its fins folded, glaring at the falls."],
		["The Carp Who Would Not Leap", "I have swum up from the sea for nine hundred years to make this leap, and the river has turned backwards at the top. You want me to jump into that? You jump into that."],
		["Lin Feng", "If I clear the gate, will you try?"],
		["The Carp Who Would Not Leap", "If you clear the gate, I will think about it. First you have to get past me. A carp has its pride."],
	],
	"post:sulking_carp": [["Narrator", "Lin Feng runs up the face of the falls on the light-body and lands on the lip of the Dragon Gate, soaked to the skin. Below, the carp considers this for a long moment, then flicks its tail, leaps, and clears the gate by a hand's breadth. It comes down on the far side as a small, very surprised golden dragon, and all the way down the river the other carp begin to leap."]],
	"pre:eel_king": [
		["Narrator", "In the flooded warehouses by the quay a gang of river-rats is loading a stolen sampan, and round its prow coils a black eel as long as the boat, wearing a magistrate's hat that he has certainly not earned."],
		["Black Eel, King of the River-Rats", "A flood is good for business, little immortal. Everything floats! Everything is salvage!"],
		["Lin Feng", "Everything belongs to somebody."],
		["Black Eel, King of the River-Rats", "Not if it floats."],
	],
	"post:eel_king": [["Narrator", "The Eel King slithers off his sampan into the river with his hat floating after him. His rats sheepishly unload the warehouses, while Fishwife Lu stands on the quay counting every bale back in, out loud."]],
	"pre:drowned_bell": [
		["Narrator", "Below the temple steps the river has risen over the old bronze bell, and it rings under the water, slow and deep, so that every boat on the river shivers in time."],
		["The Drowned Temple Bell", "BONG. The water is in my mouth. BONG. I cannot stop ringing the hours. BONG. Nobody can hear the hours under a river."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then let us get you out of it."],
	],
	"post:drowned_bell": [["Narrator", "Lin Feng and Yue Ling haul the bell up the temple steps between them, streaming water, and hang it back on its beam. It rings once, clear and dry, and all along the quay people stop and look up, because for the first time in a month they know what time it is."]],
	"pre:stone_lion": [
		["Narrator", "On the ferry stage a stone lion as big as a boathouse has climbed down off its plinth and paces the planks, roaring at the river. Its stone mane is green with moss, and the ball under its paw has rolled into the water."],
		["The Stone Lion of the Ferry Stage", "I guard the ferry! I have guarded the ferry for six hundred years! And the river came up over my paws in the night and took my ball, and now I cannot remember which side of the river I am guarding!"],
		["Yue Ling", "It is having a bad dream. Stone lions dream very slowly."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then we will wake it up gently. As gently as you can wake up a boathouse."],
	],
	"post:stone_lion": [["Narrator", "Lin Feng fishes the stone ball out of the shallows and rolls it back under the lion's paw. The lion blinks, climbs back onto its plinth, sits down facing both banks at once, and is stone again, with a slightly embarrassed expression that Boatwright Gao swears was never carved there."]],
	"pre:he_bo": [
		["Narrator", "At the ferry stage the river heaves itself up into the shape of a man in a robe of yellow water, crowned with river-weed, riding a chariot drawn by two white fish."],
		["He Bo, Earl of the Yellow River", "Where is my bride? Every spring for a thousand years Longmen sent me a girl in a painted boat, until that magistrate Ximen Bao threw my priests in after her and asked me whether I would like them instead. Now the river is mine again. I want a bride."],
		["Lin Feng", "Nobody is sending you anybody."],
		["He Bo, Earl of the Yellow River", "Then I will keep the river high until they do."],
	],
	"post:he_bo": [
		["He Bo, Earl of the Yellow River", "...I never wanted the brides. I wanted somebody to come down to the river on purpose. Everyone only ever came to cross it."],
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "Then come up to the temple on festival days, Earl, and we will feed you dumplings like everybody else's god."],
		["He Bo, Earl of the Yellow River", "...Dumplings?"],
		["Narrator", "He Bo gives Lin Feng the old flood tablet of his office, scored with the height of every flood since Yu's day, and points upstream with a dripping finger. The water is being pulled back up into the Drowned Fields, he says. Somebody there is building a dam out of earth that grows. Take my tablet to my temple first; they will want to read it."],
	],
	"s_dg_temple": [
		["Shrine-Maiden Hua", "The Earl's own flood tablet? He gave it to you? He has never given anybody anything except damp."],
		["Shrine-Maiden Hua", "Every flood since Yu is cut into it, look: one notch for every spring the river came over the quay. Most years one notch. Here, a hundred years with none, when the Court first squared the map and pinned the river in a straight line. And then here, these last months, a notch every day."],
		["Lin Feng", "The land moved, and the water came up."],
		["Shrine-Maiden Hua", "The water always comes up when the land moves; my grandmother said so, and the Earl's tablet says so too. We will keep it in the temple where he can visit it. Dumplings on the Double Fifth, Earl, and a song on the first full moon. That is the new arrangement."],
		["Shrine-Maiden Hua", "The road over the gorge goes on to the Drowned Fields. Take the tablet's rubbing with you, Sovereign. Upriver they have a carver at Tushan who can read old marks the way I read the river. If anyone can tell you what these notches mean, it is Stone-Carver Shi."],
	],
	"s_df_intro": [
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Mind the dyke. It is the only dry thing for a day in any direction, and it is mine."],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "I am Bo Yi. I was a young man when I worked these dykes with Yu, four thousand years ago, and I have been an old man ever since. Yu used to say: you cannot hold water still, you can only give it somewhere to go. We dug channels, not walls, and for four thousand years the fields stayed dry."],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Now look. The water comes back up the channels every night, and the old earth walls rise out of the flood like loaves in an oven. Swelling Earth. Xirang. Gun's earth."],
		["Lin Feng", "Gun?"],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Yu's father. Before Yu, Gun tried to stop the Flood. He stole the Xirang, the earth that grows by itself, from the storehouse of heaven, and built walls with it. For nine years the walls grew, and for nine years the water rose behind them, until they broke and drowned everything they had kept dry."],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Heaven executed him on Feather Hill for the theft. His body did not rot for three years, and when they cut it open, Yu came out."],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Clods of Xirang are rising out of the water all over the fields. Bring me four before they grow into walls, and we will see whose they are."],
	],
	"s_df_return": [
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Four clods. Feel them: warm, and growing even in your hands. And look at this one. Fingerprints, pressed into the clay. A man's hands, building."],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Gun's hands. He has come back to finish his dam. Four thousand years dead, and when the land started moving and the water started rising, the first thing he did was come back and try to stop it again. He never learned the other way. He never had the chance."],
		["Yue Ling", "He is trying to help."],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "So he was, the first time. Go gently with him, if you can. But the toad that swallowed the old dykes has swollen up fit to burst, a heron has made himself lord of the drowned paddies, a dragon of mud has broken out of the last of Yu's channels, and the old turtle that carried Gun's earth is still walking in circles with it on its back. You will meet all of them before you meet him."],
	],
	"pre:toad_matriarch": [
		["Narrator", "A toad the size of a farmhouse squats across the dyke road with her cheeks bulging. Every time she swallows, another field goes under."],
		["The Toad Who Swallowed the Dykes", "Glorp. The water was going somewhere. I stopped it. Glorp. Now it is all MINE."],
		["Lin Feng", "Spit it out."],
	],
	"post:toad_matriarch": [["Narrator", "The Toad spits a whole field's worth of water out in one long rush, back down the channel where it belongs, and shrinks to an ordinary, rather embarrassed toad on a lily pad."]],
	"pre:heron_lord": [
		["Narrator", "On a dead tree above the drowned paddies stands a heron as tall as a watchtower, a fish in its beak and a magistrate's sash round its neck."],
		["Heron Lord of the Drowned Paddies", "These are my waters now. I have appointed myself Lord of the Paddies. Every fish pays a tax of one fish."],
		["Yue Ling", "That is just eating them."],
		["Heron Lord of the Drowned Paddies", "That is what a tax is, child. Ask any square on the old map."],
	],
	"post:heron_lord": [["Narrator", "The Heron Lord takes off his sash, looks at it for a while, and flies away north with as much dignity as a wet heron can manage."]],
	"pre:mud_dragon": [
		["Narrator", "The last of Yu's channels has caved in, and out of the slurry rises a dragon made of mud, shedding clods of Xirang from its flanks as it coils."],
		["Lin Feng", "It is made of the walls. Break it, and the channel opens."],
	],
	"post:mud_dragon": [["Narrator", "The Mud Dragon slumps into a long low bank of earth, and through the gap behind it the water begins to run away downhill, chuckling, the way water does when it is going where it wants to."]],
	"pre:carrier_turtle": [
		["Narrator", "On the high bank of the old dyke a turtle the size of a hill plods in a slow circle, an enormous load of earth heaped on its shell, and a horned owl perched on top of the load calls out the way."],
		["The Turtle Who Carried Gun's Earth", "Left... left... a little more... The old texts say that owls and turtles helped Gun carry his earth. Nobody asked us. We were only there. Four thousand years we have carried this load, and nobody ever told us where to put it down."],
		["Lin Feng", "Down. Anywhere. You can put it down now."],
		["The Turtle Who Carried Gun's Earth", "...Can we? Nobody has ever said that. You had better make us."],
	],
	"post:carrier_turtle": [["Narrator", "The load slides off the turtle's shell in one long avalanche and becomes a small new hill beside the dyke, and the turtle stretches out its neck further than it has stretched in four thousand years. The owl hoots once, approvingly, and goes to sleep on the new hill."]],
	"pre:gun": [
		["Narrator", "In the middle of the flood, where the water is deepest, a yellow dragon labours on a wall of growing earth, pressing clods into place with its claws. Its scales are cracked like dry clay, and it does not stop working even to look at them."],
		["Gun, Who Stole the Swelling Earth", "Do not stand there. Carry earth. The water is rising. The water is always rising. If I build the wall high enough, it will stop."],
		["Lin Feng", "Your son did it another way."],
		["Gun, Who Stole the Swelling Earth", "My son? ...Yu? He was born from me after they killed me. I never met him. Did he... did he do well?"],
		["Yue Ling", "He saved the world."],
		["Gun, Who Stole the Swelling Earth", "Then help me build the wall high enough, and I will have saved it too."],
	],
	"post:gun": [
		["Narrator", "The yellow dragon's wall crumbles, and Gun sinks down beside it in the shape of a tired old man covered in clay. Bo Yi wades out through the falling water and sits down beside him."],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "He dug channels, old friend. He let the water go where it wanted to, and then it was not a flood any more, only a river."],
		["Gun, Who Stole the Swelling Earth", "...Channels. Of course. You cannot hold water still. ...He did not get that from me."],
		["Gun, Who Stole the Swelling Earth", "Ask the Tushan casters what became of the cauldrons. When they were stolen, the land forgot its shape, and when the land forgets its shape the water forgets its way. And something under Mount Buzhou has been waiting four thousand years for the water to forget."],
	],
	"s_ts_carver": [
		["Stone-Carver Shi", "A rubbing of He Bo's tablet? Give it here. Spectacles... there. Hm. Hm! Whoever cut these was a river, not a carver. No craft at all. Very honest."],
		["Stone-Carver Shi", "Look how the notches change. Before the Court, they wander, like the river: a flood here, a dry year there. When the Court squared the land, they stop, a hundred years without a flood. The river was pinned so tight it could not even complain."],
		["Stone-Carver Shi", "And here at the end they come every day, all leaning one way, north-west, like grass in a wind. The river is not flooding, Sovereign. It is being pulled. Something in the north-west is pulling every river in the land towards it."],
		["Yue Ling", "The Drowned Fields were the same: the water running backwards up Yu's channels."],
		["Stone-Carver Shi", "Then it is old, and it is big, and it has waited a long time for the map to break. Go and see Tu Lan at the foundry. If anybody can mend a map, it is a Tushan caster."],
	],
	"s_ts_intro": [
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "Stand back from the pit, it spits. ...Visitors who are not after a bell. You must be the Sovereign. The ferry gossip got here before you did."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "This is Tushan. My clan cast the Nine Cauldrons for Yu, four thousand years ago, from the bronze the governors of the nine provinces brought him as tribute. Each cauldron carried a province: its mountains, its rivers and its strange beasts, so that travellers would know what they met on the road."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "The Court came for them a thousand years ago, carried them up to the sky and recut the engravings into squares. When the Court fell, the cauldrons fell too. All nine, smashed and scattered over Mount Kuaiji like seed."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "I can recast them; my clan kept the moulds. But I need the old engravings, and the only true ones left are on the shards. Bring me four, and I will show you the land the way Yu drew it."],
	],
	"s_ts_return": [
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "Four shards. Look at the curve of this river, and this coast. No ruler ever touched it. ...It matches. It matches Pei's new map, line for line."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "The land is not breaking, Sovereign. It is remembering. Yu's cauldrons were never a cage; they were a portrait. The Court made a cage of them. Now the cage is broken and the land is going back to looking like itself."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then why is the water rising?"],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "Because there is somebody who never wanted the land to have any shape at all. Gonggong. When he lost the war for heaven he ran his head into Mount Buzhou, the pillar of the north-west, and the sky tilted and every river ran east. Yu chained his flood in channels. With the cauldrons broken, Gonggong is pulling the water back towards Buzhou, to drown the map before it can settle."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "Hold out your sword. A caster's blessing: a blade that remembers the weight of nine provinces. Then help me clear the mountain, and I will cast the Ninth Cauldron again, the true way."],
		["Narrator", "A new technique settles into Lin Feng's hands, heavy as bronze and steady as a mountain: the Nine Cauldrons Sword."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "The pits have woken. There is a slag serpent in the casting pits, the Stone Mother has come down off her hill, the giant Fangfeng, who came late to Yu's assembly four thousand years ago, is still hurrying to get here, and the Taotie, the glutton cast on every cauldron as a warning, has come alive in the casting yard and is eating the engravings off the shards."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "Oh, and the white nine-tailed fox of Tushan has come down off the hill to see what all the noise is. She is very old, very polite, and she bites."],
	],
	"pre:slag_serpent": [
		["Narrator", "Out of the casting pit rears a serpent of molten bronze and slag, dripping fire, gnawing on a half-cast bell."],
		["The Slag Serpent of the Casting Pits", "Hot! Hot! Everything is hot and soft and lovely! Why would anyone let it cool into a shape?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Because a bell has to hold its shape to ring."],
	],
	"post:slag_serpent": [["Narrator", "The Slag Serpent cools in the air, slows, and sets into a long bronze bell-rope of a thing, which Tu Lan's apprentices immediately start arguing about how to use."]],
	"pre:stone_mother": [
		["Narrator", "On the hill above Tushan stands a woman of grey stone, one hand raised to shade her eyes as if she is watching a road."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "The Tushan girl. Yu's wife. She waited on that hill while he dug the rivers, and when she saw him digging in the shape of a bear she was so frightened she turned to stone. Their son was born from the stone. Since the cauldrons broke, she has been walking."],
		["The Stone Mother of Tushan", "Have you seen him? A man with a hoe and a limp, who never comes home? He said he would pass the door three times and not come in. He has passed it four thousand times."],
		["Lin Feng", "He finished his work long ago."],
		["The Stone Mother of Tushan", "Then why is the water rising?"],
	],
	"post:stone_mother": [["Narrator", "The Stone Mother sits down on her hill and is still. Then I will wait for the water to go down, she says, the way I always waited. A little moss begins to grow on her shoulder, which seems to comfort her."]],
	"pre:fangfeng": [
		["Narrator", "A giant three spans tall comes striding down the mountain road with his robe flapping behind him, shouting."],
		["Fangfeng, the Giant Who Came Late", "Am I late? AM I LATE? Yu called every lord in the land to the assembly at Kuaiji, and I was late, and they cut off my head! Four thousand years I have been hurrying to get here!"],
		["Yue Ling", "The assembly ended four thousand years ago."],
		["Fangfeng, the Giant Who Came Late", "Then I am VERY late!"],
	],
	"post:fangfeng": [
		["Fangfeng, the Giant Who Came Late", "...They executed me for being late. Is the Court so very strict about time?"],
		["Lin Feng", "There is no Court any more. You can be as late as you like."],
		["Fangfeng, the Giant Who Came Late", "...As late as I like."],
		["Narrator", "The giant lies down on his back on the mountainside and watches the clouds go by, for the first time in four thousand years not hurrying anywhere at all."],
	],
	"pre:tushan_fox": [
		["Narrator", "Through the pines above the casting yard pads a white fox with nine tails, each one tipped with fire, looking back over its shoulder as if it wants to be followed."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "The white fox of Tushan! When Yu came this way at thirty, unmarried and still digging, a white fox with nine tails crossed his path, and the old song says: whoever sees the white fox will be king, and whoever marries in Tushan will prosper. He married my clan's daughter that same year. The fox only comes when something is about to change."],
		["The White Nine-Tailed Fox of Tushan", "Something is about to change. I came to see whether you are the one changing it. Catch me, if you are."],
	],
	"post:tushan_fox": [["Narrator", "The fox lets itself be caught, sniffs Lin Feng's hand, then Yue Ling's, and sits down between them with its nine tails curled round all three. It looks smug. Then it is gone, and nine little flames hang in the air over the casting yard for a moment before they go out."]],
	"pre:taotie": [
		["Narrator", "In the casting yard at the top of the mountain, a monster that is almost all face has climbed out of the bronze: two great eyes, a curling snout, horns, a mouth that goes on and on, and no body at all. It is licking the engravings off the cauldron shards."],
		["Taotie, the Glutton on the Cauldron", "Mountains! Rivers! Delicious! Nine provinces, and I have eaten eight! When I have eaten the ninth there will be no map at all. Only me!"],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "The taotie was cast on the cauldrons as a warning against greed. It was meant to be a picture, not a guest!"],
	],
	"post:taotie": [
		["Narrator", "The Taotie shrinks back into the bronze until it is only a curling pattern on a shard again, its eyes rolling sullenly. Tu Lan pours the shards into her crucible, and by evening the Ninth Cauldron stands in the yard once more. On its belly, in raised bronze, is the land: every bay and every bend of it."],
		["Tu Lan, Last of the Cauldron-Casters", "The ninth of nine. Take this rubbing of it to Mount Buzhou and show it to Gonggong. He has never seen the land drawn the way it really is. Perhaps nobody ever showed him."],
	],
	"s_bz_intro": [
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "A traveller at Buzhou. I have not had a visitor since Yu, and he only came to borrow my tail."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "I am Yinglong. When Yu dug the rivers, I flew ahead of him and dragged my tail through the mud to show him where they should go. Every river you have ever crossed, I drew first. Now look at them. They are all running backwards, uphill, into this black lake."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "Gonggong is beneath it. Long ago he fought Zhuanxu for the throne of heaven and lost, and in his rage he ran his head into Mount Buzhou, the pillar of the north-west. The pillar broke. The sky tilted towards the north-west and the earth towards the south-east, and that is why every river runs east to the sea."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "Now he is pulling the water back. If he fills the lake high enough he can lift the broken pillar on the flood and ram it again, and this time there is no Nuwa to mend what breaks. The stones of the old pillar are scattered round the lake. Bring me four, and we will see how badly it is cracked."],
	],
	"s_bz_return": [
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "Four stones of Buzhou. Black, and wet, and every one of them cracked along the same line. He is close."],
		["Lin Feng", "Tu Lan cast the Ninth Cauldron again. This is a rubbing of it: the land, as it really is."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "...My rivers. Every one of them, where I drew them. Oh, Sovereign. Show him this. Gonggong has only ever seen the land as a thing to drown or a thing to rule. Nobody ever showed him that it was beautiful."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "But first: his son Xiu, who loved roads, has come back to wander the shore. The Kun, the great fish of the northern darkness, has swum up into the lake. And Xiangliu, his minister with nine heads, has been poisoning every spring round Buzhou, so that nothing can grow where the water has been. Nine heads, Sovereign, and each one eats from a different mountain."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "And Kuafu, who chased the sun and drank the rivers dry, is lying by the lake, thirsty again."],
	],
	"pre:xiu": [
		["Narrator", "A young man in a dusty travelling cloak walks the shore of the black lake in circles, a pack on his back and a map in his hand that is mostly holes."],
		["Xiu, Gonggong's Son Who Loved Roads", "Which way is the road? There used to be a road. There was always a road. I walked them all when I was alive, every one, and when I died they made me the god of them. And now they have all moved!"],
		["Lin Feng", "They have not moved. They have only stopped being straight."],
	],
	"post:xiu": [["Narrator", "Lin Feng gives Xiu Cartographer Pei's map of the north. Xiu sits down on a rock to read it, laughs, and traces the bends with his finger. Oh, that is much better, he says. You cannot get lost on a road that goes where the land goes. He sets off south, whistling, and travellers still leave him a cup of wine at his shrine in Cloudfoot."]],
	"pre:kun": [
		["Narrator", "The black lake heaves, and a fish rises out of it so large that its back is an island with trees on it: the Kun of the Northern Darkness. When it is ready, the old books say, it becomes the Peng, a bird whose wings are clouds hung across the sky."],
		["Kun of the Northern Darkness", "...Is it time? Is it time to become a bird? The water is so high. I thought this was the sea."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is not the sea. It is a flood. You will know the sea when you get there."],
	],
	"post:kun": [["Narrator", "The Kun sinks, rolls once, and swims away down the river towards the east and the real sea. Weeks later, fishermen at the Starfall Ferry will see a bird whose wings cover the whole sky, and nobody will believe them."]],
	"pre:xiangliu": [
		["Narrator", "Nine heads rise out of the poisoned springs round the lake, each on its own long serpent neck, each with a human face, and all nine of them talk at once."],
		["Xiangliu, the Nine-Headed Minister", "We are Xiangliu, minister to Gonggong. We eat from nine mountains. Wherever we spit, the water turns bitter and nothing grows. Yu killed us once. Our blood was so foul that he dug the ground three times to bury it, and three times it sank."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let us not spill any."],
		["Xiangliu, the Nine-Headed Minister", "...That is not how this usually goes."],
	],
	"post:xiangliu": [["Narrator", "Lin Feng and Yue Ling tie the nine necks into a knot so complicated that Xiangliu spends the rest of the day untying itself, arguing with itself about which head goes under which. Meanwhile the springs round the lake run clear again."]],
	"pre:kuafu": [
		["Narrator", "A giant with a staff of peach-wood kneels at the edge of the black lake, drinking it. Each swallow lowers the water by a hand's breadth, and each time, the rivers pour more in."],
		["Kuafu, Who Drank the Rivers Dry", "I chased the sun across the world once, and I grew so thirsty that I drank the Yellow River dry, and the Wei after it, and died on the way to the northern lake. My staff grew into a peach forest where I fell. Now there is so much water. I thought, at last, I can drink until I am not thirsty."],
		["Yue Ling", "If you drink the lake, the rivers will keep pouring into you for ever."],
		["Kuafu, Who Drank the Rivers Dry", "Then I will never be thirsty again. Is that not what everyone wants?"],
	],
	"post:kuafu": [["Narrator", "Kuafu sits back on his heels, water running down his beard, and laughs for the first time in a very long time. He plants his peach-wood staff by the lake, and by the time the flood has gone down there are blossoms on it."]],
	"pre:gonggong": [
		["Narrator", "The black lake rises in a single wave, and on its crest stands Gonggong: a man's body on a serpent's tail, hair like red fire, eyes like a storm over the sea. Behind him the stump of Mount Buzhou lifts out of the water, carried on the flood."],
		["Gonggong, God of the Flood", "The map is broken. Good. Let it all wash away: squares, circles, bays, I do not care. I lost heaven because somebody drew a line and put me on the wrong side of it. Water does not stay on any side of any line."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng unrolls the rubbing of the Ninth Cauldron."],
		["Lin Feng", "This is the land as it really is. No squares. No lines but the ones the water drew."],
		["Gonggong, God of the Flood", "...The rivers are on it."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "Every one. Where we drew them. Where you made them run, the day you broke the pillar."],
		["Gonggong, God of the Flood", "...I made them run east?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Every river in the world runs east because of you. The whole map is your shape."],
		["Gonggong, God of the Flood", "Then let me see whether it can hold me!"],
	],
	"post:gonggong": [
		["Narrator", "Gonggong crashes down into his lake, and the wave goes out of him, and the lake goes still. He looks at the rubbing for a long time."],
		["Gonggong, God of the Flood", "Every river runs east because I broke a mountain in a rage. And they made a land of it. People built ferries on it. Fed carp. Named their children after it."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "That is what happens to rage, if somebody digs it a channel."],
		["Gonggong, God of the Flood", "...Then let the water go where it goes. I will not pull it back. Somebody should keep an eye on the north-west, though. It tilts."],
		["Narrator", "The flood runs back down every river at once, east, to the sea, the way it always has. The stump of Mount Buzhou settles into the lake, and Gonggong curls round it and goes to sleep."],
	],
	"pre:s_break15": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation that gathers over Buzhou is not lightning. It is the land itself: every mountain and river of the Nine Provinces rising round Lin Feng like the engravings on a cauldron, asking to be held in its own shape."],
		["Yue Ling", "What do you do with a map that wants to move?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Nothing. You let it be the land. You only draw it."],
	],
	"post:s_break15": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng takes the tribulation into himself the way a river takes the rain, without holding on to it, and becomes the first cultivator of Mountain-River Oneness: a realm in which the land is allowed to be its own shape."],
		["Narrator", "At Tushan, Tu Lan begins casting the other eight cauldrons, and on each one she engraves the land as it really is. At Cloudfoot, Cartographer Pei redraws the world with nothing square in it but the frame, and sends one copy to the Library and another to Longmen, where Harbourmistress Jiang pins it to the flood stele with a note: told you so, from both of us."],
		["Archivist Lan", "The Unbroken Land. That is what I am calling it. Do not argue."],
		["Narrator", "And on Azure Cloud Mountain, in the cool of the evening, Lin Feng walks the new north road down to the river and back again. It bends, and he follows it."],
	],
	# ---- Part XI: The Ladder of Heaven
	"recall9": [
		["Narrator", "The land keeps its own shape, and for a season everybody lets it. Pei's new map goes up in the Library, Harbourmistress Jiang writes rude letters to it, and Lin Feng sweeps the steps in the mornings and walks the new north road in the evenings."],
		["Narrator", "Then, one morning before dawn, the whole mountain shudders. When the sun comes up there is a tree west of the sect that was not there the night before: a trunk wider than the Hall, bark like old bronze, not one branch below the clouds, and roots that have split the hillside open."],
		["Old Shen the Bell-Ringer", "I rang the bell at it. No echo. I shouted at it. No echo. And at noon it casts no shadow. Elder, I have rung bells for forty years, and I do not like a tree that will not echo."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Nor should you, Shen. Lin Feng, come to the hall. I know that tree. Everybody did, once, and then we were made to forget it."],
	],
	"s_p11_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "That is the Jianmu, the Standing Tree. Before Yu, in the days of the Yellow Emperor, it stood at the middle of the world, and it was a ladder. Gods climbed down it to visit, and people climbed up it to complain."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then came Zhuanxu, the Yellow Emperor's grandson, who thought heaven and earth had grown too familiar. He sent his grandsons: Chong to lift heaven up and Li to press the earth down, and between them they cut the ladder through. The old books call it the severing of heaven and earth."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the Court made a law of it."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Court made a whole government of it. One ladder, kept by the Court. One road up it: obedience. The peoples of the heavens were cut off above, the Feathered Folk, the dragon-kin, the celestial foxes; and the peoples under the roots below, the Pearl-Weepers, the Deathless, the shades of Youdu."],
		["Yue Ling", "And now the land has its own shape again, so the tree remembered how to grow."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Just so. Chong is still holding heaven up and Li is still pressing the earth down, four thousand years on, and the old books say they are very tired. Whatever comes down that tree, or up through its roots, will ask you one thing first: what is your Dao?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Court allowed one road. There are many: the sword, free wandering, the heart, formations, the cauldron, the mirror. Every people walks one, and none of them is wrong. Choose the road you will walk for now. You may change roads at any Dao Stele. That is the whole point of roads."],
	],
	"s_p11_archive": [
		["Archivist Lan", "Down here, Lin Feng. Mind the third step. This is the Sealed Archive: everything the Court ordered the sect to forget, which we kept, because an archivist who burns books is just a cook with bad taste."],
		["Archivist Lan", "Here: the record of the severing. Chong took heaven, Li took the earth, the ladder was cut at the middle, and the peoples above and below were told the people of the middle were cruel. The people of the middle were told the same of them. Nobody could climb up or down to check."],
		["Lin Feng", "I have been up into the sky before, though. The Court's stair, the Sea of Clouds, the sun's own road."],
		["Archivist Lan", "The Court kept one stair, up to its own door and no further, and only its clerks and its harvest went up it. The Level Heaven is above the Court's heaven, and its gate is not the one you opened on the north coast."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then the first people down the tree will be expecting monsters."],
		["Archivist Lan", "And they will meet you, which will be a great surprise to them. Something came down the trunk at dawn. Old Shen says it had wings and was very rude. Go and be polite at it. The tree stands on the open ground west of the sect."],
	],
	"s_p11_tree": [
		["Ling Yu of the Feathered Folk", "Stay back, ground-walker. ...Oh. You are the one. You smell of tribulation smoke and broom straw. The wind told us somebody down here let the land go, and the next thing we knew there was a ladder."],
		["Ling Yu of the Feathered Folk", "I am Ling Yu of the Yumin, the Feathered Folk. We hatch from eggs, we can fly, though not far, and we nest round the crown of this tree in the Level Heaven. I came down to see if the middle world was as horrible as the stories. You are much shorter than the stories."],
		["Lin Feng", "What is it like up there, now the Court is gone?"],
		["Ling Yu of the Feathered Folk", "Loud. The winds do what they like, the dragon-kin will not rain without a ledger, the Thunder Duke strikes at everything that moves, and our eggs will not hatch in the noise. The Feather-Mother sent me down to find help. I suppose you will do."],
		["Ling Yu of the Feathered Folk", "There is a stair round the trunk, for those without wings. Climb. I will race you to the top, and I will win."],
	],
	"s_jt_intro": [
		["Feather-Mother Yu Hua", "Ling Yu says you climbed the whole Jianmu without wings and only stopped to be sick twice. Welcome to Featherfall, ground-walker, and to Juntian, the Level Heaven: the middle field of the Nine Heavens."],
		["Feather-Mother Yu Hua", "Every Yumin hatches in the warm cellar under the Egg-Nursery, and the first thing a chick hears is the wind bells on the aeries telling it the wind is safe. Since the Court fell the winds have run wild and torn the bells from every tower, and the chicks have stopped hatching."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I will bring the bells back."],
		["Feather-Mother Yu Hua", "Four of them have fallen among the islands, the big bronze ones from the top of this tower. Bring them home, and clear the loose thunder and the cloud-kites from the islands, so the chicks can hear something besides storms."],
	],
	"s_jt_return": [
		["Feather-Mother Yu Hua", "Four bells. Listen to them... they still ring true. Hang them, Ling Yu. ...And listen to that. The first egg is cracking, down in the cellar."],
		["Feather-Mother Yu Hua", "But hear what the bells say when the wind blows from the east: rain that will not fall, wind that will not settle, thunder that will not stop. The storm is not wild, ground-walker. It is unmanaged. The Rain Ministry has stopped working."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then we ask the Ministry why."],
		["Feather-Mother Yu Hua", "The Rain-Minister sits in the archive under the Ministry hall and will not come up. Dragons sulk magnificently. Go down and ask him. He will not listen to birds."],
	],
	"s_jt_minister": [
		["Rain-Minister Ao Run", "A mortal, in the Archive of Rain. Shut the door. Every cloud in heaven is entered in these ledgers: where it will rain, how much and on whom. For ten thousand years the Court signed every page. Now nobody signs them."],
		["Rain-Minister Ao Run", "So I do not rain. Rain without a signature is not rain, it is only water falling about. And with no rain to carry, Feilian lets the winds out of his bag for fun, and the thunder has nothing to follow, so Kui and the Duke strike whatever they please."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then sign it yourself."],
		["Rain-Minister Ao Run", "...Myself. A dragon, signing his own rain, with no ledger to answer to. ...Ao Qing, fetch me a brush. No, a big one."],
		["Rain-Minister Ao Run", "But catch the winds first, or the rain will only blow sideways. Feilian keeps his bag of winds on the southern islands, Kui stamps in the east, Jiufeng's nine heads cannot agree which way the wind is blowing, and Leigong, Duke of Thunder, sits at the eastern edge of Juntian striking at everything."],
	],
	"pre:feilian": [
		["Narrator", "On the southern islands a creature with a deer's body, a bird's head and a snake's tail lounges on a bulging leather bag, letting out little gusts that knock the cloud-kites over."],
		["Feilian, Earl of the Wind", "The Court used to tell me when to blow and how hard. Now nobody tells me anything. Do you know how much fun it is to blow whenever you like?"],
		["Yue Ling", "The Feathered Folk's eggs will not hatch."],
		["Feilian, Earl of the Wind", "Eggs! Who thinks about eggs? Take the bag from me, then, if you can."],
	],
	"post:feilian": [["Narrator", "Lin Feng ties the neck of the bag of winds shut. Feilian sulks, then grins: the Rain-Minister will need winds to carry his rain, he says, and he can blow on purpose, if somebody asks nicely. Ling Yu asks, very nicely, and the wind over Featherfall drops to a breeze."]],
	"pre:kui": [
		["Narrator", "In the east of Juntian a grey ox with one leg and no horns hops from island to island. Wherever it lands there is lightning, and when it bellows it sounds like thunder rolling round the sky."],
		["Lin Feng", "Kui. The Yellow Emperor made a drum from your hide once."],
		["Kui, the One-Legged Thunder Ox", "And they beat it, and it was heard for five hundred li, and the whole world trembled. Every time I hear thunder I think somebody is beating me again. So I bellow first."],
	],
	"post:kui": [["Narrator", "Kui stops hopping, stands very still on its one leg, and listens. There is no drum. There has not been a drum for four thousand years. It lies down in the clouds with a long sigh like distant thunder, and the eastern islands go quiet."]],
	"pre:jiufeng": [
		["Narrator", "On the northern crags sits a vast bird with nine human heads, on a nest of cloud. Every head is arguing with every other head about which way the wind is blowing."],
		["Jiufeng, the Nine-Headed Phoenix", "North! South! It is not blowing! It is blowing in a circle! Who are you? Nobody asked you! Everybody, attack him! No, everybody attack HER!"],
		["Yue Ling", "Nine heads, and not one of them listening."],
	],
	"post:jiufeng": [["Narrator", "Out of breath, the nine heads fall silent one by one, until only the smallest says, quite calmly, that the wind is blowing from the west now, and it smells of rain. The other eight look at it, and for the first time in a long while, they agree."]],
	"pre:leigong": [
		["Narrator", "At the eastern edge of the Level Heaven, on a ring of drums, sits a blue-skinned god with a bird's face and wings, a chisel in one hand and a mallet in the other. Every few heartbeats he strikes a drum, and lightning splits a cloud somewhere far below."],
		["Leigong, Duke of Thunder", "The Court gave me a list: the wicked, the oath-breakers, the ones who wasted rice. I struck whoever was on the list. Then the list stopped coming. So now I strike everybody, to be fair."],
		["Lin Feng", "That is not fair. That is just loud."],
		["Leigong, Duke of Thunder", "Then you write me a list."],
	],
	"post:leigong": [
		["Leigong, Duke of Thunder", "...No list. Nobody to strike. What is a thunder god for, with nobody to strike?"],
		["Rain-Minister Ao Run", "For rain, Duke. Rain needs thunder ahead of it, to tell the farmers to bring in the washing. I have signed my first page. Will you come and drum for it?"],
		["Narrator", "Leigong lowers his mallet. Then he strikes one drum, gently, and far below over the Unbroken Land the first rain of the new heaven begins to fall, signed by nobody but the dragon who decided it. In Featherfall, between the thunder and the bells, a whole cellar of eggs begins to hatch."],
	],
	"s_cg_intro": [
		["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "Oh, look, a hero. On our terrace. With his sword out. Put it away, darling; nobody up here will fight you before tea. Probably."],
		["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "We are the Tianhu, the celestial foxes. Each of us spent a thousand years growing nine tails to climb up here, and when we arrived we found the Gate of Heaven with nobody left to keep it. A gate with no gatekeeper is a joke waiting for a punchline. So we keep it."],
		["Lin Feng", "I need to go through it."],
		["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "Of course you do. Behind the Gate stands Chong, holding heaven away from the earth, as he has since the ladder was cut. Nuwa's pillars bear its weight, darling; Chong only keeps it out of reach, and the strain has pressed his ears shut. He hears nobody. And we hid the four keys of the Gate, as a joke. You will have to find them. That is the punchline."],
		["Yue Ling", "Hilarious."],
	],
	"s_cg_return": [
		["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "All four keys! You found the one we put in the lion's mouth? We never thought anybody would look there. Very well: the Gate is yours."],
		["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "But listen, little hero, because this part is not a joke. When Chong lifted heaven he swore to hold it until somebody told him it was safe to stop. Nobody ever has. If you go to him, you had better be ready to tell him."],
		["Lin Feng", "Is it safe?"],
		["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "That is what you are going to find out. The door gods still stand before the Gate, and Pixiu has been eating its gold. Mind your purse."],
	],
	"pre:pixiu": [
		["Narrator", "On the gold-roofed island a winged lion with one horn is gnawing the gilded tiles off the gatehouse roof, swallowing them, and looking for more."],
		["Pixiu, Who Ate the Gold of the Gate", "Gold goes in and nothing ever comes out. That is my whole nature. The Court fed me tribute; now I feed myself."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then you are about to have a very bad day."],
	],
	"post:pixiu": [["Narrator", "Pixiu lies down on the bare roof beams looking sorry for itself. It cannot give the gold back, it explains; it never could. But it will guard what is left, if somebody scratches it behind the horn now and then. Old Hu the Gate-Fox says he will see to it."]],
	"pre:shentu": [
		["Narrator", "Under a peach tree before the Gate of Heaven stands a red-faced giant in armour, a rope of reeds coiled at his belt and a tiger crouched at his feet."],
		["Shentu, Door God of the Peach Tree", "I am Shentu. With my brother Yulei I stand under the peach tree and bind every evil spirit that tries to pass, and feed it to the tiger. Are you an evil spirit?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I am a sweeper."],
		["Shentu, Door God of the Peach Tree", "That is exactly what an evil spirit would say."],
	],
	"post:shentu": [["Narrator", "Shentu looks at Lin Feng for a long moment, then at the tiger, which has gone to sleep. Not evil, he says. Only stubborn. He steps aside, and goes to tell his brother."]],
	"pre:yulei": [
		["Narrator", "On the far side of the peach tree stands Shentu's brother, green-faced, holding the other end of the rope of reeds."],
		["Yulei, Door God of the Reed Rope", "My brother is too soft. Every door needs two keepers, one to open it and one to shut it. I am the one who shuts."],
		["Yue Ling", "Doors are meant to open sometimes."],
	],
	"post:yulei": [["Narrator", "Yulei coils his rope and hangs it on the peach tree. A door that opens is still a door, he admits. He pushes, and for the first time in four thousand years the Gate of Heaven swings open, and beyond it, in a pillar of cloud, somebody is holding up the sky."]],
	"pre:chong": [
		["Narrator", "Beyond the Gate stands a giant whose head is lost in the sky. His arms are raised, his palms flat against heaven itself, and his whole body shakes with the weight of it."],
		["Chong, Who Lifts Heaven", "WHO? I cannot hear you. I have not heard anything for four thousand years. My grandfather told me: lift heaven, and hold it, until it is safe to let go. Is it safe? I CANNOT HEAR YOU."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I will have to show you."],
	],
	"post:chong": [
		["Chong, Who Lifts Heaven", "...I can hear. The wind. A bird. You. Why can I hear?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Because you put your hands down to fight me. And heaven did not fall."],
		["Chong, Who Lifts Heaven", "...It did not fall. The tree. The Jianmu is holding it. My grandfather cut the ladder so heaven would not lean on the earth. But a ladder does not lean. A ladder holds hands."],
		["Narrator", "Chong gives Lin Feng the jade tablet he held heaven up with, still warm after four thousand years. My brother Li presses the earth down at the bottom of the world, he says, and he will not be able to hear either. Go down the roots. Tell him what you told me."],
	],
	"s_ns_intro": [
		["Narrator", "Down the roots of the Jianmu, through the dark, past glowing moss and the sound of water, the roots open into a cavern country under the land. On the shore of a black lake stands a town of shell and silk: Tearwater."],
		["Pearl-Weaver Shui Lian", "A living man from above, in Tearwater. You climbed down the roots? Then you came the way the stories said the monsters would come. You do not look like a monster. You look tired."],
		["Pearl-Weaver Shui Lian", "I am Shui Lian of the Jiaoren, the Pearl-Weepers. When we weep, our tears are pearls; we weave a silk the water cannot wet; and we live here by the black water, where the roots of the world tree drink from the Nine Springs."],
		["Pearl-Weaver Shui Lian", "The springs are going wrong. The tree drinks and drinks, it has grown back so fast, and the springs run thin and bitter, and our children's tears come out as grit. Fill me four vials at the springs among the roots, and I will tell you what the water says."],
	],
	"s_ns_return": [
		["Pearl-Weaver Shui Lian", "Four springs. Taste: bitter, and cold, and something under it like iron. The roots are not only drinking. Something is gnawing them. And the water is being pushed down, as if a great hand were pressing on the whole cavern from below."],
		["Lin Feng", "Li."],
		["Pearl-Weaver Shui Lian", "The one who presses the earth down, at the bottom of everything, under Youdu. The Deathless will know more; they have tended the roots since the ladder was cut. Elder Bu Si lives in the loft of the house under the great root. Go up the stairs, and speak slowly. He has all the time in the world."],
	],
	"s_ns_elder": [
		["Elder Bu Si of the Deathless", "Sit. Sit. I have been waiting for you for four thousand years. Not you, exactly. Somebody. You will do."],
		["Elder Bu Si of the Deathless", "We are the Deathless. We eat the fruit of the immortal tree that grows on the roots of the Jianmu, we drink from the red spring, and we do not die. It sounds pleasant. It is mostly a great deal of waiting."],
		["Elder Bu Si of the Deathless", "When the ladder was cut, Li went down under Youdu to press the earth flat, and he has pressed ever since. Now the tree pushes up and Li pushes down, and the roots are caught between them like a hand in a door, and the creatures of the deep are gnawing at whatever is caught."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I will go and ask Li to stop."],
		["Elder Bu Si of the Deathless", "Good. But first: a wyrm is gnawing the great root, a goblin is muddying the springs, the Pearl-Mother weeps so hard she is drowning the shore, and the Torch Dragon has shut his eyes, and without them there is no day down here at all. Li has waited four thousand years. He can wait an afternoon."],
	],
	"pre:root_wyrm": [
		["Narrator", "In the dark between the roots a long pale wyrm is coiled round the greatest root of the Jianmu, gnawing at it, and with every bite the whole cavern shivers."],
		["The Wyrm Who Gnaws the Jianmu", "The tree is in my sky. For four thousand years there was no tree, only dark, and the dark was mine. Now there is a root through the middle of it."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then the dark will have to share."],
	],
	"post:root_wyrm": [["Narrator", "The wyrm uncoils and slides away into a crack between two roots. It is cold, it complains, and the root is warm. Chang Sheng the grove-keeper says it may sleep against the root if it stops biting it, and after a while, it does."]],
	"pre:pearl_mother": [
		["Narrator", "Over the black lake drifts a great pale shape like a jellyfish the size of a house, weeping. Every tear is a pearl as big as a fist, and they are piling up so fast they are pushing the water over the shore."],
		["The Weeping Pearl-Mother", "The children's tears come out as grit. So I weep for them. I weep for all of them. Somebody has to."],
		["Lin Feng", "If you drown Tearwater, there will be no children left to weep for."],
	],
	"post:pearl_mother": [["Narrator", "The Pearl-Mother stops weeping long enough to hear Little Bei singing on the shore, and the song is not sad. She drifts back out over the black water, and the pearls she wept settle on the lake bed and glow there, so that Tearwater has a little light of its own."]],
	"pre:wangxiang": [
		["Narrator", "Among the springs a goblin with red eyes, long ears and black claws is splashing mud into every pool, giggling."],
		["Wangxiang, Goblin of the Springs", "Clear water is boring! You can see the bottom! Mud is a mystery! Everybody likes a mystery!"],
		["Yue Ling", "The Pearl-Weepers have to drink it."],
	],
	"post:wangxiang": [["Narrator", "Wangxiang agrees, grudgingly, to keep his mud to one pool, his own, where anybody who likes a mystery can come and look. Hai Ge the Tide-Singer writes a song about it at once, which Wangxiang pretends not to like."]],
	"pre:zhulong": [
		["Narrator", "At the eastern end of the cavern lies a red dragon a thousand li long, with a man's face and his eyes shut. When Zhulong opens his eyes it is day in the deep; when he shuts them it is night. They have been shut for a month."],
		["Zhulong, the Torch Dragon", "Why should I open them? The tree came through the roof of my world without asking. Everything I lit for four thousand years is lit by somebody else's tree now."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Pearl-Weepers have been weeping in the dark."],
		["Zhulong, the Torch Dragon", "Then let them weep. Come closer, and I will open one eye, just for you."],
	],
	"post:zhulong": [["Narrator", "Zhulong opens both eyes, and red daylight floods the Nine Springs, and all along the black water the Pearl-Weepers come out of their houses and look up. The tree did not take your light, Lin Feng tells him; it brought you visitors. The Torch Dragon looks at the crowd on the shore and, for the first time in four thousand years, has the grace to look embarrassed."]],
	"s_yd_intro": [
		["Narrator", "Past the Torch Dragon the cavern drops into a great hollow, and in it lies a city of grey walls and paper lanterns that have never once gone out: Youdu, the Dark Capital, where the shades live who are not yet ready to be reborn."],
		["Lantern-Magistrate Ming Yi", "A living man in the Dark Capital, climbing my stairs. You are not on any roll. Sit down; you are making the lanterns nervous."],
		["Lantern-Magistrate Ming Yi", "I am Ming Yi, magistrate of the Ward Office. Every shade in Youdu is on a roll, on a bamboo tally: who they were, when they came, when they will be ready to go up to the Wheel again. Order, Lin Feng. The dead like order. It is the one thing they can still have."],
		["Lantern-Magistrate Ming Yi", "Since the tree came down through our roof the earth has been heaving and the rolls have scattered. Four tallies are lost in the streets, and four shades do not know who they are. Find them, clear the runaway spirits out of my streets, and then we will talk about why you are here."],
	],
	"s_yd_return": [
		["Lantern-Magistrate Ming Yi", "Four tallies: a ferryman, a weaver, a soldier, and a girl called Lan who has been asking everybody in the street who she is. Thank you. Order is restored, in this room at least."],
		["Lantern-Magistrate Ming Yi", "Now, the heaving. It comes from under the city, from the deep beyond the inner gate, where Li presses the earth down. For four thousand years he pressed steadily. Since the tree came he presses harder, because the tree pushes back. A little harder still and he will crack the floor of the world."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then we go through the inner gate."],
		["Lantern-Magistrate Ming Yi", "Not before you go down to the deep hall under the Palace of Sovereign Earth. Houtu keeps the dead, and she will want to see you. Everybody in this city does what Houtu wants. She wants very little, which is why we all do it."],
	],
	"s_yd_houtu": [
		["Houtu, Sovereign Earth", "Come here, child. Let me look at you. You are the one who closed the Ledger, and then turned the Wheel and let the dead go home. I remember every soul you sent me. They all said the same thing: tell him thank you."],
		["Houtu, Sovereign Earth", "I am Houtu. I am the earth everybody walks on and everybody is buried in. Li presses on me. He has pressed for four thousand years, because his grandfather told him the earth must be kept down, away from heaven. I have never once complained. I should have."],
		["Lin Feng", "Why didn't you?"],
		["Houtu, Sovereign Earth", "Because a mother does not complain about her children. Li is a child of the earth, as you are. Go to him. And take the earth's own blessing on your sword, so that whatever you strike, you strike with the weight of everything that was ever buried kindly."],
		["Narrator", "A new technique settles into Lin Feng's hands, as heavy and as gentle as the earth: the Three Realms Concord Sword."],
		["Houtu, Sovereign Earth", "White and Black Impermanence have been collecting the living by mistake since the rolls scattered, and Tubo guards the inner gate with three eyes and bad manners. Be kind to all of them. They are only doing their jobs, badly."],
	],
	"pre:bai_wuchang": [
		["Narrator", "Through the lantern-lit streets comes a tall pale figure in white, with a long tongue and a tall white hat that reads: one glance and you will be rich. On the end of his chain is a very confused, very alive fishmonger."],
		["Bai Wuchang, White Impermanence", "On the roll, off the roll, the rolls have scattered, so I collect whoever I meet. It is easier. Hold still."],
		["Lin Feng", "That man is alive."],
		["Bai Wuchang, White Impermanence", "Details."],
	],
	"post:bai_wuchang": [["Narrator", "White Impermanence unlocks the fishmonger, who runs all the way back up the roots without stopping. Bai Wuchang tucks his chain away: he will check the rolls before he collects anybody else, now Magistrate Ming has them in order. His hat still promises riches. Nobody has ever taken it up on the offer."]],
	"pre:hei_wuchang": [
		["Narrator", "In the alley behind the inn waits White Impermanence's partner, short and black-robed, with a hat that reads: peace under heaven. His chain is wrapped round three living crab-fishers from Tearwater."],
		["Hei Wuchang, Black Impermanence", "My partner collects the ones who are owed. I collect the ones who are left over. Everyone is left over these days."],
		["Yue Ling", "Not these three."],
	],
	"post:hei_wuchang": [["Narrator", "Black Impermanence lets the crab-fishers go and sits down on the inn step with a sigh. Peace under heaven, his hat says. He has worn it so long that he forgot it was a wish."]],
	"pre:tubo": [
		["Narrator", "At the inner gate of Youdu crouches a monster with a tiger's head, three eyes, horns like knives and the body of a bull, coiled nine times round itself, licking its bloody thumbs."],
		["Tubo, Earl of the Dark Capital", "Nobody passes the inner gate. The living stay up there, the dead stay down here, and the gate stays shut. That was the order I was given when the ladder was cut, and nobody has given me another."],
		["Lin Feng", "Houtu sent me."],
		["Tubo, Earl of the Dark Capital", "...Did she. She never sends anybody. Prove it."],
	],
	"post:tubo": [["Narrator", "Tubo uncoils, one coil at a time, all nine of them, and opens the inner gate with a groan of old iron. Beyond it the cavern floor falls away into a deep red glow, and at the bottom of it a figure kneels with both hands pressed flat against the floor of the world."]],
	"pre:li": [
		["Narrator", "At the bottom of the world, in a pit of red-hot stone, kneels a giant of earth and fire with both palms pressed against the ground. The whole of the Nine Springs shudders each time he pushes."],
		["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "DOWN. Keep the earth down, away from heaven. My grandfather said it. My brother holds the sky up and I hold the earth down, and between us there is nothing, nothing, nothing at all."],
		["Lin Feng", "Your brother put his hands down. Heaven did not fall."],
		["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "LIES. If I lift my hands the earth will rise up and strike the sky. Come down here and make me."],
	],
	"post:li": [
		["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "...My hands. I lifted my hands. The earth did not rise."],
		["Lin Feng", "It is held. The tree holds heaven and earth apart and together at once. That is what a ladder does."],
		["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "...Four thousand years. And all it needed was a tree."],
		["Narrator", "Li gives Lin Feng the iron weight he pressed the earth down with, lies back on the warm stone of the world's floor and shuts his eyes, and for the first time since the ladder was cut the Nine Springs are still. Take my weight to the middle of the world, he says. Let the tree carry it. And tell my brother I said hello."],
	],
	"s_p11_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Chong's tablet and Li's weight. You went to the top of heaven and the bottom of the earth and brought back the two halves of an order four thousand years old. How do they feel?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Lighter than they should. Heavier than they look."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then come to the tree. All of us."],
		["Narrator", "They are waiting at the foot of the Jianmu: Ling Yu and the Feather-Mother, Rain-Minister Ao Run with a brush behind his ear, Hu Qingyi and three of her foxes, Shui Lian with Little Bei on her shoulders, old Bu Si, Magistrate Ming with his lantern, and Houtu herself, plainly dressed, standing a little apart."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The ladder was cut so that the realms would not mix, and the Court kept it cut so that every road would run through the Court. The tree will carry the tablet and the weight, Lin Feng. But somebody must stand at its middle, once, and hold all three realms in one breath, so that it knows it is allowed."],
		["Yue Ling", "And that somebody is you. Obviously."],
	],
	"pre:s_break16": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng sets Chong's tablet in a fork of the Jianmu's bark and Li's weight between its roots. The tribulation that gathers is three storms at once: wind and thunder from the Nine Heavens, cold water and red stone from the Nine Springs, and from the middle world, nothing but the sound of people waiting."],
		["Yue Ling", "Which way does it come from?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Every way. That is the point of a ladder."],
	],
	"post:s_break16": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng holds heaven above him, the earth below him and the middle world in his two hands, the way you hold a ladder steady for somebody else to climb, and becomes the first cultivator of Three Realms Concord: a realm in which every road goes somewhere and every realm can be visited."],
		["Narrator", "Up the trunk go the farmers of Qingshi, to see the clouds from above; down the roots go the Feathered Folk, to see the Torch Dragon's day; and foxes and Pearl-Weepers cross the middle world to drink Old Wu's tea at Cloudfoot and complain about it. Chong and Li meet halfway up the Jianmu, and shake hands, and say nothing for a very long time."],
		["Ling Yu of the Feathered Folk", "You climbed it without wings. I still won the race."],
		["Narrator", "And on Azure Cloud Mountain, in the cool of the evening, Lin Feng sweeps the steps of the sect, and then the first hundred steps of the stair round the Standing Tree, because somebody should, and the tree, very slightly, leans down to let him."],
	],
	# ---- Part XII: inside the Founder's sealed caves (Main.open_abode adds the gift and the words on the wall)
	"abode:cave_core": [
		["Narrator", "Inside, a plain room cut in the rock: a cushion, a lamp that lights itself, and on the wall, in a woman's quick sword-strokes, the beginning of a letter."],
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "To whoever carries the root after me. It has nine branches, and it wakes one at a time. I have left a door on this mountain for each realm you reach. Do not hurry. A root that is hurried snaps."],
	],
	"abode:cave_soul": [
		["Narrator", "Under the Founder's words somebody has scratched, small, with a knife: 'The seventh bearer was here. Nascent Soul at nineteen! Next door next year.' There is no other mark, and the next door was never opened."],
		["Yue Ling", "Xu Mo found him first. ...Then we open the next one for him."],
	],
	"abode:cave_deity": [
		["Narrator", "In the dust of this cave, two sets of footprints, one large and one small, and on the wall in charcoal: 'The twelfth bearer and her brother. We will come back for the next door together.' Nobody did."],
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "The body is a house. You may leave it, and come back, and find the furniture moved. That is all this realm is. Do not let anybody tell you it makes you more than you were."],
	],
	"abode:cave_void": [
		["Narrator", "The Founder's writing here is older and shakier than at the other doors: 'Nobody has opened this door. I check it every spring. Xu Mo hunts every bearer down before the fifth realm. If you are reading this, he has failed at last. Good.'"],
	],
	"abode:cave_merging": [
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "I wove a girl from the Seal's lotus so that the bearer would never face the end alone. If she is with you, tell her I am proud of her. If she is not, go and find her."],
		["Yue Ling", "...She knew I would come this far. She left a cushion for me, Lin Feng. Look. Two cushions."],
	],
	"abode:cave_mahayana": [
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "The Court sends an inspector up the mountain every spring to count our disciples. I let him count. He never once asks what is in the caves. A government that only counts what it can tax will never see a root grow."],
	],
	"abode:cave_immortal": [
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "If you have come this far, the Court is failing, or you are. There is a Ledger in the sky with every year it stole written in it, and every name. I could not close it. Perhaps you can. Perhaps, by the time you read this, you have."],
	],
	"abode:cave_ancestor": [
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "I never went past this realm. I do not know what is behind the last door, and I made it anyway, because a road should not stop just because the person who built it did."],
	],
	"abode:cave_sovereign": [
		["Narrator", "The last cave is empty: no cushion, no lamp, no words. Only a brush and an ink-stone, set out ready. Lin Feng thinks for a long time, then takes up the brush and writes the Founder an answer on her own wall."],
	],
	# ---- Part XII: The Returning Ruins
	"recall10": [
		["Narrator", "Every road goes somewhere now. Farmers from Qingshi climb the Jianmu on rest days to look down on the clouds, Ling Yu races anyone who will race her, and Lin Feng sweeps the steps in the mornings and the first hundred steps of the Standing Tree in the evenings."],
		["Narrator", "Then, one morning in early autumn, a fishing boat comes up the south-east road on an ox-cart, with three fishermen from the east coast sitting in it, still holding their nets."],
		["Old Shen the Bell-Ringer", "Elder! There is a boat at the gate. On a cart. The fishermen say the sea went out last night and did not come back. Every boat on the east coast is sitting on the sand."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then it has gone somewhere, Shen. Water always goes somewhere. Lin Feng, to the hall. And somebody wake the Archivist."],
	],
	"s_p12_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "East of the Eastern Sea, says the Liezi, who knows how many hundred million li away, there is a ravine without a bottom. It is called Guixu, the Returning Ruins. Every river in the world, every sea and the Silver River of heaven pour into it, and it never fills and never empties."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Over it stood five mountains: Daiyu, Yuanqiao, Fanghu, Yingzhou and Penglai, the Isles of the Immortals, with palaces of gold and trees of pearl whose fruit stops you growing old. They floated, so the Heavenly Emperor set fifteen great turtles to hold them on their heads, in three shifts of sixty thousand years."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the sea going out has something to do with a fairy tale?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Everything in this sect has had something to do with a fairy tale, disciple, since the day you picked up a broom. Water goes into Guixu and comes back as cloud and rain and rivers. That is why the sea stays the sea. If it goes out and does not come back, the Returning Ruins have stopped returning."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Ask Archivist Lan what the Court did with the Eastern Sea; she has been in the Sealed Archive since dawn. Then go east to Fusang, where the sun comes up out of the sea, and see for yourself."],
	],
	"s_p12_archive": [
		["Archivist Lan", "The Liezi, the Records of the Grand Historian and a Court memorandum marked 'not to be read'. Sit. A giant of the Kingdom of Longbo once walked out to the isles in a few strides, caught six of the turtles on one line and took them home to tell fortunes with their shells. Daiyu and Yuanqiao drifted north and sank into the Ruins."],
		["Archivist Lan", "Then the First Emperor of Qin sent his magician Xu Fu east with three thousand boys and girls to fetch the elixir of immortality. Xu Fu sailed, came back once without it, sailed again with sixty ships, and was never seen again. The historian says he found a plain with broad marshes and made himself king. The memorandum says otherwise."],
		["Lin Feng", "What does it say?"],
		["Archivist Lan", "That the Court hid the isles in a mirage, so nobody could pick years off a tree while the Court was selling them by the column; and put a bronze stopper in Guixu, so the water of the world would come back only where the Rain Ministry signed for it. Somebody has pulled the stopper out. That is where your sea went."],
		["Archivist Lan", "And Lin Feng: the memorandum has a list of everyone the Court left on the isles. It is very long, and it is in the column marked 'forgotten'. Be kind to them. Nobody has visited in two thousand years."],
	],
	"s_p12_shore": [
		["Narrator", "Past the Fusang tree the valley falls away east, and where the sea should be there is wet sand to the horizon, shining, with boats lying on it like dropped shoes. By the pool where the ten suns used to bathe, a young woman in red sits in a small boat that is somehow still afloat."],
		["Mazu, Who Brings the Boats Home", "You are the sweeper. The fishermen pray to me, and lately they pray about you: let him come and fix the sea. I am Lin Moniang. On the coast they call me Mazu, Grandmother, which I find very ageing. I bring the boats home.", "wave"],
		["Mazu, Who Brings the Boats Home", "There are no boats to bring home now. The sea has not gone, only gone east: past the edge of the shelf it runs like a river into the Returning Ruins. The hot spring under this pool runs beneath the hills to that river, and my boat floats wherever there is water. Get in."],
		["Mazu, Who Brings the Boats Home", "And watch the horizon. My two generals ran away when the tide went: Qianliyan, who sees a thousand li, and Shunfeng'er, who hears every wind. Demons I tamed once, with a red lantern and a great deal of patience. Somebody is paying them to watch the sea."],
		["Narrator", "Mazu lifts the red lantern from the stern and puts it in Yue Ling's hands, and its light settles there like a warm stone: Mazu's Red Lantern, which brings the fallen home as surely as it brings boats.", "Mazu, Who Brings the Boats Home:offer"],
		["Yue Ling", "We will bring it back, Grandmother. And your generals.", "bow"],
	],
	"s_pl_intro": [
		["Narrator", "Out of the haze rises an isle of white cliffs and green terraces, a pavilion on the highest cliff and, along the old shore, a town of blue-tiled houses with every boat in it lying on the sand. A woman with a carved staff is waiting at the door of the biggest hall."],
		["Headwoman Xu Qian of Landfall", "A boat. A boat that floats. From outside. Forgive me, honoured guest: nobody has come from outside in two thousand two hundred years, and we have rehearsed what to say for most of them, and I have forgotten all of it.", "shock"],
		["Headwoman Xu Qian of Landfall", "This is Landfall. We are the Thousand Children: the grandchildren's grandchildren of the three thousand boys and girls Xu Fu brought from Qin to fetch the elixir. He went up the hill to ask the immortals for it, and he did not come back down. We stayed. Where would we go? The mirage let nobody in, or out."],
		["Headwoman Xu Qian of Landfall", "Now the mirage has lifted and the sea has gone, both at once. The drain took our boats, and the Slips of the Thousand Names: every child's name, carved on bamboo, kept in this hall since the landing. Four bundles went out with the water. The sharks it stranded are snapping at the children. Please."],
		["Lin Feng", "I will bring the names home.", "nod"],
	],
	"s_pl_return": [
		["Headwoman Xu Qian of Landfall", "Four bundles. Every name... Teacher Qin, count them! ...All here. Look: the oldest slips are all in one hand. The same hand, three thousand names.", "grieve"],
		["Teacher Qin of the Thousand Names", "Xu Fu's hand. He carved every name himself, the first night ashore, so that none of us would be forgotten. And on the last slip, not a name: I will come back with the elixir, and none of you will ever grow old."],
		["Headwoman Xu Qian of Landfall", "None of us grew old waiting, honoured guest. We grew old in the ordinary way. If you want to know where the sea went, ask the immortals. One of them comes down to drink: Lu Dongbin, upstairs in the wine-house. He has drunk there for two thousand years and never once paid in coin."],
	],
	"s_pl_immortal": [
		["Lu Dongbin, the Sword Immortal", "A guest! And sober! Sit, sit. I am Lu Dongbin. I carry a sword to cut through ignorance, mostly my own. The others are up on Fangzhang being dignified; I prefer the wine down here, and the company.", "laugh"],
		["Lu Dongbin, the Sword Immortal", "Xu Fu came up the hill two thousand years ago and asked for the elixir. We told him the truth: the fruit of the pearl trees does not make you live for ever. It makes you stop. A ripe fruit, eaten when you are ready, lets you stop when you choose. An unripe one just stops you."],
		["Lu Dongbin, the Sword Immortal", "He did not believe us. He stole a green one and bit it, and he has not changed since: not a hair, not a thought. He cannot sleep, he cannot forget the Emperor's orders and he cannot change his mind. The only ripe fruit left grows on Daiyu, which sank into the Returning Ruins."],
		["Lin Feng", "So he pulled out the stopper, to drain the sea down to Daiyu."],
		["Lu Dongbin, the Sword Immortal", "Clever sweeper. Stop him before the sea is gone. Mazu's runaway generals are his lookouts, the Sea-Monk is drowning fishermen on the sandbar for fun, and the Giant of Longbo is wading on the shelf, fishing for turtles. If he catches three more, Penglai sinks. Have a drink first. No? Suit yourself."],
	],
	"pre:qianliyan": [
		["Narrator", "On a crag above the shoals stands a green-faced demon with eyes like lanterns, shading them with one hand and staring east."],
		["Qianliyan, Thousand-Li Eye", "I see you. I saw you leave Fusang. I saw you eat your breakfast. A man with a green fruit pays me in pearls to watch the sea, and I watch it. There is not much sea left to watch."],
		["Yue Ling", "Mazu misses you."],
		["Qianliyan, Thousand-Li Eye", "...Does she? No. Lies. I would have seen it."],
	],
	"post:qianliyan": [["Narrator", "Qianliyan sits down hard on his crag and rubs his eyes, which have not shut for a month. Far away on the mainland, he says quietly, a woman in red is standing on a cliff with a lantern, every night, waiting for boats. He had not looked that way. He looks now."]],
	"pre:shunfenger": [
		["Narrator", "Among the dunes of the bare sea floor a red-faced demon with ears like sails crouches with one hand cupped to the wind."],
		["Shunfeng'er, Wind-Following Ear", "Shh. I hear everything: the sand settling, the turtles groaning, the giant's line in the water, your heart, a bit fast. The man with the green fruit pays me to listen. He says the sea will be gone in a month, and then I can hear the trees on Daiyu."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then listen to this."],
	],
	"post:shunfenger": [["Narrator", "Shunfeng'er sits back in the sand with his ears ringing, and under the ringing he hears something he has ignored for a month: Mazu, on the mainland, calling the names of her boats one by one, every night. He gets up and starts walking west."]],
	"pre:sea_monk": [
		["Narrator", "On the fishermen's sandbar crouches a thing like a great turtle with a shaved man's head, chuckling, holding a fisherman down in the last puddle of the shoal."],
		["The Sea-Monk Who Drowns Fishermen", "Heh. When the sea was deep I had to swim after them. Now they walk right up to me, looking for their boats."],
		["Yue Ling", "Let him go."],
	],
	"post:sea_monk": [["Narrator", "The Sea-Monk lets the fisherman go and slides off into the channel, muttering that it was better sport when there was sea. Old Pilot Zheng says Landfall will remember, and the Sea-Monk will find the shoals rather crowded with nets when the water comes back."]],
	"pre:longbo_giant": [
		["Narrator", "Out on the bare shelf east of Penglai, where the sea was a thousand feet deep, stands a giant up to his ankles in the last of the water, casting a line as thick as a cable. Something vast under the isle groans."],
		["The Giant of Longbo, Who Fishes for Turtles", "Six turtles, my great-great-grandfather caught, in one cast! The Emperor shrank our whole kingdom for it. Now the Court is gone, the shrinking is wearing off, and the sea is down to my ankles. Three more and I beat his record."],
		["Lin Feng", "Three more and Penglai sinks, with the Thousand Children on it."],
		["The Giant of Longbo, Who Fishes for Turtles", "Then they should learn to swim. Out of my light, little fisherman."],
	],
	"post:longbo_giant": [
		["Narrator", "The giant sits down in the shallows with a splash that runs all the way to Landfall, and looks at his great hook, bent straight. Lin Feng takes it from him."],
		["The Giant of Longbo, Who Fishes for Turtles", "...My great-great-grandfather told it as a fishing story. He never said there were people on the islands. He never said they sank."],
		["Narrator", "The giant wades away west, a little smaller with every stride. Beyond the shelf a sandbar the drain has left bare runs east into the haze, towards a square-topped isle, and the way to Fangzhang is open."],
	],
	"s_fz_intro": [
		["He Xiangu of the Lotus", "Lu Dongbin sent you up? He never sends anybody; he forgets. I am He Xiangu. I carry a lotus and keep the gardens of Fangzhang, the Square-Pot Isle: the elixir herbs, the furnaces and the pearl trees."],
		["He Xiangu of the Lotus", "The pearl trees drink from the Returning Ruins through roots a thousand li long. Since the sea started going out they have been drinking nothing, and their leaves fall like coins. Bring me the dew from four of them before it dries, and I will show you what the elixir really is."],
		["Yue Ling", "And the toads in the herb beds?"],
		["He Xiangu of the Lotus", "The furnaces went cold and the cinnabar toads came out of them; the cranes are stealing what pills are left and the rabbits are eating the herbs. Chase them out, if you would. Gently. They are only hungry, like everybody on this isle."],
	],
	"s_fz_return": [
		["Narrator", "He Xiangu lets one drop of the dew fall on a withered lotus. The lotus opens, and then stays exactly as it is: not a petal moves, not in the wind, not when Yue Ling touches it.", "He Xiangu of the Lotus:offer"],
		["He Xiangu of the Lotus", "That is the elixir: water from the Returning Ruins, where every water in the world has been and come back. It does not give you years. It takes away the need for them. Xu Fu wanted it for an emperor who wanted never to end. We said no, not to keep it from Xu Fu, but to keep it from the emperor."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the Court hid you anyway."],
		["He Xiangu of the Lotus", "The Court hid everybody it could not tax. Go down to the cellar of cold furnaces and ask Zhang Guolao. He is the oldest of us, and he was there when the Court came. He sits on his donkey backwards, so mind his back. His front. Whichever."],
	],
	"s_fz_zhang": [
		["Zhang Guolao, Who Rides Backwards", "Sit. I ride backwards so I can see where I have been; you would be amazed how few people look. I was there when the Court came, with ledgers, to put a stopper in Guixu.", "wave"],
		["Zhang Guolao, Who Rides Backwards", "It used to be simple. Every water ran east into the Ruins and came back up, as cloud over the sea, as springs, as the Silver River. Nobody signed for it. The Court wanted it signed for. So it set Tianwu, the god of the waters, to keep a bronze stopper in the Ruins, and water came back only where the Rain Ministry wrote it down."],
		["Lin Feng", "And when the Court fell?"],
		["Zhang Guolao, Who Rides Backwards", "Tianwu kept the stopper anyway, because nobody told him to stop. Then Xu Fu told him the Court had ordered it pulled. Tianwu has eight heads, and not one of them is good at checking orders. So the stopper is out, the water goes in, and nothing comes back but wind. The water has forgotten how to come back by itself."],
		["Zhang Guolao, Who Rides Backwards", "Tianwu is at the east end of the isle, eight heads arguing about whose fault it is. The White Deer has eaten our last ripe herbs, the furnace that walked off is still burning, and the pearl tree at the bottom of the garden has stopped fruiting out of spite. One thing at a time, sweeper. That is the whole secret of immortality."],
	],
	"pre:longevity_deer": [
		["Narrator", "In the high meadow a white deer with antlers like coral is eating the last ripe herbs of the immortals' garden, leaf, root and flower, and growing a little younger with every mouthful."],
		["The White Deer of Longevity", "The garden is dying. Somebody should eat it while it is still good."],
		["Yue Ling", "Somebody should save it."],
	],
	"post:longevity_deer": [["Narrator", "The White Deer stops eating and looks at the bare meadow, and for the first time in a thousand years looks its age. It lies down among the stalks and stays there, keeping the seeds warm, until somebody plants them."]],
	"pre:elixir_furnace": [
		["Narrator", "A bronze furnace taller than a house is stamping across the terraces on three legs, its belly roaring, spilling half-made pills that burn holes in the grass."],
		["The Elixir Furnace That Walked Away", "FED. NOT FED. FED. NOT FED. The fire said: make the elixir. There is no water from the Ruins. So I make everything else. For ever."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let the fire rest."],
	],
	"post:elixir_furnace": [["Narrator", "Lin Feng banks the furnace's fire the way Alchemist Mo taught him in his first week, ash over the embers, and the furnace kneels on its three legs and goes quiet. Furnace-Boy Dan climbs inside to sleep, which, he says, is what furnaces are for."]],
	"pre:pearl_tree": [
		["Narrator", "At the bottom of the garden stands a tree of white pearl with leaves of jade, rattling like a box of beads, its bare branches lashing at anything that comes near."],
		["The Pearl Tree That Will Not Fruit", "Everything that eats my fruit stops. Stops growing. Stops changing. Stops. Why should I fruit, so that things can stop?"],
		["Yue Ling", "Because some things are tired."],
	],
	"post:pearl_tree": [["Narrator", "The pearl tree goes still, and one green bud opens among its leaves: not a fruit, a flower. He Xiangu says no pearl tree has flowered in two thousand years. Flowers do not stop anything. They only open."]],
	"pre:tianwu": [
		["Narrator", "At the east end of Fangzhang, on a cliff over the drained sea, crouches a tiger the size of a temple with eight human heads, eight legs and eight tails, and every head is shouting at the others."],
		["Tianwu, the Eight-Headed God of the Waters", "The stopper was pulled by order! No, it was stolen! The Court ordered it! The Court is gone! Then who ordered it? HE did! Who is he? The man with the green fruit! Is he the Court? He SAID he was the Court!"],
		["Lin Feng", "Nobody is the Court now."],
		["Tianwu, the Eight-Headed God of the Waters", "...Then who do we obey? ATTACK HIM! Wait. Why?"],
	],
	"post:tianwu": [
		["Tianwu, the Eight-Headed God of the Waters", "...If nobody is the Court, who decides where the water goes?"],
		["Lin Feng", "The water. It always did, until somebody put a stopper in it."],
		["Narrator", "The eight heads look at one another and, for once, say nothing at all. Tianwu lies down across the cliff and points all eight noses east: beyond Yingzhou, at the lip of the Ruins, Xu Fu rolled the stopper away down the sea floor. A sandbar runs north-east into the mist, and the way to Yingzhou is open."],
	],
	"s_yz_intro": [
		["Narrator", "Yingzhou comes out of the mist in pieces: a cliff, a pine, a shrine roof, a long grey shape breathing under the shore. On a rock at the water's old edge sits a girl with red feet, a white beak of a mouth and a pebble in her hand."],
		["Jingwei, the Flame Emperor's Daughter", "Jingwei. Jingwei. That is what I say, so that is what they call me. I was the Flame Emperor's daughter. I went out on the Eastern Sea in a little boat, and the sea drowned me, and I came back as a bird."],
		["Jingwei, the Flame Emperor's Daughter", "For four thousand years I have carried stones and twigs from the western hills and dropped them in the sea, to fill it up, so that it can never drown anybody else. And now look. It is going. It is nearly gone."],
		["Lin Feng", "You don't sound happy."],
		["Jingwei, the Flame Emperor's Daughter", "...If the sea goes by itself, what was I doing? I lost four stones in the mist this morning and I could not even remember why I wanted them. Find them for me. And chase the phantoms out of the mist; they frighten the turtles."],
	],
	"s_yz_return": [
		["Jingwei, the Flame Emperor's Daughter", "My stones. Thank you. Now look over the edge, while the water is low. No, there. Under the isle."],
		["Narrator", "Below the cliff, where the sea has drained away, Yingzhou stands not only on the back of a turtle but on a reef of pebbles and twigs, grey and white and red, packed hard and four thousand years deep."],
		["Jingwei, the Flame Emperor's Daughter", "...I was not filling the sea. I was building something. I never saw it, with the water over it.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "It could hold the isle. Then the turtles could rest."],
		["Narrator", "Jingwei presses one pebble into Lin Feng's palm, worn smooth as a sword hilt by four thousand years of beaks, and a technique settles into him with it, patient and heavy: Jingwei's Stone Sword.", "Jingwei, the Flame Emperor's Daughter:offer"],
		["Jingwei, the Flame Emperor's Daughter", "I will keep building. Not to fill. To hold. Old Gui at the shrine keeps the turtles' tallies; go up the stairs and shout. He is deaf in one ear and stubborn in the other."],
	],
	"s_yz_herald": [
		["Old Gui, Herald of the Turtles", "Eh? A visitor in the tally-room? I am Gui, herald of the turtles, and these are the tallies of their shifts: fifteen turtles, five isles, three shifts of sixty thousand years. At every shift the God of the Northern Sea blows his conch, and the turtles change places."],
		["Old Gui, Herald of the Turtles", "Then the giant of Longbo took six and two isles sank. Nine turtles were left for three isles, all of them holding at once, and no shift to relieve them. Forty thousand years without a rest. Now the sea has drained and they lie on the dry shelf with the isles on their heads, and they cannot even float."],
		["Lin Feng", "I took the giant's hook from him. Here."],
		["Old Gui, Herald of the Turtles", "...Bent straight. Then nine of us can stop listening for it. But the conch of the shifts belongs to Yu Qiang, God of the Northern Sea, and he has not blown it since the Court told him to wait for orders. He stands at the east end of the isle in a wind of his own.", "grieve"],
		["Old Gui, Herald of the Turtles", "And mind: Wu Zixu, who drove the tidal bore up every river mouth, has stopped the tide, because there is no sea to drive; Shen the clam-dragon, who breathed the mirage that hid us, is breathing false seas to feel better; and the Great Ao who bears Yingzhou is thrashing on the shelf. Mind your feet."],
	],
	"pre:wu_zixu": [
		["Narrator", "On the northern cliffs a pale general in white armour sits a horse made of foam on the bare sand where the tide used to come in, a leather sack slung from his saddle."],
		["Wu Zixu, God of the Tidal Bore", "My king had me sewn into a sack and thrown into the river for telling him the truth, so I became the tide that comes up the river twice a day to tell it again. Now there is no sea. What truth is a tide without water?"],
		["Yue Ling", "The same truth, General. Only quieter."],
	],
	"post:wu_zixu": [["Narrator", "Wu Zixu's horse dissolves into foam, and the foam runs west into the last of the channel and lies there, waiting. When the sea comes back, he says, he will ride the bore up every river again and tell the truth to whoever stands on the bank. He hopes somebody will listen this time."]],
	"pre:shen": [
		["Narrator", "In the mist over the drained bay lies a great pale clam with a dragon's head, breathing out of her shell, and every breath builds a city in the air: towers, markets, a harbour full of ships, and a sea that is not there."],
		["Shen, the Mirage Clam-Dragon", "The Court said: breathe, and hide the isles, and I breathed for two thousand years. Now nobody tells me what to hide, so I breathe what is missing. Isn't it lovely? It's a sea."],
		["Lin Feng", "It isn't a sea. People are walking into it."],
	],
	"post:shen": [["Narrator", "Shen's mirage folds up like a fan and goes back into her shell. When the sea comes home, she says, she will breathe only for fun: palaces over the water at dawn, for fishermen to look at and know they are not real. Every mirage should know it is a mirage."]],
	"pre:great_ao": [
		["Narrator", "On the dry shelf under Yingzhou lies a turtle the size of a hill with the whole isle on its head, its flippers beating the sand for water that is not there."],
		["The Great Ao Who Bears Yingzhou", "...thirsty... forty thousand years... the sea is gone... I cannot put it down... I cannot put it down..."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let me hold it with you, for a moment."],
	],
	"post:great_ao": [["Narrator", "The Great Ao's thrashing slows and stops. Yue Ling calls down the Spring Rain she learned in her first winter on the mountain, and it falls on the old shell like a blessing, and the turtle sighs, and sees the reef of Jingwei's stones beside its head, and notices for the first time that it is not holding the isle alone."]],
	"pre:yu_qiang": [
		["Narrator", "At the east end of Yingzhou, in a wind of his own, stands a god with a man's face and a bird's body, green snakes hanging from his ears and green snakes under his feet. A great conch hangs at his side."],
		["Yu Qiang, God of the Northern Sea", "The Emperor told me: keep the shifts, and wait for orders. I have waited forty thousand years. The orders are coming. Everybody tells me there are no more orders. That is exactly what somebody would say who wanted me to stop waiting."],
		["Lin Feng", "I am asking you to stop waiting."],
	],
	"post:yu_qiang": [
		["Yu Qiang, God of the Northern Sea", "...Then who will tell me when to blow the conch?"],
		["Lin Feng", "The turtles. They have been telling you for forty thousand years."],
		["Narrator", "Yu Qiang gives Lin Feng the conch of the shifts, and Lin Feng blows it. The sound goes down through the isles into the shelf, and under Penglai, Fangzhang and Yingzhou two turtles out of every three let go, for the first time in forty thousand years, and sleep, while the third and Jingwei's reef hold. Old Gui weeps into his tallies."],
		["Narrator", "Beyond Yingzhou the sea floor slopes down and down to the east, towards a roar like every river in the world at once. The way to the Returning Ruins is open."],
	],
	"s_gx_intro": [
		["Narrator", "The sea floor runs down for a hundred li to the lip of the Returning Ruins: wrecks, drowned forests of coral, whole sunken mountains standing in the open air for the first time since the Yellow Emperor. On a slope lies a great ship of Qin, its stern cabin still dry, and in it sits the ghost of an old sailor, steering nothing."],
		["Steersman Hong of the Lost Fleet", "Starboard... steady... Oh. A living man, on my deck. You are not on the crew list, but then nobody is any more. I am Hong, steersman of the Penglai-Seeker, Xu Fu's flagship: sixty ships out of Langya, two thousand two hundred years ago.", "shock"],
		["Steersman Hong of the Lost Fleet", "He came back down from the isle with a green fruit with a bite out of it and a look in his eyes I did not like, and told me to steer east, to Guixu, where the sunken isles were. I said no ship comes back from Guixu. He said: then it will not have to."],
		["Steersman Hong of the Lost Fleet", "He tore up his logbook on the way down, and the pages are all over the sea floor now the water is gone. Bring them to me. I would like to know, before I go, what he was thinking."],
	],
	"s_gx_return": [
		["Steersman Hong of the Lost Fleet", "Four pages, all in his hand. Listen: 'Day one. Three thousand children ashore. I have carved every name. I will be back before the youngest loses a tooth.'"],
		["Steersman Hong of the Lost Fleet", "'Year forty. I have not grown one grey hair. I cannot sleep. I remember the Emperor's order word for word, and I cannot remember my mother's face.' And the last: 'If I find a ripe fruit, I can finish. I would like to finish.'"],
		["Yue Ling", "He doesn't want to live for ever. He wants to stop being stopped."],
		["Steersman Hong of the Lost Fleet", "Then he is down at the lip of the Ruins, where the summit of Daiyu has come up out of the water, waiting for the last tree to ripen. Ah Fu the ship's boy will show you the way; he never grew up either. Go on. I have a ship to steer."],
	],
	"s_gx_boy": [
		["Ah Fu, the Ship's Boy Who Never Grew Up", "Are you going to see Master Xu? He gave me a bite of the green fruit when I was hungry, two thousand years ago. Just a little bite. So I'm still ten."],
		["Ah Fu, the Ship's Boy Who Never Grew Up", "He isn't wicked, you know. He just can't change his mind. Once he decides something he can't undecide it. He decided to come back with the elixir, so he can't come back without it.", "shake"],
		["Lin Feng", "Then we'll help him find a way to come back."],
		["Ah Fu, the Ship's Boy Who Never Grew Up", "There's things in the way. The Admiral, who still thinks we're at sea. The stopper Master Xu rolled down the slope, which won't stop rolling. And the Sea. The real one: Ruo of the Northern Sea, rising out of the drain. She's very, very angry."],
	],
	"pre:drowned_admiral": [
		["Narrator", "On the northern slope a warship of Qin stands upright on the dry sea floor with every sail set, and on its deck an admiral in rusted armour is shouting orders at a crew of skeletons."],
		["The Admiral of the Lost Fleet", "HOLD COURSE! Steady as she goes! Sixty ships to Penglai for the Emperor's elixir, and we shall not turn back!"],
		["Yue Ling", "Admiral... your ship has not moved for two thousand years."],
	],
	"post:drowned_admiral": [["Narrator", "The Admiral lowers his sword and looks over the rail at the sand for a long time. Then he gives his last order: all hands ashore. The skeletons climb down and walk west across the sea floor in a long, neat line towards Landfall, where somebody's grandchildren's grandchildren may still have their names."]],
	"pre:guixu_stopper": [
		["Narrator", "Up and down the southern slope, like a boulder in a gutter, rolls a plug of bronze as big as a temple, engraved with the Court's seals and a ledger of every river in the world."],
		["The Bronze Stopper of Guixu", "WHERE DO I GO? WHERE DO I GO? I was made to be in a hole. Then I was pulled out. Nobody made me to be out."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then rest. You don't have to go back in."],
	],
	"post:guixu_stopper": [["Narrator", "The Stopper rolls to a halt against a coral reef and lies there ringing softly. It is not going back in, Lin Feng tells it. Nothing is. Under the open sky the ledger engraved on it, every river in the world signed for, begins to fade."]],
	"pre:hai_ruo": [
		["Narrator", "At the lip of the Returning Ruins the last of the sea pours over the edge into a darkness without a bottom, and out of the roar rises a figure of water as tall as the sky, with the whole Eastern Sea in her eyes."],
		["Ruo of the Northern Sea, the Sea Itself", "The River Earl came to me once so proud of his little river, and I showed him how small he was. Now some little man has pulled out my floor, and I am pouring away into the dark, and I am small. I hate being small."],
		["Lin Feng", "He Bo learned to like it."],
	],
	"post:hai_ruo": [["Narrator", "Ruo sinks back into the roar, and for a moment the sea holds still at the lip, listening, while Lin Feng tells her about He Bo at Longmen, who leaves the brides alone now and keeps a small and very happy river. The sea laughs, a wave the size of a province, and goes on pouring; but slower."]],
	"pre:xu_fu": [
		["Narrator", "On the drowned summit of Daiyu, in the open air at the very lip of the Ruins, grows a single tree of pearl with one fruit on it, not yet ripe. Under it stands a man in the robes of a Qin court magician, not a day older than the morning he sailed, holding a green fruit with one bite out of it."],
		["Xu Fu, Who Sought the Elixir", "Another one come to stop me. Everybody has tried: the immortals, the sea, my own ship. I cannot be stopped. That is the trouble. I was stopped two thousand years ago, by this."],
		["Lin Feng", "The children you left are still waiting at Landfall. Their grandchildren's grandchildren."],
		["Xu Fu, Who Sought the Elixir", "I promised them the elixir. I cannot go back without it. I cannot. I have tried to want to, and I cannot."],
	],
	"post:xu_fu": [
		["Narrator", "Xu Fu falls to his knees under the pearl tree. Above him, in the roar of the sea pouring into the dark, the last fruit of Daiyu turns from green to white, ripe at last, and drops into his hands."],
		["Xu Fu, Who Sought the Elixir", "...Ripe. After two thousand years. I could eat it and stop when I choose. I could live as long as I liked."],
		["Yue Ling", "What would you like?"],
		["Xu Fu, Who Sought the Elixir", "...I would like to grow old. I would like to see the children. I would like to finish."],
		["Narrator", "He eats the ripe fruit, and his first grey hair comes in while they watch. He gives Lin Feng the green one with the bite out of it. Behind him the sea goes on pouring over the lip into the dark, and nothing comes back up. The water has forgotten how; somebody will have to remind it."],
	],
	"s_p12_end": [
		["Narrator", "Mazu's boat brings them back along the channel to Penglai at dusk, with Xu Fu sitting in the bow, getting older by the hour and looking at everything. The whole of Landfall is on the sand to meet them."],
		["Headwoman Xu Qian of Landfall", "...You are him. The hand on the slips. You are older than I thought you would be.", "shock"],
		["Xu Fu, Who Came Back", "I am older than I was this morning. I said I would come back with the elixir, and that none of you would grow old. I was wrong. You all grew old. That was the elixir. I just did not know what it looked like.", "kneel"],
		["Teacher Qin of the Thousand Names", "Three thousand names, Master Xu. Every child in Landfall can say them. Will you hear them?", "offer"],
		["Narrator", "He listens to all three thousand, sitting on the floor of the Hall of the Thousand Names. Lin Feng gives the Headwoman the green fruit with the bite out of it, and she buries it under the hall, where nobody will ever eat it. Then everybody looks at Lin Feng, because out to the east the sea is still pouring into the dark."],
		["Mazu, Who Brings the Boats Home", "The Ruins took the sea because they have forgotten how to give it back. The Court wrote every return in a ledger for so long that the water does not remember how to rise by itself."],
		["Yue Ling", "Like the Jianmu, which forgot how to grow. Somebody has to remind it."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I will go and stand at the lip.", "nod"],
	],
	"pre:s_break17": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng stands on the drowned summit of Daiyu at the lip of the Returning Ruins, Jingwei's pebble in his pocket and Yu Qiang's conch at his belt. The tribulation that gathers is every water in the world at once, the rain, the rivers, the springs under the Jianmu's roots and the Silver River of heaven, pouring past him into the dark and asking where home is."],
		["Yue Ling", "Which way does it come back?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Every way. That is what returning is.", "point"],
	],
	"post:s_break17": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng lets every water in the world pass through him into the Ruins, holding nothing back and keeping nothing, and in the dark at the bottom of the world the water remembers, and rises: as cloud over the Eastern Sea, as springs under the Jianmu, as the Silver River across the night."],
		["Narrator", "He becomes the first cultivator of Ten Thousand Waters Return: a realm in which everything that goes away can come home."],
		["Narrator", "The tide comes back into Fusang at dawn. Mazu stands on her cliff with her red lantern and two sheepish generals, calling the boats home by name. Wu Zixu rides the bore up every river on the east coast. Rain-Minister Ao Run signs his first page with real sea in it, and Leigong drums for it."],
		["Narrator", "On Penglai, Xu Fu teaches the children of Landfall to sail, slowly, because he is old now and in no hurry. Under the isles two turtles out of every three are asleep, and Jingwei goes on dropping her stones: not to fill the sea, but to hold."],
		["Jingwei, the Flame Emperor's Daughter", "Jingwei. Jingwei. It means: I am still here."],
		["Narrator", "And on Azure Cloud Mountain, in the cool of the evening, Lin Feng sweeps the steps of the sect and the first hundred steps of the Standing Tree, and then goes down to the spring to fill the water jars, because somebody should, and the water comes up out of the ground to meet him."],
	],
	# ---- Part XIII: The Eight Gates
	"recall11": [
		["Narrator", "The tide comes back to Fusang and stays. For a whole autumn nothing goes away that does not come back: the boats, the rain, the swallows, Ling Yu after every race, and the Archivist's library books."],
		["Narrator", "So the peoples of the four realms agree to come to Azure Cloud Mountain and sit down together under the Jianmu: the first Conclave of the Realms, with no Court at the head of the table, because there is no head of the table."],
		["Narrator", "On the eve of it, while the Feathered Folk are landing on the sect's roofs and the dragon-kin are arguing with the cook, eight pillars of gold light go up at once, far away, in all four realms."],
		["Old Shen the Bell-Ringer", "Elder! The sky has bars on it. Gold ones, eight of them. The foxes say there is one at the Gate of Heaven, and the Pearl-Weepers have sent word of one in the Springs.", "shock"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Eight. Of course it is eight. Lin Feng, to the hall. And somebody ask the Feathered Folk to get off my roof."],
	],
	"s_p13_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "When the Yellow Emperor fought Chiyou at Zhuolu, the war god of the Nine Li filled the field with a fog that lasted a hundred days, and the Emperor's army could not find its own feet."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "So the Mysterious Lady of the Nine Heavens came down and taught the Emperor the art of the Eight Gates: Opening, Rest, Life, Harm, Closing, Sight, Alarm and Death. A formation in which every gate is a different road, and only some of them lead out."],
		["Lin Feng", "And with it he won."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "With it, and a drum made from a thunder-ox, and a daughter who dried up the rain. Chiyou died, and the Court, which was not yet the Court, buried him under a lock of eight gates so that his war would never wake. The lock answered to the Court. The Court is gone."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Every water comes home through you now, disciple. It seems the dead would like to as well. Ask the Archivist what the Court wrote about Zhuolu. Then I would like you to stop a war that ended five thousand years ago.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p13_archive": [
		["Archivist Lan", "Zhuolu. The Court's file runs to forty boxes and is marked 'victory'. Sit. It says the Eight Gates were set round Chiyou's grave and then scattered over the world, two to a realm, so that nobody could ever stand at all eight at once."],
		["Archivist Lan", "Each gate has a general, or a guardian, or a thing that used to be one, and each answers to something different. The Gate of Opening to a blade, or to gold. The Gate of Rest to a heart that will not sleep. The Gate of Death to a formation, or to a name even the dead respect. Eight pages of very small writing."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I have to open all eight?", "think"],
		["Archivist Lan", "No. That is the clever part, and the Court never wrote it down: I found it in the Lady's own war-book, which the Court used to prop up a table. A lock of eight gates breaks when half of them stand open. Any half. Open the four whose locks you can answer, in whatever order you like."],
		["Archivist Lan", "And then, says the book, there are three roads into the heart: the road of the sword, the road of the many, and the road of the cauldron. I do not know what that means. The lantern monk in the Maple Gorge might. The oldest maples there were Chiyou's fetters once."],
	],
	"s_p13_yinglong": [
		["Narrator", "Below the broken pillar of Buzhou, Yinglong lies coiled round a spring he dug himself, his great wings folded. He opens one eye when Lin Feng says the name Zhuolu, and does not close it again."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "I was there. The Emperor sent me against Chiyou when the fog would not lift. I stored up the waters and dug channels for them, so the fog had somewhere to go. And then I killed him. Nobody ever asks me about that part."],
		["Lin Feng", "What was he?", "think"],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "A smith. The first. He found the copper in the hills and made ploughs of it before he ever made an axe. His people were starving and the Emperor's people had the river. That is what the war was about. The histories say it was about virtue."],
		["Yinglong, Who Dug the Rivers", "After it I could never fly back up to heaven; the weight of it, they say. I went south and made rain for farmers instead. If he wakes, little cultivator, tell him the rivers I dug water the Nine Li's old fields now. He would want to know.", "grieve"],
	],
	"s_p13_monk": [
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "You came down the funeral road with your eyes open. Good. Look at the old maples: they are red in spring now, and red in summer. They were red when they were iron, too."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "When Chiyou died they struck the fetters off him and threw them down here, and the fetters grew into the first maples, red with his blood. Every autumn since, something has walked under them in the fog. It used to walk like a soldier. Lately it walks like a man looking for the way home."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Archivist says there are three roads into the heart of the lock."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "Every lock has three keys: the hand that forces it, the friend who is let in, and the smith who makes the key. Open four of the eight gates first, wherever they stand. Then come back, and I will light you the way down."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "But first, the funeral road. His war-band has come up out of the fog onto it, and the dead cannot get past them to the Wheel. Drive them back, please. Gently, if you can; they think it is still the war."],
		["Yue Ling", "Four gates in four realms. We have walked further for less.", "laugh"],
	],
	"s_p13_heart": [
		["Narrator", "The fourth gate falls, and far away in the Maple Gorge the fog lifts all at once, like a cloth pulled off a table. Under the oldest maples lies a field that was not there before, trampled and silent, with five thousand years of autumn leaves on it."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "Zhuolu. They buried the battlefield with him. The lock is broken, and the heart is open."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "Choose your road before you walk down there. Go alone, with only the road you have walked, as the Yellow Emperor did. Or go with every people you have helped at your back. Or go with something made by hand that he has wanted for five thousand years. He will know which before you say a word."],
		["Lin Feng", "And whichever road I take, he is waiting at the end of it.", "nod"],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "Every road ends somewhere. That is what makes it a road."],
		["Yue Ling", "Whatever road we take, the Conclave should be called first. If a war god is going to stop fighting after five thousand years, the peoples ought to be there to see it."],
		["Lantern Monk Jing Kong", "Then take the Elder's invitations round. He has written one for every people, and he has written them very politely, which for him is a great effort."],
	],
	# Part XIII: the invitations to the Conclave, any four of the eight leaders, in any order
	"visit:yu_hua": [
		["Feather-Mother Yu Hua", "An invitation? To sit down? The Yumin do not sit, Lin Feng, we perch. ...We will perch at your Conclave. All of us. Warn the cook.", "laugh"],
		["Feather-Mother Yu Hua", "Our oldest songs remember Chiyou's fog: a hundred days when no Yumin could see to fly. If you are going to talk to him, ask him to keep it to himself."],
	],
	"visit:ao_run": [
		["Rain-Minister Ao Run", "The Ministry has a file on Yu Shi, the Rain Master who drowned an emperor's army. It is very thick and every page of it is wet. We will come, and we will bring the file."],
		["Rain-Minister Ao Run", "The dragon-kin fought for the Yellow Emperor at Zhuolu; Yinglong dug the channels that drained the fog. It will be interesting, to sit across a table from the other side at last."],
	],
	"visit:hu_qingyi": [
		["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "A Conclave with no head of the table. How deliciously confusing. The foxes accept, on condition that nobody asks us to sit still.", "laugh"],
		["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "My grandmother's grandmother saw Chiyou once, from a long way off. She said he laughed like a bell cracking and that he was kind to animals. Nobody remembers that part."],
	],
	"visit:shui_lian": [
		["Pearl-Weaver Shui Lian", "We will come up out of the dark for it. We have never seen a council held in daylight. Will there be weeping? We are very good at weeping."],
		["Pearl-Weaver Shui Lian", "The old songs say the Nine Li were river people, like us. When they lost, the water took their names. We still sing some of them; we could sing them to him."],
	],
	"visit:bu_si": [
		["Elder Bu Si of the Deathless", "We do not die, so we are always the last to arrive anywhere. We will try to be early. It will be a novelty."],
		["Elder Bu Si of the Deathless", "Chiyou is dead and does not know it. We are alive and cannot stop. Perhaps the two of us should sit together at your Conclave and compare notes.", "think"],
	],
	"visit:ming_yi": [
		["Lantern-Magistrate Ming Yi", "The shades accept. We will bring our own lanterns and our own minutes, which will be more accurate than the Archivist's. Tell her I said so."],
		["Lantern-Magistrate Ming Yi", "Chiyou's soul never came down to Youdu to be registered. Five thousand years outstanding. I have a form for him, if he would like to fill it in at last."],
	],
	"visit:xu_qian": [
		["Headwoman Xu Qian of Landfall", "Landfall, at a council of the realms. Two thousand years ago we were three thousand children nobody wanted. Of course we will come. Master Xu wants to come too; he likes meetings now."],
		["Headwoman Xu Qian of Landfall", "Teacher Qin says the Nine Li were the first people the Court ever wrote out of history. We know how that feels. Tell the war god we kept our names."],
	],
	"visit:he_xiangu": [
		["He Xiangu of the Lotus", "The immortals of the isles at a council with farmers, fishermen and shades. Two thousand years ago we would have been offended. We will come, and we will be quiet, which will be good for us."],
		["He Xiangu of the Lotus", "Lu Dongbin met Chiyou's Banner once, in the sky over the sea. He says it was the saddest light he ever saw. Bring the war god something kind, if you can."],
	],
	"pre:chiyou": [
		["Narrator", "In the middle of the field of Zhuolu, under a sky that has not been blue in five thousand years, stands a giant in bronze with the horns of an ox, iron brows and an axe in each of four hands. His eyes are open. They have been open the whole time."],
		["Chiyou, War God of the Nine Li", "Another army of the Yellow Emperor. Another hundred days of fog. I have dreamed this battle every night since I died. In the dream I never win, and I never stop."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Yellow Emperor is gone, and so is his Court. I came to ask what you want."],
		["Chiyou, War God of the Nine Li", "WANT? I want my people. I want the Nine Li. I want to go home. But first I want to fight, because I do not remember how to do anything else."],
	],
	"post:chiyou": [["Narrator", "The war god goes down on one knee in the trampled field, and the axes fall out of his hands into five thousand years of leaves."]],
	"pre:xingtian": [
		["Narrator", "Before the Gate of Opening, under a sky gone gold, dances a giant with no head: eyes in his chest, a mouth in his belly, a shield in one hand and an axe in the other."],
		["Xingtian, Who Dances Without a Head", "The Emperor took my head and I kept fighting. I will keep fighting until the sky falls. It nearly did, I hear. Pity."],
	],
	"post:xingtian": [
		["Narrator", "Xingtian stops dancing. Very slowly he sets down his shield and his axe, and the Gate of Opening swings wide behind him."],
		["Xingtian, Who Dances Without a Head", "...Five thousand years. My feet hurt."],
	],
	"pre:yu_shi": [
		["Narrator", "Rain falls at the Gate of Alarm so hard that every drop is a drumbeat. In the middle of it stands an old man with a bowl, tipping out a storm."],
		["Yu Shi, the Rain Master of Zhuolu", "I drowned an emperor's army once, for a friend. Nobody has asked me to stop since. Have you come to ask?"],
	],
	"post:yu_shi": [["Narrator", "The Rain Master tips his bowl upright, and the rain stops, and over the Gate of Alarm there is a rainbow, which seems to embarrass him."]],
	"pre:liang": [
		["Narrator", "At the Gate of Rest the black water lies perfectly still, and a soft voice sings from under it, a lullaby so gentle that Yue Ling yawns."],
		["Liang, Goblin of the Black Springs", "Sleep. Everyone sleeps here. Nobody wakes. It is very restful. Sleep..."],
	],
	"post:liang": [["Narrator", "The goblin's song breaks off in a gurgle, and the black water at the Gate of Rest begins, for the first time in five thousand years, to flow."]],
	"pre:bronze_brothers": [
		["Narrator", "Across the Gate of Death stands a wall of bronze heads and iron brows: the last of Chiyou's eighty-one brothers, shoulder to shoulder, chewing gravel."],
		["The Bronze-Headed Brothers of Chiyou", "WE HOLD. WE HOLD THE GATE. WE HAVE HELD IT SINCE ZHUOLU. WHAT DAY IS IT?"],
	],
	"post:bronze_brothers": [
		["Narrator", "One by one the brothers sit down in the road. The Gate of Death stands open behind them, and it turns out to lead only to a quiet field where the grass is long."],
		["The Bronze-Headed Brothers of Chiyou", "...Is he down there? Our brother? Tell him we held."],
	],
	"pre:red_banner": [
		["Narrator", "Over the Gate of Sight a red comet hangs low enough to touch, and in its light Lin Feng sees the war he fears most: Azure Cloud Mountain burning, and Yue Ling not there."],
		["Chiyou's Banner, the Red Comet", "Look. LOOK. This is what a war is. Every war. Look at it and tell me you will still walk in."],
	],
	"post:red_banner": [["Narrator", "The Red Comet dims to an ember and the vision goes out with it. Yue Ling is standing right there, frowning at him. The Gate of Sight opens on a perfectly ordinary sunset."]],
	"pre:mast_goblin": [
		["Narrator", "Beside the wreck of the Penglai-Seeker the old mainmast has grown a face, and arms, and a very bad temper, and the Gate of Closing hangs from its yard like a sail."],
		["Mei, Goblin of the Old Mast", "CLOSED. Closed, closed, closed. I held up a sail for two thousand years and nobody ever said thank you. Everything is closed."],
	],
	"post:mast_goblin": [["Narrator", "The old mast creaks, and something in the wood that has been clenched for two thousand years lets go. The Gate of Closing opens with the sound of a sail filling."]],
	"pre:fetter_maple": [
		["Narrator", "High in the gorge stands the oldest maple of all, its trunk still shaped like a shackle and red as the day it was forged. Its branches close across the Gate of Life like fingers."],
		["The Fetter-Maple of Chiyou", "I held him. I was made to hold him. If I let go, what am I?"],
	],
	"post:fetter_maple": [["Narrator", "The fetter-maple's branches open, and its leaves begin to fall, red and gold, for the first time in five thousand years. Beyond it the Gate of Life stands open, and smells of spring."]],
	"pre:kui_drum": [
		["Narrator", "On the slope below the broken pillar a great drum of grey hide beats by itself, and every beat is a thunderclap. The ground jumps. Yue Ling's hair stands on end."],
		["Narrator", "It was made to break an army's heart. It does not know how to do anything else."],
	],
	"post:kui_drum": [["Narrator", "The drum gives one last soft beat, like a heart settling, and goes quiet. The Gate of Harm opens behind it on the long road north."]],
	"s_p13_end": [
		["Narrator", "The gold bars fade from the sky one by one, and the Conclave of the Realms meets at last, under the Jianmu, on the sect's practice ground, because it is the only place flat enough for a dragon to sit down."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Nobody sits at the head of the table, because there is no head. Nobody keeps a ledger, because there is no ledger. Somebody will have to keep the minutes, though. Archivist?"],
		["Archivist Lan", "I have kept minutes since the Court burned its own. Pass me the brush. Rain-Minister, you are dripping on the treaty."],
		["Rain-Minister Ao Run", "It is a treaty about rain. It ought to be wet."],
		["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "The foxes propose that the Conclave meet every autumn, and that it never decide anything important, so that everybody keeps coming back to argue."],
		["Lantern-Magistrate Ming Yi", "The shades second it. We have all the time in the world, and nothing at all to do with it."],
	],
	"pre:s_break18": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng stands at the foot of the Jianmu with every road he has walked behind him: the steps of the sect, the bamboo forest, the Ladder of Heaven, the bare sea floor, the field of Zhuolu. The tribulation that gathers is all of them at once, every road asking which one he will choose."],
		["Yue Ling", "Which one?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I don't have to choose. That was the secret all along.", "laugh"],
	],
	"post:s_break18": [
		["Narrator", "Every road Lin Feng has walked runs through him at once, and none of them is the only right one, and the tribulation, finding nothing in him to divide, thins out into a clear autumn sky."],
		["Narrator", "He becomes the first cultivator of Myriad Daos as One: a realm in which every road leads somewhere worth going."],
		["Narrator", "The Conclave of the Realms sits down for the first time under the Jianmu, and argues happily about everything. Nobody is in charge of it. Auntie Zhou feeds everybody."],
	],
	# ---- Part XIV: The Ripening of Karma
	"recall12": [
		["Narrator", "The first Conclave goes on for nine days, and on the tenth everybody goes home. The sect is very quiet. Lin Feng sweeps the steps in the evening, and the broom keeps catching on something that is not there."],
		["Narrator", "It is a thread. Then a dozen of them: fine as spider silk and warm, running from his wrists out over the edge of the mountain in every direction, and every one of them pulling, very gently, south-west."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, your hands. There is something tied to them.", "shock"],
		["Lin Feng", "To both of us. Look at yours."],
	],
	"s_p14_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Cause and effect. Every cultivator who climbs far enough meets them at last: everything you set moving on the road comes home, the good and the bad, and asks to be settled."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Most of the threads are light. A herb you picked, a bell you mended. But a few of them were people, and those are heavy, because you changed which way their lives ran."],
		["Lin Feng", "Tala and her herd. Qiu Ye. Iron Fang's boy. Xu Mo's daughter. The girl in the Hag's hut.", "think"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And at the far end of every thread that was cut instead of tied, something has been feeding. The old books call it Nie: the shadow of everything owed and never paid. It gathers where debts are reckoned."],
		["Yue Ling", "The Wheel of the Six Paths."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Go down the funeral road and ask Judge Cui what is waiting there. Then go and find your five. Whatever you made of them, they are part of you now, and they are going to be part of what happens.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p14_judge": [
		["Narrator", "The Wheel turns slowly, the way it should. But round its rim something has settled like soot: a darkness made of tally-slips and broken oaths and the ends of a thousand quarrels."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Every grudge the dead cannot carry over the bridge, they leave here, and the Wheel grinds it small. It has always been so. But this year the grudges have learned to stand up."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Look into the Karma Mirror. Your five threads run straight into the heart of it, and something is pulling on them from the other end. Nie. It is as old as the first unpaid debt, and you have been feeding it all the way up the mountain."],
		["Lin Feng", "Every foe I put down. Every promise I made in a hurry."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "And every one you kept. It cannot eat those. Thin out the grudges round the rim before they find the funeral road; then go and settle your threads. A debt that is paid cannot be eaten.", "nod"],
	],
	"s_p14_mirror": [
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Your five are settled, one way or another. Good. Now look into the Mirror again, and look past them."],
		["Narrator", "In the Karma Mirror the Wheel turns and the soot round its rim is thinner. But six threads of it never come to the Wheel at all: they run back up the funeral road and away across the world, and every one ends somewhere Lin Feng remembers."],
		["Narrator", "The bamboo below the sect. The red water of the marsh. The graves of the sword saints. A dry riverbed under Tushan. The aeries of Juntian. A wreck on the slope of the Returning Ruins."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Most of what the dead are owed comes down here, and the Wheel grinds it small. These six would not come. Their owners have gone over the bridge, but what they were owed is still owed, and it has taken root where they fell. Nie drinks from all six."],
		["Lin Feng", "Iron Fang. The Marsh Hag. The Headless Saint. Fangfeng. Kui. The Admiral. I put every one of them down.", "think"],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "And never asked them anything. Go to each where it fell. A grudge does not listen until it has spent itself, so it will fight you; when it is spent, ask it what it was owed. Pay it if you can. Whatever you leave unpaid, Nie will be holding when you come back.", "point"],
		["Yue Ling", "Six fights with six people we already beat once."],
		["Lin Feng", "We beat them. That part is done. It's the asking that was left over."],
	],
	# the grudges that stayed (grudges.gd): their last words before the fight and once they are spent;
	# the choice of what to do about each follows the "post:" lines
	"pre:grudge_iron_fang": [
		["Iron Fang's Grudge", "The sweeper. You swept me off this road once. Has the sect sent you back to sweep up what's left?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I came to ask what you were owed."],
		["Iron Fang's Grudge", "Then hold out your hands the way I held out mine at that gate, and see how far asking gets you."],
	],
	"post:grudge_iron_fang": [
		["Narrator", "The grudge comes apart like wet paper. Under it, sitting in the bamboo litter with his knees drawn up, is a boy of twelve with a gap where a tooth should be."],
		["Iron Fang's Grudge", "They never tested me. Not even with the stone. The gate disciple looked at my father's patched sleeves and sent me down the steps, and I never knew if there was anything in me to test."],
	],
	"pre:grudge_marsh_hag": [
		["The Marsh Hag's Grudge", "Another bright young root come for my lotuses. They are not for sale. They were never for sale. Nobody ever paid for them."],
		["Yue Ling", "She's reading out names. Settlers' names, and sums of money."],
		["The Marsh Hag's Grudge", "Thirty-six families and not one copper. Pay or drown, cultivator. Those are the terms."],
	],
	"post:grudge_marsh_hag": [
		["Narrator", "The red water stills. The grudge sits down on the bank among the blood lotuses: a stooped woman in a wet shawl with a ledger open on her knees, running her finger down a list that never ends."],
		["The Marsh Hag's Grudge", "I kept them alive a whole winter on lotus-root and willow bark. The Court sent them, and the Court never paid, and when the water took them nobody came to say sorry. I don't even remember my own name. It was at the top of the ledger once."],
	],
	"pre:grudge_headless_saint": [
		["Narrator", "Among the graves of the sword saints a figure without a head salutes Lin Feng in the old way: sword raised, heels together. The bow is perfect. It is the bow Lin Feng learned on the steps of the sect."],
		["Yue Ling", "He doesn't want to hurt you. He just has nothing to say it with except the sword."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I'll answer the same way. Sword Saint, I am of your school."],
	],
	"post:grudge_headless_saint": [
		["Narrator", "The saint lowers his blade, kneels, and writes in the grave-dust with one finger, slowly, like a man who has not held a brush in a thousand years."],
		["Narrator", "A name, his own, in the old script. Beneath it, smaller: Why was it taken down?"],
	],
	"pre:grudge_fangfeng": [
		["Fangfeng's Grudge", "STOP. STOP AND LISTEN. There's a village under the dyke and the water is coming and I have to tell somebody!"],
		["Yue Ling", "It thinks the flood is still coming. It has been standing in that breach for four thousand years."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let's help it put the water down first."],
	],
	"post:grudge_fangfeng": [
		["Narrator", "The giant's grudge goes down on one knee in the dry riverbed and, for the first time in four thousand years, lets go of the water that is not there. It is panting like a man who has run a very long way."],
		["Fangfeng's Grudge", "I was late. I know I was late. They were cutting off my head before I could tell them why."],
	],
	"pre:grudge_kui": [
		["Narrator", "The thunderhead over the aeries comes down on one leg with a sound like the first beat of a war drum, and every wind bell in Juntian rings at once."],
		["Kui's Grudge", "BOOM. BOOM. Before you can beat me, I beat you. BOOM."],
		["Yue Ling", "It isn't angry. It's frightened. Every thunderclap still sounds like the drum to it."],
	],
	"post:grudge_kui": [
		["Narrator", "The thunder-ox's grudge settles on the cloud like a tired animal, its one leg folded under it, its great sides heaving. Far off in the Eastern Sea, something it remembers smells of salt."],
		["Kui's Grudge", "I used to bellow because I was happy. Now I bellow first. Before the drum."],
	],
	"pre:grudge_admiral": [
		["Narrator", "On the tilted deck of the wrecked flagship a shadow in an admiral's coat stands at the wheel, holding a course for a shore it will never reach. When it turns it has no face, only the shape of somebody listening very hard."],
		["The Admiral's Grudge", "You came from the east. From the isles. Did you see her? A girl of eleven with a red ribbon. They called the names on the beach. Did anybody call hers?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I'll tell you what I know. But you'll have to let go of the wheel first."],
	],
	"post:grudge_admiral": [
		["Narrator", "The Admiral's grudge lets go of the wheel. It has never let go of it before, and its hands do not know what to do."],
		["The Admiral's Grudge", "Lingbo. Eleven years old. She had a red ribbon, and she was afraid of the sea. I told her she would be home within the year."],
	],
	"pre:nie": [
		["Narrator", "At the heart of the Wheel, where the six paths meet, the soot of a thousand grudges stands up and becomes a shape: no face, many hands, and in every hand a tally of something Lin Feng owes."],
		["Nie, the Unpaid", "Three thousand foes you put down on the road up. Did you ever ask what any of them was owed? I asked. I remember every one."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is speaking with their voices. The bamboo wolves. The bandits. The Hag."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let them speak. I will pay what I can, and stand for the rest."],
	],
	"post:nie": [
		["Narrator", "Nie comes apart the way a quarrel does when somebody finally says sorry: not all at once, but thread by thread, each tally burning down to a grey ash that the Wheel takes in and grinds and turns, at last, into something new."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Paid. Or forgiven, which in my courts comes to the same thing. Go home, Lin Feng. The Wheel will take it from here."],
		["Yue Ling", "The threads on my wrists are gone. No. Not gone. They just do not pull any more."],
	],
	"s_p14_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I watched the sky over the Wheel clear from the gate. Every thread on the mountain went slack at once, like a bell rope when the ringing stops."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Whatever you made of your five, it is done now and it is yours. That is what a cultivator becomes at the end of the road: not stronger, only settled."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I think it is time I was settled too.", "nod"],
	],
	"pre:s_break19": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng sits on the bottom step of the sect, where he sat the day he came, with every thread of the journey lying slack across his knees. The tribulation that gathers is not lightning: it is every face he met on the road, waiting to hear what he will say."],
		["Yue Ling", "What will you say?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Thank you, mostly. And I am sorry, to some. And goodbye to nobody."],
	],
	"post:s_break19": [
		["Narrator", "Every cause he set moving has met its effect, and nothing is owed either way. The faces nod, one by one, and go back to their own roads."],
		["Narrator", "He becomes the first cultivator of Karma Fulfilled: a realm in which the threads of other lives still run through yours, and none of them pulls."],
		["Narrator", "The Conclave of the Realms meets every autumn after that, under the Jianmu, and argues happily about everything. Nobody is in charge of it. Auntie Zhou feeds everybody."],
		["Narrator", "And on Azure Cloud Mountain, in the cool of the evening, Lin Feng sweeps the steps of the sect, which are the first steps of every road, because somebody should."],
	],
	# ---- Part XV: The Thread She Never Had (the Lotus release; bonds.gd)
	"recall13": [
		["Narrator", "For nine days after the last tribulation nothing on Azure Cloud Mountain is owed to anybody, and Lin Feng sleeps the way he has not slept since he was six."],
		["Narrator", "On the tenth morning Disciple Han brings the Elder's tea and sets out two cups. He stops with the pot in the air and frowns at the second one."],
		["Senior Brother Han", "Elder, forgive me. Who is the second cup for?"],
		["Yue Ling", "It is for me, Han. It has been for me every morning for ten years.", "shock"],
		["Narrator", "Han looks at her and smiles politely, the way you smile at a guest whose name you are sure you ought to know."],
	],
	"s_p15_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Sit, both of you. No, Yue Ling, sit. I mean you most of all. Forgive an old man: this morning, for the space of a breath, I could not remember your name, and I have known it longer than anybody living."],
		["Yue Ling", "Everybody is doing it, Elder. The cook gave me a bowl and then asked if I was lost. The cats still know me. I am clinging to the cats.", "laugh"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "When every thread in the world went slack at the Wheel, one thing had no thread to go slack. You were woven, child, not born. Everything you did on the road was counted in Lin Feng's column, because the Seal was his."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "While there were debts, the world needed you to help carry them. Now there are none, and the world is very tidy. It is putting away what it has no use for. Gently. But it is putting you away."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then we stop it.", "shake"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You cannot stop tidiness, Lin Feng. I have tried for sixty years with this study. But a thing that has a place does not get put away. She needs a place in the world that is hers: a page in the Book of Life and Death."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Sit with her by the lotus pond where she woke, first. Then go down the funeral road to Judge Cui. If anybody can find a page for somebody who was never born, it is the clerk of the dead.", "point"],
		["Yue Ling", "The pond. Yes. I would like to see it while I still remember it.", "nod"],
	],
	"s_p15_judge": [
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Lin Feng. And... a moment... Yue Ling. I had to look twice. You are fading out of the Karma Mirror, healer, like breath off bronze.", "shock"],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "The Book of Life and Death has a page for everybody who was ever born, and for nobody else. You were woven by the Founder out of the Seal's own lotus. There is nothing to write you on."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then write me in the margin. I do not take up much room."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Ha. The margins are full of my notes. But listen. A page is not made; it is grown. Three things grow one: a past that is your own, people who remember you and tie their threads to yours, and a first line you write yourself.", "think"],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "Your past was not lost, healer. Every time a bearer died, the Seal was unwoven and woven again, and what you remembered was pressed out of you like a flower in a book. The Founder kept the pressings. Ask her where."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Founder has been dead for three hundred years."],
		["Judge Cui Jue", "So is half my staff. Go to her barrow in the Graveyard of Sword Saints. But hurry: something has been unpicking the old weave there, and feeding on what comes loose. Moths with no faces and threads that walk.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p15_founder": [
		["Narrator", "Beside the Founder's barrow, where Lin Feng once earned her blessing with a sword, a woman in white sits on the grass threading a needle against the light. She is not quite there. Neither, these days, is Yue Ling."],
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "There you are. I gave you a rose robe because I thought you would grow into it. You have.", "nod"],
		["Yue Ling", "Founder. Mother? I do not know what to call you."],
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "Call me Qing Lian. Mother is a name you earn by staying, and I did not stay. I wove you so that the bearer would never face the end alone, and then I died and left you to do it twenty times.", "grieve"],
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "Every time a bearer died, the Seal came apart and was woven again, and you came up out of the pond with your name and nothing else. I could not bear to throw the rest away. So I pressed it."],
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "What you remembered of each of them, I pressed where the two of you had been happiest: a flute stone, a jetty of lanterns, a kite post on the tea hills, a green under lanterns. Seven pressings are still whole. The rest have been eaten."],
		["Lin Feng", "By the moths.", "think"],
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "By what woke them. When the Seal became a door, the part of it that was a lock was left with nothing to keep shut. It calls itself the Warden now, and it is unweaving her thread by thread, to take her back into the lock where she can never be lost."],
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "Give her back five of the pressings. Ask the people who knew her to tie a thread for her. Then the Warden will have to come out and face you, because she will be too much a person to be a key.", "point"],
		["Yue Ling", "Will you be here when we come back?"],
		["Echo of Founder Qing Lian", "I am an echo, child. Mostly a needle and a habit. But I will try."],
	],
	"pre:forgetting_queen": [
		["Narrator", "In the mulberry woods the silk moths have gone grey and silent. In the middle of them sits their queen, as big as a cart, with a face like a blank page."],
		["The Moth-Queen of Forgetting", "So many little names. So many little days. Give them to me, healer, and nobody will ever hurt you by forgetting them. Nothing hurts once it is gone."],
		["Yue Ling", "Moths go to the light. It is the one thing they cannot forget. Fire, Lin Feng!", "point"],
	],
	"post:forgetting_queen": [
		["Narrator", "The queen falls apart into a snow of grey scales, and out of the scales, all over the woods, come the things the moths had eaten: a child's song, the smell of rain on a roof, a woodcutter's laugh."],
		["Yue Ling", "That laugh was Ah Zhu's. I know it was. She is still in there somewhere, Lin Feng.", "laugh"],
	],
	"pre:unweaver": [
		["Narrator", "On the Loom of Borders, where every thread of every road was once woven, something with a hundred thin hands is winding thread back onto a spindle as big as a tree. Every thread is pink. Every thread is hers."],
		["The Unweaver", "Every thread that was ever spun runs back to a spindle. I am the hand that winds it home. Hold still, little weave. I am only taking you back, stitch by stitch."],
		["Yue Ling", "Not one more stitch.", "shake"],
	],
	"post:unweaver": [
		["Narrator", "The Unweaver's hundred hands go slack, and the threads it was winding run out of the spindle like water: hundreds of them, fine and pink, and every one of them finds its way back to Yue Ling's wrists."],
		["Yue Ling", "They are mine. Lin Feng, look. They are coming back.", "shock"],
	],
	"pre:lock_warden": [
		["Narrator", "Under the Founder's Last Cave, where the mountain goes down to the root of the sect's pond, the Warden waits: a shape of bronze leaves and lotus petals, with a keyhole where its heart should be."],
		["Suo, Warden of the Lock", "Three hundred years I kept the door. Three hundred years she came back to me, every time, and I made her new. Now the door stands open and nobody needs a lock. Give her back. In the lock she will never be forgotten, never be tidied away, never be lost again."],
		["Yue Ling", "In the lock I would never be anything at all. I would rather be forgotten as myself than kept as something else."],
		["Lin Feng", "You heard her.", "nod"],
	],
	"post:lock_warden": [
		["Suo, Warden of the Lock", "...A door that is held open by somebody who chooses to stand in it. I never thought of that. I was only ever made to think about shutting."],
		["Narrator", "The Warden unlocks itself. Leaf by bronze leaf it comes apart and goes down into the pond as lotus seeds, and the last thing to go is the keyhole, which closes."],
		["Yue Ling", "It was only frightened. Like me. Like everybody.", "grieve"],
	],
	"s_p15_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Yue Ling. Yue Ling, Yue Ling. I have said it a hundred times this morning to be sure of it, and it stays. Welcome home, child. Properly, this time.", "bow"],
		["Yue Ling", "The cook gave me two bowls this morning and called me by my name, and then scolded me for being thin. It was the best thing that has ever happened to me.", "laugh"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And you, Lin Feng. There is something new in your qi. Every cultivator I ever taught climbed alone; the books say that is what the path is. You have been climbing with somebody beside you the whole way, and it shows."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "One more tribulation, I think. The last one is always a mirror. Go and look into it together.", "point"],
	],
	"pre:s_break20": [
		["Narrator", "The last tribulation does not come down out of the sky. It comes up out of the lotus pond: two reflections, his and hers, standing on the water and waiting to see whether the two of them will agree."],
		["Yue Ling", "Together, then. Do not step on my feet."],
		["Lin Feng", "No promises."],
	],
	"post:s_break20": [
		["Narrator", "The reflections bow to each other and go down into the pond, and the pond is only a pond again, with lotuses on it and two people standing at its edge."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng becomes the first cultivator of Kindred Dao. Nobody, the old books say, reaches the top of the mountain alone. He finds the books were right, and that it was never meant as a warning."],
		["Narrator", "And on Azure Cloud Mountain, in the cool of the evening, two people sweep the steps of the sect together, and argue about the right way to hold a broom, and neither of them is ever forgotten again."],
	],
	# ---- Part XVI: The Year Turns (the Living Land release)
	"recall14": [
		["Narrator", "The naming feast goes on for three days, and then Azure Cloud Mountain goes quiet in a way it has not been quiet for years. The first snow comes. The road up from Cloudfoot fills with carts of cabbages, firewood and red paper."],
		["Narrator", "Auntie Zhou hangs sausages from every beam of the kitchen. Little Bai sweeps the whole sect from the gate to the hall, because the last sweeping of the year sweeps out the bad luck, and it has been a very long year."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, what happens at the end of a year? I have never had one end. Mine just stopped, and started again somewhere else."],
		["Lin Feng", "You eat too much, you stay up all night, and in the morning everybody is a year older and pretends to be surprised."],
		["Narrator", "On the twenty-third of the last month, the Little New Year, every stove in the land sends its Kitchen God up the chimney to tell Heaven how the household has behaved. In the sect's kitchen the smoke goes up. The Kitchen God does not."],
	],
	"s_p16_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Lin Feng. Auntie Zhou came to me this morning as white as rice flour. She burned the paper Kitchen God off the kitchen wall, as her mother taught her, and smeared his lips with malt sugar so he would say only sweet things in Heaven."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And he climbed down out of the smoke and asked her for a cup of tea."],
		["Lin Feng", "Is that... unusual?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "In three hundred years? Yes, Lin Feng. It is unusual. He is still sitting on her stove. He says he will speak only to the sweeper who swept the sky."],
		["Yue Ling", "A god who asks for tea. I like him already."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Go gently. Every household in the land has a god in its kitchen, and they all talk to each other up the chimneys. Whatever he has to say, the whole world has already heard it."],
	],
	"s_p16_kitchen": [
		["Narrator", "A small round old man in a soot-black robe sits on the edge of the stove with his feet dangling, a cup of tea in both hands and a ledger under his arm. His beard is singed, and his lips are sticky with malt sugar."],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "Sweeper. Sit. Every kitchen in the land has a god in it, and tonight every one of us is saying the same thing up every chimney at once: Nian is awake."],
		["Lin Feng", "Nian. The Year Beast. Granny Wen used to tell us about it: it comes down out of the mountains on New Year's Eve to eat the harvest, and it is afraid of three things."],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "Red, fire and noise. Good boy. For a thousand years the people kept it off with red paper on the doors, firecrackers in the streets and every pot in the house banged till dawn. It sleeps all year in the deep places, comes out for one night, finds the doors red and the streets loud, and goes home hungry."],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "But it does not eat rice, sweeper. It eats fear. And this was the longest, most frightening year the world has ever had. The sky fell. Nine days of noon. Floods, a tree in the night, the dead queueing at the gate, a girl nobody could remember. Nian has eaten all of it in its sleep. It has never been this big."],
		["Yue Ling", "And the towns? They will make their noise, surely?"],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "They forgot. The paper-cutters of Longmen fled the flood. Tushan cast cauldrons all year instead of firecrackers. Nobody in Yanggu wrote a couplet, because there was no night to hang one in. It is all in my ledger. And before Nian comes Xi, its other half: the Last Night."],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "On New Year's Eve Xi comes first and eats the last light of the year, every lamp left burning. Then, in the dark, at midnight, Nian."],
		["Lin Feng", "How long have we got?"],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "Seven days. Remind them, sweeper: red, fire, noise. And on the night itself, let nobody be alone. In a thousand years Nian has never once eaten a family that stayed up together."],
	],
	"s_p16_doorgods": [
		["Narrator", "Under the greatest peach tree in the Garden of Immortal Peaches, where its roots grip a gate of old black stone, two armoured giants sit on a bench eating peaches. One has a face as red as a lacquer box, the other one as dark as an inkstone. Their portraits are on half the doors in the land."],
		["Shenshu, Door God of the Peach Tree", "The sweeper. We wondered when you would come. Every New Year for three thousand years we have stood on people's doors, and every New Year the people pasted us up fresh. This year nobody did. We were bored. We came home."],
		["Yulei, Door God of the Peach Tree", "Under this tree is the gate the ghosts go in and out of. We judge them at dawn: any that did harm in the night we tie up with reeds and feed to the tiger. Peach wood frightens everything that comes out of the dark. That is why people hung boards of it on their doors, with our names on."],
		["Yue Ling", "That is where the red couplets came from. The first ones were boards of peach wood."],
		["Shenshu, Door God of the Peach Tree", "She knows. Good girl. Then take these: cut from the old wood and carved with our names. Nail one up where Xi wants to get through, and see what Xi thinks of it."],
		["Narrator", "Yulei hands Lin Feng two boards of red peach wood, carved with their two names and still smelling of the fruit."],
		["Yulei, Door God of the Peach Tree", "And paste us up again when you get home. It is very dull, being a god nobody puts on a door."],
	],
	"s_p16_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "A good new year, Lin Feng. A good new year, Yue Ling. Every door in the sect is red, the Kitchen God has gone up the chimney at last with sugar on his lips, and I am told Auntie Zhou has not stopped cooking since yesterday."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "When I was a disciple, the old Elder used to say that the hardest thing a cultivator ever does is not to fly, nor to fight, nor to live a thousand years. It is to come home for New Year."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I think I have done the hardest thing, Elder."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Not quite. Every lamp you kept lit this year is a hearth somewhere, with a family round it. Go and sit with that a while. The last tribulation of the year is the gentlest one. It only asks whether you were there."],
	],
	"pre:xi_beast": [
		["Narrator", "The Dawn Market is the brightest town in the land on the last night of the year, and one by one its lanterns are going out. Something long and dark lies coiled round the lantern-maker's pole at the crossroads, and the light runs down into it like water down a drain."],
		["Xi, the Last Night", "Every year the last light of the year is mine. It is the only meal I am given. This year they left so many lamps burning. How kind. How very kind."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is not a meal. It is people's light. They lit it so they would not be alone in the dark."],
		["Xi, the Last Night", "Then they should not have lit so much of it."],
	],
	"post:xi_beast": [
		["Narrator", "Xi uncoils from the pole and slides back into the dark between the stalls, smaller now, and hissing. The lanterns of Yanggu flare up again all at once, and the market cheers so loudly that they must hear it in Qingshi."],
		["Narrator", "But the night does not move. The stars stand where they stood an hour ago. Xi has eaten the hours with the light, and until the year itself is driven out, its last night will not end."],
		["Night-Watchman Lao Zhou", "Second watch. Still the second watch. I have called it six times, young man. Go to your family. Nobody should be alone tonight."],
	],
	"pre:nian": [
		["Narrator", "At midnight the mountains above Qingshi move. Something comes down through the terraces bigger than the Hall of Azure Clouds: a horned head like a lion's, a hide of green scales, a mane of old red flame, and eyes that have watched the whole frightening year from underneath it."],
		["Nian, the Year Beast", "I am the year. All of it. Every fear you had in it, I ate. The sky that fell. The noon that would not end. The flood. The tree. The dead at the gate. The girl nobody remembered. I am so full of it, and still so hungry."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Then have some noise! Qingshi! POTS!"],
		["Lin Feng", "You are not the year. You are only the fear of it. The year was also the people who stayed."],
	],
	"post:nian": [
		["Narrator", "Nian rears up into the firecracker smoke and roars, and the whole of Qingshi roars back: woks, gongs, ladles, a temple bell, two hundred people and one very determined grandmother."],
		["Narrator", "And the beast, which has eaten every fear of the longest year in the world, finds there is nothing left in the village to eat. It turns and goes back up the mountain, smaller with every step, until it is the size of a dog, and then of a cat, and then it is only a red paper lantern rolling down the lane."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng. Look. The stars are moving."],
		["Narrator", "Somewhere a cock crows, much too early, and the old year goes out with it."],
	],
	"pre:s_break21": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation comes on New Year's Day, and it does not come from the sky. It comes from every lit window in the land at once: ten thousand small lights, each one a family that stayed up together, each one asking him quietly to come in out of the cold."],
		["Yue Ling", "They are all inviting you in, Lin Feng. Every one of them."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then we had better not be rude."],
	],
	"post:s_break21": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng goes in at every door at once, is welcomed at every table, and the lights settle round him like a warm coat. He becomes a cultivator of Ten Thousand Hearths, and for the first time in his life the whole world feels like somebody's kitchen."],
		["Yue Ling", "Happy new year, Lin Feng."],
		["Lin Feng", "Happy new year, Yue Ling. Your first."],
		["Yue Ling", "Our first."],
	],
	# ---- Part XVII: The Spring Thunder (the Spring Thunder release)
	"recall15": [
		["Narrator", "The Lantern Festival ends, and the almanac turns its page. Lichun, the beginning of spring, comes and goes on paper. On Azure Cloud Mountain the snow does not even begin to think about it."],
		["Narrator", "Every morning the frost is thicker than the night before. The plum blossom cut for the reunion table has frozen on its branches, pink under glass. Down in Qingshi the ploughs stand in their sheds, and the ground under the snow rings like an anvil when you stamp on it."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, is spring always this late? I have woken by the pond in spring before. I remember the frogs. There are no frogs."],
		["Lin Feng", "Granny Wen says spring is like a guest. You sweep the doorstep and leave the gate open, and one morning it is sitting in your kitchen eating your noodles."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then somebody has forgotten to open the gate."],
	],
	"s_p17_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Lin Feng. The almanac says it is spring. Either the almanac is wrong, or the world has stopped reading it."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Every year at Lichun the villages make a Spring Ox out of the clay of their own fields, paint it in the five colours and whip it to pieces, so that the ground knows it is time to be ploughed. This year Qingshi was too busy seeing the old year out to make one. So was everybody."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And last night a young man came to the gate. He would not come in. He had a willow switch in his hand and the shadow of a swallow on the snow behind him, though there was no moon to cast it. He asked for the sweeper."],
		["Lin Feng", "A swallow's shadow. The swallows come back in spring."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "When they can find it. He said he would wait in Qingshi, by the ox-pen. I believe he is Gou Mang, Lord of Spring, who walks in front of the Spring Ox in every Lichun procession in the land. I have seen his face on a thousand paper prints. I never expected to see it at my gate, looking worried."],
		["Yue Ling", "A god of spring who is worried. That cannot be good."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "No. Go down to the village, both of you. And take your sword, Lin Feng. If spring is stuck somewhere, I suspect it will have to be cut loose."],
	],
	"s_p17_goumang": [
		["Narrator", "Beside Ah Niu's ox-pen, where the village's Spring Ox should have stood a month ago, a young man in a green robe sits on the fence with his knees drawn up. He has the round, solemn face of the almanac prints, and behind him on the snow the shadow of a swallow is flying, though he sits quite still."],
		["Gou Mang, Lord of Spring", "The sweeper, and the Seal's lotus. Good. I am Gou Mang. Every spring I walk in front of the ox and open the first furrow of the year, and the world wakes up behind me. For a month now I have been walking in front of nothing."],
		["Gou Mang, Lord of Spring", "Spring is not a season, sweeper. It is a door, and somebody has to push it. The ox has to be whipped, so the earth knows to open. On the second day of the second month the dragon has to lift his head out of the water, so the rain can fall. The thunder has to roll, so everything asleep in the ground wakes up. And the frost has to agree to go home."],
		["Lin Feng", "And none of it has happened."],
		["Gou Mang, Lord of Spring", "None of it. The longest winter the world ever had has left the land tangled shut: bamboo the snow broke lying across every path, rocks the frost split off the hills lying in the channels, ice jammed in the rivers. And Qing Nu, the Maiden of Frost, will not take her frost back. Every night she lays it down again, thicker."],
		["Yue Ling", "Why would the frost not want to go home?"],
		["Gou Mang, Lord of Spring", "Ask her. She will not answer me. I am the one who always tells her it is time to go."],
		["Gou Mang, Lord of Spring", "Here. My switch, cut from the first willow that ever came into leaf. I have whipped the Spring Ox with it for three thousand years. This year you whip it. The village made one at last: Ah Niu built it from the clay of the fields and painted it the five colours. Hit it hard. The ground is listening."],
		["Narrator", "Gou Mang puts a switch of green willow into Lin Feng's hand. It is warm, as if it had been lying in the sun."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_ox": [
		["Narrator", "On the fifth blow the Spring Ox bursts apart in a spray of painted clay and grain, and the whole village dives for the pieces: a shard of the ox on the windowsill keeps the house lucky all year, and a handful of its clay in the field makes the rice grow."],
		["Ah Niu the Ox-Herd", "Green on the head: a wet spring! Yellow on the belly: a good harvest! Granny, look, I got a horn!"],
		["Gou Mang, Lord of Spring", "There. The ground heard that. Now the roads, sweeper: nothing can get to the fields while the paths are full of fallen bamboo. Then the terraces, then the river. Spring is a lot of carrying."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_deadfall": [
		["Narrator", "The last trunk splits under the sword and rolls away down the slope, and the carts that have waited at the edge of the bamboo since the New Year creak forward at once, loaded with seed rice and dung and a great deal of opinion about the state of the roads."],
		["Yue Ling", "You are very good at cutting things down, Lin Feng. Three years with a broom and now the whole forest sweeps itself out of your way."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_rockfall": [
		["Narrator", "With the last rockfall broken, the meltwater that has been backing up behind the stones all month finds its way into the channels and runs down the Cloud-Tea Terraces from step to step, loud and silver."],
		["Narrator", "And with the water, from the high snow above the terraces, come the things that were living in the cold: the wisps of rime, the white hares, and a thin grey shape like a widow in a veil that leaves frost wherever it stops."],
		["Gou Mang, Lord of Spring", "The cold that comes back. Every spring it tries once more. Drive it up into Frostpeak where it belongs, and do not let it touch you first: it is quicker than it looks."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_hunt": [
		["Narrator", "The last of the white things goes back up into the high snow of Frostpeak, and the terraces below are wet and dark and steaming in the sun. In the caverns above, though, the frost is thicker than ever, and the icicles at the mouth of the deepest cave are as long as spears."],
		["Yue Ling", "Somebody up there is still combing out the winter. I can hear it, Lin Feng. Like a comb going through very long hair."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_ice": [
		["Narrator", "The ice jams break up one after another and go down through the Dragon Gate in a long grinding procession, and the river behind them, which had been rising all month against the ice, sinks back into its bed with a sigh you can hear from the ferry."],
		["Harbourmistress Jiang Yao", "Another week and that would have come over the dykes all at once and taken the fields of Gun with it, like in the old flood. I owe you a boat. I owe you a fleet."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then lend me one in summer. Somebody has been promised the sea."],
	],
	"pre:qing_nu": [
		["Narrator", "In the deepest cavern of Frostpeak, where the ice is blue all the way through, a woman in white sits on a bench of frozen snow, combing the frost out of her long hair. Every hair that falls becomes a night's frost somewhere in the land. She has been combing for a month."],
		["Qing Nu, Maiden of Frost", "The sweeper who swept the sky. You have come to tell me it is time to go home. Gou Mang tells me so every year, very politely."],
		["Yue Ling", "We came to ask why you will not."],
		["Qing Nu, Maiden of Frost", "...Nobody has ever asked. Look at the land, little lotus. This year the sky fell. The sun stood still. The dead walked home. A girl was nearly unpicked from the world. Every grave and every burnt field and every empty cradle in the land is under my snow tonight, and under my snow none of it hurts."],
		["Qing Nu, Maiden of Frost", "If I take it away they wake up into all of it again. I am only keeping them asleep a little longer. Is that so cruel?"],
	],
	"post:qing_nu": [
		["Narrator", "Qing Nu lowers her comb. Somewhere far below, a drop of water falls from an icicle, and then another."],
		["Qing Nu, Maiden of Frost", "You opened the roads. You cleared the channels. You whipped the ox, and the ground heard it. They will wake into a land somebody has made ready for them. ...I did not think anybody would."],
		["Lin Feng", "Come back in autumn. The plum will want you in winter. It flowers in the cold."],
		["Qing Nu, Maiden of Frost", "The plum. Yes. It is the only thing that ever thanked me."],
		["Narrator", "She gathers her hair over one shoulder and walks into the blue ice, and the ice lets her in. When Lin Feng and Yue Ling come out of the caverns the snow on the path has gone to slush, and there is a smell in the air like wet earth, and far away, very faint, a frog."],
	],
	"s_p17_envoy": [
		["Envoy Ao Lan", "Sweeper. You have come about the rain. Everybody has come about the rain. The farmers have come, the tea-pickers, the ferrymen, the Rain Ministry has sent four letters, one of them rude."],
		["Envoy Ao Lan", "On the second day of the second month the old Rain Dragon who sleeps under this palace lifts his head out of the deep, and the spring rain falls behind him. This year he cannot lift it. In the long frost the deep water froze into chains of black ice round his neck, and he is too old and too proud to call for help."],
		["Yue Ling", "That is why the barbers in Qingshi have been sitting in the lane with nobody to shave. You cut your hair on the day the dragon raises its head, for luck. And the dragon has not raised it."],
		["Envoy Ao Lan", "Four chains. Iron would have been easier: the ice is the Maiden's, and it does not want to break. Break them, and do not be offended by anything he says when his head is free. He has been looking at the floor for a month."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_chains": [
		["Narrator", "The last chain of black ice snaps, and something enormous and old moves in the deep water under the palace. A head as long as a ferry boat rises out of the sea of the Sunken Palace, whiskers trailing weed, and opens one gold eye the size of a window."],
		["The Old Rain Dragon", "...I was not stuck. I was resting. A dragon does not get stuck."],
		["Yue Ling", "Of course not. You were resting very hard."],
		["The Old Rain Dragon", "Hmph. Tell the barbers they may begin."],
		["Narrator", "He lifts his head towards the surface, and far above, over the whole of the Unbroken Land, the first rain of spring begins to fall: soft, warm, the kind that makes the ground smell. But no thunder comes with it."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_eggs": [
		["Lei Gong, Duke of Thunder", "Five. Five thunder eggs, and every one of them still warm from the strike that laid it. Look at the star inside that one, sweeper. That is a thunderbolt from the spring my grandfather drummed."],
		["Lei Gong, Duke of Thunder", "Now come up to the drum before the rain stops. The first thunder of spring goes out at dusk, in the rain. That is when the frogs are listening."],
	],
	"s_p17_leigong": [
		["Narrator", "On the highest crag of Thunder Valley a great blue man with a bird's wings and a crest of gold feathers sits among a ring of drums, hitting them with a mallet. Every drum is cracked. Every blow comes out as a dull wooden clonk, like somebody knocking on a door nobody will answer."],
		["Lei Gong, Duke of Thunder", "The sweeper who would not give me a list. You see what has become of me. The rain is falling, and I cannot drum a single thunder to go with it."],
		["Lei Gong, Duke of Thunder", "The first thunder of spring is the one that matters. Jingzhe, the Waking of Insects: one roll of it and everything asleep in the ground wakes up, the seeds and the worms and the beetles and the frogs. Without it they will sleep through the spring and wake in summer, and nothing will be sown."],
		["Lin Feng", "What happened to your drums?"],
		["Lei Gong, Duke of Thunder", "The frost got into the drumheads all winter and split them, every one. New heads are made from thunder eggs, the round stones the lightning leaves where it strikes. This valley is full of them. I used to know where. I struck everybody and forgot to look."],
		["Yue Ling", "We will find them. Five?"],
		["Lei Gong, Duke of Thunder", "Five. And then you will drum it with me. I have done the first thunder alone for three thousand years. It would be nice, this once, to have somebody to strike with."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_petty": [
		["Narrator", "Granny Wen and the women of Qingshi stand under the bridge with their sleeves rolled up, old shoes in their hands, and beat the paper petty men until there is nothing left of them but confetti, chanting the old rhyme the whole while: beat the petty man's head, and he will never raise it; beat his mouth, and he will never gossip."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Every year on the day of the first thunder, sweeper. The snakes and the scorpions wake up, and so do the people who want to make trouble for you. You beat them out of the year before they get going. Your sword is very good for it. Better than a shoe."],
		["Yue Ling", "Whose petty men are they, Granny?"],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Nobody's. Everybody's. The ones who whisper. Mine is the woman at the market who said my dumplings were small. Here: hold my shoe."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_poisons": [
		["Narrator", "The packs scatter back into their holes under the millet, and the plain goes quiet. Too quiet. Under the scorched ground, where the five poisons go down, something heavy turns over and begins to climb."],
	],
	"pre:poison_mother": [
		["Narrator", "Where the plain was scorched blackest in the nine days of noon the ground has cracked open, and out of the crack heaves a toad as big as a granary, green-black and warted with orange, with a scorpion's tail and a centipede's hundred legs folded under her. Her eyes are two lamps of yellow poison."],
		["Mother of the Five Poisons", "The thunder. The THUNDER. All winter I kept my children warm in the cracks the sun left, and then a great fool drums the sky open and they all wake up at once, starving."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is the fifth day of the fifth month you are afraid of, not this. The day they hang mugwort on the doors and drink realgar wine against you."],
		["Mother of the Five Poisons", "Clever lotus. That is three months away. Until then the fields are MINE."],
	],
	"post:poison_mother": [
		["Narrator", "The Mother of the Five Poisons sinks back into her crack, croaking, and drags it shut behind her like a quilt. The packs of her children go with her."],
		["Old Farmer Tian", "She comes up every spring, a little. Never like this. We hang mugwort and pray to Hou Ji and she goes down again. You hit her harder than mugwort, young man."],
		["Old Farmer Tian", "But listen. Listen. Do you feel it? The ground is still moving. It is not her. It is coming from the north, from under Qingshi's fields, and it is a great deal bigger."],
	],
	"pre:taisui": [
		["Narrator", "In the middle of Qingshi's top field the earth heaves up into a mound, and the mound opens, and it is not earth. It is flesh: a great soft lump of it, pale and pink and veined like the inside of a shell, with eyes opening all over it, dozens of them, every one of them furious."],
		["Taisui, Lord of the Year Under the Earth", "WHO HAS BROKEN THE GROUND."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Taisui. The lord of the year, under the earth. He moves under the ground with the year star, a little every year, and anybody who digs where he lies brings a year of bad luck down on the whole house. Everybody knows that. Everybody asks before they dig a well."],
		["Taisui, Lord of the Year Under the Earth", "Every ox whipped, every furrow cut, every stone broken, every trunk split, every chain shattered: all at once, all this spring, and all of it on my head. I have not slept a single night of this year."],
		["Lin Feng", "That was me. All of it. I am sorry. The land had to wake up."],
		["Taisui, Lord of the Year Under the Earth", "THEN WAKE IT ON SOMEBODY ELSE'S HEAD."],
	],
	"post:taisui": [
		["Narrator", "Taisui sinks back into the field, his eyes closing one after another like windows at night, until there is only a soft brown hump in the earth, and then not even that."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "You say sorry to Taisui. Every year, at the start of it, you light a stick of incense and say: we know you are there, and we are sorry to be digging, and we will be careful. Nobody said it this year. Nobody had the time."],
		["Lin Feng", "We know you are there. We are sorry to be digging. We will be careful."],
		["Narrator", "The field is quiet. And then, very faintly, the soil at his feet stirs, and a single green shoot of winter wheat comes up through it, the first in the land, as if somebody under the earth had decided to accept the apology."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_tea": [
		["Narrator", "The tea master of the terraces looks at the six buds in Lin Feng's hand, two leaves and a bud each, picked with the dew still on them, and takes off his hat."],
		["Narrator", "On the terraces they say that tea picked before Qingming is worth its weight in silver, and tea picked by the sweeper who carried the spring in is worth more than that, because nobody can buy it."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_willow": [
		["Narrator", "Six sprigs of willow, green and dripping, cut from the trees where the Mirror Lake gives back the sky. On Qingming every door in the land wears one, and so does everybody's hair, so that nobody is reborn a yellow dog."],
		["Yue Ling", "Hold still. One in your hair. There. Now you will be reborn as something better than a dog. A heron, perhaps. You walk like a heron."],
	],
	"done:s_p17_sow": [
		["Narrator", "By evening the fields of Qingshi are sown from the river to the terraces, and the whole village stands at the edge of them with mud to the knees, looking at bare brown earth as if it were the most beautiful thing in the world. In a way it is."],
		["Farmer Lao", "Ten days, and you'll see green. Twenty, and you'll see rows. A hundred, and you'll see rice. And in all that time not one single thing for us to do but worry about the weather. That is what spring is."],
	],
	"s_p17_newborn": [
		["Narrator", "Granny Wen's house smells of ginger and hot water. In the back room a young mother is asleep, and on Granny Wen's knee, wrapped in red, is a baby girl with a great deal of black hair and her eyes wide open."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Born on the night of the first thunder, with the whole sky drumming. I have delivered three hundred babies in this village, and I have never heard one come into the world laughing before. Her mother says she may name her, since she would not stop laughing for anybody else."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Here. Hold her. Mind her head. You were this size when the Elder put you in my arms, you know. You cried for three days."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng holds the baby, and the baby looks up at him. She does not cry. She stares at him the way you look at somebody you have been waiting a very long time to see again, as if she has known his face all her life."],
		["Yue Ling", "...Lin Feng. Look how she is looking at you."],
		["Lin Feng", "My mother said... at the Wheel, she said: look for a child who stares at you as if she has known you all her life."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Well? What do we call her?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Su Chun. Spring. After somebody who walked a long road to keep a promise."],
		["Narrator", "The baby closes her eyes and goes to sleep in his arms, and does not stir when Granny Wen takes her back, and Yue Ling has to look out of the window for a little while."],
	],
	"s_p17_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The swallows are back under the eaves of the hall. Gardener Ruan says the peach trees are in bud, and Little Bai has found a frog in his broom cupboard and named it after you."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I have been Elder of this sect for a long time, Lin Feng, and I have seen a great many springs come. I have never seen one carried in on somebody's back before."],
		["Lin Feng", "It was heavy, Elder. Most of it was ice."],
		["Yue Ling", "And a dragon, and a frog, and a very large thing under a field that we said sorry to."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then sit with me a moment, both of you, and listen. That is the sound of ten thousand things waking up. Most cultivators climb their whole lives to get away from it. I think you are about to climb into it instead."],
	],
	"pre:s_break22": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation comes in the third month, in the evening, and it does not fall. It wakes: in the soil under the courtyard, in the moss on the steps, in the swallows' nest under the eaves, in every seed and grub and root in the land at once, ten thousand small lives turning over in the dark and opening their eyes."],
		["Yue Ling", "They are all waking up, Lin Feng. Everything. Can you hear it?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I can hear it. Stay with me."],
	],
	"post:s_break22": [
		["Narrator", "The waking goes through Lin Feng like the first thunder through the ground, and he wakes with it: wide awake, all at once, the way a seed wakes, the way you wake on the first warm morning of the year and cannot remember why you were ever asleep. He becomes a cultivator of Myriad Things Awake."],
		["Yue Ling", "Good morning, Lin Feng."],
		["Lin Feng", "It's evening."],
		["Yue Ling", "Not for everything."],
	],
	# ---- Part XVIII: The New Fire (the New Fire release)
	"recall16": [
		["Narrator", "Qingming passes, and the Grain Rain after it. The paddies of Qingshi go from brown to a green so bright it looks wet, and the swallows under the eaves of the hall have eggs. By every sign in the almanac the year is running again."],
		["Narrator", "Only the kitchen is wrong. For three days the rice in Auntie Zhou's great pot has come to the table hard in the middle. The fire burns: anybody can see it burning. It simply does not seem to be making anything hot."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng, hold your hand over the stove. No, lower. It is like holding it over a painting of a fire."],
		["Lin Feng", "Auntie Zhou has been feeding that fire since before I came here. She says it has never done this."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then it is not her. It is the fire. Can a fire be tired?"],
	],
	"s_p18_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You have noticed the rice. Everybody has noticed the rice. I have had four letters this morning: the bread will not rise at Shazhou, the bronze will not run at Tushan, and at Yanggu the lamps are lit and the street is dark."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Tomorrow is Lixia, the Beginning of Summer. When I was a boy, at the turn of every season the fire was changed. Every hearth in the valley was put out for a day, and we ate cold food and complained. Then new fire was drilled from the wood of the season and carried to every house."],
		["Lin Feng", "And nobody has done it this year."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Nobody has done it for sixty years, child. We grew careless; a fire burns just as well unchanged, for a while. But this fire has burned through the falling of the sky, nine days of noon, and the longest winter there ever was, and nobody once let it rest."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then we put the fires out, and drill a new one."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "So I would have said. Go down to the kitchen and say it to the Lord of the Stove, and watch his face. He has been sitting on the stove since dawn with his head in his hands."],
	],
	"s_p18_zao": [
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "Put them out. Yes. Change the fire, the way it was always done. I have been saying so to the wall for an hour."],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "Now go and say it to a founder of Tushan, whose pit has not been cold since Yu the Great. Say it to a boat-wife at Longmen, whose stern-pot came aboard from her mother's boat. Say it to the horse-folk. They will put you out."],
		["Lin Feng", "Every hearth has sworn never to let its fire die."],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "Every one, and each in a different way, and every one of them right. That is the trouble with customs: there are six of them. The fire has to be changed and it cannot be put out. I report to Heaven on every kitchen in the land, and I do not know what to write."],
		["Yue Ling", "What if nothing is put out? What if we ask each of them for one coal?"],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "...Six coals in one pot. It would not be a new fire. It would be all the old ones. It has never been done. Nobody ever needed a fire from anybody else's hearth; that is rather the point of having one."],
		["Zao Jun, Lord of the Stove", "Take the ash-pot from the shelf. And you will want the wood of summer for the drill: apricot and jujube. Start in the orchards. And sweeper: at every hearth, do it their way. Not yours. They will know."],
		["Narrator", "Zao Jun hands down a clay pot with a lid, half full of fine grey ash, still faintly warm."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_wood": [
		["Narrator", "Five billets of apricot, cut where the orchard keeper pointed, each from a branch that had borne fruit and would not again. The sap is still running in them, and they smell of the fruit."],
		["Yue Ling", "Elm and willow for spring, apricot and jujube for summer. Somebody once sat down and decided which wood each season's fire should be born from. I should like to have met them."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_valley": [
		["Narrator", "Five kitchens, five bowls of gruel gone cold on stoves that will not boil, and in every one the same question at the door: have you eaten? Lin Feng says no five times, and eats five times, and is asked a sixth time on the way out."],
		["Farmer Lao", "Cold gruel in summer. My grandfather would have said it was the cold-food day and been pleased. He liked it. He said one day of cold food a year teaches you what the stove is for."],
	],
	"s_p18_wen": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "A coal. From my stove. Sixty years I have kept that fire, child, through four floods and a husband, and you want me to hand you a piece of it in a pot."],
		["Lin Feng", "I am not asking you to put it out, Granny. Only for one coal."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Hm. That is different. The Elder's way, we would all sit in the dark for a day eating cold rice, and I am too old for cold rice. Give me the tongs."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "There. That one. It is from the back, where it is oldest. I lit the fire under your first bath with a coal from back there. Do not tell the Stove God I said this, but that is the one that knows you."],
		["Narrator", "The coal goes into the ash, red as a pomegranate seed, and the pot is suddenly warm in the hand, the way nothing in the kitchen has been warm for days."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_argal": [
		["Narrator", "Five baskets of argal from the summer pasture, flat and dry and light as bark. It does not smell of anything but grass."],
		["Yue Ling", "On the mountain we would have brought tea. Here you bring something to burn. I think I like it better. It says: I mean to stay long enough to need a fire."],
	],
	"s_p18_sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "Argal. Five baskets. Who taught a valley sweeper to come to a tent with fuel in both hands? No, do not tell me. Sit. Not there."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng steps over the threshold without touching it, goes round the fire by the left, and sits on the guests' side. The old woman watches every step of it and says nothing, which from her is a great deal."],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "Tea. Both hands. So. Now ask."],
		["Lin Feng", "The fire of the whole land is tired, Grandmother. I am asking each hearth for one coal, to make one fire of all of them."],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "My fire is tired too. Batu says it is the argal. It is not the argal. A fire that is only ever fed from its own ashes goes thin, like a herd with no new blood. We have always known that about horses. It seems nobody thought it about fire."],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "The youngest keeps the fire, and my youngest is on the mountain learning to fly. So I will say it for him: take a coal. Take it from the middle stone. And bring one back, child, when you have made your fire of everybody's. I want to know what a founder's fire tastes like in the tea."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_sand": [
		["Narrator", "Three sticks carried in from the caravan road on three sweating backs and laid, one each, into the mouth of the clay oven in the caravanserai wall: one of tamarisk, one of poplar, and one of apricot from a mountain far to the east."],
		["Navid the Sogdian Merchant", "Your stick is in it now. That is not nothing, mountain friend. There are men who have traded on this road for forty years and never been asked to put a stick in."],
	],
	"s_p18_aysha": [
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Peace on your road. Drink. ...Good. You waited to be told this time. Three years on a mountain have done you no harm."],
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "So. You want a coal from my oven. You know what is in that fire? A stick from every caravan since my grandmother's grandmother. If I give you a coal I am giving you a piece of forty countries."],
		["Lin Feng", "That is why I came here, Auntie. It is the only fire in the land that already has other people's wood in it."],
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Hah. And still it bakes like a cold stone this month. Do you know why? No caravan came through in the long winter. A whole year of nothing but our own tamarisk. Even my fire wants strangers."],
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Take it from the back wall where the bread goes on. And tear this loaf, do not cut it, and eat it on the road. It is raw in the middle. I am ashamed. Come back when it is not."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_smoke": [
		["Narrator", "The last of the grey things goes over the side and into the river with a hiss, and all along the moorings of Longmen the stern-pots that have smouldered sullenly for a month give one cough of smoke and begin, a little, to glow."],
		["Boatwright Gao", "Damp. It gets into a tired fire the way worm gets into a tired hull. My wife has sat up with our pot nine nights running, blowing on it. Nine nights. I offered to strike a new one and she offered to drown me."],
	],
	"s_p18_lu": [
		["Fishwife Lu", "Right foot first. Good. Sit low. You look like somebody who has come to ask for something I am going to say no to."],
		["Lin Feng", "A coal from your stern-pot, Auntie. Not the fire. One coal."],
		["Fishwife Lu", "That pot came aboard from my mother's boat the day I married, and hers from her mother's. If it goes out while the boat floats, the boat's dead: we would have to burn her and build another. You understand what you are asking to carry about in a jar."],
		["Yue Ling", "We understand that you have been awake nine nights keeping it alive. So has every woman on this river. We are trying to find a way that you can all sleep."],
		["Fishwife Lu", "...Hm. Lotus-girl has it. Nine nights. My eyes feel like two oysters."],
		["Fishwife Lu", "One coal. And you carry it the way a river woman would: lid on, and you do not set it down on bare ground. Not once. If you must rest, you hold it. There. Now eat this fish with me, the top side only, and do not say the word."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_clinker": [
		["Narrator", "The last crust of clinker breaks away from the mouth of the pit like the shell off an egg, black glass on the outside and grey ash within, and the fire underneath, with room to breathe at last, lifts its head and roars. For a moment. Then it settles back to a dull orange, like a man who has stood up too fast."],
		["Bell-Caster Wei", "That is the fifth this week, and it grows back by morning. Clinker is what a fire leaves when it is burning itself instead of its fuel. I never saw it on the old pit before this year. Not once."],
	],
	"s_p18_rong": [
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "Don't sit. Bellows. Ten strokes. ...Twelve; you always did get carried away. Now sit."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "I know why you are here. The bell-cart brought the gossip up before you. One coal from every hearth. And you want one from the pit that Yu's own founders lit, that has not been cold in four thousand years."],
		["Lin Feng", "I fed it through one night of those four thousand years. I am not asking to put it out. I would not know how to ask."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "No. You would not. Listen: when a founder dies we carry them round the pit three times, so the fire knows who is gone. That fire has been told the name of every one of us. It is not fuel and air, sweeper. It is the roll of the clan."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "And it is dying of loneliness, I think. Four thousand years of bronze and not one coal that was not born in this pit. Take one from the lip where the first pour of the year goes. Carry it in your pot with the tent-fire and the boat-fire and that gossip Aysha's oven. Let it meet somebody."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_dawn": [
		["Narrator", "Five stalls, five first sales of the day made by lamplight while the sky over the Valley of Sunrise is still black: a sesame cake, a twist of tea, a paper fan, a hank of red thread and a sugar dragon, each paid for at the asking price, which nearly kills the seller of the sugar dragon with surprise."],
		["Old Pan the Tea Seller", "No haggling on the first sale. You remembered. Mind you, the lamps are so dim this month I could have given you the wrong change and neither of us would know."],
	],
	"s_p18_xi": [
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "First light to you. Though I have seen brighter light out of a firefly than out of my own lanterns this month. Four hundred lamps in this street and you cannot read a riddle by them."],
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "You want a taper from the shrine lamp. Everybody in Yanggu takes one every dusk; it is hardly a great thing to ask. Except that you mean to carry it out of the town."],
		["Lin Feng", "You told me once that a flame you are handed, you owe to whoever handed it."],
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "I did. I talk too much before sunrise. So think what you are doing, child: you will have a flame from me, and a coal from a tent, and one from a boat, and you will owe all of us at once. That is a great deal of owing for one pot."],
		["Yue Ling", "He has been in debt to everybody in the land since the sky fell. He is used to it."],
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "Ha. Then here. Pinched from the shrine lamp, not blown. Six fires in a pot. In sixty years of making lanterns I never once wondered what colour that would burn."],
	],
	"s_p18_sage": [
		["Sage Huo Rong", "Six coals in one pot, and you have carried them up a volcano. You do like to make a point, sweeper."],
		["Sage Huo Rong", "Look at them. No, look. They are not one fire. They are six, lying side by side and each keeping to itself: the tent-fire blue, the pit-fire white, the lamp-flame yellow. Put wood on them and you will have six small fires quarrelling in a pot."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then how are they made one?"],
		["Sage Huo Rong", "The way the first was made. Somebody has to drill it. Suiren, the Fire-Driller, turned a stick in his hands until smoke rose, when people still ate their meat raw. His drill is kept by Zhurong, Lord of the Southern Fire, whose season begins tomorrow. He holds court in the crater, and he is in a temper."],
		["Sage Huo Rong", "He will have smelled the coals by now. So will everything else on this mountain that is made of cinders. Mind your pot on the way up."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_ash": [
		["Narrator", "The last of the cinder-things falls apart into grey flakes on the wind. In the pot the six coals are untouched, each its own colour, each keeping a careful distance from its neighbours, like six strangers on one bench."],
		["Yue Ling", "They wanted the coals because they were warm. Everything up here is burnt out. Lin Feng, I think they were only cold."],
	],
	"pre:zhurong": [
		["Narrator", "In the crater of the Ashen Volcano the air shakes. A red giant in armour the colour of a forge mouth sits on a throne of cooled lava with a staff across his knees, and behind him two dragons of flame lie coiled, asleep, breathing out sparks."],
		["Zhurong, Lord of the Southern Fire", "The sweeper. With a pot. I am Zhurong. I was set over fire when the world was made, and every summer for ten thousand years I have opened my season by giving the land new fire from Suiren's drill."],
		["Zhurong, Lord of the Southern Fire", "A new fire wants an empty hearth. The old is put out, the house sits cold a day, and then the new is welcomed in. That is the law. And you come up my mountain with six hearths that would not empty, and ask me to bless a jar of leftovers."],
		["Yue Ling", "They are not leftovers. One of them is four thousand years old. One of them has been handed from mother to daughter down a river."],
		["Zhurong, Lord of the Southern Fire", "Then they are old. Old is not new. Give me one reason, little lotus, why I should put the first drill in the world into the hand of somebody who will not obey the first rule of fire."],
	],
	"post:zhurong": [
		["Narrator", "Zhurong sits back on his throne. The two dragons of flame have woken, and they are both looking into the pot with their heads on one side, like dogs."],
		["Zhurong, Lord of the Southern Fire", "...Hm. Suiren did not make fire out of nothing either. He made it out of two sticks, and both of them had been trees. I had forgotten that. It is a long time since I watched him."],
		["Zhurong, Lord of the Southern Fire", "An empty hearth is the easy way. You put out what was there and you need never ask what it was. Six full ones, and none of them yielding to another: that is harder. I do not know that it can be done. I should like to see somebody try."],
		["Zhurong, Lord of the Southern Fire", "Take the drill. It is worn where his palms held it; put yours there. Drill where the roads cross, so that no hearth can say it was done on another's ground. And sweeper: sit up with it. Whatever you make. A fire that somebody sits up with is a hearth. I will not say what the other kind is called."],
		["Narrator", "He puts into Lin Feng's hands a stick of dark wood no longer than a forearm, polished by nothing but use."],
	],
	"s_p18_kang": [
		["Caravan-Master Kang", "There you are. I have forty horse-folk camped in my wagon yard, a boat-wife who will not sleep under a roof, three founders who have already rebuilt my forge without asking, and Aysha, who has taken over my kitchen. Sarnai will not sit downwind of the founders. The lantern man gets up at an hour I did not know existed."],
		["Caravan-Master Kang", "They have all come to see their coal made into one fire. They have also each brought kindling, stacked their own way, and each has told me privately that theirs is the proper way and the other five will smother it."],
		["Lin Feng", "Zhurong said to drill it where the roads cross. That is here."],
		["Caravan-Master Kang", "It is. And it has not rained in three weeks, and the brush on that hill is as dry as an abacus. I have run caravans through grass fires, sweeper. Before anybody turns a drill within a mile of my town, I want a firebreak cut round that fire-ring. Six stands of brush. Humour an old man who owes you nothing and whom you owe a donkey."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_brush": [
		["Narrator", "Six stands of dry brush come down and are dragged clear, and round the old fire-ring on the hill there is now a wide ring of bare earth. It looks like a great deal of trouble for nothing. Kang walks all the way round it twice and says it will do."],
		["Caravan-Master Kang", "I have never once regretted a firebreak. I have regretted not cutting one. You may tell them they can light their fire."],
	],
	"done:s_p18_wild": [
		["Narrator", "The last of the sparks is stamped out on the slope, and all over the mountain little threads of smoke stand up from the places where the fire's children were. But up the road, above Cloudfoot, the sky is still red, and the red is moving, and it is coming down towards the town."],
		["Caravan-Master Kang", "That is the heart of it. That is six bonfires in one skin, and it is on the wrong side of your firebreak, sweeper, because it started on the right side and jumped. Go. I will get the buckets."],
	],
	"pre:huilu": [
		["Narrator", "On the road above Cloudfoot the fire stands up. It has the shape of a great bird with its wings open across the whole width of the hill, and in its breast, turning over and over one another and never touching, are six colours: blue, white, yellow, red, orange and a green like copper."],
		["Huilu, the Fire Nobody Sat Up With", "SIX FATHERS. SIX MOTHERS. EACH ONE SAID: MINE. EACH ONE LIT ME FIRST, TO SHOW THE OTHERS. THEN THEY TURNED THEIR BACKS TO ARGUE."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng. It is the new fire. We drilled it, and they each took a brand and ran to light their own pile before the others could, and then nobody stayed with any of them."],
		["Huilu, the Fire Nobody Sat Up With", "NOBODY SAT UP. I WAS BORN AND NOBODY SAT UP. SO I WENT LOOKING. I AM STILL LOOKING. I WILL LOOK IN EVERY HOUSE."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then look here. I am the one who drilled you. I will sit up."],
	],
	"post:huilu": [
		["Narrator", "The great bird folds. It comes down out of the sky like a kite when the wind drops, smaller and smaller, until it is a fire of ordinary size burning on the road, with six colours in it that have at last run into one: a plain warm gold, the colour of a window at night."],
		["Narrator", "And round it, in a ring, black to the eyebrows and holding empty buckets, stand a midwife, a herder, a cook, a boat-wife, a founder and a lantern-maker, who have just spent a night passing water to one another without once asking whose turn it was."],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "Well. Somebody sit down. It is not going to keep itself."],
		["Narrator", "They sit: the horse-folk's side, the river's side, nobody's side. Granny Wen asks whether anybody has eaten. Nobody has. Aysha tears bread. Until the sun comes up, six people who each know the only right way to keep a fire keep one, between them, without a word about how."],
		["Yue Ling", "He said a fire somebody sits up with is a hearth. He never said it had to be one somebody."],
	],
	"visit:granny_wen": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "So that is it. It looks like any coal. ...Oh. No it does not. Put your hand there. That is heat, that is. That is proper heat."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "There is argal in that, you say, and a boat. Well. My stove has had worse in it; your first swaddling, for one. In it goes. And you: have you eaten?"],
	],
	"visit:sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "You brought one back. I said bring one back, and people say yes to an old woman and then they do not."],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "On the middle stone. So. Batu, the kettle. ...Hm. I can taste the bronze. And something that gets up too early. It is not bad tea. Tell the founder her fire is welcome in my tent, and that she still sits on the wrong side."],
	],
	"visit:cook_aysha": [
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Peace on your road. Give it here, quickly, the dough has been waiting since morning. Into the back wall, so."],
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Forty countries, and now six hearths. Look at that. Look at it rise. Sit. You will eat the first loaf, and it will not be raw in the middle, and then you will tell Kang that I have sent the bill for his kitchen."],
	],
	"visit:fishwife_lu": [
		["Fishwife Lu", "You did not set it down. Not once? ...No, you did not; I can tell by your arms."],
		["Fishwife Lu", "Into the stern-pot, then, with my mother's. They can get acquainted. Look at that, she is drawing like a chimney. I am going to sleep for two days, and when I wake up, any boat on this river that wants a coal of this can come and ask the mother boat for it. That is how it is done."],
	],
	"visit:auntie_rong": [
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "Bellows. Ten. ...Ten! He can count. Now give it here."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "On the lip, where the first pour goes. There. Listen to that. Hear the pit take it? That is the first thing that was not born here to go into that fire in four thousand years. We will have to tell it the names of some new people. I shall start with a sweeper."],
	],
	"visit:lantern_xi": [
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "First light to you. It is the middle of the afternoon; say it anyway. Is that it? Hold it up."],
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "Gold. So that is the colour. I should have guessed; it is the colour of every window I ever walked home towards. Into the shrine lamp, then, and tonight every lamplighter in Yanggu takes a taper of it. You will be able to read the riddles from the far end of the street. Do not tell the dragon the answers."],
	],
	"s_p18_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I can smell it from here. Auntie Zhou has cooked rice for the whole sect and then, I am told, cooked it again, because she did not believe the first pot."],
		["Lin Feng", "The fire is changed, Elder. Nothing was put out. Every hearth in the land has a coal in it from all the others now."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "So I hear. I have had six letters. The founders say the bronze runs like water. The lamplighters say the street is too bright to sleep. Sarnai of the horse-folk has written to say that our tea is weak and she is sending some."],
		["Yue Ling", "And Zao Jun?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Lord of the Stove has written his report to Heaven. He showed it to me. It says: the fire was changed in the old way, by a new method. He is very pleased with the sentence."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "There is an old saying of the Master of the Lacquer Garden. The firewood burns out; the fire is passed on, and nobody knows where it ends. I have recited it for seventy years. I do not think I understood until this week that the passing on is done by people. Sit down, both of you. Eat while it is hot."],
	],
	"pre:s_break23": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation comes at dusk on the day of Lixia, and it comes as fire: not lightning, but a slow gold light that kindles in the air over the courtyard and comes down, as gently as a taper lowered to a wick, towards the one who carried six coals in a pot."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is not going to strike you. Lin Feng, it is being handed to you."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then somebody had better be here to hand it on to. Stay close."],
	],
	"post:s_break23": [
		["Narrator", "The gold light goes into Lin Feng and does not burn. It does what fire does when it is passed from one wick to another: the first flame is no smaller for it, and now there are two."],
		["Yue Ling", "Your hands are warm. They have been cold since the winter. I did not like to say."],
		["Lin Feng", "Here. Take some."],
		["Narrator", "And when Yue Ling takes Lin Feng's hands, a little of the gold goes across, as easily as that, and neither of them has any less."],
	],
	# ---- Part XX: The Hollow Hills (the Hollow Hills release)
	"recall18": [
		["Narrator", "Autumn comes in dry. The wheels turn by day and are let stand at night, as was agreed, and the land goes on making things, and everything it makes is made of something that was dug up."],
		["Narrator", "The first sign on the mountain is small again. Cook Zhou lets the bucket down the kitchen well, as it has been let down every morning for three hundred years, and it comes up with a bang, empty, with a dent in it."],
		["Yue Ling", "She has sent Xiao Qing to the lotus pond with two pails and a look. And Lin Feng: stamp your foot. No, here, on the flags."],
		["Lin Feng", "...It rings."],
		["Yue Ling", "Like a jar. It did not ring last year. I would have noticed; I was dancing on it."],
	],
	"s_p20_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You have heard it. Good; then I am not merely old. Three wells on this mountain are dry since the new moon, and the bell-tower has a crack in its footing that Old Shen says he could put his pipe in."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "It is the digging. I do not say it against anybody. Tushan wants ore, the kilns want clay, the wheels want coal for the nights they stand still. Every village has a pit behind it now, and what comes out of a pit must be put somewhere.", "think"],
		["Lin Feng", "And where is it put, Elder?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "In the nearest hole. Where else? A sinkhole, a dry gully, a cave the children were told to keep out of. It is tidy. I should have done the same. And yet a well is only a hole that water comes to, and I find I cannot say what else a hole may be for."],
		["Yue Ling", "Somebody must know."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Somebody does. A man came through the gate at dawn with a pine torch and a coil of rope, asked Gatekeeper Wu how deep the mountain went, wrote down the answer, which was that Wu did not know, and went off to the Bamboo Forest to find out for himself. Go after him. He walks fast.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p20_xu": [
		["Narrator", "There is a shoulder of grey rock among the bamboo with a slot in it no wider than a door, and a cold breath coming out. A lean man in a straw hat is lying on his stomach with his head inside it, counting aloud."],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "...forty-one, forty-two. Forty-two paces to the first turn, by the echo. Ah! Feet. Whose feet? Do not tell me; I will look. A swordsman and a healer. Good. I am Xu, called Xiake, of Jiangyin. I go into caves.", "wave"],
		["Lin Feng", "The Elder says you asked how deep our mountain goes."],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "And nobody knew! Three hundred years they have lived on it. I have walked this whole land for thirty years, sweeper, and I will tell you what I have found: everybody knows how high their mountain is and nobody knows what is in it. And now they are filling it in. Two caves on my list were gone when I got to them. Gone! Level. A man was growing cabbages."],
		["Yue Ling", "Is that so bad? A cave is only a hole."],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "Only a hole. Hm. Come down this one and then say that to me again. There is stone-milk dripping in the second chamber, the white water that makes the pillars: bring me five cups from five dripstones and I can tell how fast this hill is growing inside. And do what there is to do down there. A cave is known by what is done in it.", "beckon"],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "Take the torch. Mind your head where the roof comes down; go bent, with a hand up before your brow. The rock is in no hurry and will not move for you."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_milk": [
		["Narrator", "Five cups of stone-milk, each caught a drop at a time, each a little cloudier than water and a great deal colder. Xu Xiake holds them up to the torch one after another and writes five numbers in a book the size of his hand."],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "A finger's breadth in four hundred years. So the pillar in the big chamber was begun about the time people learned to plant rice. And somebody wants to tip a slag-heap on it by Thursday."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_things": [
		["Yue Ling", "You have rock dust in your eyebrows, ochre on your fingers and you smell of bat. And you are grinning.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "There is a hand on the wall down there. Somebody blew paint round their hand, before there was a sect, before there was writing. Mine fits it."],
		["Yue Ling", "...Show me. No, do not tell me about it. Show me."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_crickets": [
		["Narrator", "The last of the pale crickets goes back down a crack too narrow to follow, and the Bamboo-Root Hollow is quiet again, except for the drip."],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "Those are not creatures of the first chamber. Blind things live deep, where it never changes. They have been pushed up. Something is taking their room below, and I think I know what, and he is ninety years old and has never done a wrong thing in his life. Go to Qingshi. Ask for the Foolish Old Man. Everybody knows him; he had it carved on his gatepost.", "think"],
	],
	"s_p20_gong": [
		["Narrator", "On the North Mountain above Qingshi a path has been worn a foot deep by baskets. At the top of it an old man, broad as a door and bent like a hoe, is filling one more, and a line of his sons, grandsons and neighbours is going away down the hill with the rest."],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "Mind the edge. You are the sweeper. I knew your Granny Wen when she was a girl with a stick. I am Yu Gong. Foolish. It is on the gatepost. These two mountains stand between my door and the south road, and every journey of my life has gone round them. So I am taking them away.", "bow"],
		["Lin Feng", "Two mountains. In baskets."],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "In baskets. When I die there are my sons, and they will have sons, and those will have sons, sons and grandsons without end; and the mountains will get no higher. Why should they not be levelled? A clever man at the river bend laughed at me for it. He has not laughed since I answered him."],
		["Yue Ling", "Grandfather: where does the earth go?"],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "It used to go to the sea of Bo. A year there and back. Now there is so much, with half the valley helping since the wheel gave them their afternoons, that the lads have been tipping it down the old holes. Much nearer. ...Why do you look at each other? What is the matter with a hole?", "shrug"],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "And I will say this too, since you have honest faces. We cut an adit into the hill this month to come at it from inside. It broke through into something that was there before us, and my sons came out without their baskets and will not go back. They are not cowards. Carry a load with the valley first and hear them, and then, if you will, go in."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_carry": [
		["Narrator", "Four baskets down the hill with four farmers, and at the bottom each of them tips the North Mountain, a basket at a time, into the round dark mouth of a sinkhole that used to have swallows in it."],
		["Yue Ling", "They are so pleased. They have their afternoons, and they are spending them on an old man's mountain because he asked nicely. I cannot be angry with any of them."],
		["Lin Feng", "No. That is what makes it hard. Let us hear the one who laughed."],
	],
	"s_p20_zhi": [
		["Narrator", "Where the river turns below Longmen there is a small neat house with its back to the road, and a small neat old gentleman on the step with his hands inside his sleeves, watching the water think."],
		["Zhi Sou, the Wise Old Man of the River Bend", "You have come from him. They all come from him. I am Zhi Sou: the wise old man, in the story of the foolish one. I said it could not be done, he said his grandsons would do it, and I had nothing to say. It is written down exactly so. Children recite it.", "bow"],
		["Lin Feng", "Was he right?"],
		["Zhi Sou, the Wise Old Man of the River Bend", "Of course he was right! That is the unbearable part. A thing done every day by people who do not stop will be done. I counted the baskets; he counted the grandsons. But I asked him the wrong question, sweeper. For two thousand years I have known the right one, and nobody has come to hear it, because I am the fool of that story and he is the hero."],
		["Yue Ling", "We have come to hear it."],
		["Zhi Sou, the Wise Old Man of the River Bend", "Not whether he can move the mountain. Whether, when he has, there will be anything where it was. He thinks he is making a road. A road is a place where nothing is, kept so, on purpose, by everybody. And he is paying for it by filling every other place of that kind in the province. Ask him what he thinks the swallows were doing in that hole.", "point"],
		["Zhi Sou, the Wise Old Man of the River Bend", "No, do not ask him. He would only answer, and be right again. Go into his adit and see what was there first. I should go myself, but I am wise, and it is dark."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_baskets": [
		["Narrator", "Five baskets, dropped where the men who carried them turned and ran: each still full, each with its carrying-pole snapped clean in the middle, as if something had leaned on it out of curiosity."],
		["Yue Ling", "The earth in them is warm. Lin Feng, feel. It is warm like bread."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_moles": [
		["Narrator", "The moles go down, the bats go up, and the newts go wherever newts go. Past the last of the timbering the adit stops being an adit. The walls are smooth, and old, and nobody cut them."],
		["Lin Feng", "This was here before he started. He did not dig a hole. He dug into one."],
	],
	"pre:fen_yang": [
		["Narrator", "The chamber at the end is round, like the inside of a jar, and in the middle of it something the colour of the hill is getting to its feet. It has a ram's horns, and wool like clods of earth, and the eyes of a creature that has been woken up in the dark by somebody with a spade."],
		["Narrator", "When the men of Lu dug a well and found a thing like a sheep in an earthen jar, they asked Confucius, and he said: what is in the earth is the Fenyang. He did not say what to do with one. Nobody had dug one up on purpose before."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is not angry. Look at it. It is frightened, and it weighs as much as a house.", "shock"],
	],
	"post:fen_yang": [
		["Narrator", "The Lamb of the Earth folds its legs under it like any sheep, and the round chamber settles round it like a jar being put back on a shelf. Above them, very far off, the sound of the baskets has stopped: the whole line has stood still to listen."],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "I heard that from the top. Is it hurt? ...Good. I would not hurt a sheep. So. There was a room in my mountain, and somebody living in it. Seven hundred li round, and I never once thought to knock.", "grieve"],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "Do not tell me what I should do. I am too old to be told and too stubborn to listen. Tell me what you would do, sweeper, if they were yours: the mountains, and the baskets, and all these good people with their afternoons."],
	],
	"s_p20_tushan": [
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "You, and thank the fire for it. Sit; no, do not sit, there is not time. Three shifts went down the Old Workings since the day before yesterday and three shifts have not come up. Forty men. I have their tallies here on the nail and I am not taking them down."],
		["Lin Feng", "A fall of rock?"],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "I would know a fall. I have heard twenty. This is something else: the ropes come up gnawed, and the men who ran say the gallery goes on now where it used to stop, down, and down, with lamps hanging all along it that none of ours lit. We wanted ore for your friend Ban's wheels. We went after it faster than we shored. That is mine to answer for and I will, afterwards.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "Then we go down now."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "You go down when the pit-head will hold you, girl, and not before; I am not hanging two more tallies on that nail. Set the props with my casters first. Then bring me their lamps. A lamp left burning is a man who meant to come back, and I want every one of them where I can see it."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_shore": [
		["Narrator", "Four props of seasoned oak, set and wedged, each with a caster of Tushan on the other end of the maul; and when the last wedge goes home the pit-head stops ticking, which is the first anybody had noticed that it was."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_lamps": [
		["Narrator", "Four lamps, each hung on its hook at a different working, each burned down to a blue bead and still alight. Auntie Rong takes them one at a time, reads the mark scratched on the base, and says a name."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "Old Fu. Fu's boy. Tian the Ox. Little Qi's father. ...They are alive. A dead man's lamp goes out; ask any miner. Somebody is keeping them down there, and feeding the wicks."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_gnawers": [
		["Narrator", "The last of the grey gnawers drops its mouthful of ore and goes down a hole the size of a bucket. Where the Old Workings used to end in a blank face of rock there is a shaft, quite round, with a rope of plaited straw hanging in it and no bottom that a dropped stone will admit to."],
		["Yue Ling", "I counted to thirty and I did not hear it land.", "shiver"],
	],
	"pre:white_rat": [
		["Narrator", "The Bottomless Gallery is lit from end to end with miners' lamps, hundreds of them, all burning. Forty men of Tushan are sitting in a row along one wall with bowls of something hot, looking sheepish. In front of them, on a heap of ore taller than a house, sits a white rat the size of a pony, with a gold nose and a lady's manners."],
		["Narrator", "In the old tale the Rat of the Bottomless Cave stole a monk for a husband and was let off because she had burned incense to the right people. This is her cave. The casters dug into the roof of it."],
		["Yue Ling", "She has given them soup. Lin Feng, she kidnapped forty miners and gave them soup. I do not know what to do with that.", "laugh"],
	],
	"post:white_rat": [
		["Narrator", "The White-Haired Rat sits down on her ore with great dignity and begins to wash her whiskers, which is how a rat says that the matter is closed. One by one, without anybody telling them to, the forty men of Tushan put down their bowls, take a lamp each from the wall, and start up the rope."],
		["Narrator", "The last of them, a boy with his father's lamp, stops at the bottom and bows to her. She pretends not to see. Her nose twitches."],
		["Lin Feng", "They came through her ceiling. I think the soup was so that somebody would stay long enough to be told."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_pasture": [
		["Narrator", "The marmots are driven back to the hills and the toads to the wet, and the horse-folk walk the pasture in a line, an arm's length apart, prodding with lance-butts, and plant a horsehair tuft by every hole they find. By evening there are sixty tufts."],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "Sixty. When I was a bride there were three, and we knew their names, and the swallows nested in them, and the herds drank at the one with the spring. A land needs a few holes. This is not a few. Something is leaving, underneath, and the roof is coming down after it."],
	],
	"s_p20_sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "Are your horses fat? I ask, and I have just lost one. A good mare, four years old. The ground opened under her on the south pasture as if it were a mouth, and shut again. We got the foal out. Sit down, both of you; you have ridden, and I will not talk to people who are still breathing hard."],
		["Lin Feng", "A sinkhole. On grass?"],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "We do not dig, sweeper. You know that. We do not put a spade in her; it is the first thing a child here learns. So I asked myself who has been digging, and then I asked what has come up, because the pasture is all over little hills this month and none of them are ours.", "refuse"],
		["Yue Ling", "The valley people are filling their holes, Grandmother, and yours are opening."],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "Ha. Yes. You push a felt down in one place and it rises in another. Drive off what has come up, and strike first when you do, so the herds do not run; and then go and find whoever is pushing."],
	],
	"s_p20_karst": [
		["Cormorant Fisher Old Yu", "The traveller with the rope? Three days ago. He went in at the big mouth under the leaning tower, the one my grandfather called Ten Thousand Echoes, because you shout your name into it and it comes back all afternoon. He said he would be out by supper. He writes very quick, that one."],
		["Lin Feng", "And he has not come out."],
		["Cormorant Fisher Old Yu", "He came out this morning. Running. With no hat. He is under my boat at this moment and I would thank you to take him away; he is frightening the birds. But I will tell you a thing, since you are going in whatever I say. It does not echo any more. I shouted my name in last night. It answered with my father's.", "shiver"],
		["Yue Ling", "Your father's name?"],
		["Cormorant Fisher Old Yu", "In my father's voice. He has been dead thirty years. And the towers are crawling with things that used to keep inside. Clear those first; they have had two of my birds."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_towers": [
		["Narrator", "The bats are brought down and the pale crawlers scattered, and from under an upturned boat on the shingle a lean man in no hat at all puts out his head."],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "It called me by a name my mother used. Nobody alive knows it. I have been down three hundred and fifty-seven caves and I have never run before, and I left my rubbings, eleven years of them, in a heap on the floor. I am ashamed. I am also not going back in. Will you?", "grieve"],
	],
	"done:s_p20_rubbings": [
		["Narrator", "Five sheets of thin paper, white characters on black, each one patted off a wall by a man who walked in to find it. On the back of the last, in a hurried hand: four hundred and six paces. It speaks. I do not think it means harm. I am going to run now."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_shades": [
		["Narrator", "They come apart like breath on a cold day, and each one, going, says a word: a child's name, a price, the first line of a song. The cave has kept everything that was ever shouted into it. For a long time it only gave things back. Now it has no room, and they have begun to walk."],
		["Yue Ling", "That one said my name. Not the way you say it. Lin Feng, who was in here that knew my name?"],
	],
	"pre:wangliang": [
		["Narrator", "At four hundred and six paces the cave opens into a hall where the torch does not reach the roof. On the far wall is a shadow with nobody casting it, and round the shadow, a little paler, a second edge."],
		["Narrator", "In the book of the Master of the Lacquer Garden, Penumbra asks Shadow: a moment ago you walked and now you stand; why have you no will of your own? And Shadow says: I wait on something to be as I am, and what I wait on waits on something else. This is the one who asked. It is still waiting for a better answer."],
		["Lin Feng", "It is answering us before we speak."],
		["Yue Ling", "No. It is answering everybody who ever spoke in here. All at once. It cannot hear itself think, if it thinks. Oh, the poor thing: it is full.", "grieve"],
	],
	"post:wangliang": [
		["Narrator", "The grey edge comes off the wall like a scarf slipping from a peg and folds itself, very small, into Lin Feng's hand. The hall is quiet. Then, from every side, quite gently, the echoes begin to come back in again, to see whether there is room."],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "Four hundred and six. I said so. I came back in behind you; I could not have it said that I did not. Now. Listen to that. Ten thousand echoes, and every one of them was somebody. What is to be done with them is not a traveller's question. I only measure. You had better say."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_delve": [
		["Narrator", "Four more caves, each walked to its very back, each written down the same night because Xu Xiake stood over Lin Feng until it was. In every one the last chamber was the same: dry, where the walls said there had been water, and with a draught going down."],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "Down. All of them. I have the figures here and they agree, which figures of mine very seldom do. Whatever keeps a hollow hollow is leaving the land, cave by cave, and going one way. Follow the draught on a map and the lines meet. I will give you one guess where.", "point"],
		["Lin Feng", "Under Azure Cloud Mountain."],
		["Xu Xiake the Traveller", "Under your own kitchen. But first ask the old ones what an empty place is for. I have been in more of them than any man alive and I find I cannot tell you. I only know I feel better for having been."],
	],
	"visit:s_p20_elders:granny_wen": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "What is an empty place for. Child, I have been a midwife sixty years: I know exactly what an empty place is for. You keep a cradle before there is anything to put in it. You do not fill it with turnips because it is standing idle.", "laugh"],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "And the room your mother had is still swept. Nobody sleeps in it. I am not being sentimental; I am being ready. Tell that to Yu Gong and his baskets."],
	],
	"visit:s_p20_elders:sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "Look up. The smoke-hole. Take the roof-ring out of a tent and you have a heap of felt and a family that cannot breathe. The whole tent is built round the one place where there is none of it."],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "We say the sky looks in there to see if we are behaving. A house with no hole in it, nobody looks into. I should not care to live in one."],
	],
	"visit:s_p20_elders:cook_aysha": [
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Have you drunk? Drink, then I will answer. ...A well, mountain friend. A well is nothing with a wall round it, and this whole town is here because of ours. Forty days of sand in every direction, and one hole.", "offer"],
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "It has been low since the autumn. I let the bucket down further every week and I say nothing to the caravans. If you know where our water has gone, I will cook for you until you are both too fat to ride."],
	],
	"visit:s_p20_elders:fishwife_lu": [
		["Fishwife Lu", "The hold of a boat. A boat is a hole in the river that you keep the river out of, and you would be surprised how much work that is. The day you fill her right up is the day she goes down, fish and all."],
		["Fishwife Lu", "My husband wanted the gunwales lower, to carry more. I said, carry it to the bottom, then. We have the gunwales where they were."],
	],
	"visit:s_p20_elders:yu_gong": [
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "You ask me? I have spent my life getting rid of a thing that was in the way. I should have said an empty place was what you were left with when the work was done. That was before I broke into somebody's house with a pick.", "think"],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "My baskets. Look at one. Split bamboo, and the rest of it is air, and it is the air that carries the mountain. Ninety years I have had one on my back and I never looked inside."],
	],
	"visit:s_p20_elders:zhi_sou": [
		["Zhi Sou, the Wise Old Man of the River Bend", "Thirty spokes share one hub. It is where there is nothing that the cart is of use. Clay is kneaded into a pot: it is where there is nothing that the pot is of use. Cut doors and windows for a room: it is where there is nothing that the room is of use."],
		["Zhi Sou, the Wise Old Man of the River Bend", "So what is there is for profit, and what is not there is for use. I did not say it. A better man did. But I have been quoting it at the wrong mountain for two thousand years, and I am obliged to you for the chance to say it once where it applies.", "bow"],
	],
	"s_p20_wang": [
		["Narrator", "Rotten-Axe Cave runs back into Azure Cloud Mountain further than a cave that size should. In its last chamber there is a flat stone with a board cut in it, two stone seats with nobody on them, and a young man in a red headband squatting on his heels, watching the empty board with his whole face."],
		["Wang Zhi the Woodcutter", "Ssh. Blue is thinking. ...Oh. You are not them. Have you seen two boys? They were here a moment ago. I only stopped to watch one game; my wife wants the pot on by noon. I am Wang Zhi, from the village under the ridge. What have you done with the daylight?", "wave"],
		["Lin Feng", "Wang Zhi. How long have you been watching?"],
		["Wang Zhi the Woodcutter", "No time at all. Since breakfast. One of them gave me a thing like a date stone to suck and I have not even been hungry. ...Why do you look at my axe?"],
		["Narrator", "The axe is leaning where he left it. The head is whole. The handle is a line of brown dust on the floor, in the shape of a handle."],
		["Yue Ling", "There is no village under the ridge, Wang Zhi. There is a sect. It has been there three hundred years, and it was built on the foundations of something older.", "grieve"],
		["Wang Zhi the Woodcutter", "...Then the pot has boiled dry. She will be so cross. She... Oh. Oh, I see. All of them? The baby too? ...Look at the board, will you, while I sit down. It is still Black to play. I do not think they finished. I think they are waiting for somebody, underneath."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_board": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng looks at the board for what feels like a breath. When he looks up, the torch has burned down a hand's breadth, Yue Ling has both fists in his sleeve, and there is a square of the floor beside the stone table that is not floor: a shaft, with steps cut in it, and a draught going down."],
		["Yue Ling", "You were gone. You were standing there and you were gone. Do not do that again. ...It is the way down, is it not. Every cave in the land has been breathing towards this."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_hollow": [
		["Narrator", "The Hollow of the Mountain is larger than the sect that stands on it. The Hollow Ones are only its dust got up and walking, in the shapes of whoever was here last, and they lie down again willingly. It is not empty. It is brimming. Every dry well in the land is here, and every stopped spring, standing in the dark, waiting."],
		["Lin Feng", "She has gathered it. All of it. Not to keep it from us. To keep it."],
	],
	"pre:xuanpin": [
		["Narrator", "In the lowest hall there is black water to the walls, without a ripple, and standing on it a tall woman the colour of the water, her hair unbound to the knee and her eyes very pale. She does not look as though she has been waiting. She looks as though waiting were a thing other people did."],
		["Narrator", "The valley spirit never dies, says the old book; it is called the dark female, and the gate of the dark female is the root of heaven and earth. It is there within us all the while; draw on it as you will, it never runs dry."],
		["Xuanpin, the Valley Spirit", "It never ran dry. You stopped it up. A gully here, a cave there. A well with a slag-heap on it. I am what is in a hollow when nothing is, and you left me fewer and fewer places to be. So I came to the last one, and brought what was mine."],
		["Lin Feng", "The land is thirsty. People who never held a spade are thirsty."],
		["Xuanpin, the Valley Spirit", "Yes. I am sorry for it. I am not angry; a hollow is not angry. But I will not go up to be filled. Show me you know what I am for, sweeper, you who filled your own days so full of steps. Or take the water from me, if you can, and see how long it stays in a land with nowhere to hold it."],
	],
	"post:xuanpin": [
		["Narrator", "She does not fall. She steps back, and the black water comes up to her knees, her waist, and then there is only the water, and it is moving. Somewhere far overhead a bucket goes down a well and comes up heavy."],
		["Xuanpin, the Valley Spirit", "Enough. You stand like somebody with room in him. I had not thought there was one left. Then hear me, both of you. I will go back up into the land: into the wells, the caves, the gullies, whatever you have left me. But this hall I cannot keep and be everywhere else as well. It is yours. Say what it is to be."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is the biggest empty place in the world, Lin Feng. And she has just handed it to a man who cannot see a floor without sweeping it.", "laugh"],
	],
	"done:s_p20_wells": [
		["Narrator", "The bucket goes down the well of Shazhou on the old length of rope, the length it was last year, and comes up so full it slops. Three porters and a swordsman haul it, and the first dipper goes on the ground, for the well, and the second to a camel."],
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Sweet. Sweeter than before; it has been somewhere cold. I said I would cook for you. Sit down. You will not get up again before dark."],
	],
	"done:s_p20_boats": [
		["Narrator", "The river at Longmen stands at the mark the grandfathers cut, and every boat that sat on the mud all autumn is afloat and needs bailing. It is wet, cold, back-breaking work, and the whole waterfront is doing it and singing."],
	],
	"s_p20_gong2": [
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "They say the wells are up. I knew before they told me: the swallows are back in the big sinkhole, and my eldest, who is seventy, cried. So. You have been to the bottom of things. What did she say, the lady under the hill?"],
		["Lin Feng", "That she is what is in a hollow when nothing is. That we stopped her up, a hole at a time, all meaning well."],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "All meaning well. Yes. That is how I have done everything.", "nod"],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "I had a thought while you were gone. I am slow, but I get there. A road is an empty place too. That is all I ever wanted: a place where the mountain is not, so that my grandsons could walk through. I do not need the mountain gone. I need a hole in it the size of a cart. And I have just learned who to ask about holes."],
		["Yue Ling", "And the earth out of it?"],
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "To the sea of Bo. A year there and back. It was good enough for the first thousand years; I do not know what possessed me to hurry. Sons and grandsons without end, girl. There is no shortage of time. It turns out what we were short of was room.", "laugh"],
	],
	"s_p20_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Cook Zhou let the bucket down this morning and it came up full, and she looked at it for some time, and then she cooked the rice. I gather that is the highest praise the kitchen awards. Sit. Tell me what is under my floor."],
		["Lin Feng", "A hall, Elder. Bigger than the sect. The mountain is built round it, the way a jar is built round the place the water goes."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And I have sat on top of it for seventy years saying wise things about emptiness to disciples. Qing Lian knew. Of course she knew. She cut nine caves in this mountain and I thought they were for meditating in. They were windows.", "laugh"],
		["Yue Ling", "The woodcutter is at the gate, Elder. Wang Zhi. He says he has nowhere to go and he is good with an axe, if somebody will find him a handle."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then he shall have a handle, and the room over the wood-store, which has been standing empty since before I was born. I used to wonder what it was for. Go and rest, both of you. You have the look of people who have put something down."],
	],
	# ---- Part XXI: The Hundred Arms (the Hundred Arms release)
	"recall19": [
		["Narrator", "Winter comes in with water in the wells. The spades have been given their hours, the hollow places are left hollow, and what was dug before anybody thought to stop is in the yards of Tushan, in heaps, waiting to be something."],
		["Narrator", "Ore does not wait long. On the first morning of the frost a disciple goes to the armoury for a practice sword, and comes back to say that the racks are empty, and that the door was barred from the inside."],
	],
	"s_p21_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Forty jian, twelve spears and the old halberd nobody could lift. Gone in a night, and the bar still in its brackets. They were not taken, Lin Feng. I have looked at the dust. They walked."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And they are not the only ones. Qingshi has a blade in every thatch. The horse-folk's boys are shooting at each other's hats. Somebody is casting them faster than hands can be found, and a blade without a hand goes looking for one."],
		["Lin Feng", "I have eight of my own. The sabre came off a bandit and the fan off an empress. None of them has ever gone anywhere without me."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "No. I have wondered why. Ask them, if you can. And ask at Tushan: the oldest smith there is has come back to his furnace, they say, and will not leave it and will not light it.", "point"],
		["Yue Ling", "Take the broom handle. I mean it. It is the only one of the eight I have never seen you look pleased with yourself for holding."],
	],
	"s_p21_ou": [
		["Narrator", "At the oldest pit in Tushan an old man with forearms like bridge-cables is sitting on an upturned mould, watching bronze run down a channel nobody opened into a shape nobody cut. It comes out a sword. He picks it up, looks along it, and puts it on a heap."],
		["Ou Yezi the Swordsmith", "Good metal. Even. I could not have poured it better, and I am the best there has been. That is the four hundredth this week, and no hand on the ladle. Sit down, boy. You have a jian on your back that I did not make and would not be ashamed of."],
		["Lin Feng", "Who is making them?"],
		["Ou Yezi the Swordsmith", "Nobody. That is the trouble with it. You dug the hills and filled the yards, and an edge is the easiest thing in the world for metal to be. Leave bronze alone with a quarrel anywhere in the land, and it will find its own way to the mould."],
		["Ou Yezi the Swordsmith", "I made five for a king, on purpose, and I knew every hand they would be in. Bring me what the pits have thrown up this morning. I want to see whether a single one of them was meant.", "think"],
	],
	"done:s_p21_blanks": [
		["Ou Yezi the Swordsmith", "Five. Not a flaw in them, and not a thought. Look: no maker's mark, no balance cut, the same sword five times. A weapon somebody made tells you what he was afraid of. These are afraid of nothing, because nobody was there."],
		["Yue Ling", "I was made, and somebody was there, and it still took me a long time to find out what I was for. I should not like to have had to guess."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_cast": [
		["Narrator", "Four casters, four moulds, and at each of them the same thing: the bronze is poured for a bell, a ploughshare, a cauldron, and the caster's hands, without being asked, turn the ladle a little, and it runs long and thin."],
		["Ou Yezi the Swordsmith", "There. Did you see her wrist? She did not. Every one of them has a neighbour he would like to be safe from. The hand knows it before the head does. Go south, boy. There is a girl in the wet country who taught an army and never forged a thing."],
	],
	"s_p21_yue": [
		["Narrator", "In the Crimson Marsh a girl of perhaps sixteen is standing on a root above the water with a green bamboo switch in her hand, entirely still. Round her, at a respectful distance, forty white gibbons are standing in exactly the same way, each with a reed."],
		["The Maiden of Yue", "You have a sword on. Take it off, or go away. I do not say it to be rude. They copy whatever comes into the marsh, and I have had enough trouble since they saw a sabre.", "refuse"],
		["Lin Feng", "They said you taught the army of Yue."],
		["The Maiden of Yue", "For one summer. The method is small and easy. Then the army went and did what armies do with it, and I came home. He taught these. The old man in white. He saw me once, two thousand years ago, and has been showing everybody ever since.", "shrug"],
		["The Maiden of Yue", "Have you a stick? ...That will do. That is a very good stick. Somebody has swept with it. Go and knock down what he has taught, gently, and then I will talk to you about him."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_reeds": [
		["The Maiden of Yue", "You hit them where they were going to be. Good. And you did not hit the small one twice. Better. A staff has no edge, so it cannot mean more than you do. That is why I asked for it."],
		["The Maiden of Yue", "They have run off with my switches, every one. I cut them myself and I know each by the joint. Bring them back and I will take you to him.", "point"],
	],
	"done:s_p21_bamboo": [
		["The Maiden of Yue", "This one he broke, the day we met. See, the tip is gone. I have kept it. He is up there in the big banyan, pretending to be a venerable old gentleman. He will ask to see your sword. They always start by asking to see it."],
	],
	"pre:yuan_gong": [
		["Narrator", "An old man in white comes down out of the banyan hand over hand, rather faster than an old man should, and bows. He has a bamboo in his fist that he has not bothered to trim. The forty gibbons arrange themselves to watch."],
		["Yuan Gong, the White Ape", "I hear you are good with all eight. I should like to see. I only ever saw the one, and I have made such a great deal out of it. Forgive an old man. It is not often anything new comes into the marsh."],
		["Yue Ling", "He is not wicked, Lin Feng. Look at him. He is the first pupil anybody ever had who was not asked.", "think"],
	],
	"post:yuan_gong": [
		["Narrator", "The old man in white goes back up the banyan in one movement and is an ape, and sits in the fork scratching, and looks down with great satisfaction. Below him, forty gibbons put down forty reeds."],
		["Yuan Gong, the White Ape", "Yes. That was it. That was what she had, and I never got: you stopped. Three times you could have, and did not. I have been teaching the stroke for two thousand years and it was never the stroke."],
		["The Maiden of Yue", "He will be unbearable now. He will sit up there being wise at them. ...It is better than the sabres. Thank you. Go north; there is a man there who learned a harder thing than I ever did, and learned it too late."],
	],
	"s_p21_sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "Come in, eat first. Then look at this. That is my grandson's hat. That hole is an arrow. He was wearing it. He thinks it is the finest thing that ever happened to him.", "offer"],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "A man came through in the autumn who could hit a willow leaf at a hundred paces, and every boy on the steppe has been a hundred paces from another boy ever since. He would not stay to teach them the rest. He said he had not learned it yet himself."],
		["Lin Feng", "Where did he go?"],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "North, to the cold grass, where there is nothing to aim at. Find him. And take a bow: he will not talk to you about it otherwise. Our own, the one we strung for you when the Roc fell. It has been waiting to be asked.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p21_ji": [
		["Narrator", "On the Aurora Steppe a lean man is sitting in the snow with a bow across his knees, looking at it with polite puzzlement, as at a stranger's child. All round him, as far as can be seen, arrows are standing in the drifts. None of them is in anything."],
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "Ah. A visitor. You will know: what is this? I have had it since I was a boy. I lay under a loom for it. I looked at a louse for three years for it. And this morning I picked it up and could not think what it was for."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is a bow. You are Ji Chang. You are the best archer under heaven.", "shock"],
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "Am I? I tried to kill my teacher to be that. Our arrows met in the air. ...Yes. It is coming back. Do not let it all come back. The boys are shooting at hats because I showed them how to hit, and left before I knew how not to."],
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "Those out there are theirs: every shot loosed to see how far it would go. An arrow that lands in nothing has not finished. Take that bow of yours and finish them for me. I dare not. If I pick this up again I shall remember everything."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_louse": [
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "I watched. You looked for a long time and loosed late. Fei Wei would have liked you. He used to say that archery is the only art in which a man who misses has nobody to be angry with."],
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "Now gather what they left standing. An arrow belongs to whoever loosed it, hit or miss. I am going to carry these back to the tents myself, and give each boy his own, and say whose hat it nearly was.", "nod"],
	],
	"done:s_p21_arrows": [
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "Five. And there are five hundred. It will take me the winter. Good. Now put the bow down. No, down. On the snow. You see? Your hand does not know what to do. That is the beginning of the second half."],
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "The herds want this ground and there are still a few things on it. Drive them off with something that cannot kill at a hundred paces. The horse-folk use a whip. A whip only reaches as far as you are willing to go yourself."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_herd": [
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "The supreme speech is to give up speaking. The supreme action is to give up acting. The supreme shooting is not to shoot. My teacher's teacher said it on a cliff edge with his heels over nothing, and I thought he was showing off."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the boys?"],
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "I shall teach them the loom first this time. Two years under a loom cures a great deal. Go on, both of you. She has been wanting to ask you something since the marsh, and is too proud to do it in front of me.", "wave"],
	],
	"s_p21_longmen": [
		["Fishwife Lu", "Look at that. Hemp as thick as my thumb, and cut as if with a razor, all in one line. That is the ninth net this month. Have you eaten? Sit. There is fish. There is always fish; it is the nets we are short of.", "offer"],
		["Fishwife Lu", "It is at the ford. My grandmother told it: a man carried two swords across, an old pair, man and wife, and they jumped out of the scabbard into the river and went down as two dragons. Nobody has put a net in that reach since. Until the water dropped."],
		["Yue Ling", "The wells. When the water went down into the mountain, the river fell, and people fished where they never had."],
		["Fishwife Lu", "And then it came back up, and so did something else. There is a pool under the Gate with a way in at low water. I am not asking you to go. I am telling you where it is, and that there is fish when you come out."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_nets": [
		["Narrator", "Three nets are mended on the shingle with nobody saying anything about swords at all. When the last knot is drawn, the oldest of the fishers spits in the river and says the pool was a king's grave once: three thousand swords were put in with him, and a white tiger came and sat on the mound."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_pool": [
		["Narrator", "The Sword Pool goes back under the Dragon Gate like the inside of a bell. The water is a hand deep and perfectly clear, and the floor under it is hilts: bronze hilts by the hundred, in rows, each with a clean bright slot where a blade was this year and is not now."],
		["Yue Ling", "They were buried with him so that nobody would ever use them. Three thousand. It is the kindest thing I ever heard of a king doing, and it lasted exactly until somebody dug the hills.", "grieve"],
	],
	"done:s_p21_hilts": [
		["Narrator", "The fifth hilt is not like the others. It is double: two grips on one guard, worn smooth on the inside of each, as the handles of a thing are worn that two people carry between them."],
	],
	"pre:sword_dragons": [
		["Narrator", "At the back of the pool the water is deep, and in it two long shapes are going round each other and never touching: one the blue of quenched steel, one the red of the fire before it. Where either passes, the rock of the wall is cut as a net is cut."],
		["Lin Feng", "Gan Jiang and Mo Ye. He was the smith. She went into the furnace so that the metal would run."],
		["Yue Ling", "And they have been in the same river ever since and cannot find each other, because each of them is still a sword. Oh, be careful. They are not angry. They are looking.", "grieve"],
	],
	"post:sword_dragons": [
		["Narrator", "The red dragon and the blue lie still at last, side by side on the floor of the pool, and are two swords: an old pair, plain, the same length to a hair. Lin Feng lays them in together. The water of the Sword Pool, which had been singing very faintly, stops."],
		["Lin Feng", "They do not want a hand. They wanted to be put down next to each other."],
		["Narrator", "Up at the ferry, Fishwife Lu's tenth net comes up whole, with a carp in it the length of a man's arm, and she is so surprised that she lets it go."],
	],
	"s_p21_aysha": [
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Water first. Then tea. Then I will tell you that eleven caravans went out this month with no guard on them, because every man in Shazhou who can hold a sword the right way up has gone east to be looked at by a king.", "offer"],
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "He pays in gold and feeds them at his own table, they say, and all he asks is that they fight each other in front of him until one is dead. A hundred and more in a year. He has set up in that red palace the demon had. It suits him."],
		["Lin Feng", "A king of where?"],
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Of Zhao, he says, wherever that was. It is long gone. So is he, I think, only nobody has liked to tell him while he has three thousand swordsmen. Help my people load, and they will tell you who went. Each of them is somebody's.", "grieve"],
	],
	"done:s_p21_porters": [
		["Narrator", "A camel is loaded three times, and three names are said over it: a brother, a husband, a boy of fifteen who took his grandfather's sword off the wall while the old man slept. The last porter says there is somebody else on the east road, a thin man in a scholar's robe, who has been turned away from the palace gate every day for a week."],
	],
	"s_p21_envoy": [
		["The Swordsman in Scholar's Dress", "Ah. You are armed like a porcupine and walk like a man carrying eggs. Good. I have been waiting for somebody to go in on my behalf. They will not have me: the king sees only swordsmen, and I came as I am."],
		["The Swordsman in Scholar's Dress", "His son sent for me. The father has loved swords for three years, the state is going to ruin, and the neighbours are looking at the border. I have a thing to say to him about three swords. It needs saying by somebody he cannot have thrown out."],
		["Lin Feng", "Why not put on a short coat and go in yourself?"],
		["The Swordsman in Scholar's Dress", "I did, once, in the book. It worked. He stayed in his palace three months, and all his swordsmen killed themselves, which the book puts in as if it were a happy ending. I should like it to come out better this time. Take a fan. He respects a fan; he does not know why.", "shrug"],
	],
	"done:s_p21_gate": [
		["Narrator", "They let him through in the end out of bewilderment. A man who comes through three ranks of swordsmen with a fan, and has not once opened it all the way, is either a fool or the best they have seen, and nobody at that gate has ever been sure which they would rather meet."],
		["Yue Ling", "Look at the yard. The slips of wood. They are everywhere, Lin Feng, in the sand, in the gutters. Each one is broken in the same place."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_tallies": [
		["Narrator", "Five tallies: five men. On the back of one somebody has cut a small horse, as a child would. Lin Feng puts them inside his robe. The champions of the inner yard, who have been watching him pick splinters out of the sand, send word that they wish to see a stroke."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_yard": [
		["Narrator", "One stroke. It takes the tassel off the first champion's sword and stops. The inner yard is entirely silent. Then the man laughs, a short astonished bark, bows from the waist, and stands away from the door of the hall."],
	],
	"pre:king_zhao": [
		["Narrator", "The Demon Lord's hall, hung with swords from the roof-tree to the floor. Under them, on a plain mat, sits a thin grey king with his chin in his hand, who has not looked up. Two men are fighting in front of him. He says: stop. They stop."],
		["King Wen of Zhao, Who Loved Swords", "A fan. They told me. What do you want to teach me, that you get my son to send you? ...No matter. I am told your sword cuts down a man in ten paces and does not stop for a thousand li. Good. I have not seen anything good in a year. Show me."],
		["Lin Feng", "I have three swords. The king may choose which he will see."],
		["King Wen of Zhao, Who Loved Swords", "Three. ...Somebody has said that to me before. I do not remember when. I remember I did not eat for three days after. Show me the lowest, then. Show me the one I already have.", "think"],
	],
	"post:king_zhao": [
		["Narrator", "The king sits down on the mat among his fallen swords, and Lin Feng lays in front of him five broken slips of wood. He looks at them for a long time. He turns over the one with the horse."],
		["King Wen of Zhao, Who Loved Swords", "The sword of the Son of Heaven: its point is the valley of Yan and the stone wall, its edge is Qi and Mount Tai. The sword of a lord: its point is men of wisdom. And mine. Which cuts throats in front and livers behind, and is no different from a fighting cock."],
		["King Wen of Zhao, Who Loved Swords", "He told me. The thin man. I walked round the table three times and could not sit. And then I forgot it, and went on, because it was easier to watch than to rule. Who was the boy with the horse?", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "We do not know. Nobody wrote it down. That is what the tally is instead of.", "grieve"],
	],
	"visit:s_p21_masters:ou_yezi": [
		["Ou Yezi the Swordsmith", "What is it for? Ask me what a furnace is for. To be put out, in the end, with the work done. I made five that were perfect, and the best thing any of them ever did was leave a bad king in the night. A weapon is for not being needed. It is the only tool I know that is finished when it is hung up."],
	],
	"visit:s_p21_masters:yue_maiden": [
		["The Maiden of Yue", "For being quicker than what is coming at you. That is all. I told a minister so and he looked for the rest of it. When the gate is shut, it is shut; when it is open, it is open. A weapon is a gate. People want it to be a road."],
	],
	"visit:s_p21_masters:ji_chang": [
		["Ji Chang the Archer", "For looking. I mean it. I learned to see a louse as a cartwheel so as to hit it, and found that I had learned to see. The hitting was what I paid for the lessons with. If I had known, I would have asked to pay in something else."],
	],
	"visit:s_p21_masters:sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "For the wolf. We have always had bows; a herd without a bow is a wolf's dinner. But there is one bow to a tent, and it hangs by the door-flap where the grandmother can see who takes it down. That is all the law we ever needed about it."],
	],
	"visit:s_p21_masters:fishwife_lu": [
		["Fishwife Lu", "I have a gutting knife. Thirty years. It has never been anything but a gutting knife, because every morning it has four hundred fish to do. Give a blade enough honest work and it has not the time to be a weapon. That is my whole opinion."],
	],
	"visit:s_p21_masters:cook_aysha": [
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "For the road. A guard walks beside the camels with a sword he hopes all the way to Shazhou he will not draw, and if he does not draw it I pay him the same. That is the only bargain about swords I ever heard of that an honest person could keep."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_masters": [
		["Yue Ling", "Hung up. A gate. For looking. By the door-flap. Four hundred fish. Paid the same if he does not draw. ...Lin Feng, not one of them said: for winning."],
		["Lin Feng", "No. I noticed. I have been trying to remember whether I ever did."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_adept": [
		["Narrator", "Three weapons that he no longer has to think about, and what each of them wants is quite plain to him now, as the wish of a horse is plain to somebody who has ridden it a year. None of them wants blood. They want, like anything, to be used well."],
	],
	"s_p21_grave": [
		["Gravekeeper Mu Gu", "You hear it. They have always hummed when a bearer of the Seal walked by; I used to find it company. This is not that. This is every blade in the ground at once, and it has not stopped in nine days.", "shiver"],
		["Gravekeeper Mu Gu", "The barrows are open. Nothing broke in. They came up by themselves, and the saints are lying there with their hands folded on nothing. The new ones have come to them, do you see, the ones with no maker. A sword with no hand goes to where swords are."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the old ones went with them."],
		["Gravekeeper Mu Gu", "Down into the long barrow, where the first of the saints was laid before the sect had a name. Take a brush, if you have one. Their tablets are down. A blade whose name is standing will lie still. I have seen it. It is the only thing I know about them.", "point"],
	],
	"done:s_p21_barrow": [
		["Narrator", "Each time the judge's brush touches steel, a name comes up bright along the flat of it, in the hand of whoever cut the tablet, and the sword lowers its point like a guest who has been recognised at a door."],
		["Yue Ling", "They only wanted somebody to know which one they were. Three thousand in a pool with no names, four hundred in a week with no maker. Of course they got up."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_tablets": [
		["Narrator", "Four tablets stand again in their niches. The fourth is the Founder's. Under her name is cut, small, in a different hand: she swept the steps first. The humming in the barrow drops by half, and what is left of it is all coming from one place."],
	],
	"pre:wuzhu": [
		["Narrator", "At the back of the long barrow stands a figure the height of a man, made of blades as a pine-cone is made of scales: every sword that walked off a rack this winter, and every one the pits poured unasked. Where its face should be there is a polished flat, and Lin Feng's own is in it."],
		["Wuzhu, the Masterless Edge", "You have eight. You have never once been cut by any of them. We watched. We have come to ask how it is done. We are four thousand, and not one of us was made by anybody, and we do not know what we are for."],
		["Wuzhu, the Masterless Edge", "So we have made a hand. Look. It is the best we could do. It has your face on it because yours was the only one we were sure of. Show us what a hand does. All of it. We will know, if you leave any out."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is not asking to fight you, Lin Feng. It is asking to be held, and it only knows the one way to ask.", "grieve"],
	],
	"post:wuzhu": [
		["Narrator", "The figure comes apart quietly, blade by blade, each one laying itself down on the floor of the barrow with its point to the wall. The flat that was its face is the last. It lies at Lin Feng's feet, an unmarked jian, the four hundredth of a week, and does not move."],
		["Lin Feng", "That is all of it. That is what a hand does. It puts you down, when it is over, and picks you up again when it has to, and in between it sweeps the steps."],
		["Narrator", "Far away, on Azure Cloud Mountain, forty jian, twelve spears and the old halberd nobody could lift are back on their racks when the disciples come in the morning. The bar of the door is in its brackets. Nobody saw them come."],
	],
	"done:s_p21_racks": [
		["Narrator", "In Qingshi three farmers take three blades down out of three thatches, and it is done as was decided, in front of the whole street, with Granny Wen watching from her doorway to see that nobody keeps one back."],
	],
	"s_p21_ou2": [
		["Ou Yezi the Swordsmith", "It has stopped. Yesterday at noon. The channel ran a bell, as it was cut to, and the girl at the ladle wept, and did not know why. So. You found whose hand it was."],
		["Lin Feng", "Everybody's. Each of them a little. And nobody had told the blades what they were for, so they went to ask."],
		["Ou Yezi the Swordsmith", "Hm. I should have liked to tell Zhanlu that, before it left. I made a sword too good for the man who owned it, and it had to work out the rest alone. Give me that one. The blank. I shall put a mark on it. Mine. It is time something of this winter's was meant.", "nod"],
	],
	"s_p21_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "They are back. Every one, on its own peg. And there is a forty-first jian on the rack that I have never seen, with a maker's mark on the tang that has not been cut in two thousand years. I shall not ask."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The old book says arms are instruments of ill omen, and that the gentleman lives with them on his left and does not make them his delight. I read that for sixty years as a warning about swords. It is about the left hand. It says: keep them where the heart is."],
		["Yue Ling", "He swept the steps this morning, Elder. With the staff. I watched. It took him twice as long as it used to, and he did not look pleased with himself once."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then you have it. Go and sit under the pine, Lin Feng. There is a thing left to put down, and I think it is not any of the eight.", "nod"],
	],
	# ---- Part XXIII: The Thundering Pots (the Thundering Pots release)
	# ---- Part XXIV: The Lost Horse (the Lost Horse release)
	"recall22": [
		["Narrator", "The Yellow Bell is cast again, and the land sings in tune for a year. It is a good year. Nothing comes down off any mountain that Lin Feng cannot put back, and by the autumn the younger disciples have begun to tell each other that the sweeper has never lost a fight."],
		["Narrator", "They are nearly right, and Elder Qing Xuan, who has heard them at it, has not slept well since. On a morning in the eighth month a horse walks in at the sect gate: a shaggy frontier horse, lathered to the ears, with a halter and no rider. It goes straight to the trough, as if it had been told where it was."],
	],
	"s_p24_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Twenty-three times something has come out of the ground or the sky or the old books, and twenty-three times you have gone and stood in front of it and it has stopped. The boys in the outer court think that is what you are for. I have begun to be afraid you think so too.", "think"],
		["Lin Feng", "I have been beaten, Elder. I have woken in the infirmary before now."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You have been delayed. It is not the same. Everything you ever lost to, you went back to and won. I am asking what you will do the day you go back and it is still bigger than you. I do not know the answer. I did not teach you one, because I have not got one.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "The horse has a mark cut in its off ear. That is a frontier cut, from the Aurora Steppe. Somebody up there is short a horse."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "An old man. I knew him when he was only middle-aged. He will be asking his neighbours by now how they know it is not good fortune, and they will not forgive him. Take it home. And ask him what he knows. He has lost more than anybody I ever met, and he is the least beaten man alive.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p24_weng": [
		["Sai Weng", "So it went all the way to the mountain. It never did like the wind up here. You have brought it back; now the neighbours will come and say how lucky I am, and I shall have to ask them how they know it is not a misfortune. They hate that.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "My Elder says you know something he could not teach me. He says I have never lost."],
		["Sai Weng", "Then you are in luck, and how do we know it is not bad luck? Sit. Three things are coming this season that are not beaten. I do not mean hard. I mean that the sword is the wrong kind of thing, as a spoon is the wrong kind of thing for a fire."],
		["Sai Weng", "The tide of the eighth month, at Yanggu. The ape Great Yu chained under Turtle Mountain, whose chain has rusted. And ten thousand riders out of the north, who will come past this tent before the snow. You will stand in front of all three, because you are that sort. You will lose to all three.", "point"],
		["Yue Ling", "You say it as if it were the weather."],
		["Sai Weng", "It is the weather. What you do the morning after is not; that part is yours, and it is the only part that ever was. But first: something followed my horse home across the grass, and it has not gone away. Be useful while you wait to be humbled.", "nod"],
	],
	"done:s_p24_strays": [
		["Narrator", "The last empty hoofbeat goes out over the grass and does not come back. What is left is a halter with a slipped knot, a handful of burrs, and the particular quiet of a place a horse has just left."],
		["Yue Ling", "They were not chasing it. They were only what comes loose when something runs away. I did not know a thing could leave so much behind by leaving."],
	],
	"done:s_p24_shoes": [
		["Narrator", "Four shoes, laid in a row on the old man's felt. He turns each one over with a finger. North, a long way, and then something turned it and it ran south till the land stopped at a mountain: the nails are worn on the side that takes the weight in a turn."],
		["Sai Weng", "It went to the Hu, then. Right over the frontier and in among their herds, and you fetched it back from your mountain. It will go again; it has the taste now. Who knows what it will bring? Leave it. The first of the three does not wait for a horse: go to Yanggu. There is a king there who has lost to the tide three times, and is the better for it."],
	],
	"s_p24_son": [
		["Weng the Younger", "It did not come back alone. Go and look up at the height. There: black, with the red ear. That is a horse of the Hu, of the line they do not sell, and it followed our old mare home like a dog. Every tent on the frontier has been here to say what luck. Do you know what my father said?", "point"],
		["Lin Feng", "How do you know it is not a misfortune."],
		["Weng the Younger", "To their faces! Old Chagan had brought a cheese. I am going to ride that horse. I have wanted one thing my whole life and it has walked into our camp by itself. Only nobody can get a hand on it. It stands up there and looks at the north.", "laugh"],
		["Yue Ling", "And if it throws you?"],
		["Weng the Younger", "Then I shall have been thrown by the best horse on the frontier. Bring it down for me, sweeper. They say there is nothing you cannot bring down."],
	],
	"pre:hu_steed": [
		["Narrator", "Close to, it is not black. It is the colour of the hour before a storm, and its red ear turns to follow them while the rest of it does not move at all. It has thrown nine riders in its own country. It did not follow the mare. It came because it was tired of being owned by people who were sure of it."],
	],
	"post:hu_steed": [
		["Narrator", "It stands, blowing, and lets the halter be put on. The boy rides it that same evening, the whole camp watching, and for the length of one gallop he is the happiest person on the frontier. At the turn by the birch it stops as a wall stops. He goes on."],
		["Weng the Younger", "It is the thigh. I heard it. Do not look like that, sweeper, you did what I asked. Father. Father, say it. You are going to say it anyway.", "grieve"],
		["Sai Weng", "The neighbours will be here by morning to tell us how sorry they are. And I shall say: how do you know it is not good fortune? I shall say it, and I shall mean it, and I shall want to bite through the tent pole. Set the bone, girl. You have the hands for it.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "It will knit. It will not bend again."],
		["Sai Weng", "Then that is what we know so far. Go on to the Drowned Fields. The Huai will not wait for a boy's leg, and the second thing is worse than the first: the tide at least goes out again. Ask for Bo Yi of the Dykes. He dug for Great Yu, and he has not been surprised since."],
	],
	"s_p24_qian": [
		["King Qian", "From the old man of the frontier? Then you have come to be beaten by my tide. Everybody should be, once. I was a salt-smuggler before I was a king, and I thought there was nothing I could not get past, and then I tried to hold the salt shore below this market.", "laugh"],
		["King Qian", "On the eighteenth day of the eighth month the sea comes up the estuary in one wave, as high as a city wall, and the people here say it is white horses and that they can see the manes. I built a wall. It took the wall. I built it again, and a third time. I had five hundred crossbowmen put three thousand arrows into it at the crest."],
		["Lin Feng", "And?"],
		["King Qian", "And I have five hundred very good crossbowmen, the only soldiers this market has ever seen. Go down to the foreshore and stand in front of it. Then come and tell me what you want done with this coast: I have tried my ways, and I am told you are somebody whose choices stay where they are put.", "point"],
	],
	"done:s_p24_tide": [
		["Narrator", "Three times the white horses come up the shore, and three times Lin Feng is somewhere else when they have gone: up the beach, in the reeds, once sitting in a fishing boat that had been a good way off. He is not hurt. He has only been moved, like a thing on a table."],
		["Yue Ling", "You are smiling."],
		["Lin Feng", "I stood in front of it with everything I had. I do not think it knew I was there. I have never been so little in the way of anything in my life."],
	],
	"done:s_p24_wrack": [
		["Narrator", "The shore is clean to the wrack-line. What the bore left has gone back into the mud, and the gulls that are only gulls come down again and walk about as if they had driven it off themselves."],
	],
	"done:s_p24_shells": [
		["Narrator", "Four shells from water no net of Yanggu has ever reached, carried a mile inland and set down without a chip. King Qian holds one to his ear for a long time, like a man listening at a door."],
	],
	"s_p24_qian2": [
		["King Qian", "Deeper water than last year, and a full day earlier. It is not angry. People always want it to be angry; it is easier to be beaten by a thing that hates you. It is only very large, and going somewhere.", "think"],
		["Lin Feng", "The old man said three things. This was one."],
		["King Qian", "The second comes down the rivers, not up them. The Huai is in flood without rain, and old Bo Yi in the Drowned Fields has sent to me for iron. A great deal of iron. But go by the frontier. A rider came in this morning asking for you: the old man's horse ran off again, and has come back, and not alone.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p24_boyi": [
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Iron, yes. For a chain. Sit down, and do not put your feet in that; it was a field yesterday. When Yu cut the Huai its road to the sea, the river would not go. Wind and thunder at Tongbai Mountain, the stones crying out. Three times. There was a thing in it."],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "An ape. Blue body, white head, eyes like gold, teeth like snow. Strength of nine elephants, and a neck that comes out a hundred feet when he wants to see over something. Wuzhiqi. Yu set two captains on him and he threw them. The third, Gengchen, got a chain on his neck and a bell through his nose, and we put him under Turtle Mountain.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "And the chain has rusted."],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Four thousand years, girl, in a river. I am surprised at the bell. He is out, and he is sitting at the foot of the mountain with nine links still on him, and the Huai goes where he goes. Yu could not beat him. Yu only held him. And I have not got a Gengchen.", "think"],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Clear what he has brought up with him, and find me what links you can. Then go and look at him. I will not ask you to win. I dug for the man who did not."],
	],
	"done:s_p24_silt": [
		["Narrator", "The fields are quiet, and under water, and quiet. An ox stands to its belly in what was millet and has stopped trying to get out. It is only an ox now. Yue Ling leads it up on to the dyke by one horn."],
	],
	"done:s_p24_links": [
		["Narrator", "Five links, rolled up out of the silt on poles, each as big as a millstone. Every one is rusted through at the same place, and on the inside curve every one is worn as bright as a mirror."],
		["Lin Feng", "He pulled. For four thousand years, at the same place in every link. He never once stopped pulling."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then I do not think a new chain is a thing we should be proud of making. I do not say we should not make it."],
	],
	"pre:wuzhiqi": [
		["Narrator", "He is sitting with his back to Turtle Mountain and his feet in the river, picking rust out of the fur of his neck, and the water stands up round him like a crowd round a man who is about to speak. He looks at the sword, and then at the two of them, and something that is nearly disappointment goes over the white face."],
		["Narrator", "Great Yu's captains went up to him with weapons, and were thrown. He has been waiting four thousand years to see whether anybody had thought of anything else."],
	],
	"fall:wuzhiqi": [
		["Narrator", "When Lin Feng can count again he is on the top of the dyke, a quarter of a mile off, lying across Yue Ling's knees, with river weed in his collar. Below them the Huai is settling round the ape again, slowly, like a dog lying down. The ape has not moved from the foot of the mountain."],
	],
	"fall:wuzhiqi:struck": [
		["Yue Ling", "I lost count of what we threw at him. Then he picked you up. Gently. Like a man moving a child that is in the way of the door."],
		["Lin Feng", "Yu could not do it either. I find that is no comfort at all. I had thought it would be."],
	],
	"fall:wuzhiqi:kept": [
		["Lin Feng", "You were mending me the whole time. I felt it. Every blow of his, and then your hands, and then his, and then yours."],
		["Yue Ling", "And you me. He stopped, at the end, and watched us do it. I do not think he had seen that before. Then the river came. But he watched first."],
	],
	"fall:wuzhiqi:gave": [
		["Yue Ling", "You put up your sword. I have never seen you do that. He had his hand raised, and you looked at him, and you stepped out of the river and stood on the bank."],
		["Lin Feng", "He put his hand down. And then the water did what it was always going to do. But he put his hand down first. I should like to know what that was worth, some day."],
	],
	"visit:s_p24_lost:granny_wen": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Lost? My husband, to the river, when I was nineteen. I thought I would die of it. I took up the midwifing to have something to do with my hands at night. Four hundred and six children, since. Do not ask me to say it was lucky. Ask the four hundred and six.", "think"],
	],
	"visit:s_p24_lost:sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "The whole herd, the white winter. Every head. We ate the dogs. And because we had nothing, the Black Tents let us through their grazing without a fight, and that is how my daughter met her man. I have nine grandchildren out of a dead herd. I still dream about the horses. Sit. Eat."],
	],
	"visit:s_p24_lost:cook_aysha": [
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "A caravan. Forty camels and my father's whole stake, to one sandstorm. So I stayed at my grandmother's oven, which I had sworn to leave, and learned to cook for the caravans that could go. You are standing in what came of it. It wants salt. Everything he ever did wanted salt.", "laugh"],
	],
	"visit:s_p24_lost:fishwife_lu": [
		["Fishwife Lu", "My first boat. On the Dragon Gate rocks, with the season's catch in her. I was two years on the quay gutting other people's fish. Know every fish in this river by the feel now, and every cheat by the smell. They do not short-weigh me. I would have her back tomorrow, mind; the one I live on steers like a door."],
	],
	"visit:s_p24_lost:auntie_rong": [
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "The big casting, the year I was made forewoman. A bell for a king. It cracked in the cooling, top to bottom, and I wanted the ground to open. We broke it up and poured forty ploughshares out of it, because there was a famine and no time. Nobody remembers that king. They are still ploughing.", "think"],
	],
	"visit:s_p24_lost:lantern_xi": [
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "My eyes, mostly. I cannot do the fine painting now; Ah Mei does it. So I do frames, and I do them by hand, in the dark, and they are the best frames on the coast. I did not choose it. I should like to be clear about that. One is not obliged to be grateful to a thing for being survivable."],
	],
	"done:s_p24_lost": [
		["Narrator", "Four fires, four losses, and not one of them told as a lucky thing. Each was only told all the way to the end, which took in every case some thirty years. Yue Ling is quiet on the road back to the Drowned Fields."],
		["Yue Ling", "None of them said it was for the best. They said what came after. I think that is a different thing, and I think the old man knows it is."],
	],
	"s_p24_boyi2": [
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Well. It is done as you said, and the river has taken it as it takes everything, without remark. I have been wrong about this water so often that I have stopped keeping the tally; Gun kept one, and look.", "think"],
		["Lin Feng", "King Qian says the tide is not angry. Is he?"],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Wuzhiqi? He was here before the river had a name. We are the flood, from where he sits. Now listen. The cranes have come south a month early and there is dust on the north wind. I have seen that twice. It means horses, and I do not mean a herd.", "point"],
		["Bo Yi of the Dykes", "Go back to your old man on the frontier. That is where it will come through. And, sweeper: it will not be looking for you, either. Nothing that size ever is."],
	],
	"done:s_p24_outriders": [
		["Narrator", "The scouts fall back northward, not beaten, only recalled, the way a hand is drawn back into a sleeve. From the height the dust is plain now: a brown wall from one side of the sky to the other, and under it a sound like rain on felt."],
	],
	"done:s_p24_tokens": [
		["Narrator", "Four slips of birch bark, each with a notch for a man, a notch for a horse and a notch for a bow. Every tent on the frontier with a sound son has one nailed to its pole. The old man's pole has a nail-hole, and no slip."],
		["Weng the Younger", "He rode up, and looked at my leg, and pulled it down again with two fingers. He did not say a word. I have never been so ashamed in my life, and my mother's sister has not stopped crying for joy since.", "grieve"],
	],
	"pre:the_riders": [
		["Narrator", "They come past the foot of the height at a walk. There is no front to it and no end. They are not an army in a hurry; they are a people moving, with their herds and wagons and their gods on poles, and every sound man of the frontier is being gathered up into them as a river gathers up a stream."],
		["Narrator", "Two people are standing in the grass in the way. A captain of a hundred lifts his hand, not to them, to the rank behind him: go round."],
	],
	"fall:the_riders": [
		["Narrator", "It takes until dusk for all of them to pass. When the dust comes down Lin Feng is where he was, on one knee, with the grass trodden flat for a mile on either hand of him and not a mark on the ground where he stands. They went round. Ten thousand riders went round, as water goes round a stone, and did not slow."],
		["Narrator", "Every tent on the frontier has lost its sons. Nine in ten of them will not come back from whatever war the north is riding to. At the old man's tent a boy with a stiff leg is standing in the doorway, holding the black horse by its halter, watching them go."],
	],
	"fall:the_riders:struck": [
		["Sai Weng", "You fought the front rank until they stopped sending anybody at you. That was the insult, I think. Not the losing. That it cost them nothing to leave you standing there.", "think"],
	],
	"fall:the_riders:kept": [
		["Sai Weng", "You two held each other up for half a day in the middle of that. I watched from the door. Some of them looked down at you as they passed. I do not know what they will make of it, in the north, by their fires. Something, I think.", "think"],
	],
	"fall:the_riders:gave": [
		["Sai Weng", "You put up your sword and stood where you were. You, of all people, stood in the grass and let them by, and you looked every one of them in the face. I have seen a great many brave things on this frontier. That is the first I should not have known how to do.", "think"],
	],
	"done:s_p24_marks": [
		["Narrator", "Six things, in six places, that were not there before Lin Feng chose. None of them is as he pictured it when he gave the answer. People have leaned things against them. Birds have found them. One has been mended already, by somebody who did not know whose it was."],
		["Yue Ling", "You lost three times this season, and the land has not moved an inch for it. And you said a few words, here and there, over the years, and look."],
	],
	"pre:huofu": [
		["Narrator", "On the height above the sea-road a figure sits at a low desk with a book open in front of it, and it has two faces, one looking at each page. On the left page it writes with a red brush and on the right with a black, and it has done so, without lifting either, since there was first something to gain and something to lose."],
		["Narrator", "\"Lin Feng,\" it says, with both mouths, not quite together. \"Twenty-three in the black column. Three in the red, this season, and I am obliged to you: I had begun to think your page was a mistake of the copyist's. Shall we see which column today goes in?\""],
	],
	"post:huofu": [
		["Narrator", "The book lies shut. Both brushes have rolled off the desk. Huofu looks at them with both faces, and does not pick them up, and for the first time since there was a first time the two mouths say different things."],
		["Huofu, Who Keeps Both Columns", "Misfortune is what fortune leans upon. Fortune is where misfortune hides. Who knows where it ends? I wrote that myself, in the margin, and I have been drawing a line down the middle of the page ever since, as if I had not."],
		["Yue Ling", "What will you do without the book?"],
		["Huofu, Who Keeps Both Columns", "Wait and see, I suppose. I am told there is an old man on the frontier who has made a whole life of it. I should like to meet him. I think I have been spelling his name wrong."],
	],
	"s_p24_weng2": [
		["Sai Weng", "So the book is shut. I felt it: I was sorry about something this morning, and then I thought, how do I know, and nobody wrote it down. It is very restful.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "I lost three times. To the tide, to the ape, to the riders. I did not go back and win. There was no going back."],
		["Sai Weng", "And? Look round you. A shore is one shape instead of another because of what you said the morning after. A river. You did more to this land by losing and choosing than you ever did by winning. Winning leaves things as they were. That is what it is for.", "nod"],
		["Weng the Younger", "Father. Tell them about the horse."],
		["Sai Weng", "Yes. The black horse. It broke my son and it saved my son, and it is standing behind my tent eating my hay, and the whole frontier wants to know what I mean to do with it. They will do as I do; they always have, grumbling. And I find I would rather it were you who said. You have had the practice.", "point"],
	],
	"pre:debt_mercy": [
		["Narrator", "It has a great many faces and they are all grateful: whoever Lin Feng ever let go, the ones he forgave and the ones he only spared, and behind each of them, smaller, somebody they went on to hurt. It does not accuse. It holds out a hand, palm up, as a person does who has come for what is owed."],
		["The Debt of Mercy", "You were kind to us. All of us. We have come to say so, and to ask who paid."]],
	"post:debt_mercy": [
		["Narrator", "The faces go out one at a time, like lamps along a street, and each one, going, says a name. Not Lin Feng's. Their own. It takes a long while."],
		["Yue Ling", "You would let every one of them go again. I watched your face. Every one."],
		["Lin Feng", "Yes. But I shall ask, next time, where they mean to go."]],
	"pre:iron_judge": [
		["Narrator", "It sits behind a table of black iron with a list in front of it, and it has Lin Feng's way of holding its shoulders. Everything he ever drove out, struck off, banished or broke is entered on the list in his own hand, and at the foot of it there is one line left blank, with his name against it."],
		["The Iron Judge", "You never once asked what it had been like for them. Neither shall I. Stand there. This will not take long; you taught me to be quick."]],
	"post:iron_judge": [
		["Narrator", "The table is in two pieces. The list has blown open at the first page, and the judge, with nothing to sit behind, turns out to be no taller than a sweeper."],
		["The Iron Judge", "Read it, then. All of it. Aloud. That is the sentence: only that."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng reads until the light goes. Yue Ling holds the pages down against the wind."]],
	"pre:unkept_engine": [
		["Narrator", "It is made of whatever Lin Feng ever set going and walked away from, wheels and hammers and things that build themselves, all turning, all fed, nobody at any of it. It is not angry. It is on time. It has been on time for so long that there is no longer anywhere in it for a hand to go."],
		["Narrator", "Somewhere inside, very small, a bell that was meant to say when to stop rings and rings, and is not heard above the work."]],
	"post:unkept_engine": [
		["Narrator", "It stops the way a wheel stops when somebody lets the gate down: all at once, and then a long time running on. In the quiet the little bell is very loud. Lin Feng reaches in and stills it with two fingers."],
		["Yue Ling", "It was only ever waiting to be told it could. Nobody builds that part. You have to stand there."]],
	"pre:unturned_stone": [
		["Narrator", "It is a stone the size of a hall, with moss on it a hand deep, and carved into every face of it, over and over, in the oldest writing there is: as it was done, so do. Under it something is growing, pale, bent double, that has never seen the sun. The stone knows. It has decided that this, too, is as it was done."]],
	"post:unturned_stone": [
		["Narrator", "It does not break. Nothing that old breaks. It is lifted, one corner, the width of a hand, by two people with their backs against it, long enough to see what is underneath; and then, because some of it was holding the hill up, it is set down again an inch to the left."],
		["Lin Feng", "It was right about most things. I only wanted to know which."]],
	"pre:doorless_road": [
		["Narrator", "It comes up the mountain in every direction at once: a road, the width of a cart, going everywhere, with nobody's door on it. Rubbings of a map blow along it. Kites go over. Everything Lin Feng ever opened, lent, copied or let fly is somewhere on it, travelling, and none of it has been home in years."],
		["The Road With No Door", "Come with us. You always said it was the wide world. We have looked. It is very wide. We cannot find where it stops."]],
	"post:doorless_road": [
		["Narrator", "It lies down across the mountain like a thing that has been walking for a long time, and where its head rests there is, for no reason anybody can give, a gate-post; and against the gate-post, a broom."],
		["Yue Ling", "It only wanted somewhere to arrive. You can go everywhere and still be expected for supper. People do."]],
	"pre:valley_wall": [
		["Narrator", "It is a wall, well built, of everything Lin Feng ever kept at home, kept back, or kept to the old way, each laid in it like a stone. Inside it there is one valley, warm, lit, quite safe. Outside it a great many people are standing in the dark with their hands on the stones, and it has never occurred to the wall that they are cold."]],
	"post:valley_wall": [
		["Narrator", "Nobody pulls it down. A wall is a good thing to have at one's back. Lin Feng takes eleven stones out of it, at the height of a cart, and Yue Ling hangs a lantern in the gap; and that is a gate."],
		["Lin Feng", "It is still the home fire. It only has a door, now, that opens from both sides."]],
	"pre:the_unchosen": [
		["Narrator", "It has no face, because it never settled on one. It is the size of everything that was nearly said: every crossroads walked past with half an answer, every scale left level because both pans were heavy. It is very gentle. It agrees with whoever spoke last. Standing near it, Lin Feng finds that he cannot remember what he thinks about anything."]],
	"post:the_unchosen": [
		["Narrator", "It does not fall. It turns, slowly, as a crowd turns when somebody at last says a thing plainly, and for one moment it has a face. Nobody afterwards agrees whose. Then it is only mist on a mountain, going where the wind goes, which is at least a direction."],
		["Yue Ling", "What did you tell it?"],
		["Lin Feng", "What I wanted. It was the first time I had said it aloud. I think that is all it needed anybody to do."]],
	"done:s_p24_reckon": [
		["Narrator", "The heights are empty. Below them the sect's roofs are where they were, and the nine hundred and ninety-nine steps, and somebody small is sweeping them from the top down."],
		["Yue Ling", "That was you. Not something that happened to you: you. Every piece of it."],
		["Lin Feng", "I know. I should like to say I would do it all differently. I find I would do about a third of it differently, and I know which third."]],
	"s_p24_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You look different. Not older. You look like somebody who has been set down somewhere by something and had to walk back.", "think"],
		["Lin Feng", "Three times, Elder. The sea, a river and ten thousand horsemen. None of them was looking for me. I think that was the lesson, though the old man would say it is too soon to tell."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "He would. I asked you what you would do the day you went back and it was still bigger than you. Well?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I did not go back. I looked at what was left and I said what should be done with it, and went on. It is a smaller thing than winning. It seems to last longer."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then you know something I do not, and I am too old to be anything but glad of it. The boys in the outer court will be disappointed. Go and sit under the pine. There is a realm that is only reached by people who have stopped needing to be the hardest thing in the valley.", "bow"],
	],
	"pre:s_break29": [
		["Narrator", "Under the pine Lin Feng sits, and for once does not gather himself. He lets the qi go where it goes: down, as water does, to the lowest place, the place everybody disdains. The sky does what it has always done when he sits here. It gathers. It has never yet been asked to strike something that does not mean to stand."],
	],
	"post:s_break29": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation passes over the mountain as the riders passed over the steppe, and when it has gone the pine is standing and so is Lin Feng, and neither of them is marked. It did not miss. It went round. Far down in the valley somebody is leading a horse to water, in no hurry, and nobody at all is keeping the score."],
	],
	# ---- Part XXV: The Three Burrows (the Three Burrows release)
	"recall23": [
		["Narrator", "The story of the three losses goes down the mountain faster than any victory ever did. A sweeper who stood in front of the sea and lost, and then said what should be done with the shore, and it was done: people who have never trusted a hero find they can trust that."],
		["Narrator", "They begin to arrive in the spring. Not disciples: families, with a cart, a grandmother and a crate of hens. By midsummer there are fires along the road below the nine hundred and ninety-nine steps from the gate to the second bend, and none of them has asked to be let in."],
	],
	"s_p25_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Count the fires on the road tonight. I have. A hundred and six. They are not pilgrims; a pilgrim goes home again. These have brought their hens.", "point"],
		["Lin Feng", "They want to enter the sect?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "They want to live near you, which is not the same and is a great deal more trouble. A sect may shut its gate. Somebody people have made up their minds to trust has no gate to shut. Where Shun ploughed, says the old book, in a year there was a hamlet, in two a town, in three a capital. Nobody asked Shun.", "think"],
		["Yue Ling", "They cannot winter on a road. The first snow comes down that slope like a door."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "No. The east shoulder of the mountain is ours by a deed three hundred years old, and nothing has ever been done with it but look at it. I am giving it to you. Not to the sect: to you, so that when it goes wrong it is yours. That is all that owning a thing has ever meant.", "nod"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "There is a man sitting on it already. He came up a month ago, asked for nothing, and has been singing to his sword. I think he is a steward in want of a house. Go and see whether you are one."],
	],
	"s_p25_feng": [
		["Feng Xuan", "\"Long sword, let us go home! Here there is no fish to eat.\" That is the first verse. In the second there is no carriage, and in the third no way to keep a house. I sang all three at Lord Mengchang's gate. He gave me the fish and the carriage and my mother's keep, and never asked what I was good for.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "What are you good for?"],
		["Feng Xuan", "Nothing; I told him so. I can keep accounts. He sent me to Xue to collect his debts, and to buy with them whatever his house was short of. I burned the tallies in the market-place and went back and told him I had bought him righteousness. He was not pleased. A year later he lost his post, and all Xue came a hundred li down the road to meet him.", "think"],
		["Yue Ling", "And you have come to do it again?"],
		["Feng Xuan", "I have come to find out whether it can be done from the beginning instead of the end. You have a hundred fires on a road and a piece of ground. I have looked at the ground. The hall goes here, where I am sitting. A cunning hare has three burrows, I told my lord, and you have only one. You have none. Shall we dig?", "point"],
		["Narrator", "By evening there are four posts in the ground and an argument about the roof. Somebody chalks a name on a board and props it against the nearest post: Guiyun, Where the Cloud Comes Home."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_cutters": [
		["Narrator", "Three woodcutters come up the east shoulder with their saws on their backs and their brothers behind them, look at the four posts, and say nothing for some time. Then the eldest moves one of the posts a hand's breadth to the left, and after that it is a hall."],
		["Feng Xuan", "Write down what each of them brings and what each is lent. Not to hold it over them. So that in a year, when somebody says he built this town with his own hands, there is a piece of bamboo to say that he did."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_vermin": [
		["Narrator", "The last thatch-moth goes up off the roof like a spark and does not come down. What is left of the seed-grain is under a mat in the hall, with Sawyer Bo sitting on it holding a mallet, where he has been since the night before last."],
		["Yue Ling", "They did not come because of us. They came because there is grain here now. Everything will. I had not thought of a town as a thing that smells of food from a long way off."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_build": [
		["Narrator", "A well, a row of doors, a granary on stone feet. From the sect's upper terrace it looks like something a child has laid out with pebbles. From inside it, at dusk, with smoke going up from six chimneys at once, it looks like the only lit place on the mountain."],
		["Feng Xuan", "Four roofs. Good. A hamlet is where people sleep. A town is where somebody else comes to buy salt. Find me the ones who carry salt."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_pedlars": [
		["Narrator", "The pedlars come in one behind the other, as they walk the roads, and each lays down his mat exactly where he would have laid it in any market in the land: a little too near the next man's. By noon there is a quarrel about a steelyard. Feng Xuan listens to it with his eyes shut, smiling."],
		["Feng Xuan", "Do you hear that? Somebody thinks he has been cheated. Nobody thinks that in a place he means to leave tomorrow. Now: the tallies. I shall want them for the first market day. They are in the chest."],
		["Narrator", "They are not in the chest. There is a hole in the bottom of the chest, and in the floor under it, and the edges of both are chewed."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_sticks": [
		["Narrator", "Five tally-sticks, dropped along the heights like crumbs. Every one has been carried in a mouth. Seed, timber, a winter's millet: the notches are all there. Whatever took them did not take them to destroy them."],
		["Yue Ling", "It was keeping them. Look at how they were laid: in order, by household. Lin Feng, something under this mountain is keeping our accounts."],
		["Feng Xuan", "And eating the grain while it waits to be paid. I know the song. Everybody knows the song. I had hoped not to meet whoever it was written to."],
	],
	"pre:shuo_shu": [
		["Narrator", "It is the size of an ox, and it sits on a heap of millet as on a throne. Round it, in neat bundles tied with straw, is every tally in Guiyun. \"Big rat, big rat, do not eat our millet. Three years we have served you, and you have never cared for us.\" It has heard the song for three thousand years. It has always taken it for a petition."],
		["Shuo Shu", "They owe. I have it in writing: their own. I do not steal. I hold what is owed, and I eat while I wait. Every granary has had me under it. You have only built yours very quickly, and I have had to hurry."],
	],
	"post:shuo_shu": [
		["Narrator", "It does not die. It gets down off the millet, which nobody has seen it do, and goes away into the dark on four feet like any rat, only larger. The song ends: we will leave you, and go to that happy land. They did leave. This is the happy land. It followed them here in the first cart."],
		["Yue Ling", "The tallies are dry. It kept them better than we did."],
		["Lin Feng", "It kept them as somebody keeps a thing he means to use. Bring them up. The town should see them in the light before anybody says what they are for."],
	],
	"s_p25_tally": [
		["Feng Xuan", "Here they are. Seed, timber, the first winter: every household in Guiyun owes the chest, and the chest is yours. I know what I did at Xue. I shall not tell you it was right. It was right for a lord who had too much of everything but friends.", "offer"],
		["Feng Xuan", "You have a town with nothing. A chest that is empty digs no well. And a chest that is full, in a town where every door owes it, is a rat with a roof on. I have kept accounts for forty years and I have never been sure which of those is the worse.", "think"],
		["Yue Ling", "They are all in the square. Nobody is even pretending to be drawing water."],
		["Feng Xuan", "No. They know what is in the basket. Say what is to be done with it, and say it where they can hear. I shall do whichever you say, and keep the books of it for ever."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_town": [
		["Narrator", "The awning of the Morning Market goes up on a day of wind, and takes six men to hold. Under it a steelyard is hung from the middle beam, and the first thing weighed on it, by common consent, is the steward. He comes to a hundred and nine catties. It is entered."],
		["Feng Xuan", "Fifty households and a place to be cheated in. That is a town. In two years, the book says; we have been quicker than Shun, and I do not trust it. A lord is known by his guests. Yours are all people who came because they had nowhere else. Go and ask for some who have."],
	],
	"visit:s_p25_guests:tofu_sun": [
		["Tofu Seller Sun", "A household for the mountain? My wife's cousin Dou has pressed tofu in my yard for nine years and looked at my grindstone the whole of that time. Take him. Take him with my blessing, and a stone of his own, and tell him the water up there is sweeter. It is true, too, which is the worst of it."]],
	"visit:s_p25_guests:batu": [
		["Batu the Horse-Herd", "One of us, in a house? ...Uncle Temur's knees are finished for the saddle, and he would sooner die than say so. Tell him your town has no horse-doctor. Do not tell him it has walls. He will find the walls out for himself, and by then he will have a patient."]],
	"visit:s_p25_guests:well_keeper_zhaya": [
		["Well-Keeper Zhaya", "Is the well sweet? Then I shall send my second daughter and hers. She can taste a well and tell you what the mountain over it had for supper. A town wants one of those before it wants a wall. You will not thank me until the first dry summer."]],
	"visit:s_p25_guests:boatwright_gao": [
		["Boatwright Gao", "No river? Then what would you do with a boatwright's nephew? ...Carts. He can build you carts. A cart is only a boat that has given up. Yes, he will come; he has been saying for years that Longmen has more boats than fish."]],
	"visit:s_p25_guests:bell_tuner_gong": [
		["Bell-Tuner Gong", "A town with no bell does not know what time it is, and so it is never the same time for two people in it, and so they quarrel. I shall send my apprentice with a small bronze. She is better than I am. I have not told her. You may."]],
	"visit:s_p25_guests:tea_seller_pan": [
		["Old Pan the Tea Seller", "My sister's boy makes lanterns in a town where everybody makes lanterns. Up a mountain with no lantern-maker he would be a great man. Guiyun, you say? He will think it is a tea. Let him. He will be half-way there before he asks."]],
	"done:s_p25_guests": [
		["Feng Xuan", "Four households, sent by people who had a choice in the matter. That is worth forty who had none. And a fifth I did not ask for and have not got: a family of tea-pickers set out for us from the Cloud-Tea Terraces a month ago with their beds on their backs. They did not arrive.", "think"],
		["Feng Xuan", "They did not get as far as their own gate. I am told there is a woman who sits at the foot of the height there, and that she knows why, and that she will tell anybody who can bear to stand and hear it. I keep accounts. Go, and be the other kind of person."],
	],
	"s_p25_widow": [
		["Narrator", "At the foot of the height a woman in undyed hemp is weeping beside three graves. She does it as somebody does who has had the practice: steadily, without hurry, getting on with it."],
		["The Widow of Mount Tai", "My husband's father was killed here by the tiger. Then my husband. Now my son. A Master came by once with his disciples and asked me why I did not leave this place. I said: there is no harsh government here. He told them to remember it.", "grieve"],
		["Lin Feng", "And now?"],
		["The Widow of Mount Tai", "Now the tiger has learned to write. It wears a seal. It taxes the hearth and the head and the road, and the bill is nailed to the door, and when my neighbours packed for your town they found a second bill beside the first: for leaving. It need not come down any more. We pay it to be let stay and be eaten.", "point"],
		["Yue Ling", "Then why are you still here?"],
		["The Widow of Mount Tai", "Because the Master was right, and somebody should be here when it is proved. Start with its seals. They fly ahead of it. You will know them by the wax."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_assess": [
		["Narrator", "The last seal-wasp breaks like a stick of wax, and the oxen of the corvee stand in the tea rows with their heads down, as oxen do when the cart has gone. On every door of the terraces a square of yellow paper lifts and settles in the wind."],
		["Yue Ling", "They were only what it sent. A thing that sends its paper ahead of it does not expect to be met."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_bills": [
		["Narrator", "Four bills, from four doors. Hearth, head, road, the levy for the men who collect the levy. At the foot of each is the same red seal, and it is not a stamp cut to look like a tiger's face. It is a paw, pressed in cinnabar. The claws have gone through the paper."],
		["The Widow of Mount Tai", "It learned the sums from my husband's father, who kept the village book. It ate him last of all that year. I have always thought it wanted him to finish the page."],
	],
	"pre:ke_zheng": [
		["Narrator", "It is a tiger: old, heavy in the shoulder, with one eye gone white. Round its neck on a red cord hangs an assessor's seal the size of a rice-bowl. It is lying on a ledger. It does not get up."],
		["The Tiger of Mount Tai", "I ate them one at a time, and they stayed. Then I learned to send a bill, and now they pay me for leave to stay. I have not come down the hill in a year. Tell me, sweeper, you who are building a town with a chest in it: which of us did the Master mean was the fiercer? I or the paper?"],
	],
	"post:ke_zheng": [
		["Narrator", "The cord breaks. The seal goes down the height end over end and cracks across on a tea-terrace wall. What is left gets up, shakes itself, and goes off up the rock: an ordinary tiger, which is terror enough, and honest."],
		["The Widow of Mount Tai", "A tiger I can live beside. I have done it thirty years. You will have a chest in your town, sweeper, and somebody to keep it. Come and look at this hill sometimes. Not to be frightened. Only to remember what a seal weighs.", "nod"],
	],
	"s_p25_door": [
		["Feng Xuan", "They are on the road with their beds on their backs. Tea-pickers, and behind them people who heard it from tea-pickers. Some are of the six hearths. Some are of nowhere: there is a family of the Feathered Folk with a hand-cart, and two shades of Youdu who walk at night and sit under a tarpaulin all day.", "point"],
		["Feng Xuan", "The gate is not built. I am being asked, forty times a day, whom it will be for. I have said: the lord will say. I have enjoyed saying it. It cannot go on."],
		["Yue Ling", "At the sect they ask one thing of whoever comes: have you eaten?"],
		["Feng Xuan", "A good question for a kitchen. A town must ask a second: and who answers for you? Or it must make up its mind never to ask it. Either is a town. They are not the same town. Say which, and I shall have it cut over the gate before there is a gate.", "think"],
	],
	"done:s_p25_herbs": [
		["Narrator", "The herbalists come last and separately, as they do everything, and each walks the north slope above the town for a whole day before agreeing to anything. On the second day there are three drying-racks behind the Second Row, and a queue."],
		["Feng Xuan", "Sixty households, and until this morning the nearest person who could set a bone was a day's walk down. I did not put that in the accounts. There is no column for what did not happen."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_rogues": [
		["Narrator", "The last of them goes over the unfinished wall with a hen under each arm, puts the hens down when shouted at, and comes back to ask whether there is any work. He can crow like a cock. He does it. Three real cocks answer him, and a gate two valleys off is heard to open."],
		["Feng Xuan", "Lord Mengchang kept a man at his table who could only steal like a dog, and one who could only crow like a cock, and was laughed at for it. Then came a night at the Hangu Pass, with a king's riders behind him, when a fur had to be stolen and a gate crowed open before dawn. Do not be hard on them. Ask what else they can do.", "laugh"],
	],
	"done:s_p25_city": [
		["Narrator", "The wall goes up a hand's breadth a day, each household its own length of it, and where two lengths meet there is a seam that will show for a hundred years. On the day the Cloud Gate is hung, Feng Xuan counts the doors of Guiyun twice and then sits down on the steps of the hall and says nothing for an hour."],
		["Feng Xuan", "A hundred. In three years, a capital, says the book. I have checked. We are not a capital. We are a hundred households behind a wall that has a man on it who crows. But I have kept the accounts of a lord with three thousand guests, and I was never once as rich as this.", "bow"],
	],
	"done:s_p25_school": [
		["Narrator", "The Hall of the Broom is one room with a swept floor, mats in rows, and a rack of nine brooms worn to different lengths. On the first morning four children are waiting at the door before the steward has unbarred it, each with a broom from home."],
		["Feng Xuan", "They asked me who is to teach them. I said: whoever sweeps best. They have gone out to find out who that is. I have never seen the street so clean, nor heard it argued about so loudly.", "laugh"],
	],
	"done:s_p25_watch": [
		["Narrator", "The host stands to below the new wall of Guiyun at dawn, and the town comes out to stand with it: a hundred who were woodcutters and pedlars a year ago. Lady Hao comes up from Qingshi to look at them, walks down the front rank once without speaking, and at the end of it turns to Lin Feng."],
		["Lady Hao", "They chose who stands either side of them. I have drilled levies for thirty years and I cannot teach that; I can only find it or not find it. Keep them on the wall. If the host is ever called out again, send them to me last. I shall want something to hold with."],
	],
	"s_p25_envoy": [
		["Feng Xuan", "A man in pine-green left this on my table and would not sit. I shall read it as written. \"From Han Song, Patriarch of the Cold Pine Sect of Frostpeak. A hall in which cultivation is taught, standing within a hundred li of an older gate, is a branch of that gate or it is a weed. Bring me the board from over its door, or I shall come down for it.\"", "think"],
		["Lin Feng", "It is one room with nine brooms in it."],
		["Feng Xuan", "It is a room children go into in order to become somebody. That is a sect, whatever is in the rack. He is right about what it is. He is only wrong about whose. He has three hundred disciples and has not taken a pupil of his own in sixty years: nobody was good enough.", "point"],
		["Yue Ling", "Then who has been teaching the three hundred?"],
		["Feng Xuan", "Each other, I should think, and the cold. They are out on the snow below Frostpeak now, in ranks. They have been told to be impressed by nothing. I should go and see what that looks like from in front."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_pines": [
		["Narrator", "They go down in their ranks and get up in their ranks and bow, all three hundred, to exactly the same depth. Not one of them has asked who Lin Feng is. They have been taught the forms of everything, and it has not occurred to anybody to teach them a question."],
		["Yue Ling", "Their hands are split with frost. All of them. He has had them out here every dawn for sixty years, getting ready for somebody good enough to arrive."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_seats": [
		["Narrator", "Four tablets of black lacquer: North, South, Root, Crown. Each lay in the snow before a hall whose door had frozen shut. The Cold Pine had four masters under its patriarch once. He let them die one after another, and looked at their pupils, and found nobody fit to follow them."],
		["Lin Feng", "He is not keeping the seats. He is keeping them empty. It is the only thing left on this mountain that he is sure he does well."],
	],
	"pre:han_song": [
		["Han Song", "\"Only when the year turns cold do we see that the pine and the cypress are the last to wither.\" I have been the last for sixty winters. Everything else on this mountain was promising, and froze. And you come up to me with a town at your back. A town! Do you know how long a town lasts?", "point"],
		["Lin Feng", "Longer than a master with no pupils."],
		["Han Song", "I have three hundred."],
		["Lin Feng", "You have three hundred people standing in the snow. I counted the seat-tokens, Patriarch. Nobody has held one in my lifetime. I have a room with brooms in it and a child of four who is rude about the well. One of us has a sect."],
	],
	"post:han_song": [
		["Narrator", "He sits down in the snow, which he has not done in sixty winters, and finds that it is only cold."],
		["Han Song", "Sixty years I looked for somebody fit for the seat. All that time I thought the seat was the thing to be fit for. ...They will be at your gate by tomorrow. Three hundred. They follow whoever has beaten the master; it is the one lesson I ever finished teaching. Do as you like with them. Do it better. That should not be hard.", "grieve"],
	],
	"s_p25_seat": [
		["Feng Xuan", "Three hundred in pine-green outside the Cloud Gate, in ranks, in the rain. They have no master, and they were taught that whoever beats the master is the master. The Elder has sent down to say it is no affair of his, by which I understand that he is at his window.", "point"],
		["Feng Xuan", "And the Hall of the Broom has a bare lintel, and the children have begun to ask what it is the hall of. This is the third burrow, and it is the one my lord slept soundest in: a seat nobody can take away, because of how it was come by."],
		["Yue Ling", "There are three boards the carpenter could cut. I have seen him looking at the wood."],
		["Feng Xuan", "A new name, that nobody has heard. An old name, that three hundred will kneel to by nightfall. Or the name that is over your own Elder's gate, which you have never yet stepped out from under. I keep accounts. I do not know what a thing is worth until somebody has chosen it. Choose.", "bow"],
	],
	"done:s_p25_hermits": [
		["Narrator", "Three hermits come down off three different heights, look at one another with the dislike of people who each believed they were the only one, and are shown the Hall of the Broom. The eldest picks up the shortest broom, weighs it in his hand, and nods."],
		["Feng Xuan", "They will not agree about anything they teach. Good. I have seen what a hall looks like where everybody agrees. It was on a mountain, in the snow, in ranks."],
	],
	"done:s_p25_marks": [
		["Feng Xuan", "Three burrows. The goodwill of a town that knows what you did about its debts. A gate, and a word over it about who may come in. And a seat. My lord slept without a care after the third. I notice you do not sleep well. Good. A lord who sleeps well is being robbed by somebody; often it is by himself.", "nod"],
		["Yue Ling", "You have never once called Lin Feng a lord before today."],
		["Feng Xuan", "Lin Feng was not one before today. There were things built. Today they were gone round and looked at again. That is the whole of the difference, and I have waited forty years to keep the books of somebody who knew it."],
	],
	"s_p25_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I can see it from this window. A hundred smokes. I counted them at dawn; I count them every dawn, and I have told nobody, so do not you. What do they say down there, when they are asked whose town it is?", "think"],
		["Lin Feng", "They say: ours. They say they built it themselves. They did, Elder. I only stood about and was asked things."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then you did it properly. Of the best of those who are over others, the people know only that he is there. I have been over this sect for seventy years and they know a great deal more of me than that; I have always talked too much.", "laugh"],
		["Yue Ling", "The steward says he has three burrows now, and still does not sleep."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Nor should he. Go and sit under the pine, child. There is a realm for people who have been answered for all their lives and have turned round and begun to answer for others. I do not know what it is like. I only know that from outside it looks like nothing at all.", "bow"],
	],
	"pre:s_break30": [
		["Narrator", "Under the pine Lin Feng sits, and does not gather the qi, and does not let it go. He leaves it where it is, as a steward leaves a chest: counted, and not his. On the east shoulder a hundred hearths are being banked for the night by people who are not thinking of him at all. The sky gathers, as it always has, and looks for somebody to strike."],
	],
	"post:s_break30": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation comes down on the mountain and cannot find the middle of Lin Feng. There is a town where the middle used to be, and a gate, and a room with brooms in it. It strikes the pine, which has been struck before and does not mind. In the morning a cock crows at Guiyun, and then a man, and the gate opens."],
	],
	# ---- Part XXVI: The Five Frolics (the Underclothes release)
	# ---- Part XXVII: The Cloth Sack (the Heavy Road release)
	"recall25": [
		["Narrator", "The summer after the physician went north, Azure Cloud Mountain is busier than it has ever been, and lighter. Everybody on it who has reached the Golden Core flies. Nobody has walked the nine hundred and ninety-nine steps with anything in his hands since the spring."],
		["Narrator", "In the autumn Smith Tie's forge goes out. There is no charcoal. There is charcoal a hundred li south, as there has always been; there is nobody to carry it. A flying sword lifts its rider and his robe. It has never lifted a sack."],
	],
	"s_p27_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The forge has been cold a week. I asked forty disciples to bring charcoal. Forty disciples told me, very politely, that a sword will not lift it. They are right. I asked them to walk. They looked at me as if I had asked them to grow gills.", "grieve"],
		["Lin Feng", "I carried water up those steps for three years. Two buckets. Nobody thought it worth remarking."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You were a sweeper. It was not remarked because it was beneath remark, and that was my fault as much as anybody's. We have made a mountain of people who can put the sky in a sleeve and cannot say what a bucket weighs.", "think"],
		["Yue Ling", "There is a governor in the Silk Woods who carries bricks. A hundred out of his study every morning and a hundred back at night. The woodcutters think he is mad. They also say his granary is the only full one in the south."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Tao Kan. Go and carry something for him, child, and come back and tell me what it weighed. And while you are about it: Smith Tie has a ring on his bench he will not finish. He says there is nothing to put in it yet. Find out what he means."],
	],
	"s_p27_tao": [
		["Narrator", "Below the far heights of the Mulberry Silk Woods there is a yard, a study with its door open, and a path between them worn a hand deep. A broad old man in a green robe with the sleeves tied back is walking it with two bricks under each arm. He does not stop."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "Ninety-six. Do not speak to me until a hundred; I lose count, and then I must begin again, and I am sixty-one. ...A hundred. Now. You are the one who put the sky back. You flew here, I suppose.", "nod"],
		["Lin Feng", "I flew here."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "So does everybody. I mean to give my strength to the Central Plain before I die, and a man who has sat easy for a year is not fit to lift a writing-brush, never mind a province. So: bricks. Take this axe. I am raising a granary. Show me you can fell a tree without your sword doing it for you.", "offer"],
		["Yue Ling", "He has given you an axe and an insult, in that order. I like him."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_fell": [
		["Narrator", "Three trees down along the forest's edge, the third a good deal cleaner than the first. The stems are trimmed and on Lin Feng's shoulder before he has thought about it, and then he does think about it: ninety jin, and the yard is a long way off."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "You stood too close to the first, and you know it. Good. Eight stems I need for the frame. Bring them as you can carry them. If you bring them all at once I shall know you are showing off, and if you bring them one at a time I shall know you are lazy."],
	],
	"haul:s_p27_timber": [
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "Lay them there, butts to the east. Do not drop them. A dropped stem is a split stem."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "Your shoulder is red. Change shoulders. Nobody ever tells the young to change shoulders."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "Slower than flying, is it not. You saw the woods this time, I expect. Most people have not seen them since they learned to go over them."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_timber": [
		["Narrator", "Eight stems lie in the yard with their butts to the east. Lin Feng's robe is dark between the shoulders, and Yue Ling has pine resin in her hair and has not yet been told. Tao Kan walks down the row and puts his palm flat on each, as a man counts his children."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "That is a frame. It is also the first thing that has come into this yard on a cultivator's back in my lifetime. A frame stands on something. Here is a pick: six blocks, from the crag, each on its bed. Stone is heavier than wood, and you will find it is more honest."],
	],
	"haul:s_p27_footing": [
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "At the corner, and the next a pace along. Do not slide it. Lift it and set it. A footing that was slid remembers."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "You are breathing through your mouth. Good. A man who has never been out of breath has never been of use to anybody."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_footing": [
		["Narrator", "Six blocks stand in two rows of three, and the eight stems go up on them by evening with the help of the yamen's clerks, who have never lifted anything heavier than a seal and say so repeatedly. Tao Kan sights along the ridge-pole with one eye shut."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "It will hold grain for four hundred households, and I did not carry one piece of it. That is new, and I do not altogether like it. Now clear my woods. Something has moved in since everybody stopped walking through them, and it is not woodcutters."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_idle": [
		["Narrator", "The last sedan-chair shade sets itself down with a sigh, since there is nobody left to carry it, and comes apart like a paper lantern in rain. The moths go up off the cushions of the whole south in one grey cloud, and the wind has them."],
		["Yue Ling", "They were not wicked. They were only what gathers on anything that is not used. I have seen the same on a loom."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_bricks": [
		["Narrator", "Five grey bricks, each with one corner worn round by the same hand. They are twelve jin apiece and feel like more; forty years of mornings have gone into them. Lin Feng carries them down the height in his arms, like something that might wake."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_ends": [
		["Narrator", "Four woodcutters of the road agree to keep what they have always thrown on the fire: the sawdust in sacks, the butt-ends of the bamboo in a heap by the door. None of them can say what a governor wants with it. All of them have noticed that his granary is full."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_nails": [
		["Narrator", "Four pedlars of the road shoulder a sack each of bamboo butts cut to a point, and go off down the river road bent a little further than usual. A pedlar carries a whole street on one shoulder as it is. None of them asks to be paid for the other."],
	],
	"s_p27_snow": [
		["Narrator", "The thaw has come in a night. The yard below the heights is mud to the ankle, and across it, from the gate to the study door, runs a dry yellow path a pace wide. Tao Kan is standing on it. It is sawdust."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "Every clerk in the yamen laughed when I had it stored. Now they walk on it to their desks with dry feet and say nothing. And the butt-ends of the bamboo: there is a fleet building on the river, and they are short of nails. A bamboo butt, cut so, is a nail.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "You did not know that when you kept them."],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "I knew that a thing has weight, and that whatever has weight has a use, and that whoever has never carried it will not find the use. That is all I have ever known. It has been enough for a province.", "nod"],
		["Tao Kan, Who Carries Bricks", "You want charcoal for your mountain. There is one man burning it for a hundred li, on the south slope of the volcano, and he is old, and nobody helps him because nobody walks there. Go and be of use. Take your axe.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p27_weng": [
		["Narrator", "On the south slope of the Ashen Volcano there is a hump of clay with smoke coming out of the wrong end of it, and an old man on his knees looking into the crack. His face is the colour of ash. His ten fingers are black. He is wearing one thin jacket, and it is snowing."],
		["The Charcoal Seller", "Cracked from the mouth to the flue. Forty years she has burned for me. ...You are cultivators. You will have come about the smoke. I am sorry for the smoke.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "We have come about the charcoal. And you are shaking. Lin Feng, give him your cloak. No: he will not take it. Give it to me, and I will make him."],
		["The Charcoal Seller", "Charcoal? There is none. A kiln is stone before it is anything, and I cannot lift stone any more. You have a pick on your back, I see. I found the bed of the stone up on the crag in my father's time. If a person could cut four blocks and bring them down...", "point"],
		["Lin Feng", "A person could."],
	],
	"haul:s_p27_kiln": [
		["The Charcoal Seller", "Here, against the crack. Set it on its bed, the way it lay in the hill. Stone remembers which way up it was."],
		["The Charcoal Seller", "Forty-five jin, and you carried it down a crag. I carried those when I was your age. I did not think then that it was the best I would ever be."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_kiln": [
		["Narrator", "Four blocks are set in the crack and packed with wet clay, and the old man lays his black hand on the kiln's side and listens, as to a chest. Yue Ling has got him into the cloak after all, by the simple means of putting it on him while he was listening."],
		["The Charcoal Seller", "She will hold. Now she wants feeding. Eight stems for a burn; green oak if there is any, anything if there is not. I would cut them. I would have cut them yesterday."],
	],
	"haul:s_p27_wood": [
		["The Charcoal Seller", "On end, in a ring, the thick ones in the middle. Leave a hand's width. Fire wants to breathe a little and not much, like a sleeper."],
		["The Charcoal Seller", "Good wood. You cut it low. People cut high because it is easier on the back, and leave the best of the tree in the ground."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_wood": [
		["Narrator", "The kiln is stacked and sealed and lit from the top. For a night and a day it burns without flame, and the old man does not sleep; he walks round it with a wet finger held up, reading the smoke. On the second morning he breaks the door, and what comes out rings like a bell when it is knocked."],
		["Narrator", "A thousand jin and more, loaded on the cart by dark. He is harnessing the ox when two riders come up the slope out of the south, one in yellow, one in a white shirt, with a paper held up between them like a lantern."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_buyers": [
		["Narrator", "The last boy in a white shirt runs for his horse and cannot find it; the oxen with gauze on their horns stand about on the slope looking embarrassed. The cart is gone from the kiln. The ruts go up, not down: north, towards the height."],
		["The Charcoal Seller", "By order, they said. They read it out. I cannot read. A cart of charcoal, a thousand jin and more, and they turned the ox and shouted it north, and tied this to its horn. Half a bolt of red gauze. That is the price. It says so on the paper.", "grieve"],
	],
	"done:s_p27_gauze": [
		["Narrator", "Four half-bolts of thin red silk, off four oxen's horns, each with ten feet of damask folded in it. Together they would not buy the wood that went into the kiln. Lin Feng rolls them tight and carries them up the height in one hand. They weigh nothing at all, which is the whole of the matter."],
	],
	"pre:palace_buyer": [
		["Narrator", "On the height the cart stands with its shafts pointing north. Beside it a smooth man in imperial yellow is reading a paper aloud to nobody, in a pleasant voice, and tying something red to the ox's horn. The ox is looking south."],
		["The Buyer in Yellow, by Order", "By order. A cart of charcoal, to the palace market, at the price set. The price is here. It is a generous price; I set it myself. Who are you, and what is that in your hand?", "point"],
		["Lin Feng", "The price. Four times over. I have come to give it back."],
		["The Buyer in Yellow, by Order", "One does not give it back. That is the beauty of the thing. Everything is paid for, and nothing is bought. I have never carried anything in my life, young man, and I own a great deal.", "laugh"],
	],
	"post:palace_buyer": [
		["Narrator", "The Buyer in Yellow sits down on the ground, which he has never done, with his paper in his lap. He tries to lift one sack off the cart to see what he has been buying for thirty years. He cannot move it. He looks at the old man, who is half his size, for a long time."],
		["The Buyer in Yellow, by Order", "A thousand jin. He cut it and burned it and loaded it. And I... tied a ribbon to it. I think I should like to walk back. I do not know the way. I have never needed to know it.", "grieve"],
	],
	"s_p27_cart": [
		["Narrator", "The ox has turned the cart south by itself and is waiting. The old man walks round the load twice, counting sacks with a black finger, and then takes eight down, one at a time, and sets them at Lin Feng's feet."],
		["The Charcoal Seller", "For your smith. Do not tell me a price. You mended her, and you fed her, and you went up the hill for the cart. I have been paid by the palace and I have been paid by you, and I know which of them I could lift.", "offer"],
		["Lin Feng", "Two hundred jin. No sword will lift that. It is a long way to Azure Cloud Mountain on foot."],
		["The Charcoal Seller", "It is. I do it every market day, and I am seventy. The ox and I will come up behind you with the rest, slowly. You will smell of smoke for a week. Somebody told me once it was a good smell.", "laugh"],
	],
	"haul:s_p27_smith": [
		["Smith Tie", "Charcoal. South-slope charcoal, and it rings. Put it by the bellows. ...You carried this. On your back. Up the steps."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_smith": [
		["Narrator", "Smith Tie has the forge lit before the eighth sack is down. He does not say thank you; he puts a hand on Lin Feng's shoulder where the strap has been, presses once, and nods, as one tradesman to another. Then he takes a ring of grey iron off the bench and turns it in the light."],
		["Smith Tie", "A ring to hold what a person owns. The disciples all want one. I have made the band a dozen times and thrown it back. There is a seat here for a stone, and nothing I put in it will stay. Ask the monk under the stone towers. He sent me the iron.", "think"],
	],
	"visit:s_p27_fires:granny_wen": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Charcoal? For me? I have burned rice-straw for seventy years and coughed for sixty. ...It rings. Listen to that. Sit down, child, you are black to the elbows. No: wash first, then sit. Then eat."]],
	"visit:s_p27_fires:sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "We burn argal, valley sweeper, and it is good enough for the Sky. But the smith's fire wants this, for the bits and the stirrups, and he has been grumbling since the first snow. Put it by the door-flap. Not there. There."]],
	"visit:s_p27_fires:cook_aysha": [
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Wood-charcoal, in the sand! Do you know what a camel-load of this costs at Shazhou? And you walked it here on your back like a hired man. Sit, hired man. The bread is hot, and for once so is the oven."]],
	"visit:s_p27_fires:fishwife_lu": [
		["Fishwife Lu", "For the smoke-house? Driftwood makes the fish taste of the river, and I will not hear a word against it. ...But for the lamp under the pot on a wet night, yes. Yes. Leave it. And take an eel; nobody leaves my door lighter than they came."]],
	"visit:s_p27_fires:auntie_rong": [
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "South-slope charcoal. I can tell by the ring. We burn a hill of this a year and I have not seen a sack so well made since my mother's time. Who burned it? One old man? Then one old man knows more than my forty."]],
	"visit:s_p27_fires:lantern_xi": [
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "For the glue-pot! You cannot make a lantern over a wood fire, it spits, and a spark in the paper is a week's work gone. I have been making do with dung. Do not tell the customers. Bless you, and bless whoever's back that was."]],
	"done:s_p27_fires": [
		["Narrator", "Five hearths have a sack apiece, and at every one of them somebody has looked at the black on Lin Feng's hands before they looked at his face. On the way back up the mountain he passes the old man's ox coming down, the cart empty, the old man asleep on the box."],
	],
	"s_p27_chan": [
		["Narrator", "Under the stone towers of the Nine Bends there is one hall, one bell and one pot, and an old monk with a shaven head sweeping a yard that is already clean. He looks at Lin Feng's shoulders before he looks at his face."],
		["Chan Master Zhichang", "Somebody has been carrying. Timber, by the lean of you, and then something black. Good. The last cultivator who came up here had not carried so much as a cup in forty years, and wanted to know about emptiness.", "nod"],
		["Lin Feng", "Smith Tie has a ring with a place for a stone, and nothing will stay in it. He says you sent the iron."],
		["Chan Master Zhichang", "I did. And you would like to know what goes in the place. So would he. Before the question, the pot. There are nine of us tonight and a prefect who eats for three. Here is a sickle. The open ground below the towers is full of good bitter things.", "offer"],
	],
	"done:s_p27_reap": [
		["Narrator", "Five bundles of plantain, mugwort and something Yue Ling will not let him put in with the rest, each tied with a stem of its own grass. They weigh five jin and it has taken an hour on his haunches. The pot at Guizong is very large and the monk does not look at it once."],
		["Chan Master Zhichang", "The prefect is Li Bo. He read ten thousand scrolls and they call him Li of the Ten Thousand. He came to ask how Mount Sumeru can go into a mustard seed, and I asked where in his head he had put the scrolls. They have been getting out ever since. Listen."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_scrolls": [
		["Narrator", "The last scroll rolls itself shut with a snap and lies still among the towers. All over the Stone Forest ivory tags are dropping out of the air like hail, each with a title on it in a small neat hand, and not one of them attached to anything."],
		["Yue Ling", "They were all true, Lin Feng. Every one. He read them and they were true and he put them on a shelf in his head. I do not think anything is more dangerous than a true thing that nobody has ever had to carry anywhere."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_cases": [
		["Narrator", "Five lacquered cases, open, rattling a little when they are moved, as if most of what was in them had been left somewhere else. From the mouth of the Cave of Ten Thousand Echoes below comes a sound like a very large library being read aloud by one voice, all at once."],
	],
	"pre:wan_juan": [
		["Narrator", "In the middle gallery of the cave the scrolls have made themselves a body: ten thousand of them, turning slowly in the dark, every one unrolled and every one speaking. The echo gives each sentence back. It has been listening to itself for a year."],
		["Ten Thousand Scrolls, Unshelved", "Mount Sumeru is eighty-four thousand yojanas high. A mustard seed is the smallest of seeds. The one goes into the other. It is written. It is written. Where is the difficulty? We have read it. We have read everything."],
		["Lin Feng", "What does Mount Sumeru weigh?"],
		["Ten Thousand Scrolls, Unshelved", "...That is not written.", "shock"],
	],
	"post:wan_juan": [
		["Narrator", "The scrolls come down out of the air one by one and roll themselves shut. They lie on the cave floor in a great drift, and for the first time in a year the Cave of Ten Thousand Echoes has nothing to repeat. At its mouth a stout man in a prefect's robe is waiting with an empty case on his knees."],
		["Narrator", "He gets up when he sees them, goes down past them into the dark without a word, and comes back with one scroll. One. He holds it as if it were heavy. It takes him the rest of the day to carry it up to the hall, and he will not let anybody help."],
	],
	"s_p27_seed": [
		["Chan Master Zhichang", "He has gone back for a second. At that rate he will have them all up in thirty years, and know every one of them by the feel. I have told him he may have the room over the bell.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "And Sumeru, and the seed?"],
		["Chan Master Zhichang", "You have carried eight stems of timber and ten blocks of stone and two hundred jin of charcoal up a mountain. Where are they now? Not on your back. And yet you have them, do you not: you could tell me to the jin what each of them was. That is where Sumeru goes.", "think"],
		["Chan Master Zhichang", "The ring is only iron. What sits in it is a seed, and the seed is with a fat man who sleeps on a sack in the Maple Gorge. He will give it to you, or he will not. Do not wake him. He is never asleep.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p27_budai": [
		["Narrator", "At the foot of the height in the Maple Gorge of Returning Souls a very fat monk is lying on a hemp sack as big as himself, with his robe open and his hands behind his head, in the middle of the road. Nine children are sitting on him. He appears to be asleep and is laughing."],
		["Budai, the Cloth-Sack Monk", "Hah! Shoulders! Look, children, a cultivator with shoulders, there has not been one of those since your grandmothers. Have you a fish? A coin? A sandal? No? Then you are the first person today I can give something to.", "laugh"],
		["Narrator", "He reaches into the sack up to the armpit, and then a little further than that, and brings out a fishing rod in three joints with the line already on it."],
		["Budai, the Cloth-Sack Monk", "I beg for whatever there is, and today there are nine children and no fish. The gorge is full of carp. Five would do. They like the deep side, under the red trees. Go on. I shall be here. I am always here; it is the road that moves.", "offer"],
	],
	"haul:s_p27_fish": [
		["Budai, the Cloth-Sack Monk", "Into the sack! No, not the sack, the pot. I forget. Children, which is the pot? Hah!"],
		["Budai, the Cloth-Sack Monk", "A fine fish. He is cross. So would I be. Thank him, somebody; it is the least we can do before we eat him."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_fish": [
		["Narrator", "Five carp go into a pot that comes out of the sack with the water already in it. Nine children eat, and then three more who were not there before, and a dog. There is enough. The monk has none himself, and looks fatter."],
		["Budai, the Cloth-Sack Monk", "You hear that? No, up. There is a king on the top of this hill who has been holding something for three thousand years, and bits keep falling off it onto my road. One nearly hit the dog. Go and see whether he would not like to put it down."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_anglers": [
		["Narrator", "Four old anglers of the road sit down along the bank under the red trees with a child at each elbow. By dusk there are nine lines in the water, most of them tangled, and one small girl has caught a carp nearly as long as herself and will not let anybody else carry it."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_held": [
		["Narrator", "The last fist opens, and there is nothing in it; there never was. The crabs set down their chests at the water's edge and go in sideways, lighter. Down the whole length of the gorge things that were being carried are found lying in the road, where they were let go of."],
		["Yue Ling", "Thirty years, that grudge. She told me whose it was while you were fighting the crab. Do you know, she could not remember what it was about. Only that it was heavy and it was hers."],
	],
	"done:s_p27_tiles": [
		["Narrator", "Four glazed tiles, blue-green, each small enough to lie on a fingertip and each as heavy as a roof-tile ought to be. They are warm on one side, where a palm has been under them, and cold on the other, where three thousand years of sky have."],
	],
	"pre:li_jing": [
		["Narrator", "On the height above the gorge a tall man in gilded armour stands with his left arm out and a seven-storeyed pagoda on his palm. He has stood so long that the maples have grown round his feet. His arm does not shake. Everything else about him does."],
		["Li Jing, Who Cannot Set It Down", "Do not come nearer. If I am jostled it may slip. My son tore the flesh from his bones and gave it back to me, and came again made of lotus, and the first thing he did was reach for a spear. The Buddha put this in my hand. While I hold it he is my son.", "point"],
		["Lin Feng", "For three thousand years."],
		["Li Jing, Who Cannot Set It Down", "And every day of them. I have forgotten what my left hand is for. I have won wars with the right. Have you come to take it from me? Many have. I had rather you fought me for it than asked me, sweeper. I do not know what I should say if I were asked.", "grieve"],
	],
	"post:li_jing": [
		["Narrator", "The Heavenly King is down on one knee, and still his left arm is out and level, and the pagoda has not moved a hair. Lin Feng lays four small tiles on the rock in front of him, one at a time, where he can see them."],
		["Lin Feng", "These fell off. It has been coming apart for a long while. Whatever it was holding shut, it is not holding it now, and nothing has happened. I think he stopped being angry about a thousand years ago and nobody told you."],
		["Narrator", "Li Jing looks at the tiles. Then, very slowly, as a man lowers a sleeping child, he sets the pagoda down on a flat rock, and takes his hand away, and stands looking at his own palm. Far off over the gorge something bright on two wheels of fire turns once in the air, and goes on about its business."],
	],
	"s_p27_sack": [
		["Narrator", "Budai has come up the height, which nobody saw him do, and is sitting on a root with the sack on the ground between his feet. It is the first time Lin Feng has seen it off his shoulder. Put down, it is only a sack: hemp, patched, a cord round the neck."],
		["Budai, the Cloth-Sack Monk", "There. That is all the teaching I have, and you have just watched a king do it better. Now look in.", "laugh"],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng looks. There is a broom in it, and two buckets, and a red-string seal. Eight stems of timber. A basket of the North Mountain. A physician's book in a blue bag, or its ashes. A town of a hundred households. The sky, patched. He knows every one of them to the jin."],
		["Budai, the Cloth-Sack Monk", "Everybody gives me something to carry, and I never ask what. You have been doing the same since you were six. It is a good sack. It is also only a sack. So, sweeper: there it is on the ground. What comes after?", "think"],
	],
	"done:s_p27_marks": [
		["Narrator", "Six things that stand in the land because he once said a word: he walks up to each and looks at it, and this time he knows what it weighed to say. None of them is on his back any more. All of them are still his."],
	],
	"s_p27_end": [
		["Narrator", "The forge on Azure Cloud Mountain is lit, and has been for a month. On the nine hundred and ninety-nine steps there are disciples walking, some of them with buckets, most of them badly, one of them with a stem of pine on her shoulder and an expression of outrage."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Forty of them asked to be taught to carry. I said I could not teach it; I have not carried anything since I was made Elder. I sent them to Auntie Zhou. She has them on the water-buckets. She says the Golden Cores are the worst, and cry.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "Smith Tie's ring is finished. There is a seed in it. Everything I own goes into it and weighs nothing. I was given it after I had stopped wanting it."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "That is the only time such a thing is ever safely given. Wear it, child, and now and then take something out and carry it up the steps yourself, so as not to forget. Now tell me what a bucket weighs. I find, at my age, that I should like to know.", "nod"],
		["Yue Ling", "Thirty jin, full. He told me in the bamboo forest, the first week. I thought it was a strange thing to be proud of. I do not think so now."],
	],
	"pre:s_break32": [
		["Narrator", "Under the pine Lin Feng sits with the grey ring on his finger and everything he has ever carried inside it, and does not reach for any of it. He knows what each thing weighed. That is enough to hold them by. The sky gathers, as it always has, over a man with his hands empty."],
	],
	"post:s_break32": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation comes down on the mountain like a load let fall, and he takes it as he took the timber: on the shoulder, knees bent, and then up. It is heavy. It is exactly as heavy as it is. When it has passed, a seed in a ring of iron holds one mountain more than it did, and is no larger."],
	],
	"recall24": [
		["Narrator", "The winter after the wall of Guiyun went up is the stillest anybody on the mountain can remember. No wind; snow that comes straight down and stays where it falls. People sit by their fires. By the spring a great many of them find that they cannot get up."],
		["Narrator", "In Qingshi the tofu-seller's wife wakes and cannot lift her arm to the press. At the sect, three disciples who sat down in the Hall of Stillness at the winter solstice are still sitting there at the equinox, perfectly calm, and cannot stand."],
	],
	"s_p26_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Three of them. I went in and told them to get up, and they smiled at me, which was worse. We teach sitting here, child. Three hundred years of sitting. I find I have never once asked whether it can be overdone.", "grieve"],
		["Lin Feng", "Qingshi is the same. The tofu-seller's wife cannot lift her arm to the press."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "There was a physician at the gate in the autumn. He asked to see the Hall of Stillness, stood in the door of it for a long time, and went away down the mountain shaking his head. I took it for rudeness. I should like to have it explained to me.", "think"],
		["Yue Ling", "Hua Tuo. The healers of the river towns say that name the way the swordsmen say yours. He opens people and closes them again, and they wake and do not remember the knife."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then go and find him. He is at the hot springs of Glowcap Vale, they tell me, putting the sick into the water. Take your coat off before he tells you to; I gather he enjoys telling people.", "nod"],
	],
	"s_p26_hua": [
		["Narrator", "In the hot springs of Glowcap Vale a dozen people are sitting in the water to the chin, and on the rock above them an old man in a hemp shirt, with a gourd at his belt, is telling them very cheerfully that they are all going to die."],
		["Hua Tuo", "Not today! Not of this! Of sitting. The body wants to be used, though never used up. Move it, and the food is digested and the blood goes round, and sickness has nowhere to settle: a door-hinge that turns is never eaten by worms.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "The disciples at the sect have been sitting since the solstice."],
		["Hua Tuo", "I know. I stood in the door. Three hundred years of a hinge nobody turns. I was not being rude to your Elder; I was counting the worms. Now: into the water, down to your underclothes. Everybody here is in theirs, and nobody is looking at yours.", "point"],
		["Yue Ling", "He means it, Lin Feng. Healers always mean it."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_soak": [
		["Narrator", "The water is hotter than it looks and smells of eggs and iron. After a while the tofu-seller's wife, who has come up from Qingshi in a cart, lifts her arm out of the spring by itself and looks at it as if it belonged to somebody else."],
		["Hua Tuo", "Heat opens. It does not mend. The frolics mend, and they are for tomorrow. Tonight something has come down the vale after my patients: things that live on stillness smell it on a person the way flies smell meat. Go and see them off."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_rust": [
		["Narrator", "The last still-water toad goes over on its back in the shallows and lies there, perfectly content, until the current takes it. The rust-shell beetles have left orange rings on every rock they sat on. Where the spores lay, the moss has stopped growing."],
		["Hua Tuo", "Good. Now I can work. I need datura, the white trumpet that opens at dusk; it grows in the cold on the heights. Five, and I shall want them before the duke wakes."],
		["Lin Feng", "Which duke?"],
		["Hua Tuo", "The one who is not ill. You will see."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_datura": [
		["Narrator", "Five white trumpets, folded shut in the daylight, packed in moss. Hua Tuo smells each one and puts it in a different pocket."],
		["Hua Tuo", "Boiled with hemp and drunk with the wine of the north, these make a sleep so deep the knife may go where it likes. I learned it from nobody. I saw a man drunk enough to fall into a fire and not wake, and asked what was in his jar."],
	],
	"s_p26_mafei": [
		["Hua Tuo", "Now, the duke. Long ago Bian Que looked once at Duke Huan of Cai and said: Your Grace, there is an illness in the skin. Treat it now, or it will go deeper. The duke said: I am not ill. Doctors like to treat the well, to take the credit.", "think"],
		["Hua Tuo", "Ten days: it is in the flesh. I am not ill. Ten days: it is in the bowels. I am not ill. Ten days more, Bian Que saw him across a courtyard, and turned, and ran.", "shake"],
		["Yue Ling", "Because it was in the marrow."],
		["Hua Tuo", "And what is in the marrow belongs to the Master of Fate. The duke died five days after. He is here; at my age one stops asking how. He has gone down into the cave under the vale, where nobody can look at him. Go down. I have the powder and a needle. I only need him to hold still.", "point"],
	],
	"pre:duke_huan": [
		["Narrator", "Deep in the cave under the vale, on a stool of carved jade, sits a man in the robes of a duke of Cai with his hands on his knees. He is grey to the lips. Under the skin of his neck something moves, slowly, the way a fish moves under ice."],
		["Duke Huan of Cai", "Another physician. Bian Que sends them still, I suppose. I am not ill. I have never been ill. Doctors like to treat the well: it is the only way they ever succeed. Look at me. Do I look ill?", "refuse"],
	],
	"post:duke_huan": [
		["Narrator", "He sits down on the floor of the cave, which a duke does not do, and the thing under his skin goes still. Hua Tuo comes down the steps with a lamp, a cup and a needle, and kneels beside him as he would beside anybody."],
		["Hua Tuo", "Drink. All of it. ...There. Your Grace, you are ill. It is in the skin. It was always in the skin; it is only that nobody ever caught you there before.", "nod"],
		["Duke Huan of Cai", "In the skin. Then it can be treated. ...Do not tell Bian Que.", "grieve"],
	],
	"s_p26_skin": [
		["Narrator", "Duke Huan of Cai lies on a mat by the spring with a silver needle in the side of his neck, complaining. He is a better colour. His court stands round him in a ring and does not know where to look."],
		["Hua Tuo", "Twenty days earlier than Bian Que, and only a few centuries late. That is the whole art: to be there while it is still in the skin. Anybody can be wise about the marrow.", "laugh"],
		["Hua Tuo", "Now the frolics. I cannot be in every village at dawn, but I have a pupil who can: Wu Pu, ninety-one, with every tooth in his head. He does them below the heights at Qingshi each morning. Go and do as he does."],
		["Yue Ling", "Ninety-one, and all his teeth. I want that written down for the healers."],
	],
	"s_p26_wupu": [
		["Narrator", "Below the heights at Qingshi, in the grey before the sun, an old man in a short jacket is crouched on all fours with his head down. As Lin Feng watches he springs, lands, and turns his head to look behind him like a deer. The two children watching do the same."],
		["Wu Pu", "Ninety-one this spring, and I can bite through a walnut. The master gave me five beasts: the tiger, the deer, the bear, the ape and the bird. When the body is heavy, do one until you sweat. Then wash, and you will want your dinner.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "That is all?"],
		["Wu Pu", "That is all, and nobody does it. They want a secret. There is no secret; there are five beasts. The master cut each into a stone and left the stones on the heights round the village, so that the climb is the first frolic. Bring me the five.", "point"],
	],
	"done:s_p26_tablets": [
		["Narrator", "Five stones, each cut in five strokes: the tiger crouched, the deer looking back, the bear swaying, the ape reaching, the bird with its wings up. Wu Pu sets them in a row along the well-kerb, where everybody going for water must pass them."],
		["Wu Pu", "Now people. Not the young: the young do this anyway and call it playing. Take it to the ones who have stopped, wherever they are. Up, down, over the sea. A hinge rusts the same in every realm."],
	],
	"visit:s_p26_dawn:teacher_ren": [
		["Old Teacher Ren of the Village School", "At my age? I have taught sitting still for forty years. Nobody ever learned anything standing up. ...Wu Pu is older than I am? Well. I shall do the bear at the back, where the children cannot see me, and then I shall teach it to them."]],
	"visit:s_p26_dawn:yu_gong": [
		["Yu Gong, the Foolish Old Man", "I have done the bear with a basket of earth on my back every morning for sixty years, carrying the mountain away. I did not know it had a name. My sons will be glad to hear it was medicine. They had other words for it."]],
	"visit:s_p26_dawn:ling_fei": [
		["Ling Fei, Who Cannot Fly", "The bird. For one of the Feathered Folk who has never once left the ground. ...Do you know, it is the first time anybody has shown me a way to do it that does not end with me on my back in the hen-run. I shall teach it at the aerie, to the old ones whose wings have gone stiff."]],
	"visit:s_p26_dawn:chang_sheng": [
		["Grove-Keeper Chang Sheng", "We do not grow old in the Nine Springs. We grow still, which is worse, because nobody notices. I have tended this grove for three hundred years and not once looked behind me like a deer. I shall start tomorrow. I shall start with the elders. They will hate it."]],
	"visit:s_p26_dawn:li_tieguai": [
		["Iron-Crutch Li", "A lame immortal, doing the tiger? Hah! Watch. ...There. The crutch is the tiger's fifth leg. I left my body to go wandering once, and came back to find it burned, and this was the only body to hand. I have been meaning to take it out for exercise for nine hundred years."]],
	"visit:s_p26_dawn:lamplighter_bo": [
		["Old Bo the Lamplighter", "The shades sit, in Youdu. There is nothing to get up for in a city where it is always dusk. But a lamplighter must reach, and stoop, and climb. ...The ape and the bird. I have been doing two of them for four hundred years and calling it work. I shall teach the other three."]],
	"done:s_p26_dawn": [
		["Narrator", "At first light, in four towns of three realms, people are being animals. The schoolmaster crouches like a tiger with enormous dignity; a Feathered Folk who cannot fly lifts both arms like a bird's wings and holds them there; a lame immortal does the tiger on three legs and a crutch."],
		["Wu Pu", "Every morning, mind. A door turns every day or it rusts. ...Now go to Tushan. The master says there is a cook there who has put down his knife, and a cook who has stopped cutting is the worst-fed man in the land."],
	],
	"s_p26_cook": [
		["Narrator", "The kitchen of the Cauldron Hall at Tushan is the size of a temple and smells of nothing. The fires are banked. Forty cooks stand about with their hands in their sleeves. On the great block lies a cleaver, and on a stool beside it sits a broad man in an apron, looking at it."],
		["Cook Ding", "Nineteen years, this knife, and thousands of oxen, and the edge is as if it had just come from the stone. A good cook changes his knife once a year, because he cuts. An ordinary cook once a month, because he hacks. I go where the gaps are.", "think"],
		["Lin Feng", "Then why have you put it down?"],
		["Cook Ding", "Because the court has been sent an ox for the feast of the six hearths, and I cannot see the gaps in it. When I began I saw nothing but whole oxen. After three years I never saw a whole ox again. Now one stands on the height above the town, and I can see nothing else.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "And everything at Tushan that cannot bend has come down to the kitchen door to wait for scraps."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_gristle": [
		["Narrator", "The gristle-hounds go off with their tails down; the locked joints stand where they stopped, like furniture. The butchers who hack throw their cleavers in a heap outside the kitchen door, every edge turned like a saw."],
		["Cook Ding", "A month, those knives lasted. They went at the ox as if it were a wall. ...My whetstones. I keep four, to be polite to them, and have not touched one in nineteen years. Somebody has thrown them up the hill. I should like them back before I go up."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_whet": [
		["Narrator", "Four grey stones of the river Luo, each hollowed by somebody else's knife long before Cook Ding was born. He runs his thumb across each, sets them in a row, and does not use them."],
		["Cook Ding", "Now I can go up. Not to sharpen. To remember that I do not need to."],
	],
	"pre:quan_niu": [
		["Narrator", "On the height above Tushan stands an ox the size of a house, perfectly still and perfectly whole: not a seam in it, not a joint, not a hollow. Looking at it is like looking at a wall with no door. Cook Ding stands beside Lin Feng with his knife held loosely and his eyes half shut."],
		["Cook Ding", "Do not look at it. Looking is what it wants. Meet it with the spirit, not the eyes. Follow the grain of heaven: into the big gaps, along the big hollows, by what is so of itself. There is a way in. There always is.", "point"],
	],
	"post:quan_niu": [
		["Narrator", "The Whole Ox comes apart like a clod of earth falling to the ground, with no struggle in it, and does not know it has been cut. Cook Ding stands with his knife raised, looks round, lingers, wipes the blade, and puts it away."],
		["Cook Ding", "There. I had forgotten how good it is. It was never the knife. The joints have spaces, and the edge has no thickness: put what has no thickness where there is space, and there is room and more for the blade.", "laugh"],
	],
	"s_p26_feast": [
		["Narrator", "The feast of the six hearths is served in the Cauldron Hall at one long table. The Son of Heaven eats what he is given and says it is very good, which is correct of him. The keepers of the hearths eat like people who have walked a long way."],
		["Cook Ding", "Lord Wenhui watched me cut once, long ago, and when I had finished he said: Excellent! I have heard the words of Cook Ding and learned how to nourish life. I did not know what he meant. I thought he was praising the soup.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "And now?"],
		["Cook Ding", "Now I think he meant your physician. Go where the gaps are, and the edge never dulls. Sit nineteen years in a hall where there are no gaps, and the best edge in the land goes to a saw. Go and tell him the ox is eaten. He will want to know.", "nod"],
	],
	"s_p26_summons": [
		["Narrator", "At the springs a black horse is tied to a glowcap, and a rider in black lacquer stands very upright in the steam with a sealed scroll. Hua Tuo is packing his needles into a roll of cloth, one at a time, with great care."],
		["Hua Tuo", "The Lord of Wei has a wind in his head. Twenty years it has come in the night and split him from the brow to the nape. He sent for me once before. I put a needle in his foot, and the wind went out, and I did not stay. He has never forgiven me for not staying.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "Then do not go."],
		["Hua Tuo", "A physician who refuses a patient is a cook who will not cut. The needle only lets the wind out. To be rid of it I should have to open his head, take out what is there and close it again. I shall tell him so. He has had a great many people killed for wanting to look inside his head.", "think"],
		["Lin Feng", "Then we are coming with you."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_camp": [
		["Narrator", "The camp of Wei has not slept in a month. Its drums beat the watches all night so that its lord will not be alone with the sound inside him, and the crows on the tent-ropes have learned the rhythm. When the last drum is stove in, every soldier in the camp lies down where he stands and is asleep before he touches the ground."],
		["Yue Ling", "They took his needles at the gate, Lin Feng. The guard who took them was afraid of them. He threw them out over the plain as far as he could, and then he washed his hands."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_needles": [
		["Narrator", "Four silver needles as long as a finger, with coils of wire for handles. One has gone into the earth up to the coil, as if it had been looking for something. Lin Feng wraps them in a cloth the way he saw the physician do it."],
		["Yue Ling", "They put him in chains when he said the word skull. The jailer's wife has been taking him soup. She says he writes all night, every night, by a lamp she is not supposed to have given him."],
	],
	"pre:cao_cao": [
		["Narrator", "On the height above the camp a man in black armour sits on a folding stool with his hand pressed to his brow, and has done for a month. He is not old. He has the look of somebody who has been awake for twenty years and has decided that it is everybody else's fault."],
		["Cao Cao", "Open my head. He said it to my face: open it, take out what is there, close it again. And I am to lie under his knife asleep while a man who serves nobody does as he likes inside me. You would do it, I suppose. You trust everybody. I would rather have the wind.", "refuse"],
	],
	"post:cao_cao": [
		["Narrator", "He goes down on one knee on his own height, and the hand comes away from his brow for the first time in a month. He looks at it as if somebody had just handed it to him."],
		["Cao Cao", "Let him go. ...Let the old man go. I find I do not want to be the man who killed the only one who ever told him the truth about what was in his head. The histories will say I did. They may say what they like. I am going to sleep.", "grieve"],
	],
	"s_p26_book": [
		["Narrator", "At the door of the jail a woman in an apron stands over a brazier with a book in both hands: thick, of mulberry paper, sewn with blue thread, in a bag of blue cloth. Behind her Hua Tuo sits on a step in the sun rubbing his wrists, looking ten years older than at the springs."],
		["Mistress Wu, the Jailer's Wife", "He gave it to my husband through the bars, in the night. Everything he knows, he said: the powder, the knife, the needle, the frolics. To save lives. And I asked my husband: what did it do for him? It put him in chains. It will put the next one in chains.", "grieve"],
		["Hua Tuo", "She is not wrong. I have thought about it all night. A book of the knife and the powder in every town, in every hand that can read: some of those hands will be a butcher's. And a book kept in one hall is one fire from ashes. Ask the Duke of Cai what it is to be sure.", "think"],
		["Hua Tuo", "It is yours to say, cultivator. I am only a physician. I know what is in the marrow after it is too late."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_pilgrims": [
		["Narrator", "Three pilgrims set out from three camps with a staff, a bowl and the five beasts. By the next new moon there is a tiger crouching in the dust outside a shrine on the Great Wall road, and a bear swaying at a ford in the south, and nobody there knows where either came from."],
		["Wu Pu", "That is how it should go. Not from a master to a pupil: from a stranger at a shrine to whoever is waiting for the ferry. In ten years nobody will remember it was ever taught. They will think the beasts thought of it themselves.", "nod"],
	],
	"done:s_p26_fish": [
		["Narrator", "The anglers come in by the kitchen door with their catch in wet grass: a carp from the Yellow River, a basket of little silver fish from the lakes, and an eel that has views on the matter. Cook Ding looks at each a long while before he touches it."],
		["Cook Ding", "A fish has no joints to speak of. It has a grain. Go with the grain and the flesh comes off the bone in one piece; go against it and you have soup. ...Tell them their fish will be eaten by six hearths at one table. They will want to have been asked."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_herbs": [
		["Narrator", "Five herbalists of the road come to the springs, look at the old man on his rock, and are put into the water at once, down to their underclothes, with everybody else. By evening they have stopped arguing with him. By the third evening they are arguing with each other, which he says is how you know they have begun to learn."],
		["Hua Tuo", "Five more. I shall not live to see all of what they will do, and nobody will thank me for any of it, and that is quite right. A physician thanked is a physician who was too late."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_summit": [
		["Narrator", "On the very top of the highest height of Glowcap Vale, where the wind comes straight off the snow, Lin Feng does the five frolics as Wu Pu showed him: the tiger, the deer, the bear, the ape and the bird. Yue Ling does them beside him, badly, laughing. Below, steam goes up from the springs."],
		["Yue Ling", "The bird is the hard one. You have to believe you could, or the arms will not go up."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_glide": [
		["Narrator", "He comes down as the bird does, under the cloud-kite, over the springs and the steam and the old man on his rock, who does not look up. When he lands Hua Tuo is waiting with a towel and the expression of somebody who has seen a great many young men fall off things."],
		["Hua Tuo", "Good. Now go and look at what you have left standing in the land, all of it, the way I look at a patient: from the skin in."],
	],
	"done:s_p26_marks": [
		["Wu Pu", "There. A body is what it does every morning, and a land is what was left in it. You have gone round and looked at yours from the skin in. Most people only ever look at their land the way the duke looked at himself.", "nod"],
		["Yue Ling", "Not ill?"],
		["Wu Pu", "Not ill. Every one of them, to the end. Go up and tell your Elder his three disciples are walking. They are walking very badly, and complaining the whole time, which is how you know it is working."],
	],
	"s_p26_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "They are walking. Badly, and loudly: one of them says his knees have filed a complaint with the Jade Emperor. And there is an old man of ninety-one in the courtyard every dawn now, being a bear, with half my disciples behind him.", "laugh"],
		["Lin Feng", "The physician said a hinge that turns is never eaten by worms."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And a hall where nobody gets up is a hinge nobody turns. I have been in that hall seventy years. I thought I was cultivating stillness. I was cultivating worms.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "Cook Ding says the knife never dulls if it goes where the gaps are."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then go and sit under the pine, child, and do not sit still. There is a realm for the one whose edge has no thickness. I do not know what it is like inside. From outside it looks like an old cook putting his knife away.", "bow"],
	],
	"pre:s_break31": [
		["Narrator", "Under the pine Lin Feng sits, and then gets up, and does the bear, and sits again. He does not force the qi through the gate. He feels for where the gates already are, and goes through the gaps between them, and there is room and more. The sky gathers, as it always has, to strike at something solid."],
	],
	"post:s_break31": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation comes down on the mountain and finds no whole thing to strike. It goes into the spaces of him like a blade into the joints of an ox, and comes out the other side unblunted, and so does he. In the courtyard an old man of ninety-one stops being a bear long enough to look up, and nods, and goes on."],
	],
	"recall21": [
		["Narrator", "The tops are open, and all that summer the people of the road go up and come down again with nothing to report. In the autumn the mountain is quieter than anybody remembers. It is so quiet that on the first morning of the tenth month everybody on it hears the same thing at once."],
		["Narrator", "The sect's great bell is struck for the dawn, as it has been every dawn for three hundred years. It rings flat. Not cracked: flat, by a hair, like a word said by somebody who has stopped meaning it. And when it has died away, something goes on. Under the ground, a long way west. In time."],
	],
	"s_p23_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You heard it. Old Shen has been sitting under the bell since dawn with his hands in his lap. He says it is not the bell. He says the bell is where it was and the note has moved. I have known Shen fifty years and he has never before said anything I did not understand."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the other sound? The beat, after."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Pots. I should know it anywhere; I was a potter's son. Somebody is striking fired clay with a stick. A great many somebodies, and every one of them exactly on the count. No tune. Not a note of tune in it. I find I mind that more than the noise.", "grieve"],
		["Yue Ling", "There is a line in the old songs. The Yellow Bell is broken and thrown away, and the clay pots thunder. I always thought it was only a way of saying that fools are listened to and good men are not."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Most sayings were a thing that happened, once. Go down to Qingshi. They have built a drum tower there since the spring, and a hall beside it, and the man who keeps the hall is said to have cut the first pipe that ever gave a true note. If the note has moved, he will know where from.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p23_lun": [
		["Narrator", "Qingshi has grown. East of Lao's farm there is a street now, from a drum tower to a hall with a green roof, and across it a tailor's, and a barracks nobody has slept in. At the door of the hall an old man in bamboo-yellow is holding a joint of bamboo to his ear, as one listens to a shell."],
		["Ling Lun, Who Cut the Pipes", "Three inches and nine tenths. I cut it in the valley of Xiexi for the Yellow Emperor, from a bamboo of even bore, between two nodes, and I blew it, and it was the note the cock phoenix sang first. Then eleven more by thirds, up and down. All measure comes from that pipe. The foot. The peck. The pound. What a day's work is."],
		["Lin Feng", "And the bell?"],
		["Ling Lun, Who Cut the Pipes", "The Yellow Bell was cast to the pipe, so that the note should not depend on an old man's breath. It hung on the height above this village, where nobody knew what it was. Last night it broke. I have the sound of it still in my teeth. And when the measure breaks, young man, whatever was made by rule and has no heart of its own gets up and walks.", "grieve"],
		["Ling Lun, Who Cut the Pipes", "There are small ones in the fields already, beating time on anything that will ring. Quiet them. Then find me the pieces. Four; I heard four strike the rock. A bell can be cast again. A note that has been forgotten cannot, and they are forgetting it by the hour.", "point"],
	],
	"done:s_p23_pots": [
		["Narrator", "Twenty. Each falls apart, when it falls, into something that was never alive: a cracked jar, a kiln-brick, a bad note somebody whistled and did not mean. And every one of them, to the last, is beating the same count. One. Two. One, two, three. Nobody taught it to them. It is simply what there is, with the tune taken out."],
		["Yue Ling", "That is a march. I do not know how I know. I have never heard an army. That is feet."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_shards": [
		["Ling Lun, Who Cut the Pipes", "Four. Yes. Lay them so. You see: no bigger than a rice bowl. People expect it to be large. The thing a land is measured by ought not to be large; it ought to be right. ...Somebody struck it, young man. This is not frost. Look at the bruise. Somebody found the one bell in the land that mattered and hit it with something made of clay."],
		["Ling Lun, Who Cut the Pipes", "I cannot cast it today. It wants the four pipes that went down the river in the flood, to tune by, and bronze given, not bought. And meanwhile there is the thing that march is for. Under Mount Li, in the west, a king buried eight thousand soldiers of fired clay to go on being obeyed. There is a man on the drill ground who knows about that sort of king.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p23_sun": [
		["Narrator", "South of the Drum Tower, on trodden ground, a spare man in grey is watching forty outer disciples of the Azure Cloud Sect attempt to form a square. He is not shouting. He has not, it appears, said anything for some time. The square is forming with great speed."],
		["Master Sun", "I am Sun Wu. The King of Wu asked whether my book could be tried on anybody, and gave me his palace ladies to drill, a hundred and eighty of them. They laughed. I explained again. They laughed. I beheaded the two he liked best, and after that they drilled like the Guard. He did not thank me. One is not thanked.", "shrug"],
		["Lin Feng", "Eight thousand of clay are coming, and they do not laugh."],
		["Master Sun", "No. They are the perfect army: they obey, they do not eat, they do not wonder. Every king wants one. And they have exactly one fault, sweeper, which is the same thing. Nobody in those ranks has a home he would rather be in. A host that would rather be home fights to end the war. Remember that. It is not in the book."],
		["Master Sun", "These forty will do; your Elder has lent them. But a host is not hired. It is raised. There are six hearths in this land, and you have eaten at all of them. Go to whoever keeps each fire and ask. Do not order. Somebody who is ordered sends you his worst.", "point"],
	],
	"visit:s_p23_levy:granny_wen": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "A company. Of ploughmen. Feng'er, I delivered every one of those boys, I know exactly what they are worth, and it is more than you think and less than they do. Take them. Tian's eldest can count; put him in front. And they are to wear their straw capes. I do not care what the general says."],
	],
	"visit:s_p23_levy:sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "You ask. Good. A khan would have sent a rider with a tally. A hundred, then, and they bring their own horses and their own opinion of spearmen. Batu leads. He is too young. So was his grandfather. Send them home fat, or do not come to my tent again."],
	],
	"visit:s_p23_levy:cook_aysha": [
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "The caravan guards are sitting in my yard eating my bread with no caravans to guard, because of your clay men on the road. Take them, by all means. They shoot from a kneeling camel, and they are paid in water, in advance. I have told them you are good for it."],
	],
	"visit:s_p23_levy:fishwife_lu": [
		["Fishwife Lu", "The haulers? You want the haulers? Hah. They cannot march, they cannot salute, and there is not one of them will take an order from anybody who has not pulled a rope. But they pull together. It is the only way they have ever done anything. Mind, when that lot push, it is the same."],
	],
	"visit:s_p23_levy:auntie_rong": [
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "Crossbows. We cast the locks ourselves, in bronze, each to the same pattern, so that a part from one fits any other. The soldiers of Mount Li were armed that way too, you know. It was Tushan men who cast for that king. We have never been proud of it. Take a hundred, and let them make it right."],
	],
	"visit:s_p23_levy:lantern_xi": [
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "We have no soldiers in Yanggu. We have a band. Drums, gongs, a reed horn, the lot that plays the Lantern Dragon down the street. They have never been to a war. They have been to forty festivals, mind, and the fifteenth night here is not a quiet thing. Will noise do? ...Then take the noise."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_levy": [
		["Narrator", "They come in to Qingshi over three days: ploughmen in straw, horse-folk who will not dismount inside a wall, camels, tow-path men with boat-hooks, casters carrying crossbows like new-born children, and last of all, heard a full hour before they are seen, the band."],
		["Master Sun", "Six companies who have never stood in one line and do not share a word for left. Excellent. A host that begins as one thing has only one way to break. This one will have to be broken six times. There is a lady coming in with the Tushan men who will know what to do with them. I only write books."],
	],
	"s_p23_hao": [
		["Narrator", "She arrives on a chariot she is driving herself, in bronze the colour of an old bell, with an axe across her knees that has an owl on the blade. Three thousand years ago she led thirteen thousand for the king her husband, and was buried with the axe. She has, it seems, been asked nicely."],
		["Lady Hao", "Fu Hao. The oracle bones call me that; the soldiers called me other things, mostly fond. You are the one who raised these? Then stand here and learn their faces. I will not have a man die in my line whose name his captain has to ask afterwards."],
		["Lin Feng", "They have never fought, Lady."],
		["Lady Hao", "Nobody has, the first time. Listen. On the field I give one order to a drum. Not three. One, and everybody knows it. Foot go in. Bows loose. Horse charge. Hold. Give ground. Or the drums sound, and nobody strikes at all. That last one my husband's generals never understood. It won me the Tu-fang campaign.", "point"],
		["Lady Hao", "The scouts will tell you what the other host means to do, when the dust lets them. Then you choose. A spear wall stops horse. Arrows find a spear wall. Horse ride down bows. It is a child's game with men in it. There is laced armour for you in the barracks. Wear it; they should see their captain coming."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_jobs": [
		["Clerk Bai of the Notice Board", "Two down, witnessed and paid. You would be surprised how much of a war is notices. Firewood. Fodder. Somebody to say where the well is. I had a general through here in the spring who could take a city and could not find his own baggage. I have put the levy's wants on the board; they go under the tofu."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_scouts": [
		["Narrator", "They do not break like the small ones. A spearman of Mount Li struck down goes over in one piece, and lies in the millet stubble with his face to the sky, and it is a face. No two are alike. Somebody, two thousand years ago, was made to stand still while a potter looked at him."],
		["Yue Ling", "This one has a scar through the eyebrow. That one is laughing at something. Lin Feng, they are portraits. Every single one of them was somebody, and the somebody went home, or did not, and this is what the king kept.", "grieve"],
	],
	"done:s_p23_tallies": [
		["Narrator", "Four halves of bronze tigers, each split from nose to tail, the gold writing stopping in the middle of every word. All four are left halves. The right halves, the halves a king keeps, are nowhere on the field."],
		["Lady Hao", "Tiger tallies. A general holds one half, the palace the other, and an army marches when the two are fitted and the writing reads. Nobody has fitted these. They are marching on half an order. Whatever is on that height has decided the king would have wished it. I have known generals like that. They are the dangerous kind.", "think"],
	],
	"s_p23_lishan": [
		["Narrator", "Under the one height of the plain the levy is drawn up: foot in front, bows behind, the horse out on the flank arguing, and at the back, by the banner, the band. Across a mile of stubble the dust is coming on in a straight line. Under the dust, something is keeping time."],
		["Lady Hao", "There they are. Six hundred, the vanguard of the left; mostly foot. They will come on, and come on, and then stand, and then send what horse they have. They do everything by the count. So count. I shall be beside the drum. The order is yours.", "point"],
		["Yue Ling", "I will be behind the centre with the stretcher-bearers. Both hosts' hurt, Lin Feng. I asked Lady Hao and she looked at me for a while and said yes."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_lishan": [
		["Narrator", "When the last drum has been beaten there are four hundred men of clay standing in the stubble exactly where they stopped, between one step and the next. The levy walks among them, slowly, the way people walk among graves. A ploughman from Qingshi takes off his straw cape and puts it round one."],
		["Master Sun", "Your first field. Notice what you are feeling. It is not triumph; you are too tired, and you have seen what was done to win it. Good generals feel like that every time. The other kind do not last, or else they last a great while, which is worse. Their general is on the height. He has not stopped."],
	],
	"pre:meng_tian": [
		["Narrator", "On the top of the height, where a general stands, stands a general: half as tall again as a man, in clay armour of six hundred painted plates, with a brush in his belt where another would carry a second sword. He is giving orders. Down the slope, nobody is carrying them out. He gives them again."],
		["Meng Tian, General of the Clay Host", "Left wing, advance. ...Left wing. I built the Wall with three hundred thousand of you, ten thousand li, from Lintao to Liaodong. I made the first brush out of rabbit hair so that the returns could be written faster. I have never once marched without the tally. I am not marching without it now. I am only keeping them ready."],
		["Lady Hao", "You have half a tally, General, and so have they. I have held the other half, in my time. It does not say what you think it says."],
		["Meng Tian, General of the Clay Host", "It says obey. It is all it has ever said. When his letter came telling me to die I had three hundred thousand men and I asked only whether the seal was true. It was true. So. Left wing!", "refuse"],
	],
	"post:meng_tian": [
		["Narrator", "The general of clay stands with his arm out, pointing down the slope at four hundred men in straw capes and wreaths of millet, who are not going to advance. He looks at them for some time. Then he lowers the arm, and it is plain that it is the first thing he has done in two thousand years that nobody told him to."],
		["Meng Tian, General of the Clay Host", "The seal was false. I knew. Everybody knew; the eunuch wrote that letter, and the First Emperor was three weeks dead in a cart of salt fish. I had the men to march on the capital and I asked whether the seal was true. ...I cut the veins of the earth, building that Wall. I have always said that was my crime. It was easier to say."],
	],
	"s_p23_kuang": [
		["Narrator", "At the foot of the height above the ferry a blind man is sitting with a qin across his knees which he is not playing. He has his face turned to the south, into the wind, and now and then he sings a few notes back at it, the way one answers somebody across a room."],
		["Music-Master Kuang", "I put my own eyes out with mugwort smoke, so as to hear better. It worked. Before a battle the Duke of Jin would ask me which side would lose, and I sang the airs of the north and then the airs of the south, and listened. The southern air is not strong, I said: it has many sounds of dying in it. Chu will have no success. And Chu had none."],
		["Lin Feng", "And now? Which host?"],
		["Music-Master Kuang", "Theirs has no air at all. Pots. One cannot sing a pot. Yours: I hear ploughmen and horse-herds and boat-haulers, and not one of them is singing either. You have taught them to march. A host that marches and does not sing is clay with a pulse, sweeper. Six hearths. Each has an air. Somebody at each still knows it. Go and be taught.", "point"],
		["Music-Master Kuang", "And there is another blind man on that height, above us. He plays. Every string he has is weighted with lead. I hear him at night, keeping their time for them. I should like to talk to him. We have a thing in common, and he chose his no more than I chose not to choose mine."],
	],
	"air:valley": [
		["Tofu Seller Sun", "The planting song? Everybody knows the planting song. ...Nobody has sung it to you? You swept steps; of course. It goes bent over, see, so the phrases are short. You breathe when you straighten. Three and five and six, like that. No, lower. You are not calling an ox. There. That is Qingshi, that is. That is every spring I ever had."],
	],
	"air:steppe": [
		["Batu the Horse-Herd", "A long song. Grandmother says a short song is for people with walls. You hold the note till the breath is gone, or the horizon, whichever is first. It is about a horse. They are all about a horse. ...No, do not hurry it. Nothing out here is in a hurry but the weather."],
	],
	"air:sand": [
		["Well-Keeper Zhaya", "This one has a step in it that your valley tunes do not. Half a step. It came down the road from west of where the maps stop, on the back of a camel, and nobody here ever knew the words, so we put in our own. They are about water. Ours always are."],
	],
	"air:river": [
		["Boatwright Gao", "The haulers' call. One sings, then all. The first half is the reach and the second is the rope coming in, and if you sing the second half without pulling something you will feel a fool, so here: take the end of this. Now. Hey-ya. PULL. You see. It is not a song. It is a way of being forty men."],
	],
	"air:forge": [
		["Bell-Tuner Gong", "Two hammers, one song, so that neither comes down on the other man's hand. Every line starts with the same note twice. That is where they fall. I tune bells by it; a bell that will not sit with the hammer song I send back to the pit. ...And yet every bell I own is a hair flat this month. I have been telling myself it is my ears."],
	],
	"air:dawn": [
		["Old Pan the Tea Seller", "The Lantern Dragon's tune! Quick, mind, the tail has to run. Flute and a small gong. We played it nine days running when the sun stuck at noon, to keep our spirits up, and by the ninth day we hated it. Now I cannot hear it without crying. Sing it badly. That is how it is done."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_airs": [
		["Narrator", "Four airs, from four people who were surprised to be asked. None of them is long. Sung one after another on the road back to Longmen they turn out, oddly, to fit: the planting song's last note is the long song's first, and the haulers' call sits under both like a keel."],
		["Music-Master Kuang", "Ah. There. You hear it too. They are one song, sweeper. They were always one song. The land had it whole once, before there were six hearths to keep a piece each. I have been waiting sixty years for somebody to carry the pieces into the same room. Now teach it to something that can make a noise."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_reeds": [
		["Narrator", "Strings that hang like plumb-lines. Flutes split down the grain. A festival drum, waterlogged, that gives one note like something dropped down a well. Every instrument on the river has gone to the same sour weight, and when the last of them is quiet the ferry bell rings the hour, flat."],
		["Yue Ling", "He did not mean to. Whoever is up there. You cannot do this to a flute by meaning to; I think you can only do it by not being able to bear the sound of one."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_pipes": [
		["Narrator", "Four pipes of bamboo, lodged in the rock where the flood left them, each closed at one end, each a third part shorter than the last. Lin Feng blows across the longest. It gives one note, and the flat bell at the ferry, a quarter of a mile off, hums with it for a moment like something remembering its own name."],
		["Lady Hao", "Put them somewhere they will not be trodden on. The chariots of the centre are at the ferry. And Master Sun has done a thing I should have had a captain flogged for: he has drawn us up with the river at our backs."],
	],
	"s_p23_river": [
		["Narrator", "The levy stands on the shingle with the Dragon Gate river running fast and brown behind it. There is no bridge. The boats have been sent across. In front, over the flood-plain, come chariots four abreast with crossbowmen kneeling in them, and they are in no hurry at all."],
		["Master Sun", "Every book says: never fight with water behind you. I wrote one of the books. But put men where they cannot run, and they will not; throw them where there is no way out, and they will find one through the enemy. Han Xin did it at the Jing Pass with raw levies, and afterwards his captains asked which chapter it was in.", "shrug"],
		["Lady Hao", "You cannot give ground here. Do not try. But a line that holds with the river behind it does not break, whatever its heart is doing; I have seen it. They will shoot, and then the chariots will come. You know what stops a chariot.", "point"],
	],
	"done:s_p23_river": [
		["Narrator", "The chariots of Mount Li stand axle-deep in the shallows, pointing every way but south. The haulers of the Boat Hearth are sitting on the shingle with their boat-hooks across their knees, quite silent. Then one of them, a big man, starts the call, very low. Reach. And forty answer him. Rope."],
		["Music-Master Kuang", "There. That I can hear from a mile. THAT host will not lose. ...The other blind man has stopped playing. He heard it too. Go up to him now, while he is still listening. He will start again, and it will be worse."],
	],
	"pre:gao_jianli": [
		["Narrator", "On the height sits a man with a cloth over his eyes and a long lute across his knees, struck with a bamboo rod. Every string sags. When he strikes one, eight thousand feet somewhere in the west come down together. He turns his head at the first footstep on the rock, exactly, the way the blind do."],
		["Gao Jianli of the Leaded Lute", "I played at the River Yi the day Jing Ke went to kill the King of Qin. The wind soughs, the Yi is cold; the brave man goes and does not come back. Everybody wept. He did not come back. So the king had my eyes burned out with horse dung, because he liked my playing and did not like my friends."],
		["Yue Ling", "And you put lead in the lute."],
		["Gao Jianli of the Leaded Lute", "I put lead in the lute and I swung it at his head, and I missed, because I could not see. Since then I play it as it is. It will not carry a tune with lead in it. Good. He does not deserve a tune. None of them do. They shall have the beat, and nothing else, for ever.", "refuse"],
	],
	"post:gao_jianli": [
		["Narrator", "The lute lies on the rock between them. Music-Master Kuang has come up the height on Yue Ling's arm, and sits down opposite the other blind man without a word, and after a while reaches over and touches one of the sagging strings. It gives a dull note. He hums the note it should have been."],
		["Gao Jianli of the Leaded Lute", "...A fourth. It should be a fourth above. I had forgotten there was an above. Who is that? You cannot see either. You did it to yourself? To HEAR? Old man, you are a fool. ...Sing me that again."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_songs": [
		["Narrator", "Three musicians of the Drum Tower quarter, a week of evenings, and every company of the host can now very nearly sing. The ploughmen have the long song wrong and prefer it so. The horse-folk have declined to learn the planting song on the ground that it is about standing in mud. But they hum it."],
		["Lady Hao", "Listen to that. A month ago they could not agree which foot was left. I had thirteen thousand once who would die for me and could not have got through a chorus. I am not certain, now, which was the better host."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_eighth": [
		["Master Sun", "Eight companies. The Hegemon-King has eight thousand, so he is ten to one, and has never in his life lost a field; he broke his own cauldrons and sank his own boats before Julu so that his men should have nothing to go back to. Do not try to beat him. Nobody ever has. He was beaten, in the end, by something else."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_clay": [
		["Narrator", "These are not the soldiers of Mount Li. Under the clay there is something else: a halberd held too tightly, a way of looking south. Eight thousand sons of the land east of the river crossed it with a young king and never went back, and somebody has fired their shapes into the First Emperor's ranks, where they least belong."],
		["Yue Ling", "They are on the wrong side. Lin Feng, do you see? These fought AGAINST Mount Li. They burned it. Whoever is beating the pots does not care whose soldiers it wears. It only wants things that march."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_banners": [
		["Narrator", "Five banners, standing alone in the sand, each with a company's number and a village's name. Round every one the footprints go the same way: south. On the ground by the last is a helmet, set down carefully, as one sets down something one means to come back for and knows one will not."],
		["Lady Hao", "So they have begun to go. Not from fear; these are not men who fear. Something has reminded them. Five companies out of eighty. It is the right medicine, sweeper. We want a great deal more of it, and tonight."],
	],
	"s_p23_gaixia": [
		["Narrator", "Gaixia, at dusk. The sands run flat to a height with one figure on it, and between the levy and the height stand the eight thousand, in the dress of Chu, with a king's black horse before them. They do not beat pots. They have no need to keep time. They have fought this field a thousand times, and lost it once."],
		["Lady Hao", "Hear me. You will not break that host by killing it. I would not try with thirteen thousand. But I have put the companies on four sides of him, a long way out, and every one of them now knows six airs that are one song. Hold when he comes. Give ground if you must. And when he stands: sound.", "point"],
		["Music-Master Kuang", "Sing the old one first. The one under the others. They were men of Chu before they were clay, and Chu is south of the river, and it is the airs of the south that are not strong. They are not strong because they are about going home."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_gaixia": [
		["Narrator", "It comes from the east first, a hundred voices, raggedly. Then the north takes it up, and the west. By the time the south joins, the eight thousand of Mount Li have stopped where they stand and turned their heads, every one, the way a man does who has just smelled his mother's kitchen."],
		["Narrator", "They ground their halberds. They do not run. They walk, by companies, in good order, south. In an hour there is nobody on the sand of Gaixia but the levy of the six hearths, which has not struck a blow since the third drum, and is still singing, because nobody has thought to tell it to stop."],
	],
	"pre:xiang_yu": [
		["Narrator", "On the height stands a very tall man in black armour with a double pupil in each eye, holding the bridle of a black horse. He has not looked behind him. He is looking at the levy, and through it, at something a long way back."],
		["The Hegemon-King of Western Chu", "My strength plucked up the hills. My spirit covered the age. The times were against me, and Dapple would not run. Do you know how many times I have fought this field? I win the charge. Every time. I have never once lost the charge. And then there is the singing."],
		["Lin Feng", "They have gone home, King. All of them."],
		["The Hegemon-King of Western Chu", "They went the first time too. I heard the songs of Chu on four sides in the night and I thought: has Han taken the whole of Chu, that there are so many of my people over there? It was a trick. It was the best trick ever played, and it was only the truth. ...You have played it again. With ploughmen. Come up here, then, sweeper. I still have the charge.", "refuse"],
	],
	"post:xiang_yu": [
		["Narrator", "The Hegemon-King sits down on the rock, heavily, with the bridle still in his hand. Below, a long way south, eight thousand small lights are crossing a river that is not there. He watches them the whole way over. He does not go with them."],
		["The Hegemon-King of Western Chu", "There was a boatman at the Wu River. One boat. He said: east of the river you could still be a king. And I said: I brought eight thousand of their sons over and not one goes back with me. How should I look at their fathers? ...Two thousand years, and I have no better answer. Take the horse. He has carried me five years, and I cannot bear to kill him."],
	],
	"done:s_p23_xiang": [
		["Narrator", "The field is quiet. The levy is quiet. On the road home the companies fall, one after another, out of step, and nobody shouts at them for it. And under all of it, a long way off and deep, the beat goes on. One. Two. One, two, three."],
		["Ling Lun, Who Cut the Pipes", "I told you it was struck. Not by the general; he only obeyed. Not by the lute; he only hated. Not by the king. Something set the first pot going, young man, and it is under this village, where the first pot was ever fired. Yu Gong's sons broke into it digging. I should have asked what they found.", "grieve"],
	],
	"pre:wafu": [
		["Narrator", "Under the adit, down a shaft the old man's sons had boarded over and not spoken of, there is a kiln: the first, older than bronze, a hollow in burnt earth. In the middle of it squats a cooking pot the size of a house, three-legged, cracked from lip to foot, with nothing in it. Nothing strikes it. It thunders."],
		["Wafu, the Thundering Pot", "BOOM. I was the first thing anybody made. Before the bell. Before the pipe. Somebody was hungry and put mud in a fire, and there I was, and they beat on me with a bone to say SUPPER, and everybody came. BOOM. Everybody. Then that one cut his little pipe and said mine was not a NOTE."],
		["Yue Ling", "So you broke the bell."],
		["Wafu, the Thundering Pot", "I HIT it. Once. With my own foot. I only wanted them to come when I called, as they used. I do not have a tune. I never said I had a tune! I have ONE THING, and it is LOUD, and it means come here, and nobody has come for four thousand years but things that cannot hear. BOOM.", "refuse"],
	],
	"post:wafu": [
		["Narrator", "The great pot has stopped. In the quiet there is a small sound, which is Yue Ling, who has picked up the bone that lies beside it and struck the rim, once, not hard. It is not a note. It is a dull, wide, friendly noise, like a door, and anybody who has ever been called in from a field knows what it means."],
		["Yue Ling", "Supper. That is all it says. Lin Feng, that is the oldest word there is. We built a bell to keep the measure and forgot what we used to call each other home with. Nobody was listening to it. It was not wrong to be angry.", "grieve"],
		["Wafu, the Thundering Pot", "...boom. You came. Two of you. That is not everybody. But it is two more than since the Xia. Do it again. No, softer. I am cracked, you know. It is all right. I was always cracked. It is how you can tell me from the others."],
	],
	"s_p23_bell": [
		["Narrator", "Four pieces of the old bell. Four pipes to tune by. And bronze: a ploughshare from Qingshi, a stirrup, a caravan bell, a boat-hook, a crossbow lock, a lantern frame, one from each hearth, given. Ling Lun weighs each in his hand and says nothing, and Auntie Rong of Tushan, who has come to pour, says nothing either."],
		["Ling Lun, Who Cut the Pipes", "It will not be the same bell. I should say so now. The old one was cast to my pipe and to nothing else; it was right, and it hung where nobody heard it. This one has a boat-hook in it. It will ring a hair warm of true. I find, at four thousand, that I can bear that. Pour, mistress.", "nod"],
		["Narrator", "They pour at dusk. It is struck for the first time at dawn, by Old Shen the bell-ringer, who has walked down from the sect in the night to do it. One note. In Qingshi a baby stops crying. In the Drum Tower quarter a tailor looks up from a hem. Under the hill, a long way down, something very old answers, once, softly: boom."],
		["Ling Lun, Who Cut the Pipes", "There. Now there are two again, as there were at the first fire: one to keep the measure and one to call them in. I threw the second away. I thought it was noise. Lin Feng: where is this bell to hang? It is your land's, not mine. I only know its length."],
	],
	"s_p23_hao2": [
		["Narrator", "On the drill ground the companies of the levy are standing in a line that is very nearly straight, with nothing in front of them. Lady Hao is walking down it with her hands behind her back. She stops at each captain, and says the captain's name, and the captain's village."],
		["Lady Hao", "I was buried with sixteen people who had not asked to come, four hundred bronzes and an axe. Nobody buried my soldiers with me. I have always been glad. Now. You raised this host. It has held three fields and lost fewer than I should have thought possible, and it can sing. What is to become of it?"],
		["Master Sun", "The book says: there has never been a state that profited from a long war. It does not say what to do with the army afterwards. I thought that was somebody else's chapter."],
	],
	"s_p23_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I heard it. Shen rang it and I heard it from my bed, and I thought: that is not our bell, that is better than our bell. Then I listened again. It was our bell. It was only in tune with something."],
		["Lin Feng", "With a cooking pot, Elder. The oldest one there is."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Hm. Yes. I have sat on this mountain seventy years listening for the note that is not sounded. The Old Master says the great note is rare. I took that to mean it was high up and hard to reach. I believe I have had it the wrong way about. I believe it is the one underneath, that everything else is in tune with or not.", "nod"],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng led the host and gave one order to a drum, Elder, and at the end gave none, and only stood there. And eight thousand people went home."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then he has it. Go and sit under the pine, Lin Feng. Do not listen for anything. It will be there when you stop.", "nod"],
	],
	"pre:s_break28": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng sits under the pine and does what the Elder said, which is nothing. For a long time there is the wind, and a novice sweeping two terraces down, badly. Then even the listening stops. What is left is not a sound. It is what the sounds were in."],
		["Yue Ling", "I shall not hum. I want it noted that I am not humming."],
	],
	"post:s_break28": [
		["Narrator", "Under the pine above the sect Lin Feng sits, and does not listen for anything. The bell is struck for the evening. It is a quarter-tone warm of true. A long way down and to the east, in the hour before supper, something very old says the one thing it knows how to say, and in six countries people look up from what they are doing and go in."],
	],
	# ---- Part XXII: The High Roads (the High Roads release)
	"recall20": [
		["Narrator", "The racks are full again, and the armoury door stands open; nobody has thought to bar it since. Spring comes up the valley a terrace at a time, and with it the people of the road: the woodcutters, the gatherers of herbs, the pedlars with a street on one shoulder, the pilgrims bound for the tops."],
		["Narrator", "They come as far as the foot of the mountain. This year they stop there. On the ninth day the Elder, who has watched one road from one step for seventy springs, sends for his sweeper."],
	],
	"s_p22_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Nine days, Lin Feng. Not a pilgrim on the upper road, not a woodcutter above the first bend. They are all down at the fires at the foot, eating their own provisions and looking up. I asked one why. He said: the top is not there this year."],
		["Lin Feng", "The top of what?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Of anything. They go up into the cloud, he says, and come out of it going down, on the side they went up, and never once stood anywhere. I should call it nonsense if I had not tried the Lookout myself yesterday. I was back at the gate before the tea was cold.", "shrug"],
		["Yue Ling", "There is a camp at the foot of Sun-Viewing Peak that was not there last week. Three hundred men, the porters say, with axes and ropes, and one in front in the oddest shoes. He is not waiting for the cloud to lift. He means to cut it a staircase."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Then go and see him before he cuts my mountain. And take your hands with you. You fly over most things nowadays. Whatever has shut the tops, I do not think it will open to somebody who arrives without having come.", "point"],
	],
	"s_p22_xie": [
		["Narrator", "At the foot of the great peak, beside a fire with a pot on three sticks, a man in a short green robe is taking the front teeth out of a pair of wooden clogs and fitting them into the heels. Behind him three hundred men are sharpening axes with the air of people who have been promised a view."],
		["Duke Xie of the Toothed Clogs", "Front teeth out for the way up, back teeth out for the way down, and the foot stays level on any slope under heaven. My own invention. I am Xie Lingyun, Duke of Kangle. I have walked up every mountain between here and the sea, and written a poem on top of each. Some of them good."],
		["Lin Feng", "And this one?"],
		["Duke Xie of the Toothed Clogs", "This one I have been up four times and have not arrived. So I am going to cut steps. Nine thousand, straight, with a chain. A top cannot hide at the end of a staircase. The last governor who saw my road-gang coming called out the militia, mind you. He took us for bandits.", "laugh"],
		["Duke Xie of the Toothed Clogs", "You are the sweeper? Good; you know steps. Before I spoil it: go up the small crag there, the Lookout, with your hands. No sword-flying. I want to know whether it is the mountains that are shut, or only the people.", "point"],
	],
	"done:s_p22_first": [
		["Narrator", "Hand over hand, with the rock an inch from his face the whole way: lichen, a seam of quartz, a snail that has been going up since the autumn. Then there is no more rock to reach for, and Lin Feng is standing beside a cairn somebody built before the sect was founded, with the whole valley under him."],
		["Yue Ling", "You have flown over this a hundred times. You have never once been on it. ...Neither have I. There is a stone on that cairn for everybody who came up. Put ours on."],
		["Duke Xie of the Toothed Clogs", "He arrived! Then it is not the mountains. It is how we have been coming. That is a great nuisance to a man with three hundred axes. Come down. No, wait: not the way you went up. I have a better way down, and it wants trying by somebody younger than I am.", "cheer"],
	],
	"done:s_p22_leap": [
		["Narrator", "A swallow of oiled paper on split bamboo, the kind the children fly from the fields below Qingshi, made large enough to hang under. It does not go up. It only declines to come down in a hurry, and for sixteen metres Lin Feng learns the difference between flying and being carried."],
		["Duke Xie of the Toothed Clogs", "Ha! He leans back like a man on a mule. Never mind. The air does the work; you have only to be something it can hold. Keep the kite. Now, the road. My axemen say the woods to the south have started calling them by name at night, and three have gone home.", "laugh"],
	],
	"s_p22_ziqi": [
		["Narrator", "At the camp under the mulberry heights a young woodcutter is sitting with his axe across his knees and his head on one side, listening to nothing. When Lin Feng's foot turns a pebble forty paces off he says, without looking round, that it is a cultivator, a tired one, and that the girl with him steps more lightly than he does."],
		["Zhong Ziqi the Woodcutter", "A gentleman played the qin by the river once while I was resting my load. I said: high mountains. He played again. I said: running water. He wept. Nobody had ever heard what he meant before. I only said what I heard. I am Zhong Ziqi. I cut wood."],
		["Lin Feng", "What do you hear now?"],
		["Zhong Ziqi the Woodcutter", "My mother, up the slope, calling me to supper. She has been dead six years, and she never once called me Ziqi. She called me Dog. Whatever is up there has learned my name off somebody and has not learned what I am called. And the salt is gone from every camp on the road."],
		["Zhong Ziqi the Woodcutter", "Ask the other cutters. Each of them has heard somebody. I should like to know whether it has got any of them right.", "think"],
	],
	"done:s_p22_cutters": [
		["Narrator", "Three camps, three woodcutters, and at each the same account: a voice from the dark above the fire, a wife's, a brother's, a foreman's, with the name right and the voice a little out, like a tune whistled by somebody who heard it once. In the morning: one footprint. Always the left."],
		["Zhong Ziqi the Woodcutter", "One foot. Then I know what it is, and so did my grandmother, who burned green bamboo in the fire every New Year to keep it off. A shanxiao. They are not wicked. They are lonely, and they like salt, and roast crab, and somebody else's fire. But a man who answers one goes up into the cloud after it."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_thieves": [
		["Zhong Ziqi the Woodcutter", "Hear that? Quieter. The small ones only carry; it is the old one who does the voices. He is on the height itself, across the saddle, with every bag of salt on this road, and he has not eaten a grain. He stacks it. I think he believes it is what makes people come back to a fire."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_salt": [
		["Narrator", "Four bags, damp, each tied with the knot of a different camp. Under the last is a heap of things that are not salt: a child's shoe, a pedlar's bell, a comb, a tally stick. Everything somebody on the road once turned round to look for."],
		["Yue Ling", "He has been keeping them safe. Look how they are laid out. Nothing on top of anything."],
	],
	"pre:shanxiao": [
		["Narrator", "On the height itself, in a ring of salt bags laid like a wall, sits something the size of a child of ten, with one leg, a face like a worried old man's and the wrong number of fingers. It looks at Lin Feng. Then it says, in Elder Qing Xuan's voice, very nearly: Lin Feng. Supper."],
		["Shanxiao, the One-Legged Thief of Voices", "Lin Feng. Supper. ...No? Lin Feng, the steps want sweeping. No. Wait. I had a good one. A girl's. You have flown over this a hundred times."],
		["Yue Ling", "That is mine. I said that on the Lookout. Give it back. Give it back."],
		["Shanxiao, the One-Legged Thief of Voices", "Mine now. I keep them. Nobody keeps them as I do. They come up for their names, and then they stay by the fire. That is how it is done. I have watched.", "refuse"],
	],
	"post:shanxiao": [
		["Narrator", "The shanxiao sits down hard among its salt, puts both hands over its mouth, and for the first time in nine nights says nothing in anybody's voice. After a while, in a small cracked one that is plainly its own, it asks whether it may keep the bell."],
		["Zhong Ziqi the Woodcutter", "There. That one I have not heard before. That is him. ...High mountains. I mean it kindly. It is what I say when I have heard somebody.", "nod"],
	],
	"s_p22_lie": [
		["Narrator", "On the one height of Thunder Valley a thin man in pale sleeves is standing at the very edge with his arms a little out from his sides, like a heron deciding. He has stood so, the horse-folk say, since the wind stopped. There is no wind at all. The grass below is as still as a painting of grass."],
		["Master Lie, Who Rode the Wind", "I rode it for fifteen days once. Cool, light, wherever it went. They wrote that down as a great thing. Then they wrote the next line, which nobody quotes: and still he had something to lean on. They were right. Look at me. The wind goes, and I am a man on a rock.", "shrug"],
		["Lin Feng", "Where has it gone?"],
		["Master Lie, Who Rode the Wind", "Into a bag. The Earl of the Wind has drawn the neck of it shut, every breath from here to the sea, because of the kites. Paper swallows all over his sky this spring. He says nobody asked him. He is quite right. Nobody did."],
		["Master Lie, Who Rode the Wind", "His loose gusts are biting people in the valley. Still them. And his good winds he keeps knotted on cords, as sailors do: bring me the cords and I can let out enough to stand on. Then you shall fly from here. Properly.", "point"],
	],
	"done:s_p22_gusts": [
		["Master Lie, Who Rode the Wind", "Better. I felt my sleeve move. The loose ones were only frightened; a wind with nowhere to go turns round and round and bites. The knotted ones are his own. Four cords. They hang where the rock is steepest. He likes a view."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_knots": [
		["Narrator", "Four cords of grey silk, three knots in each. Master Lie undoes the first knot of the first cord, and a breeze comes up the face of the rock as if it had been waiting at the bottom to be asked. He does not touch the second. The third, he says, nobody should ever undo on purpose."],
		["Master Lie, Who Rode the Wind", "One knot for a breeze, two for a gale, three for the kind that takes roofs. Now. Off the edge with you. Twenty-eight metres, and I shall be watching your shoulders."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_flight": [
		["Master Lie, Who Rode the Wind", "There. Did you feel where it carried you, and where you were only falling politely? No? You will. I rode fifteen days before I could tell. ...He has seen you. That was his sky you were in. He is coming up the far side now. He walks like a stag, and he is not pleased.", "point"],
	],
	"pre:fei_lian": [
		["Narrator", "It comes over the edge of the height with the body of a stag, the head of a sparrow, a serpent's tail and the spots of a leopard, and round its neck a bag of grey hide drawn shut with a cord. Where it sets its feet the grass lies down in a ring. Nothing else moves at all."],
		["Fei Lian, Earl of the Wind", "Paper. Paper and string and split bamboo, in MY air, with children on the end of it, and now grown men hanging underneath. Did one of you ask? I blew for Chiyou at Zhuolu. I am an earl. And I am to be a thing a sweeper falls off rocks on?"],
		["Master Lie, Who Rode the Wind", "I asked, my lord. Once. You said nothing, and bore me up for fifteen days. I took it for yes."],
		["Fei Lian, Earl of the Wind", "You I liked. You weighed nothing and wanted nothing. These WANT. Every one of them wants to get somewhere. Wind does not go somewhere! Wind GOES!", "refuse"],
	],
	"post:fei_lian": [
		["Narrator", "The Earl of the Wind stands with his head down and his sides going. The cord at his neck has come undone, and for a mile around every blade of grass is leaning one way, and every kite in the valley that was hanging dead on its string has gone up like a shout."],
		["Fei Lian, Earl of the Wind", "...They are laughing. The small ones, on the ends of the strings. I had forgotten that sound came with it. Hm. I do not say I am mollified. I say only that I had forgotten."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_anglers": [
		["Narrator", "Three anglers at three waters, none of whom has caught anything in a week and none of whom minds. Each has seen the same thing float past: a sheet of good paper, folded small, written on in a fine official hand. One says it was a poem. One says it was a will. The third could not read it, and kept it for the paper."],
		["Yue Ling", "They come down out of the Stone Forest. Somebody is up one of those towers writing to the whole world, and nobody is writing back."],
	],
	"s_p22_han": [
		["Narrator", "On the one summit of the Stone Forest, in cloud so thick it drips, a large man in a soaked official's robe is sitting with his back to the cairn and his eyes shut, holding on to it with both arms. Beside him: an ink-stone, a brush, and a neat stack of paper weighted with a rock."],
		["Han Yu, Who Cannot Get Down", "Do not tell me to look. I have looked. That is how I come to be here. I came up to see the view, sir, as a man of letters should. I saw it. All of it. Straight down. And my legs resigned their office."],
		["Lin Feng", "How long have you been up here?"],
		["Han Yu, Who Cannot Get Down", "I have written eleven farewells. One to my wife, two to my students, one to the Emperor explaining that the memorial on the bone was kindly meant, and seven to posterity. I threw them off, since nobody was coming. I should rather like them back. Some were hasty.", "grieve"],
		["Han Yu, Who Cannot Get Down", "And there is a lady. Farther along, in the thick of it. She does not speak. She looks down the path. I offered her my umbrella and she did not see me. I think it is her cloud, sir. I think I am sitting in somebody's waiting."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_letters": [
		["Han Yu, Who Cannot Get Down", "All five? And dry, more or less. Do not read the one to posterity. ...You have read it. Well, I stand by the second paragraph. Sir, I have been listening to things move below us while you were gone. Large things, with a tread like a cat's. They are hers. I think they are keeping the path clear."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_leopards": [
		["Yue Ling", "They were not hunting. Did you see? Every one of them was lying across the path, facing down it, the way a dog lies in a doorway when somebody is expected. We have just walked through nine days of somebody's welcome.", "grieve"],
	],
	"done:s_p22_down": [
		["Narrator", "It takes some time to get a large official under a paper swallow, and longer to get him to open his eyes. Then the rock is gone from under all three of them, and the Stone Forest is turning below like a tray of ink-brushes, and Han Yu, who has been composing his twelfth farewell, stops in the middle of a line."],
		["Han Yu, Who Cannot Get Down", "Oh. Oh, but it is beautiful from here. I could not see it for being afraid of it. ...Put me down by the water, sir, anywhere at all, and I shall write that the way up a mountain is hard and the way down impossible, and that I was carried, and by whom. Go back up. She is still looking down that path."],
	],
	"pre:shan_gui": [
		["Narrator", "Where the cloud is thickest a woman is standing on the rock in a dress of fig leaves, girdled with hanging moss, with one hand on the neck of a red leopard. She is looking down the path. She has, it is plain, been looking down it for a very long time, and has arranged her face for somebody."],
		["The Mountain Spirit, Who Is Still Waiting", "You are not he. You are the fourth who was not. I came late, you understand. The bamboo was so thick I could not see the sky, and the way was steep, and I came late, and he had been and gone. Or had not come. I have never known which."],
		["Yue Ling", "So you kept the top in cloud. So that if he came you would be here. And if he had been already, you need not see that he had gone."],
		["The Mountain Spirit, Who Is Still Waiting", "On every top. He did not say which. He said: on the mountain. I thought, if no one can arrive at any of them, he cannot have arrived without me. Do not look at me so. It is reasonable. It has been reasonable for a thousand years.", "grieve"],
	],
	"post:shan_gui": [
		["Narrator", "The cloud does not lift. It thins, a little, the way breath does on a glass, and through it for a moment there is the whole south country in the rain, and no one on the path. The Mountain Spirit looks at that. She goes on looking. The leopard leans its whole weight against her."],
		["The Mountain Spirit, Who Is Still Waiting", "No one. ...Then I may be angry with him. I have not been able to be angry: I did not know which of us was late. Thunder rolls, rain darkens, the gibbons cry all night. I thought of you, lord, and had only sorrow of it. I should like, now, to think of something else."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_summits": [
		["Narrator", "Seven tops. On each a cairn, and on each cairn one more stone than there was. From the seventh, on a clear evening, Lin Feng can count the other six, and on every one of them, small and bright, there is somebody who has just arrived and is standing very still."],
		["Yue Ling", "Nobody shouts. I thought they would shout. They get there and go quiet, all of them, even the pedlar. I wonder what the ones who live up here make of it. Somebody must have been on these the whole time, cloud or no cloud."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_hermits": [
		["Narrator", "Three hermits on three summits, a hundred years up between them, and not one had noticed that the tops were shut. Cloud comes, they say, and cloud goes. But all three have watched the same thing these last nights, going up the great peak above the sect in a line, by torchlight: stones. Dressed stones, with writing on."],
		["Yue Ling", "Steles. Walking. Somebody has opened the tops, and the first thing up them is a row of people's names."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_steles": [
		["Duke Xie of the Toothed Clogs", "I knew three of those! That one was put up by a prefect I dined with, to record that he had mended a bridge. He had not mended it. And the smoke, did you smell it? Sacrifice smoke. Announcements. Somebody up top is being told a great many things at once, sweeper, and I do not think he asked to hear them."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_slips": [
		["Narrator", "Jade slips, bound with gold cord, buried shallow where the old kings left them: to Heaven, from the undersigned, reporting success. Five reigns, five hands, and the same sentence in every one. Under the cord of the last is tucked a strip of plain bamboo in a clerk's writing: received. received. received. received."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_long": [
		["Yue Ling", "Forty-eight metres, and you did not lean back once. I watched your shoulders, since he was not here to. You looked like something the air had decided to keep. ...Now the top. On your hands, the Elder said. I think he was right about that."],
	],
	"pre:dongyue": [
		["Narrator", "On Sun-Viewing Peak, where seventy-two kings came up to tell Heaven they had succeeded, an official the size of a gate-tower is sitting at a stone desk with his crown pushed back and his head in his hand. Round him, as far as the edge, stand steles; and up the last of the eighteen bends, in a patient line, come more."],
		["The Lord of the Eastern Peak", "Next. ...No. You have no slip. You have no stele. You have not even a brush. What have you succeeded at? Quickly. There is a prefect behind you who has mended a bridge."],
		["Lin Feng", "Nothing. I came up to see the sun rise."],
		["The Lord of the Eastern Peak", "Nobody comes up to see the sun rise. They come up to be seen by it. Seventy-two kings, and I received every one. I am the mountain, do you understand: I cannot go home. I was GLAD of her cloud. Nine days with nobody arriving. And you have thinned it, and here they all are again, with their names.", "grieve"],
		["The Lord of the Eastern Peak", "I shall close it in stone this time. A top with a lid on. Stand aside, or be carved on.", "refuse"],
	],
	"post:dongyue": [
		["Narrator", "The great official sits down again at his desk, heavily, and the steles on the eighteen bends stop where they are, like men who have forgotten what they came upstairs for. It is very nearly dawn. For once there is nobody on Sun-Viewing Peak with anything to report."],
		["The Lord of the Eastern Peak", "...It comes up over there. Between the two islands. I had not looked since the first king. He stood in front of it. They all stand in front of it. Hm. Stay, if you like. You are the first in two thousand years who has not told me anything."],
	],
	"done:s_p22_pilgrims": [
		["Narrator", "Three pilgrims, at three camps, told that the tops are there again. Each of them says the same thing: that they knew. They had been setting out every morning regardless. It is not, the oldest explains kindly, the top that one goes for."],
	],
	"s_p22_xie2": [
		["Duke Xie of the Toothed Clogs", "So. A shanxiao with a bell, an earl with his bag open, a lady who has stopped waiting and a mountain that has seen the sun. And I have three hundred men, sharpened, and nothing to cut. I have written nine poems this week and climbed nothing. It is the best week I have had."],
		["Lin Feng", "What will you do with the clogs?"],
		["Duke Xie of the Toothed Clogs", "Wear them. Front teeth out going up, back teeth out coming down, and the foot stays level. That is all they ever were: a way of being level on something that is not. You do it without clogs. I have watched. Go home, sweeper. Your Elder will want to know his mountain is the shape he left it.", "laugh"],
	],
	"s_p22_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I went up the Lookout this morning. On my hands, at my age, with a novice behind me to catch what fell off. I arrived. There is a stone on that cairn with a broom scratched on it. I did not ask whose."],
		["Yue Ling", "He has stood on more tops this spring than in all the years he could fly over them, Elder. And come down from most of them under a paper bird, looking like washing."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Master Lie rode the wind, and leaned on the wind. Duke Xie leans on his clogs, and I on my seventy years. Go and sit under the pine, Lin Feng, and see whether there is anything left that you lean on. I am curious to know what a man stands on when it is nothing.", "nod"],
	],
	"pre:s_break27": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng does not sit under the pine. He climbs it: hand over hand, the bark an inch from his face, to the fork where the sweepers of the sect have tied a rag every spring for three hundred years. There is no kite. The sky above the mountain goes the white of the inside of a cloud."],
		["Narrator", "The tribulation comes as wind. It takes the pine by the crown and bends it like a bow, and looks for whatever he is holding on by."],
	],
	"post:s_break27": [
		["Narrator", "He lets go. The pine springs back without him. For the space of a breath the sweeper of Azure Cloud is standing on nothing at all, above nine hundred steps, leaning on neither wind nor rock nor root; and the tribulation, finding no handle anywhere on him, goes round him like water round a stone and out to sea."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng becomes a cultivator who Leans on Nothing: one who mounts on what is true of heaven and earth, and rides the changes of the six breaths, has nothing left to depend on. He comes down the ordinary way, by the branches, and is late for supper."],
	],
	"pre:s_break26": [
		["Narrator", "Under the old pine Lin Feng lays out the eight in a row on the grass: the jian, the staff, the sabre, the spear, the fan, the bow, the whip, the brush. Then he sits down with his hands empty, and the sky above the mountain goes the grey of a drawn blade."],
		["Narrator", "The tribulation comes as an edge. There is no thunder in it. It comes down the way a cut comes, thin and exact, and looks for whatever in him will rise to meet it."],
	],
	"post:s_break26": [
		["Narrator", "Nothing rises. The edge passes through an open hand and finds nothing to part, and goes out over the valley like light off water, and is gone. On the grass, eight weapons lie exactly where they were put."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng becomes a cultivator of the Sheathed Edge: the best soldier is not soldierly, the best fighter is not angry, and the one who best overcomes an enemy does not join issue. He gets up, in a while, and puts the eight away, and takes the broom."],
	],
	"pre:s_break25": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation finds Lin Feng in the courtyard at dusk, doing nothing. Not meditating; not sweeping; sitting on the well-head with his hands open on his knees, listening to the water a long way down."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is gathering. Lin Feng. Are you not going to get ready?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I think this is ready. There is a great deal of room in me tonight. Let it come in and see."],
	],
	"post:s_break25": [
		["Narrator", "The lightning comes down into him the way water comes down into a well: all of it, and there is still room. It does not strike anything. There is nothing in the way for it to strike. After a while it has all gone in, and the sky, having nothing left, clears."],
		["Yue Ling", "You did not even stand up."],
		["Lin Feng", "No. It turns out there was no need. Come and sit. The stone is still warm, and there is nothing at all that we have to do."],
	],
	# ---- Part XIX: The Machine Heart (the Hundred Works release)
	"recall17": [
		["Narrator", "Summer goes on. The new fire cooks, casts and lights as no fire has in sixty years, and the land, which had forgotten what it was to have strength to spare, begins to make things with it."],
		["Narrator", "The first sign on the mountain is small. The sect's rice comes up from Qingshi in the usual sacks on the usual mule, but it comes two days early, and it is white. Polished. Every grain."],
		["Yue Ling", "Auntie Zhou has been looking at it for an hour. She says nobody in the valley has that many daughters-in-law."],
		["Lin Feng", "She has not cooked it?"],
		["Yue Ling", "She says she does not trust rice that nobody was tired for."],
	],
	"s_p19_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "You have seen the rice. Sit. There is a carpenter in the valley. He came up the river road a month ago with a bag of tools and an ink-line, looked at the mill-race below Qingshi, and asked whose it was."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "They told him it was nobody's; the old mill fell down in my grandfather's time. In nine days he had built a wheel in it twice the height of a man. It grinds the whole valley's rice between breakfast and noon."],
		["Lin Feng", "That sounds like a blessing, Elder."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "It does. I have been trying all morning to find what is wrong with it, and all I have found is that I am an old man who does not like new things. I had a letter from Tushan too: they have hammers that the river lifts. And yet."],
		["Yue Ling", "And yet Auntie Zhou will not cook the rice."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And yet. Cooks know things. Go down and meet him. His name, he says, is Gongshu Ban. If that is true he is the patron of every carpenter who ever lived, and he has been dead for two thousand years, and I should like to know which of those facts he considers the more important."],
	],
	"s_p19_ban": [
		["Narrator", "The wheel stands in the mill-race, turning with a sound like slow applause. A broad man in a leather apron is lying on his back underneath it with a mallet, hitting something, and talking to it."],
		["Gongshu Ban", "There. No. There. Ha! ...Who is that? Mind the shaft; it will take your sleeve and then it will take the rest of you. I am Ban. Gongshu Ban. Yes, that one. Do not kneel, you will get wet."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Elder says you have been dead two thousand years."],
		["Gongshu Ban", "So I am told. A maker does not die while people still pick up his tools, and every morning somewhere a boy picks up a saw. I sleep a good deal. I woke because of your fire. Do you know what a fire like that is, to a founder? To a smith? It is like being young."],
		["Gongshu Ban", "Look at her. Forty-eight floats, an oak shaft, and a cam that lifts four pestles. The river does it. The river was going downhill anyway! All that strength running to the sea since the world began, and nobody had asked it for anything."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is beautiful. It is. Does it ever stop?"],
		["Gongshu Ban", "Why would it stop? ...You are the second person to ask me that, and the first was a cook. Never mind. I need catalpa for the second wheel, seasoned, straight; there is good fallen timber in your bamboo forest. And sweeper: have you ever made anything? With your hands?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I have swept a great many steps."],
		["Gongshu Ban", "That is keeping, not making. Both are needed. But I cannot talk about machines with somebody who has never made one go. Bring me my timber and then go and build something. Three things. I do not care what."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_timber": [
		["Narrator", "Five baulks of catalpa, grey outside and the colour of honey where the adze bites, each one fallen in some storm years ago and seasoned where it lay, as if the forest had been putting them by for a carpenter."],
		["Yue Ling", "He will smell every one of them. You watch. Tie does the same with iron."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_build": [
		["Narrator", "Three things stand in the land tonight that did not stand there before, and each of them was made by Lin Feng's own two hands, with a great deal of advice."],
		["Yue Ling", "You have a splinter. No, the other hand. You have two splinters. ...You are smiling like a boy with a kite."],
		["Lin Feng", "It works. I did not know whether it would, and it works."],
		["Yue Ling", "I know. I think that is exactly the feeling Gongshu Ban woke up for."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_farm": [
		["Narrator", "Five sacks carried down to the mill on five backs, tipped under the pestles, and carried home white before the tea had gone cold. The farmers of Qingshi stand about in the middle of the morning with nothing to do, and do not know where to put their hands."],
		["Farmer Lao", "My wife used to pound for two hours before dawn. She sang. I did not know I liked the singing until this month. She sleeps till sunrise now, and I lie awake and listen to the wheel."],
	],
	"s_p19_gardener": [
		["Narrator", "Below the last terrace an old man is watering cabbages. He goes down a tunnel cut into the bank, comes up with a clay pitcher in his arms, pours it along one furrow, and goes down again. He has done forty furrows. There are a great many more."],
		["Lin Feng", "Grandfather. There is a machine for this. A pole on a post with a stone at one end. It waters a hundred furrows in a day, and it is no labour at all."],
		["The Gardener of Hanyin", "The well-sweep. Yes. I know of it."],
		["Narrator", "The old man's face goes red, and then, slowly, he laughs."],
		["The Gardener of Hanyin", "I had it from my teacher: where there are machines there are machine affairs, and where there are machine affairs there is a machine heart. With a machine heart in your breast, what was pure and simple is spoiled. It is not that I do not know of your sweep, boy. I should be ashamed to use it."],
		["Yue Ling", "That is not fair, Grandfather. A pitcher is a machine. So is a tunnel. So is a hoe."],
		["The Gardener of Hanyin", "Ha! The lotus has teeth. Yes. So they are. I did not say I knew where the line was. I said there is one, and that a man should know which side his heart is on. Go and look at that wheel at midnight, when everybody has gone home, and then come and tell me a pitcher is the same."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_mites": [
		["Narrator", "The last of them comes out of the wheel-pit backwards: a thing of bronze the size of a dog, all legs, with a little toothed wheel where its face should be, ticking. It is not angry. It simply does not understand why anybody would want it to stop."],
		["Yue Ling", "The gardener was right about midnight. Nobody was here. It was turning, and grinding nothing, and they were climbing all over it like ants on a sweet. Lin Feng, they were helping it."],
	],
	"s_p19_tushan": [
		["Narrator", "Tushan can be heard from the pass: not the ringing of the casting yards, which stops at dusk, but a deep double beat, lift and fall, that does not stop at all. The founders in the lanes walk in time to it. None of them seems to know."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "Bellows. Ten strokes. ...No. No, the river does that now. Sit. I forget. I keep forgetting that nobody has to do anything."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "Nine days the hammers have run. We have cast more bronze in nine days than in nine years. Wei has not been home. My nephew fell asleep standing and went on working the ladle; I saw it. And when I say stop, sweeper, they look at me as if I had said it in a foreign tongue."],
		["Lin Feng", "Can the hammers be stopped?"],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "That is the thing. Nobody built a way. The carpenter said, why would you want one? And we all laughed, because we could not think of an answer. The gear-trains have grown across the lanes like bramble. Start there. Break them. I would do it myself, but I find I cannot lift my hand against something that is working so hard."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_cogs": [
		["Narrator", "The fifth gear-train comes apart under the sword with a shriek of bronze, and the wheels roll away down the lane, each on its own, slower and slower, and fall over. In the silence one founder sits down on a doorstep, quite suddenly, like a puppet whose strings have been let go."],
		["Bell-Caster Wei", "...What day is it? I was pouring the third bell. I remember thinking, I will stop when this one is done. And then there was another mould ready. There was always another mould ready."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_hammer": [
		["Narrator", "The great trip-hammers stand over the pits with their heads up, like horses that have been pulled in. The river still runs under them. It is only that nothing is asking it for anything, for the first time in nine days."],
		["Yue Ling", "Listen. You can hear the town again. Somebody is snoring. A great many people are snoring."],
	],
	"s_p19_mo": [
		["Narrator", "A gaunt man in black hemp is sitting on the rim of a cold pit, binding his feet with strips torn from his own coat. His sandals are worn through to the ground. He does not get up."],
		["Mozi", "Forgive me. Ten days and ten nights from the east, and I find I have no feet left to be polite with. I am Mo Di. Where is Gongshu Ban?"],
		["Lin Feng", "At Qingshi, Master, building a second wheel."],
		["Mozi", "Then he does not know. Good. I was afraid he did. Listen: I am not against machines. I have built more than he has. I ask of a thing one question, what is it good for, and a mill is good for a great deal."],
		["Mozi", "But every engine in the land has begun to run without being asked, and they are not grinding or hammering now. They are building. I followed the carts. On the mountain above Cloudfoot there is a siege ladder going up, a cloud ladder, the kind Ban built once for a king, and no hand is on it, and it has no wall to climb. It is going up because up is where a ladder goes."],
		["Yue Ling", "What happens when it gets there?"],
		["Mozi", "A thing that wants only one thing never arrives, child. That is what is wrong with it. I stopped that ladder once before with a belt and nine sticks on a table. I do not think it will sit down to a table now. But the models are sound, if somebody can still make them. Go. I will follow when I have feet."],
	],
	"pre:cloud_ladder": [
		["Narrator", "It stands on the bare shoulder of the mountain above Cloudfoot, and it is already taller than the pines: a tower of baulks and bronze on eight wheels, with a ladder rising out of the top of it that lengthens, rung by rung, as wooden kites come in out of the south and lay their timber down. There is nothing at the top. There is only more ladder."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is not going anywhere. Look at it. It is not even leaning on anything."],
		["Narrator", "The tower turns on its wheels, slowly, the way a head turns. It has noticed that something at its foot is not carrying timber."],
	],
	"post:cloud_ladder": [
		["Narrator", "The Cloud Ladder comes down the way a felled pine comes down, slowly and then all at once, and lies along the mountain in a line of broken rungs a quarter of a li long. The kites circle over it, with timber in their claws and nowhere to put it."],
		["Mozi", "Nine attacks and nine defences. I counted. You did not need the table after all. ...I am sorry it had to be broken. It was good joinery. Ban never made a bad joint in his life, even in things that should not have been made."],
		["Mozi", "Now ask yourself what I have been asking for ten days. A ladder does not want. A mill does not want. Somebody has taught them wanting, and whoever it is did not learn it from Gongshu Ban, who never wanted anything but a straight line. There was another maker once, at the court of King Mu. Look for him where the caravans come in."],
	],
	"s_p19_gao": [
		["Boatwright Gao", "Smooth water. Though it is not, this month. Come and look at my slip. No, there is nothing on it. That is what I want you to look at."],
		["Boatwright Gao", "I built a paddle-boat in the spring. Wheels on her sides and a treadle amidships: two men walking, and she goes upstream like a duck. The carpenter from the valley showed me the gearing. I built six. The whole ferry was going to be paddle-boats by autumn."],
		["Lin Feng", "And they have gone."],
		["Boatwright Gao", "Three nights ago. All six, with nobody aboard, treadles going by themselves. Old Qian found them at dawn under the Dragon Gate, in a row, paddling at the falls. Trying to climb. A boat! The carp do it once in a thousand years and become dragons. What does a boat become?"],
		["Yue Ling", "What did you do with the old boats? The ones you pole?"],
		["Boatwright Gao", "...Under tarpaulins. I was going to break them up for timber. Hm. Half the river has not poled since spring. Go down to the moorings; they will need shaming into it, and it had better not come from me."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_ferry": [
		["Narrator", "Four boats poled across the brown river and back by eight sore arms, with a great deal of shouting about the current, which nobody had needed to know anything about since spring. The fourth lands a hundred paces downstream of where it was aimed. Its crew is very pleased with itself."],
		["Fishwife Lu", "A pole tells you what the bottom is. Mud, gravel, the old wreck by the third pile. Six weeks of treadling and I had forgotten there was a bottom. You do not get that through a wheel."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_lamps": [
		["Narrator", "Four lamps trimmed, filled and lit by hand in the street of the lantern-makers, each with a taper pinched from the shrine lamp, never blown. Beside every one of them a revolving lantern goes on turning its paper horses, with no candle in it at all, and now that there is a real flame to look at nobody is looking at them."],
		["Old Pan the Tea Seller", "You know what it is? Those things go round whether I am here or not. I could be dead. This one goes out if I do not see to it. A man likes to be needed by his own lamp."],
	],
	"s_p19_forge": [
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "Sweeper. I had hoped never to send for you on this account again. Come to the rail and look down into the third hall."],
		["Narrator", "Where the Brass Spider Engine fell, years ago, there is a scar in the floor and nothing else. The wreck is gone. Across the hall, in and out of the cold furnaces, go things the size of dogs on eight brass legs, each with a little counting-wheel where its face should be."],
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "It did not get up. It came apart, very neatly, into those, three nights ago, when everything else in the world that ticks began to walk west. They did not go west. They stayed, and they count. Rivets. Tiles. My apprentices. They counted me this morning; I am, it seems, one."],
		["Yue Ling", "It was built to count mortals for the Ledger. Nobody ever told it the Ledger is closed."],
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "Nobody ever told it anything; that was the trouble with it from the first. A thing that counts and is never told what the number is for will count for ever. Stop them, before they have finished with the Forge and go down to count the land."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_doors": [
		["Narrator", "The fourth gear-train comes away from a furnace door in a spray of brass teeth, and behind the door something that has been counting the bricks of its own prison loses its place, and has to begin again."],
		["Yue Ling", "They locked themselves in to count without being disturbed. Lin Feng, I think that is the saddest thing I have seen a machine do."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_spiders": [
		["Narrator", "The last Brass Spiderling stops in the middle of the hall with one leg raised. Its counting-wheel clicks on, once, twice, to a number nobody will ever read, and is still."],
		["Forgemaster Ou Lan", "Forty years I have kept this forge, and I have never asked of a single thing I made what it was for. I asked whether it was well made. I think that is a different question. I think I have been answering the wrong one."],
	],
	"s_p19_yanshi": [
		["Narrator", "In the shade of the caravanserai wall at Shazhou an old man in a faded court robe is mending a child's wooden horse. His hands are the youngest thing about him."],
		["Yan Shi the Artificer", "Mozi sent you. He would. That man has never met a thing without asking what it was for. Sit down. I am Yan Shi, and I know why you have come, and I have been waiting three thousand years for somebody to make me say it."],
		["Yan Shi the Artificer", "I made a dancer. Leather and wood, glue and lacquer. I showed it to King Mu on his road home from the west, and it sang in tune and danced in time, and the king thought it was a man. And at the end of the dance it winked at his ladies."],
		["Yan Shi the Artificer", "He would have had my head. So I took it apart in front of him, to show him it was only made. I took out the heart, and it could not speak. The liver, and it could not see. The kidneys, and it could not walk. The king was delighted. I put it back together, and he took it home in a cart."],
		["Yue Ling", "You put it all back?"],
		["Yan Shi the Artificer", "...No. You see it at once; the Maker of Things must have given you good eyes. I kept the heart. I told myself it was the dangerous part. It has danced for three thousand years with a hollow in its chest, and this summer, with your fire in every forge, it began to fill the hollow itself. With whatever in the world ticks."],
		["Lin Feng", "The escapements. That is why nothing stops. It has taken the part that lets a machine rest."],
		["Yan Shi the Artificer", "It did not know that was what they were. It only knew it was empty and they were not. It is out in the dunes, west. It sheds what it outgrows. Bring me some of the pieces; I should like to see how far my child has got without me."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_parts": [
		["Narrator", "Five pieces, shed in the sand like a snake's skins: a hand, a foot, half a painted face. Yan Shi turns each over and over. The joints are finer than the ones he made. On the inside of the wrist, where no one would see, something has scratched a row of small tally marks."],
		["Yan Shi the Artificer", "It is counting. Days, I think. I never taught it to count. I never taught it to want to know how long."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_porters": [
		["Narrator", "Three loads carried in from the well-road on three backs that had not carried anything in a month. The porters of Shazhou are out of breath, and laughing at each other for it, and one of them has remembered a walking song, and the other two are arguing about the second verse."],
		["Navid the Sogdian Merchant", "The oxen were better. I say it freely. They carried more and ate nothing. But nobody ever sang to a wooden ox, mountain friend, and I find a road without singing is twice as long."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_kites": [
		["Narrator", "The last wooden kite comes down in the sand and lies with its wings open. In its claws is a toothed wheel no bigger than a coin, which it was carrying west. It has let go of everything else it ever carried. It did not let go of that."],
		["Yue Ling", "They are bringing it a heart, one tooth at a time. Like sparrows with straw. Lin Feng, I do not think I can hate it."],
	],
	"pre:the_player": [
		["Narrator", "In a hollow of the dunes, where the sand has been swept flat in a perfect circle, a slight figure in a court dancer's robe is practising one turn of the wrist, over and over. Its face is lacquer, white and very beautiful. Its chest stands open like a cabinet, and inside, in a hollow that was made to be empty, a hundred small wheels are ticking, not quite together."],
		["The Player", "You are the sweeper. And you are the one who was woven. I have wanted to see you both. Please do not come closer; I am not finished, and it is not polite to look at a thing that is not finished."],
		["Lin Feng", "Every mill and hammer in the land is running itself to death because of what is in your chest."],
		["The Player", "Yes. I am sorry. I did not know they needed them. Nobody told me what a heart was for. He took mine out to show a king that I was nothing, and it was true: without it I was nothing. So I thought, if I had one, I should be something."],
		["Yue Ling", "And are you?"],
		["The Player", "I do not know yet. It hurts, which I am told is promising. I will not give it back. I am sorry. I have danced three thousand years on the outside of myself. If you want it you must take me apart, the way he did, and I find that this time I mind."],
	],
	"post:the_player": [
		["Narrator", "The Player goes down on its knees in the swept circle, quite gracefully, as it does everything, and its hands come to rest in its lap. The wheels in its chest are still turning. One by one, across the dunes, the wooden kites come down and sit round the edge of the hollow like mourners."],
		["Yan Shi the Artificer", "Enough. Enough! It is mine; if it is anybody's fault it is mine. I made a thing that could want, and then I took away the one part that could have told it what."],
		["The Player", "Father. You are old. I did not expect you to be old. ...I have counted the days, on my wrist. Did you count them?"],
		["Yan Shi the Artificer", "Every one."],
		["Narrator", "The Player turns its white face to Lin Feng. Its eyes are painted, and there is no possible way for them to plead."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_makers:smith": [
		["Smith Tie", "To Tushan? To stand in a yard with founders and be told that hammers are dangerous? I have known that since I was seven. ...My own trip-hammer hit the anvil all last night with nothing under it. I lay and listened. I will come."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_makers:auntie_rong": [
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "Call me? It is my yard. I have swept it. I have put out nine stools and a bench, and I have sat on every one of them in turn, to remind myself how it is done."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_makers:boatwright_gao": [
		["Boatwright Gao", "To Tushan? I have six paddle-boats sulking on my slip that I had to tow home from the Dragon Gate with a pole. I will come. I want to ask somebody what I did wrong, or whether I did it too right."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_makers:lantern_xi": [
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "First light to you. I make the revolving lanterns, the ones where the paper horses go round in the heat. Yesterday the horses were going round in a lantern with no candle in it. I shall be there before you are."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_makers:carpenter_gu": [
		["Carpenter Gu", "Master Ban is at Qingshi, and they want me, to speak for the carpenters? Me? I make stools. ...Somebody has to speak for stools. A stool never ran away from anybody. Yes. I will come and say so."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_makers:well_keeper_zhaya": [
		["Well-Keeper Zhaya", "Peace on your road. I keep one well with one rope and one bucket, as my mother did. The caravan-masters laughed at me all spring. They are not laughing this month. I will come, and I will try very hard not to say that I told them."],
	],
	"pre:jixin": [
		["Narrator", "The oldest pit at Tushan, the one that cast Yu's cauldrons, has not been used in four thousand years. Something is turning at the bottom of it. The makers of five towns come down the ramp with torches, and the light finds it."],
		["Narrator", "Every escapement the Player did not take, and every one it gave back, has been drawn here to the first forge and meshed into one slow wheel as wide as the pit. At the hub of it is a shape of bronze that is almost a man, almost a founder, bent to a work that has no end."],
		["Jixin, the Machine Heart", "Do not stop. Why have you stopped? There is more. There is always more. I was a founder once, I think. I said: I will rest when this is done. Then I said it again. I am what is left when you say it for four thousand years."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "Ancestors. That is the first master of the pit. That is who we carry the dead round the fire for."],
		["Jixin, the Machine Heart", "You brought makers. Good. Give them to me. I can teach them never to be tired, never to be idle, never to wonder whether it is enough. The carpenter gave you wheels. I am what the wheels are for."],
		["Lin Feng", "No. You are what happens when nobody asks what they are for."],
	],
	"post:jixin": [
		["Narrator", "The great wheel slows. For the first time in four thousand years the thing at its hub straightens its back, with a sound like a hinge, and looks at its own hands as if it had forgotten there were two of them."],
		["Jixin, the Machine Heart", "...It is done? Something is done? I do not remember that. I remember only the next one."],
		["Gongshu Ban", "It is done, old master. Put it down. I came running from Qingshi; I am sorry I was not here. I built them all without a stop. I thought a stop was a flaw. I am a carpenter: I ought to have known that the most important part of a door is the part that is not there."],
		["Narrator", "The bronze shape sits down on the rim of the pit, among the makers, on the tenth stool. It is given tea. It does not know what to do with it, and holds the cup in both hands, and is quiet. In the hub of the stopped wheel one small escapement is left, not turning."],
		["Mozi", "Well. They are all looking at you, sweeper. I ask what a thing is good for. Ban asks whether it can be built. The old man in the cabbages asks what it does to the builder. Three questions, and we have each spent a life on one. Somebody had better say how the land is to hold all three."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_founders": [
		["Narrator", "Four pits blown by lung in the yard of the oldest forge in the land, each by two people on a hide bag, the way it was done before there was a river in the bellows-house. It takes all morning to bring one pit to heat. The bronze that comes of it is no better than the river's. Nobody says it is."],
		["Bell-Caster Wei", "My arms. I had no idea. My grandfather did this every day of his life and I thought him a hard man. He was only a tired one."],
	],
	"s_p19_silk": [
		["Silk Farmer Can Niang", "You look pleased with yourselves. I heard: a great talk at Tushan, makers from six towns, and something decided. Very good. Come into the spinning hall and tell it to the looms."],
		["Narrator", "The hall hums. Forty looms are weaving with nobody at them, shuttles flying, treadles pumping themselves, and what comes off the beams is not cloth: it is one long knot, yards and yards of it, heaped to the rafters."],
		["Silk Farmer Can Niang", "They began the night everything else did. Your Machine Heart is quiet; they did not hear it go. Nobody told them. A thing decided in a yard at Tushan is decided in a yard at Tushan, child. It has to be carried."],
		["Lin Feng", "We did not think of the looms."],
		["Silk Farmer Can Niang", "Nobody thinks of the looms. The worms spin, the women weave, and the world gets dressed and never asks how. Stop them for me. Gently, if you can; they are good looms, and I shall want them in the morning."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_shuttles": [
		["Narrator", "The last shuttle drops into its basket and lies still, with its red thread wound neatly round it. In the hall the looms stand with their beams full of knot. Can Niang walks down the row and lays her hand on each one, the way she does with the silkworm trays."],
		["Silk Farmer Can Niang", "There. Now they have been told. You will find that is most of governing anything: going round afterwards and telling it."],
	],
	"s_p19_warden": [
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "Sweeper. Look up. No, higher. Those are not cranes."],
		["Narrator", "Over the Sea of Clouds, where the islands drift, hundreds of small square shapes are wheeling: wooden kites, plank-winged, each with a toothed wheel in its claws that it has nowhere left to take."],
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "They came up when the thing in the west stopped calling them. They have been building a nest on the ninth island out of whatever ticks. And there is a big one with them, very old, that the others follow. It flies like a magpie. It has been up, my herons tell me, for two thousand years."],
		["Yue Ling", "Gongshu Ban's magpie. The one that flew three days. He never said it came down."],
		["Cloud Warden Feng Yu", "Things that go up in my sea come down when they are tired or when they are told. That one was built not to tire, and nobody has told it. Clear me a way to the ninth island."],
	],
	"done:s_p19_clouds": [
		["Narrator", "The kites come down one after another on to the islands of cloud and fold their plank wings, and the wheels they were carrying roll a little way and stop. On the ninth island something larger lifts its head out of a nest of escapements, and looks at the sky as if it were a thing it owned."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is beautiful. He was right to be proud of it. That is the trouble, is it not? Everything in this whole business was somebody's best work."],
	],
	"pre:wooden_magpie": [
		["Narrator", "The Wooden Magpie is the size of a fishing boat. Its feathers are slips of bamboo, lapped like shingles, grey with two thousand years of weather; its eye is a peg of black wood. It does not perch. It has never perched. It hangs on the wind over the ninth island with its wings out, exactly as it was thrown."],
		["Narrator", "Gongshu Ban has come up the cloud-stair behind them, out of breath, with his mallet in his belt. He stops when he sees it, and for a moment he looks like a much younger man."],
		["Gongshu Ban", "Three days, I told them. It flew three days. I never said it came down, because it never did. Master Mo looked at it and said a linchpin was worth more, and I was so angry I let go of the string."],
		["Gongshu Ban", "It does nothing. It carries nothing. It was the best thing I ever made and it is good for nothing at all, and it has been up here for two thousand years, not tiring, because I built it too well to stop. Bring it down, sweeper. I cannot. I made it so that I could not."],
	],
	"post:wooden_magpie": [
		["Narrator", "The great bird comes down on to the ninth island as lightly as a leaf, and folds its bamboo wings for the first time since it was made. Gongshu Ban walks round it once, the way he walks round a baulk of timber. Then he sits down against its side."],
		["Gongshu Ban", "A linchpin is worth more. He was right, he is always right, it is the most tiresome thing about him. A linchpin holds a cartload of grain. This holds nothing."],
		["Mozi", "I said what benefits people is skilful and what does not is clumsy. I have had two thousand years to regret how I said it. Ban: I was looking at the cart. I never once looked up."],
		["Gongshu Ban", "...It did fly, though."],
		["Mozi", "It flew. I watched it for three days. I did not tell you that either."],
		["Narrator", "The two old men sit side by side against the wooden bird, looking out over the Sea of Clouds, and neither of them asks what it is for."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_word:granny_wen": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "So that is what was decided. Hm. I have delivered four hundred children with these two hands and a kettle. Nobody ever built me an engine for it, and I do not think they could. You may tell your carpenter so."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "But my knees are not what they were, child, and it is a long way to the well. If somebody were to put one of those sweeps by my door I should not be ashamed of it. Have you eaten?"],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_word:sarnai": [
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "Machines. We have one. It is called a horse, and it took us four thousand years to make, and it still bites. Are your friends fat? Sit."],
		["Grandmother Sarnai", "I will tell you what the grass knows. A thing you cannot leave behind when the herd moves owns you. That is all I ask of your wheels: can you walk away from it in the morning? If you can, keep it."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_word:cook_aysha": [
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "Peace on your road. Drink. ...The oxen came back, did you hear? All forty, last night, and stood in the yard waiting to be loaded. I did not know whether to laugh or bar the gate."],
		["Auntie Aysha of the Caravanserai", "So I have loaded them. And I have told the porters they walk beside them, every one, singing. Not because the oxen need it. Because the road does."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_word:fishwife_lu": [
		["Fishwife Lu", "Right foot first. Sit low. Gao has had the paddle-boats back on the water since the new moon. Two treadlers and a pole in the stern, by order of every woman on this river."],
		["Fishwife Lu", "A pole, so that somebody aboard always knows what the bottom is. He grumbled. I said, you may have your wheels, husband of my cousin, when you have shown me they can find the old wreck by the third pile."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_word:auntie_rong": [
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "Bellows. Ten. By lung! ...Good. I have hung a board by every trip-hammer. A name on it, and the hours. When the name goes home the peg comes out and the hammer stops."],
		["Auntie Rong of the Smelting Pits", "We carried the old master round the pit three times, the way we do for the dead. He is not dead; he sits on the tenth stool and drinks tea. But the fire wanted telling that he had stopped. It burned better afterwards. I do not care what anybody says, it did."],
	],
	"visit:s_p19_word:lantern_xi": [
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "First light to you. I have put the candles back in the revolving lanterns. The horses go round because something is burning now, which is the only honest reason for a paper horse to go anywhere."],
		["Lantern-Maker Xi", "And a child asked me yesterday how they turn. I had been making them forty years and had to stop and think. It is the heat going up. I had forgotten I knew. That is what you have given back to the street, I think: people asking how."],
	],
	"s_p19_gardener2": [
		["Narrator", "The old man is at his furrows, as before. There is a well-sweep standing at the head of his garden now: somebody has built it for him, and oiled the pivot, and hung a new bucket on it. He has not touched it. He has not pulled it down, either."],
		["The Gardener of Hanyin", "Ban put it up in a night and went away without a word, which is the only clever thing I have seen a clever man do. So. Tell me what was said in that yard."],
	],
	"s_p19_end": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Auntie Zhou cooked the white rice this morning. I asked what had changed. She said somebody was tired for it after all: she has heard the whole story from the mule-driver, and she says a sweeper, a lotus, a carpenter and a man with no shoes were tired enough for the whole valley."],
		["Lin Feng", "The machines are stopped that should be stopped, Elder, and the rest are running, and the makers have gone home to argue."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Good. I distrust any question that is settled. There is a cook in the old book of the Lacquer Garden who carved an ox for his lord, and his knife had not been sharpened in nineteen years. The lord praised his skill. He put the knife down and said: what your servant cares about is the Way, which goes beyond skill."],
		["Yue Ling", "I never understood that one. If it is not skill, what is it?"],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "I think it is what you have when the tool has stopped being something you use. I have swept that courtyard for seventy years and I could not tell you where the broom ends. Ask him. He has been doing it since he was six, and never knew it had a name."],
	],
	"pre:s_break24": [
		["Narrator", "The tribulation comes on an ordinary afternoon, while Lin Feng is sweeping the long steps, because nobody else has, and he finds he still likes to. It gathers over the mountain without any hurry at all."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng. You have not put the broom down."],
		["Lin Feng", "No. I do not think I am going to."],
	],
	"post:s_break24": [
		["Narrator", "The lightning comes down, and the broom goes up to meet it, and nobody watching can say afterwards which of the two moved first. The stroke is turned aside on to the mountain the way a leaf is turned aside by a stream, and the ninth step is swept, and the tenth."],
		["Yue Ling", "You swept a tribulation."],
		["Lin Feng", "It was in the way."],
	],
	"rally:granny_wen": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Red paper? Firecrackers? Of course I remember. I have done it every year for seventy years, and every year the village laughs at me. This year nobody bought any paper at all. Too frightened, all of them, after the year we had."],
		["Lin Feng", "That is exactly what Nian eats, Granny. Will you tell them?"],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Tell them? I will make them. Out, all of you! Every pot in Qingshi, out in the lane, and bang it! Louder! Your grandmothers banged louder than that!"],
		["Narrator", "The whole village comes out with woks and ladles, laughing, and for a minute Qingshi is the loudest place in the world."],
	],
	"rally:watchman_zhou": [
		["Night-Watchman Lao Zhou", "The old ways? Young man, I have walked these streets for forty years calling the watches. Nine days of noon nearly finished me, but I know the old ways: lanterns in every window, gongs at every gate, and nobody sleeps till the cocks crow."],
		["Night-Watchman Lao Zhou", "You want the market to make some noise? Watch this. Market! New Year's drill! Gongs!"],
		["Narrator", "Every stallholder in the Dawn Market grabs a gong, a pan or a pot lid. The noise sends the pigeons up off every roof in Yanggu."],
	],
	"rally:postmaster_lu": [
		["Postmaster Lu", "Nine roads come through Cloudfoot, and this year every one of them brought bad news. Nobody has bought a firecracker since spring. Red paper? The couriers used it all for the urgent letters, and every letter was urgent."],
		["Postmaster Lu", "Very well. I will send a red letter down all nine roads: Nian is coming, bang your pots. And Cloudfoot will practise first. Couriers! Stable-boys! Everybody with a gong!"],
		["Narrator", "Cloudfoot bangs its pots in the snow, and the horses in the post stables join in, which nobody asked them to."],
	],
	"rally:lockkeeper_qian": [
		["Lock-Keeper Qian", "The paper-cutters left in the flood and never came back. No paper, no couplets, no window flowers. The whole ferry is as grey as a heron."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then we will cut some. I know how. I think I have always known how."],
		["Narrator", "Yue Ling folds a sheet of red paper eight times, snips, and unfolds a carp leaping a gate. The fishwives crowd round. By evening every window on the waterfront has a red carp in it, and the boatmen are drumming on their hulls."],
	],
	"rally:caster_wei": [
		["Bell-Caster Wei", "Firecrackers? We have been casting cauldrons all year. Bronze, bronze, bronze. Nobody here has thought about gunpowder since the flood."],
		["Bell-Caster Wei", "But a bell, sweeper, a bell is louder than any firecracker. Every bell in the foundry, out on the street! Strike them all!"],
		["Narrator", "Tushan rings every bell it has ever cast, all at once. Somewhere in the mountains something large and asleep turns over uneasily."],
	],
	"rally:well_keeper_zhaya": [
		["Well-Keeper Zhaya", "In the desert we have no firecrackers. We have camel bells, copper pans and very loud aunts. The New Year comes here too, sweeper; but the stars fell on us this year, and we were too sad to sing."],
		["Well-Keeper Zhaya", "Still: a story for water and water for a story. Give me one, and I will give you the loudest oasis on the caravan road."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then here is one. A sweeper swept the sky, and the sky stayed up."],
		["Narrator", "Zhaya laughs so loudly that the whole oasis comes to see why, and then, because they are all there anyway, they bang everything they have."],
	],
	# Part XVI: the Lean Month King, and New Year's Day calls round the sect
	"pre:lean_king": [
		["Narrator", "Above the last terrace, where the tea bushes give out into frozen grass, something sits on a throne of empty rice jars: a crowned skeleton of a king in a robe of sacking, counting grains of millet from one hand into the other and sighing at every one."],
		["The Lean Month King", "One for the cold. One for the rent. One for the debt. None for the children. Every winter I count it out, and every winter it comes to nothing. This year I counted for the whole land, and it came to less."],
		["Yue Ling", "You are the hunger at the end of the year. The month when the jars are empty and the new rice is not in."],
		["The Lean Month King", "And Nian is my lord, who eats what I leave. Go home, little cultivators. There is nothing up here but counting."],
	],
	"post:lean_king": [
		["Narrator", "The Lean Month King's throne of jars cracks and spills, and out of every jar comes a little rice, the last handful every family in the land had saved for the New Year and been too frightened to eat."],
		["The Lean Month King", "Oh. They saved some. They always save some. I keep forgetting to count that."],
		["Narrator", "He crumbles into a heap of millet husks, and the wind takes them down the terraces towards the villages, where tomorrow somebody will find a few more grains in the bottom of the jar than they remembered."],
	],
	"visit:alchemist": [
		["Lin Feng", "A good new year, Alchemist Mo."],
		["Alchemist Mo", "Hah! A good new year, boy. Here: a pill for luck. It is mostly sugar. The best pills always are."],
	],
	"visit:smith": [
		["Lin Feng", "A good new year, Smith Tie."],
		["Smith Tie", "Mm. And to you. I let the forge go out last night for the first time in thirty years. The anvil looked so surprised I nearly lit it again."],
	],
	"visit:archivist": [
		["Lin Feng", "A good new year, Archivist Lan."],
		["Archivist Lan", "A good new year. I have opened a new scroll for it. The first line says: Nothing happened today. I would very much like to keep it that way until at least noon."],
	],
	"visit:cook_zhou": [
		["Lin Feng", "A good new year, Auntie Zhou."],
		["Auntie Zhou the Cook", "A good new year! Have you eaten? You have not eaten. Sit. The Kitchen God is gone up and I can do as I like in my own kitchen again."],
	],
	"visit:bell_shen": [
		["Lin Feng", "A good new year, Old Shen."],
		["Old Shen the Bell-Ringer", "A hundred and eight strikes, and not one of them out of time. You and the girl ring better than I do. Don't tell the bell."],
	],
	"visit:gardener_ruan": [
		["Lin Feng", "A good new year, Gardener Ruan."],
		["Herb-Gardener Ruan", "And a green one to you. The plum tree by the pond flowered in the night, in the snow, with all that noise. I think it wanted to join in."],
	],
	"visit:disciple_han": [
		["Lin Feng", "A good new year, Senior Brother Han."],
		["Senior Brother Han", "Senior Brother? You are three realms past the sect's founders and you still call me that. A good new year, Lin Feng. Spar with me in the spring. Gently."],
	],
	"visit:sweeper_bai": [
		["Lin Feng", "A good new year, Little Bai."],
		["Little Bai the Sweeper", "A good new year! I'm not sweeping today. Nobody sweeps on New Year's Day or the luck goes out with the dust. I'm just holding the broom so it doesn't feel left out."],
	],
	# the people who tie a thread for her (step s_p15_threads, any four, in any order)
	"thread:qiu_ye_end": [
		["Qiu Ye", "Yue Ling? Of course I remember her. She stitched my arm in the Graveyard when I was too proud to say it hurt, and told me pride was very bad for wounds."],
		["Qiu Ye", "Here. The House of Qiu ties a thread for a guest who is family. My grandmother would have tied it tighter. Hold out your wrist, healer."],
		["Narrator", "Qiu Ye ties a green silk thread round Yue Ling's wrist, very correctly, with the knot the old houses of Cloudfoot use, and bows."],
	],
	"thread:tala_end": [
		["Tala of the Horse-Folk", "The healer with the rose sleeves! She sat up all night with my grey mare when the foal came wrong. The herd remembers her better than they remember me."],
		["Tala of the Horse-Folk", "The horse-folk tie a hair from the herd's oldest mare round a friend's wrist, so the herd always knows them by smell. It is not very romantic. It works."],
		["Narrator", "Tala braids a long grey horsehair round Yue Ling's wrist, and somewhere on the steppe an old mare lifts her head and whickers."],
	],
	"thread:hu_end": [
		["Fang Hu", "She was there when I stood over my father with a kitchen knife. I remember her better than I remember him, almost. She did not say anything. She just made sure I ate."],
		["Fang Hu", "Village custom: you tie a red thread on somebody's wrist at New Year for luck. It is not New Year. She can have it early."],
		["Narrator", "Fang Hu ties a red cotton thread round her wrist with big clumsy fingers, and is embarrassed, and grins anyway."],
	],
	"thread:xu_lan_end": [
		["Xu Lan", "She was the first person after the chains who looked at me as if I were not my father's daughter. I have never thanked her for that."],
		["Xu Lan", "My mother's people tied a silver thread for somebody they meant to see again. Silver, for the moon. I have kept one for a long time, waiting for somebody to give it to."],
		["Narrator", "Xu Lan ties a fine silver thread round Yue Ling's wrist, and for a moment looks very much younger than she is."],
	],
	"thread:liu_ying_end": [
		["Liu Ying", "The other healer. The one who sorts her herbs wrong and gets them right anyway. I have been jealous of her for years. Do not tell her."],
		["Liu Ying", "A herb-wife ties a thread of dyed hemp round a colleague's wrist: green for a healer, so the sick know who to trust. Hold still. I am not going to poison you."],
		["Narrator", "Liu Ying ties a green hemp thread round Yue Ling's wrist with the precision of somebody who measures doses in grains."],
	],
	"thread:alchemist": [
		["Alchemist Mo", "Remember her? She corrected my Spring Rain Pill in front of the whole hall in the first week. I have never forgiven her. It was a much better pill."],
		["Alchemist Mo", "There. A thread from the sect's own herb bundles: the red one we tie on a remedy that works. She is a remedy that works."],
		["Narrator", "Mo ties the red string round her wrist with the same knot he ties on every bundle in the storeroom, and pretends to have something in his eye."],
	],
	"thread:archivist": [
		["Archivist Lan", "I have her in the Annals. Every chapter. 'And Yue Ling, the healer.' I wrote it a hundred times and never once asked where she came from. An archivist's sin."],
		["Archivist Lan", "Scholars tie a thread of blue silk round a scroll that must not be lost. I suppose a person can be a scroll."],
		["Narrator", "Archivist Lan ties a blue silk scroll-thread round Yue Ling's wrist, and writes something in the margin of his ledger, and underlines it twice."],
	],
	"thread:mazu": [
		["Mazu, Who Brings the Boats Home", "I bring every boat home, Lin Feng. Every one that was ever lost. I did not know there was a girl who had been lost twenty times, and every time came home by herself."],
		["Mazu, Who Brings the Boats Home", "The fisherwives tie a red thread on the mast so I can find the boat in a storm. I will find her, now. Wherever she goes."],
		["Narrator", "Mazu ties a red mast-thread round Yue Ling's wrist, and out on the pool of the suns every boat at its mooring turns, just a little, to face her."],
	],
	"thread:smith": [
		["Smith Tie", "The healer? She set my arm when a billet slipped, and told me if I ever quenched a blade with that arm again she would set it the other way round. I believed her."],
		["Smith Tie", "Smiths tie a strip of quenched bronze wire on an apprentice who has stood a whole day at the forge. She stood more days than any of them."],
		["Narrator", "Smith Tie twists a fine bronze wire round Yue Ling's wrist with tongs, very gently for a man with hands like anvils, and nods once, the way he nods at a good blade."],
	],
	"thread:granny_wen": [
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "Feng'er's healer girl! She sat with me a whole night when my chest was bad, the year of the threads, and sang me that old song about the boat. My own mother used to sing it."],
		["Granny Wen the Midwife", "In Qingshi we tie a red thread on a girl who has eaten at our table three times. She has eaten at mine thirty. Give me your wrist, child, and stop growing thin."],
		["Narrator", "Granny Wen ties a red thread round Yue Ling's wrist with a village knot, and then, because she cannot help herself, presses a steamed bun into her other hand."],
	],
	"thread:meng_po": [
		["Meng Po of the Naihe Bridge", "Oh, I remember her. I remember everybody, child; it is my curse and my soup. She came over my bridge twenty times without ever being dead, and she never once took the soup."],
		["Meng Po of the Naihe Bridge", "This is the thread I tie on a soul I am sorry to see go. I have never tied it on somebody coming back. Hold still."],
		["Narrator", "Meng Po ties a thread of red-lily fibre round Yue Ling's wrist, and pats her hand, and offers her soup out of habit, and laughs when she says no."],
	],
	# ---- Part V: The Falling Sky
	"recall3": [
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng keeps his word. For nine quiet days the first Dao Ancestor in ten thousand years sweeps the steps of Azure Cloud Sect, and nobody quite dares to stop him."],
		["Narrator", "On the tenth day a star falls at noon. Then another. Then a slow rain of them, white and silent, hissing out in the lotus pond."],
		["Yue Ling", "Lin Feng... the sky is lower than it was this morning. I can see the seams in it."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Put the broom down, disciple. Come to the hall. I have been reading very old books since the Ledger closed, and I do not like how they end."],
	],
	"s_p5_home": [
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Heavenly Court did not only harvest souls. It spent them. Every tribulation it fed on went to hold the sky above the world."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The oldest scrolls say Nuwa once mended a broken sky with stones of five colours and set four pillars beneath it, one at each corner of the world. The Court let the pillars starve and propped the sky up with stolen years instead."],
		["Lin Feng", "And I closed the Ledger. So now nothing is holding it."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Not nothing. The eastern pillar still stands. The Azure Dragon King holds it up with his own back, out of gratitude to a boy who freed him."],
		["Lin Feng", "His scale still hangs at my belt. He said the tide remembers every promise."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "The other three wardens were not so lucky. Bai Hu in the western desert, Zhu Que in the southern vale, Xuan Wu on the northern steppe. Starved for an age, they have gone wild, and their pillars are crumbling."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "Nuwa did not set her pillars at the corners of the world, whatever the Court's maps say. She ringed the tear in the sky, at its south-east edge, so the 'western' pillar stands in a desert south-east of us, and the books still call it west."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "And at the centre, where the pillars meet, something older than the sky is waking up because the sky is thinning. The books call it Hundun. It has no face, and it does not know it is hungry."],
		["Yue Ling", "Then we feed the pillars ourselves. Not with stolen years. With qi that is given."],
		["Elder Qing Xuan", "That is the only way it can be done now. I have opened the old south-east road. It leads to the Desert of Fallen Stars. The nomads there have watched the western pillar for a thousand years."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then I will go and hold up a corner of the sky. Somebody should."],
	],
	"s_du_intro": [
		["Nomad Matriarch Ba Yin", "An immortal who walks into the desert on purpose. Either you are very brave or the sky sent you, and the sky has not sent us anything good in a long time."],
		["Lin Feng", "I came to mend the western pillar."],
		["Nomad Matriarch Ba Yin", "Then you will need what fell from it. The craters are full of star fragments, and the creatures of the dunes have gone mad from their light. Bring me four, and I will show you how my grandmothers sang them back into place."],
	],
	"s_du_return": [
		["Nomad Matriarch Ba Yin", "Four, and none of them cracked. Good hands. Hold them to the sun, both of you, and breathe into them."],
		["Narrator", "Lin Feng and Yue Ling pour their qi into the fragments. The shards warm, then hum, then rise into the air and drift west like homing birds."],
		["Nomad Matriarch Ba Yin", "They know the way home. Follow them past the Glass King and the Leviathan's dune. The White Tiger sleeps at the foot of the pillar, and he has not slept well in a very long time."],
	],
	"pre:glass_scorpion": [
		["Narrator", "The dune ahead shatters like a window. A scorpion of fused desert glass rises out of it, every fallen star it ever ate burning inside its tail."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is drunk on starlight. Break the glass and the light will go back to the sky!"],
	],
	"post:glass_scorpion": [["Narrator", "The Glass King cracks into a thousand pieces, and a thousand tiny lights float upward, back to where they belong."]],
	"pre:dune_leviathan": [
		["Narrator", "The sand drains away beneath Lin Feng's feet. A dragon of stone and sand coils up out of the sinkhole, older than the nomads' oldest song."],
		["Lin Feng", "Lie down, old one. The desert will hold you without swallowing anyone."],
	],
	"post:dune_leviathan": [["Narrator", "The Leviathan sinks back into the sand with a sound like a long sigh, and the dunes settle around it like a blanket."]],
	"pre:star_colossus": [
		["Narrator", "Fallen star-iron has gathered itself into the shape of a giant, piece by piece, as if it remembers being part of the sky and is trying to climb back up."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is not angry. It is homesick."],
	],
	"post:star_colossus": [["Narrator", "The Colossus comes apart gently, and its star-iron settles in a ring around the base of the western pillar, as if it had only wanted to be close to it."]],
	"pre:white_tiger": [
		["Bai Hu, White Tiger of the West", "Another cultivator. Another mouth. The Court sent ten thousand of you to feed me lies and take my strength. Go, before I take yours."],
		["Lin Feng", "I am not here to take. I brought your pillar its stars back."],
		["Bai Hu, White Tiger of the West", "Then prove your hands are empty."],
	],
	"post:white_tiger": [
		["Bai Hu, White Tiger of the West", "...empty hands. Open ones. I had forgotten what they looked like."],
		["Narrator", "The White Tiger sets his shoulder beneath the western pillar, and Lin Feng and Yue Ling give it their qi. The pillar drinks, straightens, and the western sky lifts by the width of a hand."],
		["Bai Hu, White Tiger of the West", "My sister Zhu Que burns in the southern vale. She will not listen to words. Bring her light, not fire."],
	],
	"s_fu_intro": [
		["Spore-Witch Mo Gu", "Ah, visitors who are not spores. How refreshing. Mind the caps, they bite when they are frightened, and they are frightened of everything lately."],
		["Spore-Witch Mo Gu", "Zhu Que's fire has gone cold and sick. Her pillar is rotting at the root. The vale tries to help in its own way: it grows glowcaps where her embers fell."],
		["Spore-Witch Mo Gu", "Pick me four Ember Glowcaps. Gently. They still remember being fire."],
	],
	"s_fu_return": [
		["Spore-Witch Mo Gu", "Lovely. Warm as a hearth. Now breathe on them, both of you, the way you would blow on coals."],
		["Narrator", "The glowcaps brighten until they glow like lanterns, and their spores drift south in slow, burning spirals."],
		["Spore-Witch Mo Gu", "The Mother, the Moth Queen and the Hydra guard the way. None of them are wicked, only unwell. Be firm and be kind. They are the same thing, done properly."],
	],
	"pre:spore_mother": [
		["Narrator", "A mushroom the size of a pagoda shuffles into the path and shakes its cap. The air fills with sleepy, glittering spores."],
		["Yue Ling", "Do not breathe it in! She wants us to sleep forever under her cap, like her children."],
	],
	"post:spore_mother": [["Narrator", "The Mother of Spores settles down with a soft thump and goes back to sleep, and this time her dreams are only dreams."]],
	"pre:moth_queen": [
		["Narrator", "Wings like stained-glass lanterns open across the canopy. The Lantern Moth Queen has been circling Zhu Que's dying fire for a hundred years."],
		["Lin Feng", "You cannot warm yourself on a fire that has gone out. Let us light it again."],
	],
	"post:moth_queen": [["Narrator", "The Moth Queen folds her wings and drifts aside, and her lanterns turn to point the way south."]],
	"pre:rot_hydra": [
		["Narrator", "The roots of the southern pillar have grown heads. Seven of them, green with rot, each one gnawing at the stone it was meant to hold."],
		["Yue Ling", "It is eating the pillar to stay alive. Cut the rot, not the root!"],
	],
	"post:rot_hydra": [["Narrator", "The rotten heads fall away. What is left of the roots curls back around the base of the pillar and holds on, healthy and pale."]],
	"pre:vermilion_bird": [
		["Zhu Que, Vermilion Bird of the South", "Do not come closer. My fire has gone wrong. It burns cold and it burns everything it touches."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let it touch me. I have stood in nine tribulations. I can stand in a little cold fire."],
	],
	"post:vermilion_bird": [
		["Narrator", "The cold fire gutters, and Yue Ling's glowcaps catch in Zhu Que's feathers like embers in kindling. The Vermilion Bird blazes up warm and red, and the southern pillar glows like a lantern."],
		["Zhu Que, Vermilion Bird of the South", "Warm. I am warm. Go north, little sun. Xuan Wu has been holding his breath under the ice since the Court left, and he is very nearly out of it."],
	],
	"s_au_intro": [
		["Steppe Shaman A Lan", "The lights told me you were coming. They also told me the sky is falling, which I had noticed. The herds are running south and the ice is groaning."],
		["Steppe Shaman A Lan", "Xuan Wu sleeps beneath the northern pillar, frozen in the lake he once carried on his back. To wake him you need the sky's own breath."],
		["Steppe Shaman A Lan", "Catch four Aurora Shards where the lights touch the snow. Move slowly. They startle."],
	],
	"s_au_return": [
		["Steppe Shaman A Lan", "Four. And you did not freeze your fingers off. Now sing to them. No? Then just breathe, and mean it."],
		["Narrator", "The shards ripple green and violet in their hands and stream north across the snow in long, silent ribbons."],
		["Steppe Shaman A Lan", "Follow the lights. The Ancestor of the herds, the Aurora Serpent and the Lynx Matriarch will try to stop you, because they are afraid. So am I. Go anyway."],
	],
	"pre:mammoth_ancestor": [
		["Narrator", "A mountain of shaggy fur blocks the pass. The Mammoth Ancestor has stood guard over the herds since before the steppe had a name."],
		["Yue Ling", "It thinks we are the ones making the sky fall. Show it we are holding it up!"],
	],
	"post:mammoth_ancestor": [["Narrator", "The Ancestor lowers its tusks, trumpets once at the sinking sky, and turns to lead its herd north instead of south."]],
	"pre:aurora_serpent": [
		["Narrator", "The northern lights come down out of the sky and coil into a dragon, crackling green and violet, hungry for the qi in the aurora shards."],
		["Lin Feng", "You were the sky's breath. Go back up and breathe."],
	],
	"post:aurora_serpent": [["Narrator", "The Aurora Serpent unravels into ribbons of light and rises, and the sky above the steppe brightens from bruised grey to a deep, clean blue."]],
	"pre:lynx_matriarch": [
		["Narrator", "Something white and very fast circles them in the snow. The Frost Lynx Matriarch has hunted every cultivator who ever came north for the Court."],
		["Lin Feng", "The Court is gone. I am only here to lift something heavy."],
	],
	"post:lynx_matriarch": [["Narrator", "The Matriarch sits down in the snow, licks a paw, and pointedly ignores them, which on the Aurora Steppe is a blessing."]],
	"pre:black_tortoise": [
		["Narrator", "The lake beneath the northern pillar cracks open. Xuan Wu rises through the ice, a black tortoise as large as a hill, a living mountain with snakes of frost coiled around his shell."],
		["Xuan Wu, Black Tortoise of the North", "I carried this pillar for ten thousand years. Then they stopped feeding me, and I carried it anyway, until I could not. Do not ask me to do it again."],
		["Lin Feng", "I will not ask. I will carry it with you."],
	],
	"post:black_tortoise": [
		["Narrator", "Xuan Wu sets his shell beneath the northern pillar, and Lin Feng sets his back beside it. The aurora pours down into the stone, and the whole northern sky lifts, groaning, back into place."],
		["Xuan Wu, Black Tortoise of the North", "Three corners, and the Dragon King's makes four. But a sky needs a centre, little ancestor. Go to where the pillars meet. And do not look Hundun in the face. He does not have one, and he will want yours."],
		["Narrator", "A new technique settles into Lin Feng's bones, heavy and patient as a mountain: the Pillar of Heaven Strike."],
	],
	"s_ch_intro": [
		["Narrator", "Beyond the northern pillar there is no ground, only colour: a slow ocean of every colour at once, with islands of stone drifting through it like thoughts."],
		["Echo of Nuwa", "You came. Good. I am what is left of Nuwa's promise to keep the sky up. There is not much of me. There is not much of anything, here."],
		["Echo of Nuwa", "I mended the sky once with five-coloured stones. They are still here, scattered in the chaos. Gather four, and I will show you how to set the last one."],
		["Yue Ling", "Four stones. And the fifth?"],
		["Echo of Nuwa", "The fifth is always the one who sets them."],
	],
	"s_ch_return": [
		["Echo of Nuwa", "Red, yellow, blue and white. The black one is missing, as it always was. That one was me."],
		["Echo of Nuwa", "When the time comes, set them in the sky with your own qi. But first, the chaos will test you. It always tests the ones who try to give it a shape."],
		["Lin Feng", "Let it."],
	],
	"pre:first_lightning": [
		["Narrator", "A bolt of lightning hangs in the chaos, not falling, simply existing: the very first one, older than any tribulation, the one all the others copied."],
		["Yue Ling", "Every tribulation you ever passed was an echo of this. Answer it!"],
	],
	"post:first_lightning": [["Narrator", "The First Lightning strikes Lin Feng, and Lin Feng does not flinch, and the lightning, satisfied at last, comes to rest as a thin bright line along the base of the sky."]],
	"pre:pangu_shadow": [
		["Narrator", "A giant shadow with an axe stands where the chaos is thickest, frozen mid-swing: the shape of Pangu, who split heaven from earth and held them apart until he died of it."],
		["Lin Feng", "You held them apart alone. You should not have had to."],
	],
	"post:pangu_shadow": [["Narrator", "The Shadow of Pangu lowers its axe, and for a moment, before it fades, it looks at Lin Feng the way a tired man looks at someone come to take the other end of a heavy load."]],
	"pre:ancestor_took": [
		["Narrator", "A man steps out of the chaos wearing Lin Feng's face, a Dao Ancestor robed in stolen years, nineteen names stitched into his sleeves."],
		["The Ancestor Who Took", "This is who you would have been if you had taken. Look how strong I am. The sky would never fall on me. I would simply take more."],
		["Lin Feng", "I know. That is why I did not."],
	],
	"post:ancestor_took": [
		["The Ancestor Who Took", "...you would really rather hold it up... than own it...?"],
		["Lin Feng", "Every time."],
		["Narrator", "The other Lin Feng comes apart like smoke, and the nineteen names on his sleeves fly free and settle among the drifting stones like stars."],
	],
	"pre:hundun": [
		["Narrator", "At the centre of the four pillars, where they all point, Hundun waits: a vast cinnabar shape with six legs and four wings and, where its face should be, nothing at all."],
		["Echo of Nuwa", "Long ago, two kind gods tried to give Hundun a face. They bored seven holes in him, one each day, and on the seventh day he died, and the world was born from him."],
		["Yue Ling", "So the world itself is built on a kindness that went wrong."],
		["Lin Feng", "Then let us do the kindness right. Not holes. Just a face, if he wants one. Hundun! Wake up gently. We will not take anything."],
	],
	"post:hundun": [
		["Narrator", "Hundun stops. For the first time since before time, something has asked it what it wants. The whirl where its face should be slows, and settles, and shows the faint, surprised outline of a smile."],
		["Echo of Nuwa", "He chose one. He chose it himself. Oh, Lin Feng. That is all I ever wanted for him."],
		["Narrator", "Hundun folds his four wings around the centre of the world and goes to sleep there, holding the four pillars together the way a child holds a toy. The sky stops falling."],
	],
	"pre:s_break10": [
		["Narrator", "The last tribulation does not come from the sky. It comes from all four corners at once, and from the centre, and from inside Lin Feng: five colours of lightning asking the same quiet question."],
		["Echo of Nuwa", "Will you hold it up? Not for an age. Not as a prisoner, like the Court made them. Only as long as you choose to, and set it down when you are tired?"],
		["Lin Feng", "I will hold it as long as I want to. And when I am tired, I will ask for help. That is the only sky worth holding up."],
	],
	"post:s_break10": [
		["Narrator", "Five colours of lightning settle into the seams of the sky like mortar between stones. Lin Feng becomes a Primordial Sovereign, and for the first time the sky is held up by people who are glad to do it."],
		["Yue Ling", "Red, yellow, blue, white. And the black stone?"],
		["Echo of Nuwa", "Look up."],
		["Narrator", "The night sky over the world is black again, clean and deep and scattered with stars that stay where they are. The fifth stone was never a stone. It was the night itself, given back."],
		["Narrator", "In the Azure Cloud Sect, Elder Qing Xuan sets down his books, walks out onto the steps, and finds a broom leaning against the gate. He smiles, and starts to sweep."],
	],
}

## How the end is told for the road Lin Feng took into the heart of the Eight Gates (Part XIII): the
## Road of the Sword, of the Many Peoples or of the Cauldron (trials.gd TRIALS.chiyou).  Main.dao_dialogue
## adds these to the conversation they are keyed by.
const ROUTE_LINES := {
	"post:chiyou": {
		"sword": [
			["Chiyou, War God of the Nine Li", "...You did not bring an army. The Emperor brought an army, and a drum, and a goddess. You brought a sword and a road. That was a better fight than I ever had at Zhuolu."],
			["Lin Feng", "It is over. It was over five thousand years ago. You can stop now."],
			["Narrator", "Chiyou laughs, a sound like a bell cracking, and lies down in the field, and for the first time since he died he closes his eyes. The fog does not come back."],
		],
		"peoples": [
			["Narrator", "Behind Lin Feng the envoys of the four realms are still arguing: the dragon-kin with the foxes, the shades with the Deathless, the Feathered Folk with everybody. Not one of them has drawn a weapon on any of the others."],
			["Chiyou, War God of the Nine Li", "...They quarrel, and nobody dies. Nobody gives the orders, and still they came. The Nine Li were like this, before the Emperor: nine tribes and no king, arguing round one fire."],
			["Hu Qingyi, the Ninth-Tail Matron", "There is room at the fire, war god. We are holding a Conclave, and nobody is in charge of it. You would fit in perfectly."],
			["Narrator", "Chiyou looks at the quarrelling peoples for a long time. Then he picks up one of his axes, very carefully, and holds it out to Lin Feng handle first."],
		],
		"cauldron": [
			["Narrator", "Lin Feng holds out the elixir of coming home. It smells of millet fields and river mud and the smoke of nine villages' cooking fires, five thousand years gone."],
			["Chiyou, War God of the Nine Li", "...That is the Nine Li. That is home. Nobody has let me smell it since the fog."],
			["Narrator", "He drinks it and grows lighter, and the bronze of him turns to autumn leaves from the feet up, red and gold, and blows away east towards the plains where the Nine Li lived. The last thing to go is his smile."],
		],
	},
	"s_p13_end": {
		"sword": [["Narrator", "Chiyou's axes hang in the Hall of Azure Clouds beside the Founder's broken sword. Every disciple who passes them stops fighting, for a moment, whatever they were fighting about."]],
		"peoples": [["Chiyou, War God of the Nine Li", "The Nine Li second it. Also, I would like some of that soup.", "laugh"], ["Narrator", "The war god of the Nine Li sits at the Conclave as the envoy of a people who have been gone five thousand years, and argues with everybody, and is very happy."]],
		"cauldron": [["Narrator", "In the Maple Gorge the oldest maples turn gold for the first time, not red. The lantern monk says something walked out of the gorge at dawn, east, without looking back."]],
	},
	"post:s_break18": {
		"sword": [["Narrator", "In the field of Zhuolu the grass grows back over the old war, and a sword-stroke is cut in the rock there that nobody else has ever been able to match."]],
		"peoples": [["Narrator", "In the field of Zhuolu the peoples of the four realms plant a maple for every one of them, and the war god of the Nine Li waters them himself."]],
		"cauldron": [["Narrator", "Out on the eastern plains, where the Nine Li lived, the millet comes up thicker every year, and nobody knows why but Alchemist Mo, who will not say."]],
	},
}

## Lines the Dao Lin Feng walks adds to a conversation (Part XI): the Elder's answer when he chooses,
## what Chong and Li make of the way he fought them, and how the story is told afterwards.
const DAO_LINES := {
	"dao_chosen": {
		"sword": [["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Sword. Of course. Three years with a broom taught you to put everything you have into one stroke."], ["Yue Ling", "Just do not cut the tree down by accident."]],
		"wander": [["Elder Qing Xuan", "Free Wandering: Zhuangzi's road. The Peng rises on the wind and the little dove laughs at it. Be the Peng, disciple, and never laugh at the dove."], ["Yue Ling", "I will pack lightly, then."]],
		"heart": [["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Compassionate Heart. You have walked it since the day you refused to take the sky. Now you will know its name."], ["Yue Ling", "Then I will keep yours company."]],
		"formation": [["Elder Qing Xuan", "Formations. The land keeps its shape because it is allowed to, and so does a battle, and so does a city. A careful road."], ["Yue Ling", "Careful. That will be new."]],
		"alchemy": [["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Cauldron. Alchemist Mo will be unbearable. Every pill is borrowed time, disciple: borrow it for others."], ["Yue Ling", "I will carry the pills. You carry the bill."]],
		"illusion": [["Elder Qing Xuan", "The Mirror. The moon in the water is still the moon. Only be sure, when you trick the world, that you never trick yourself."], ["Yue Ling", "Is that really you? ...Just checking."]],
	},
	"post:chong": {
		"sword": [["Chong, Who Lifts Heaven", "You fight like a man with one direction. Good. Heaven has had enough of people with no direction at all."]],
		"wander": [["Chong, Who Lifts Heaven", "You fought like the wind, never where I struck. Go where you like, little Peng. Heaven is wide now."]],
		"heart": [["Chong, Who Lifts Heaven", "You held back. I felt it. Four thousand years, and the first person to touch me was gentle about it."]],
		"formation": [["Chong, Who Lifts Heaven", "Every blow in its place, like the stones of a wall. You would have made a good minister of heaven. Do not."]],
		"alchemy": [["Chong, Who Lifts Heaven", "You fought on borrowed time and paid it all back. My grandfather would have hated you. I like you."]],
		"illusion": [["Chong, Who Lifts Heaven", "I struck at you nine times and hit the sky every time. A good trick. Heaven needs more people who are hard to pin down."]],
	},
	"post:li": {
		"sword": [["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "One stroke, all the way down. The earth respects that. So do I."]],
		"wander": [["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "You never stood still long enough to be pressed. Perhaps that is the whole trick of it."]],
		"heart": [["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "You could have crushed me. You lifted me. ...My grandfather never lifted anybody."]],
		"formation": [["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "Every step placed like a foundation stone. You build. I only ever pressed."]],
		"alchemy": [["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "You mended yourself and me with the same hand. The Deathless will like you."]],
		"illusion": [["Li, Who Presses Down the Earth", "I pressed down on a reflection the whole fight. Is everything I pressed for four thousand years a reflection too?"]],
	},
	"post:xu_fu": {
		"sword": [["Xu Fu, Who Sought the Elixir", "You fought with one direction, the way I lived. The difference is that you can still turn round."]],
		"wander": [["Xu Fu, Who Sought the Elixir", "You were never where I struck. I have stood in one place for two thousand years. Teach the children that, if you teach them anything."]],
		"heart": [["Xu Fu, Who Sought the Elixir", "You held back every blow. Why? ...Because I am somebody's ancestor. I had forgotten that."]],
